/* THE PSYCHIATRIC RESIDENT: PUBLIC TEACHING PREVIEW
   Made automatically from the members files. Free lines in full;
   members lines show the plain-English version and the first two points only.
   The full members teaching lives in content/members/, which Netlify
   only sends to signed-in accounts holding curriculum, member or admin. */
var TEACH = (typeof TEACH === "undefined") ? {} : TEACH;
TEACH["1.1.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Two different ways of learning, and your dog shows you both. Ring a bell before dinner every night and eventually the bell alone makes your dog dribble. Nobody taught the dog anything, the bell just borrowed the food's job. That is classical conditioning. Now give your dog a biscuit every time it sits. It sits more. That is operant conditioning: the dog does something, something good follows, so it does it again. One is about what comes BEFORE. The other is about what comes AFTER.","teach":["Classical conditioning, Pavlov. Food is the unconditioned stimulus because it works without training. The bell becomes the conditioned stimulus. Get these the wrong way round and you lose a mark you already knew.","Operant conditioning, Skinner. Reinforcement increases behaviour, punishment decreases it. Positive means something is added, negative means something is removed.","Negative reinforcement is relief, not punishment. The tablet stops the pain so you take it again. It INCREASES the behaviour.","Observational learning, Bandura. Four requirements: attention, retention, reproduction, motivation.","Incubation: brief exposure to the feared thing alone can make the fear stronger, not weaker. This is why avoidance keeps a phobia alive.","Preparedness, Seligman. We learn to fear snakes and heights far more easily than we learn to fear plug sockets. Evolution loaded the dice.","Two-factor theory, Mowrer. Fear is learned classically, avoidance is maintained operantly by the relief. This one sentence explains phobia treatment."],"cards":[["In Pavlov's experiment, which is the unconditioned stimulus?","The food. It causes salivation with no learning. The bell is the conditioned stimulus."],["Which reinforcement schedule is most resistant to extinction?","Variable ratio. Reward after an unpredictable number of responses. The fruit machine."],["What is reciprocal inhibition and who described it?","Wolpe. You cannot be relaxed and anxious at once, so pairing relaxation with graded exposure inhibits the anxiety. The basis of systematic desensitisation."],["Distinguish shaping from chaining.","Shaping reinforces successive approximations to a target behaviour. Chaining links a sequence of behaviours, each cueing the next, built forwards or backwards."]],"note":["Delayed (forward) conditioning, CS starting before and overlapping the UCS, gives the strongest learning. Backward conditioning, UCS first, is weakest.","Fixed interval gives a scalloped response curve. Fixed ratio gives a post-reinforcement pause. Continuous reinforcement gives fastest acquisition and fastest extinction.","Extinction is presenting the CS without the UCS. Spontaneous recovery is its return after a rest.","Premack principle: a high-frequency behaviour can reinforce a low-frequency one.","Punishment suppresses rather than unlearns behaviour, works only when immediate and consistent, and models aggression. It is the weakest tool in the set.","Watson and Rayner, 1920, Little Albert. Fear conditioned to a white rat generalised to a rabbit and a fur coat. He was never deconditioned."],"refs":["gross","atkinson"]};
TEACH["1.1.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Your eyes send up a mess and your brain sends down a guess. Look at a coin from the side: on your retina it is a squashed oval, but you see a round coin, because your brain has decided coins are round and is not going to be argued with. Perception is not a camera. It is a very fast, very confident bet about what is out there, and almost all of it is right, which is why the times it is wrong are so interesting.","teach":["Figure-ground: the brain splits every scene into an object and a background. It cannot hold both as figure at once, which is what the vase-and-faces picture shows.","Object constancy: size, shape, colour and brightness are held stable even though the retinal image changes constantly.","Set: expectation changes what you see. The same shape reads as B or 13 depending on what is beside it.","Perception is active. Bottom-up is driven by the data. Top-down is driven by expectation. Both run at once.","The clinical payoff. An illusion is a wrong reading of something real. A hallucination is a reading of nothing at all. Turn the sensory input down, as in deafness or blindness, and the top-down guess takes over. That is Charles Bonnet syndrome, and it is why very-late-onset psychosis is associated with deafness.","Development: Hubel and Wiesel showed a critical period. Deprive one eye of input early and the cortical territory is permanently lost. Nature builds the machinery, environment tunes it."],"cards":[["Name four Gestalt principles of perceptual organisation.","Proximity, similarity, closure, continuity. Also common fate and figure-ground."],["Distinguish an illusion from a hallucination.","An illusion is a misperception of a real stimulus. A hallucination is a percept without any stimulus, in external space, with the full force of a real perception, and not under voluntary control."],["What is Charles Bonnet syndrome?","Complex visual hallucinations in a person with visual impairment, with insight preserved and no psychiatric illness."],["What did Hubel and Wiesel's monocular deprivation work demonstrate?","A critical period in visual cortical development. Input is required at the right time or the capacity is permanently lost."]],"note":["Perceptual constancies: size, shape, colour, brightness, location.","Visual illusions used in teaching: Muller-Lyer, Ponzo, Necker cube, Kanizsa triangle.","Auditory: the cocktail party effect shows selective attention with breakthrough for salient items such as your own name.","Functional hallucination: hallucination and a real stimulus in the same modality together. Reflex hallucination: stimulus in one modality triggers hallucination in another. Extracampine: outside the sensory field.","Pseudohallucination is experienced in inner subjective space and recognised as not real."],"refs":["gross","atkinson","oyebode"]};
TEACH["1.1.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Imagine standing in a train station where every announcement, every conversation and every advert is shouting at once. You cannot listen to all of it, so you have a doorman who lets one thing through at a time. Attention is that doorman. In schizophrenia the doorman seems to let too much through, so ordinary things arrive with the volume up and the meaning turned strange.","teach":["Broadbent: an early filter, selecting by physical characteristics before meaning is processed.","Treisman: the filter attenuates rather than blocks. Unattended channels are turned down, not off, which is why your own name gets through."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","atkinson","noptp"],"n":{"teach":6,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["1.1.4"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Memory is not one box, it is three. There is a box that holds what you just saw for about half a second. There is a small workbench that holds a phone number while you dial it and drops it the moment you are distracted. And there is a warehouse that keeps your first day at school. A man called HM had the door to the warehouse taken out surgically. He could learn to draw in a mirror, getting better every day, while swearing he had never done it before. His hands remembered. He did not.","teach":["Sensory memory: iconic about half a second, echoic about two to four seconds.","Short-term memory: seven items plus or minus two, Miller, for fifteen to thirty seconds without rehearsal. Chunking is how you beat the limit.","Working memory, Baddeley and Hitch: central executive, phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, and the episodic buffer added later.","Long-term memory splits into declarative and procedural. Declarative splits again into episodic, which is events and is hippocampal, and semantic, which is facts and lives in temporal neocortex.","Forgetting is not one process: decay, interference (proactive and retroactive), retrieval failure, and motivated forgetting.","Encoding specificity, Tulving: you remember best in the state and place you learned. This is why revising in the pub is a bad plan.","Memory is reconstructive, not a recording. Schemata fill the gaps, which is how Loftus produced confident false memories with nothing but a leading question."],"cards":[["Name the components of Baddeley and Hitch's working memory model.","Central executive, phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, and the episodic buffer added in 2000."],["Which memory system is spared in the classic amnesic syndrome?","Procedural or implicit memory, and short-term or digit span. Episodic memory is lost."],["What are primacy and recency effects, and how do they dissociate?","In free recall, first and last items are best remembered. A filled delay abolishes recency but not primacy, which is evidence for separate short-term and long-term stores."],["Which structures are damaged in Korsakoff syndrome?","Mammillary bodies and the dorsomedial nucleus of the thalamus, with anterograde amnesia and confabulation."]],"note":["HM, bilateral medial temporal lobectomy 1953: dense anterograde amnesia with intact procedural learning and intact digit span.","Levels of processing, Craik and Lockhart: deeper semantic processing gives better retention than shallow structural processing.","State-dependent and mood-congruent recall. Depressed patients preferentially retrieve negative memories, which is a maintaining factor.","Overgeneral autobiographical memory is characteristic of depression and PTSD.","Confabulation is not lying. It requires no intent to deceive and is associated with frontal and basal forebrain damage."],"refs":["gross","atkinson","lishman"]};
TEACH["1.1.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Your brain is lazy on purpose. Working everything out properly would take all day, so instead it uses shortcuts that are right often enough to be worth the speed. Ask people whether more words start with K or have K third, and most say the first, because starting-with-K words are easier to think of. They are wrong. The shortcut is not random, it fails in a predictable direction, and that is exactly why examiners like it.","teach":["Concepts are stored as prototypes, a best example, rather than as strict definitions. A robin is a better bird than a penguin.","Deductive reasoning goes from general rule to specific case and can be logically certain. Inductive goes from cases to a rule and never can."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","atkinson"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["1.1.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Personality is what you do when nothing much is happening. There are two ways to study it. You can measure the same few dials in everybody and compare, which tells you where a person sits relative to others. Or you can sit with one person and ask what the world looks like from inside their head, which tells you something no comparison can. The first is nomothetic. The second is idiographic. Both are needed and neither is enough.","teach":["Nomothetic: general laws, dimensions, comparison across people. Traits and factor analysis.","Idiographic: the individual case in its own terms. Personal construct theory and the repertory grid."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","atkinson","noptp"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["1.1.7"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Why does anybody get out of bed? One answer is that something is missing and you move to fix it: you are thirsty, so you drink. That is a drive being reduced. But it does not explain why a well-fed child takes a clock apart, or why people climb mountains that offer nothing at the top. Some of what we do is done because doing it is the point.","teach":["Drive reduction, Hull: a need creates a drive, behaviour reduces the drive, the reduction reinforces the behaviour. Homeostasis is the model.","Primary drives are biological. Secondary drives are learned by association with primary ones. Money is the classic secondary reinforcer."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","atkinson"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["1.1.8"] = {"free":false,"simple":"You see a bear. Your heart pounds and you run. Here is the argument that has run for a century: does your heart pound because you are afraid, or are you afraid because your heart is pounding? James said the body goes first and the feeling is your reading of the body. Cannon said that cannot be right, because your heart does the same thing in fear, rage and love, and you can tell those apart perfectly well.","teach":["James-Lange: stimulus, then bodily response, then emotion as the perception of that response. You are afraid because you run.","Cannon-Bard: the thalamus sends signals to cortex and body simultaneously, so emotion and arousal happen together, not in sequence."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","atkinson","oyebode"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["1.1.9"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Stress is not what happens to you. It is the gap between what is being demanded and what you think you can manage, and that is why two people in the same job can have completely different weeks. The same ward round is a threat to one doctor and a challenge to another. Change what somebody believes they can handle, or give them control over something, and you have changed the stress without touching the workload.","teach":["Selye's general adaptation syndrome: alarm, resistance, exhaustion. A physiological model.","Lazarus's transactional model: stress is the relationship between demand and appraised coping resources. Primary appraisal is is this a threat, secondary is can I cope.","Holmes and Rahe Social Readjustment Rating Scale, 1967, scored in life change units. Criticised for ignoring desirability, context and personal meaning.","Brown and Harris, Camberwell 1978: the contextual threat method. Severe life events with long-term threat precede depression. Vulnerability factors: loss of mother before 11, three or more children under 14 at home, no confiding relationship, no employment outside the home.","Daily hassles predict distress at least as well as major life events.","Locus of control, Rotter: internal versus external. Learned helplessness, Seligman, is what happens when the link between action and outcome is broken.","Type A behaviour, Friedman and Rosenman: hostility is the component that actually predicts cardiac risk; time urgency and competitiveness do not.","Resilience is not the absence of adversity. Rutter's steeling effect: manageable stress, successfully overcome, increases later resistance."],"cards":[["Name Brown and Harris's four vulnerability factors for depression.","Loss of mother before age 11; three or more children under 14 at home; lack of a confiding relationship; lack of employment outside the home."],["What is the key methodological advance of the contextual threat method?","It rates the threat a life event would carry for anyone in that person's circumstances, judged by independent raters, removing the bias of the depressed person's own account."],["Which component of Type A behaviour predicts coronary risk?","Hostility. Time urgency and achievement striving do not independently predict it."]],"note":["Life events cluster in the months before onset of depression, and to a lesser degree before psychosis and mania.","Loss events precede depression; danger events precede anxiety. Humiliation and entrapment are the most depressogenic.","The HPA axis is the physiological substrate: CRH, ACTH, cortisol, with negative feedback. See syllabus item 3.6.2.","Kindling: successive episodes require progressively smaller triggers, described in both depression and bipolar disorder.","Coping is classified as problem-focused, emotion-focused and avoidant. Avoidant coping predicts worse outcomes."],"refs":["gross","morgan","noptp"]};
TEACH["1.1.10"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Sleep is not the brain switched off. It is the brain running a different programme, in a fixed order, about ninety minutes at a time. The deep repairing part is loaded into the first half of the night and the dreaming into the second. That is why the person who sleepwalks does it before midnight and the person with nightmares wakes at five.","teach":["Stages: N1, N2 with K complexes and sleep spindles, N3 slow wave, and REM with low-voltage mixed-frequency EEG, sawtooth waves and muscle atonia.","A cycle is about 90 minutes. REM is 20 to 25 per cent of adult sleep and the REM periods lengthen across the night."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","agrawal","noptp"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["1.2.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"An attitude has three parts: what you think, what you feel, and what you do. They often disagree. Almost everybody thinks exercise is good and feels positive about it, and most people do not do it. That gap is the whole of health promotion.","teach":["Three components: cognitive (belief), affective (feeling), behavioural (action tendency).","Measurement. Thurstone: judges scale statements, respondents endorse. Likert: agreement on a five or seven point scale, summed. Osgood's semantic differential: bipolar adjective pairs."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","atkinson","morgan"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["1.2.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There is the person you are, the person you think you are, and the person you wish you were. Rogers argued that unhappiness comes from the distance between the second and the third, and that therapy works by closing it, mostly by making the person feel accepted enough to look.","teach":["Self-concept is the descriptive picture. Self-esteem is the evaluation of it. Self-image is how you believe you appear.","Rogers: the gap between the actual self and the ideal self drives distress. Unconditional positive regard permits it to narrow."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","atkinson"],"n":{"teach":6,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["1.2.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"When somebody cuts you up in traffic they are a selfish idiot. When you do it, you were late for something important. Same act, two completely different explanations, and the difference is only whose behaviour is being explained. This is the single most reliable finding in social psychology and it happens in ward rounds every day.","teach":["Attribution is the process of explaining behaviour, internally by disposition or externally by situation.","Kelley's covariation model: we use consensus, distinctiveness and consistency. Low consensus, low distinctiveness and high consistency point to an internal cause.","Fundamental attribution error: overweighting disposition and underweighting situation when explaining other people. Weaker in collectivist cultures.","Actor-observer bias: our own behaviour is explained situationally, other people's dispositionally.","Person perception: primacy effects, the halo effect, and implicit personality theory. The first impression sets the frame the rest is read through.","Affiliation and friendship: proximity, similarity, reciprocity, and mere exposure. Physical attractiveness has a large and uncomfortable effect on initial judgements.","Theory of mind: understanding that others hold beliefs that may be false. Passed on the Sally-Anne task around four. Impaired in autism, and the mentalising deficit model is central to mentalisation-based treatment in borderline personality disorder."],"cards":[["What is the fundamental attribution error?","Overweighting dispositional and underweighting situational causes when explaining another person's behaviour."],["Name Kelley's three covariation dimensions.","Consensus, distinctiveness and consistency."],["When is theory of mind typically acquired and how is it tested?","Around four years, by a false belief task such as Sally-Anne or Smarties."]],"note":["Mere exposure effect, Zajonc: repeated exposure to a neutral stimulus increases liking.","The halo effect: one positive attribute biases judgement of unrelated attributes.","Attributional style is a vulnerability factor in depression and a target in CBT.","Mentalisation-based treatment, Bateman and Fonagy, grew directly from theory of mind research and has trial evidence in borderline personality disorder.","Paranoia is associated with an externalising personal attribution bias: negative events are blamed on other people rather than on circumstances."],"refs":["gross","atkinson","noptp"]};
TEACH["1.2.4"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Put one honest person in a room with seven people who all say a short line is long, and about a third of the time the honest person agrees with them. Then put an ordinary person in front of a dial and tell them to shock a stranger, and about two thirds go all the way to the end. Neither study is about bad people. Both are about how much of what we call character is actually the room you are standing in.","teach":["Asch, conformity. Around 75 per cent conformed at least once; conformity occurred on about a third of critical trials. One dissenting ally reduced it dramatically.","Normative influence is to be accepted. Informational influence is to be right.","Milgram, obedience. 65 per cent, 26 of 40, went to 450 volts in the baseline condition. Obedience fell with proximity to the victim, distance from the authority, and loss of institutional prestige.","French and Raven's bases of social power: reward, coercive, legitimate, referent, expert. Informational was added later.","Group polarisation: discussion pushes a group towards a more extreme version of its initial leaning.","Groupthink, Janis: cohesion plus directive leadership plus insulation from outside view produces bad decisions and suppressed dissent.","Deindividuation: anonymity and diffused responsibility in crowds reduce self-monitoring.","The clinical translation. Every serious incident inquiry that finds a team where nobody spoke up is describing these findings. Speaking up is a skill that has to be trained because the default is not to."],"cards":[["What proportion of Milgram's participants delivered the maximum shock?","65 per cent, 26 of 40, in the baseline voice-feedback condition."],["What most powerfully reduces conformity in the Asch paradigm?","A single dissenting ally. Unanimity of the majority is the critical variable."],["Name French and Raven's five bases of social power.","Reward, coercive, legitimate, referent, expert."]],"note":["Milgram conducted the studies at Yale, August 1961 to May 1962, explicitly framed around the Eichmann trial.","Diana Baumrind's 1964 critique in American Psychologist is the canonical ethical objection.","Zimbardo's Stanford prison study, 1971, is heavily criticised: archival work by Le Texier in 2019 showed the guards were coached.","Bystander effect, Latane and Darley: helping falls as the number of bystanders rises, through diffusion of responsibility and pluralistic ignorance.","Minority influence requires consistency, confidence and flexibility, and works through conversion rather than compliance."],"refs":["gross","atkinson","morgan"]};
TEACH["1.2.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Split a group of boys at summer camp into two teams and within days they are stealing each other's flags. Nothing was different about the boys. The only thing that changed was the line drawn between them. Then make them push a broken-down bus together and the hostility falls apart, because now there is only one group.","teach":["Sherif's Robbers Cave: competition for scarce resources produced intergroup hostility, and superordinate goals requiring cooperation reduced it.","Realistic conflict theory: hostility comes from competition over real resources."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","morgan","bhugra"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["1.2.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Bandura's children watched an adult punch an inflatable clown, and then they punched it too. Nobody rewarded them and nobody told them to. They had simply seen it done, and seeing it done was enough to make it a thing you could do.","teach":["Social learning theory, Bandura: aggression is acquired by observation and imitation, without direct reinforcement. Bobo doll studies, 1961 and 1963.","Same-sex models are imitated more readily; seeing the model rewarded increases imitation, which is vicarious reinforcement."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","atkinson","rutter"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["1.2.7"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Thirty-eight people were said to have watched a woman be attacked in New York and none of them called the police. The story turned out to be largely wrong, but the question it provoked was right: why does having more people around make help less likely, not more? Because if everybody could act, nobody in particular has to.","teach":["Bystander effect, Latane and Darley: the more bystanders, the lower the probability that any one of them helps.","Two mechanisms: diffusion of responsibility, and pluralistic ignorance, in which each person reads everyone else's inaction as evidence that nothing is wrong."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","atkinson"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["1.3.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Where you are born changes how likely you are to become mentally unwell, how quickly anyone notices, and what happens to you afterwards. That is not a small effect at the edges. In severe mental illness it is one of the largest effects there is.","teach":["Social class in the UK is now measured by the National Statistics Socio-economic Classification, based on employment relations, replacing the older Registrar General's scale.","The inverse care law, Tudor Hart: availability of good medical care varies inversely with need in the population served."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["morgan","bhugra","noptp"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["1.3.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Being ill and being a patient are two different things, and the second one needs permission. Parsons said society offers a deal: you are let off work and nobody blames you, and in exchange you have to want to get better and you have to go and see somebody qualified. Psychiatric illness fits that deal badly, which is a large part of why it gets treated differently.","teach":["Parsons' sick role has four elements: exemption from normal social roles; not held responsible for the condition; an obligation to want to get well; an obligation to seek competent help and cooperate.","Why psychiatry fits badly. Responsibility is contested, wanting to get well may be absent when insight is impaired, and cooperation may be compelled rather than given.","Illness behaviour, Mechanic: how a person perceives, evaluates and acts on symptoms. It varies with culture, personality, and social context far more than with disease severity.","Abnormal illness behaviour, Pilowsky: illness behaviour disproportionate to the demonstrable pathology, in either direction. It includes both denial and amplification.","The doctor's role: Parsons described it as universalistic, functionally specific, affectively neutral and collectivity-oriented.","Freidson's critique: professional dominance. The profession, not the patient, defines what counts as illness.","Consultation styles run from paternalistic through informed to shared decision making. Shared decision making is the current standard and is explicit in NICE guidance."],"cards":[["Name the four elements of Parsons' sick role.","Exemption from normal roles; not held responsible; obligation to want to get well; obligation to seek and cooperate with competent help."],["Distinguish illness behaviour from abnormal illness behaviour, naming the authors.","Illness behaviour (Mechanic): how symptoms are perceived, evaluated and acted upon. Abnormal illness behaviour (Pilowsky): a response disproportionate to the demonstrable pathology."],["Why does psychiatric illness fit the sick role poorly?","Responsibility is often attributed to the patient, insight may be absent so the person may not wish to recover, and treatment may be compulsory rather than sought."]],"note":["Disease is the pathology, illness is the experience, sickness is the social role. Learn the three-word distinction.","Zola's five triggers to consultation: interpersonal crisis, perceived interference with social relations, sanctioning by others, perceived interference with work, and setting an external time limit.","The Health Belief Model and the theory of planned behaviour are the two predictive models of help-seeking.","Around a third of GP consultations involve a significant psychological component.","Illich's concept of iatrogenesis and the medicalisation critique belong here."],"refs":["morgan","noptp","kaplanc"]};
TEACH["1.3.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Two people leave hospital equally well, on the same medication, and one relapses within nine months and the other does not. The difference is often not in the patient at all. It is in the tone of voice used at home. Not whether the family cares, but how much criticism and how much anxious over-involvement the person is living inside.","teach":["Expressed emotion is measured by the Camberwell Family Interview and has three key components: critical comments, hostility, and emotional over-involvement.","Warmth and positive remarks are also rated but do not define high EE.","High EE predicts relapse in schizophrenia, and the finding replicates in depression, eating disorders and bipolar disorder.","Face-to-face contact matters: relapse risk is reduced when contact is under about 35 hours per week in a high-EE household.","It is not blame. High EE is frequently a response to living with severe illness, not a cause that precedes it, and families need support rather than accusation.","Family intervention, delivered over at least ten sessions, reduces relapse and readmission. It is recommended by NICE for schizophrenia where the person is in close contact with family.","Cultural caveat: EE thresholds derived in the UK do not transfer directly, and rates of high EE differ substantially between cultures."],"cards":[["Name the components of expressed emotion and the instrument used to measure it.","Critical comments, hostility and emotional over-involvement, measured by the Camberwell Family Interview. Warmth and positive remarks are also rated."],["What level of face-to-face contact reduces relapse risk in a high-EE household?","Fewer than about 35 hours per week."],["What is the evidence for family intervention in schizophrenia?","It reduces relapse and readmission and improves adherence. NICE recommends it where the person is in close contact with family, delivered over at least ten planned sessions."]],"note":["Brown, Birley and Wing established the association; Vaughn and Leff, 1976, produced the replication and the contact threshold.","Relapse rates in high-EE households are roughly double those in low-EE households at nine months.","Expressed emotion is a state, not a trait, and can be modified by intervention.","Double bind theory (Bateson) and schizophrenogenic mother theory are historically important, discredited, and harmful. Do not confuse them with EE, which is an empirical measure.","High EE also predicts outcome in anorexia nervosa, where emotional over-involvement predominates over criticism."],"refs":["morgan","semgen","nice"]};
TEACH["1.3.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A bereavement, a redundancy and a house move all count as life events, but they do not weigh the same, and the same event does not weigh the same for two different people. Losing a job matters more if it was the only thing keeping you afloat. That is why counting events on a checklist misses the point, and why the researchers who got this right stopped counting and started asking what the event actually meant.","teach":["Depression: severe life events with long-term threat cluster in the months before onset. Loss, humiliation and entrapment are the most depressogenic categories.","The contextual threat method rates what an event would mean for anyone in those circumstances, judged independently, which removes the bias of a depressed person's own recall."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["morgan","noptp","semgen"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["1.3.5"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Goffman went to work in a mental hospital as an assistant athletics director and watched what the building did to the people in it. He found that the wards were producing behaviour of their own: flatness, dependence, loss of ordinary skills. Not symptoms of the illness. Symptoms of the institution.","teach":["Goffman's total institution: all aspects of life conducted in the same place under one authority, in the company of a large batch of others treated alike, on a tight schedule imposed from above, serving an official aim.","Mortification of the self: admission procedures strip identity, possessions, name, clothing and privacy.","Binary management and the privilege system. Secondary adjustments are the small ways inmates work the system to recover a sense of self.","Barton's institutional neurosis, 1959: apathy, loss of initiative, submissiveness, loss of interest in the outside world, deterioration in habits. Caused by the environment, and reversible.","Wing and Brown, Three Hospitals Study, 1970: clinical poverty syndrome correlated with the poverty of the social environment, and improved when the environment improved. This is the key empirical demonstration.","Deinstitutionalisation: chlorpromazine from 1952, the Mental Health Act 1959, Powell's 1961 Hospital Plan, Goffman's Asylums in 1961, and the economics.","The new institutions. Transinstitutionalisation into prisons, private hospitals and care homes is the recognised risk, and Winterbourne View and Whorlton Hall are what it looks like when it happens."],"cards":[["Define a total institution and name its author.","Goffman, 1961. A place of residence and work where a large number of similarly situated people, cut off from wider society, lead an enclosed formally administered life under a single authority."],["Name the features of institutional neurosis and its author.","Barton, 1959: apathy, loss of initiative, submissiveness, loss of interest in the outside world, deterioration of personal habits, and a characteristic posture and gait."],["What did Wing and Brown's Three Hospitals Study show?","Negative symptoms and clinical poverty correlated with the poverty of the social environment across three hospitals, and improved when the social environment was enriched."]],"note":["Goffman, Asylums, 1961. Barton, Institutional Neurosis, 1959. Wing and Brown, Institutionalism and Schizophrenia, 1970.","Enoch Powell's water tower speech, 1961, announced the intended closure of the asylums.","Ely Hospital inquiry, 1969, chaired by Geoffrey Howe, led to the Hospital Advisory Service.","Penrose's law proposes an inverse relationship between psychiatric beds and prison populations. It is much cited and contested.","The recovery model and the balanced care model are the contemporary answers to what replaced the asylum."],"refs":["morgan","noptp","semgen"]};
TEACH["1.3.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Two questions that look like one. Why do people commit crimes, and what should be done to them afterwards? Criminology answers the first and penology the second, and psychiatry gets pulled into both because the court wants to know whether this particular person could have chosen otherwise.","teach":["Anomie and strain, Merton: deviance arises from the gap between culturally endorsed goals and the legitimate means available. Adaptations: conformity, innovation, ritualism, retreatism, rebellion.","Differential association, Sutherland: criminal behaviour is learned in intimate personal groups, including techniques and the motives that justify them."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["morgan","puriforen","noptp"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["1.3.7"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Stigma is three things wearing one coat. What other people believe about you. What you come to believe about yourself. And the door that quietly does not open, whatever anybody believes. Fix the first and the third can stay exactly where it was.","teach":["Goffman, 1963: stigma as a spoiled identity. Discredited attributes are visible; discreditable attributes are concealable, which creates the work of passing.","Three levels. Public stigma is the attitudes of others. Self-stigma is internalised, producing shame, secrecy and the why-try effect. Structural stigma is embedded in policy and institutions and operates regardless of anyone's attitude.","Thornicroft's three components: ignorance (a knowledge problem), prejudice (an attitude problem), and discrimination (a behaviour problem). Interventions must be matched to the right component.","Courtesy stigma extends to family, carers and staff by association.","Diagnostic overshadowing: physical symptoms attributed to the psychiatric diagnosis. It is a direct contributor to the excess physical morbidity and mortality in severe mental illness.","What works: direct social contact with a person with lived experience is more effective than education alone, and far more effective than protest.","The biogenetic paradox. Explaining mental illness as a brain disease reduces blame but tends to increase perceived dangerousness and pessimism about recovery. A more effective message is one of recovery and ordinariness."],"cards":[["Name Thornicroft's three components of stigma and the intervention for each.","Ignorance, addressed by knowledge; prejudice, addressed by attitude change, best through contact; discrimination, addressed by behaviour and legislation."],["Distinguish public, self and structural stigma.","Public: the attitudes of others. Self: internalised stigma producing shame and withdrawal. Structural: institutional policies and practices that disadvantage, independent of individual attitudes."],["What is diagnostic overshadowing?","Attributing physical symptoms to a person's psychiatric diagnosis or intellectual disability rather than investigating them, resulting in missed physical illness."]],"note":["Goffman, Stigma: Notes on the Management of Spoiled Identity, 1963.","Contact is the single most effective anti-stigma intervention and the evidence base behind Time to Change in England.","Self-stigma predicts reduced help-seeking, poorer adherence, lower self-esteem and poorer functional outcome.","Schizophrenia carries the greatest stigma of any psychiatric diagnosis in most surveyed populations; depression and anxiety carry substantially less.","Mental health professionals are not exempt: measured stigmatising attitudes among clinicians are comparable to those of the general public."],"refs":["morgan","bhugra","noptp"]};
TEACH["1.3.8"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Distress has a grammar, and the grammar is local. In one country a person says their heart is heavy, in another they say their chest is burning, in another they say nothing at all and simply stop eating. The illness underneath may be identical. The task is to hear the local language and still find the illness, without deciding the person is wrong for not using yours.","teach":["Emic is the view from within a culture. Etic is comparison across cultures. Most cross-cultural error is applying an etic instrument as though it were emic.","Idioms of distress: culturally shared ways of expressing suffering. Somatic presentation where emotional language is not customary is the commonest example.","Acculturation, Berry: four strategies from crossing maintenance of heritage culture with participation in the host culture. Integration, assimilation, separation, marginalisation. Integration gives the best adaptation outcome and marginalisation the worst.","Migration and psychosis: incidence is raised in migrant and minority ethnic groups, particularly in the second generation, and the effect is largest where the person forms a smaller proportion of their local area. That is the ethnic density effect, and it points to social rather than genetic explanation.","In the UK, black Caribbean and black African groups have markedly raised rates of detention under the Mental Health Act, of police involvement in the pathway to care, and of admission to secure services.","Culture-bound syndromes: koro, dhat, ataque de nervios, amok, latah, taijin kyofusho, brain fag. ICD-11 and DSM-5 have moved towards cultural concepts of distress rather than fixed syndromes.","The Cultural Formulation Interview covers cultural identity, cultural explanation of the illness, cultural factors in psychosocial environment and functioning, cultural elements of the clinician-patient relationship, and overall cultural assessment."],"cards":[["Name Berry's four acculturation strategies and the best outcome.","Integration, assimilation, separation, marginalisation. Integration, maintaining heritage culture while participating in the host culture, has the best adaptation outcome."],["What is the ethnic density effect?","The incidence of psychosis in a minority group rises as that group forms a smaller proportion of the local population, independent of overall area deprivation."],["Name the domains of the Cultural Formulation Interview.","Cultural identity; cultural conceptualisation of distress; psychosocial stressors and supports including cultural features of vulnerability and resilience; cultural features of the relationship between individual and clinician; overall cultural assessment."]],"note":["The AESOP study is the key UK source on raised psychosis incidence in black Caribbean and black African populations.","Explanations favoured by the evidence are social: discrimination, social defeat, disadvantage and urbanicity. Higher rates are not found in the countries of origin, which argues against a genetic explanation.","David Bennett inquiry, published 2004, chaired by Sir John Blofeld, found institutional racism in NHS mental health services.","The 2018 independent review of the Mental Health Act identified racial disparity in detention as a principal concern.","Interpreters should be professional, not family members, and never children."],"refs":["bhugra","morgan","noptp"]};
TEACH["1.3.9"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Medicine's four rules sound obvious until two of them disagree, which is most of the time in psychiatry. Respect the person's choices. Do good. Do no harm. Be fair. Now apply all four at once to a man who is refusing food because he believes it is poisoned, and you will see why this subject exists.","teach":["The four principles, Beauchamp and Childress: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice. They are a common language, not an algorithm, and they give no rule for what to do when they conflict.","Consequentialism judges the act by its outcomes. Deontology judges it by duties and rules. Virtue ethics asks what a good doctor would do. Each gives a different answer to compulsory treatment."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["noptp","oxhandbk","nice"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"When a parent is mentally unwell, the child is affected, and not mainly through genes. It is through what happens between them: whether the parent notices the child, responds to them, and is predictable from one day to the next.","teach":["Around a quarter to a third of adult mental health service users are parents of dependent children, and this is very often not recorded.","Children of parents with mental illness have a substantially increased risk of psychiatric disorder themselves, of the order of two to three times, through genetic, prenatal, parenting and social pathways.","The pathway matters clinically because parenting and social pathways are modifiable while genes are not.","Maternal depression is the best-studied exemplar. It affects parenting through reduced responsiveness, flattened affect, reduced verbal interaction, inconsistency, and either withdrawal or intrusiveness.","The still-face paradigm demonstrates the mechanism directly: an infant whose mother becomes unresponsive for two minutes shows distress, protest, then withdrawal. Chronic unresponsiveness produces the same pattern chronically.","Effects vary by developmental stage. In infancy the impact is on attachment security and emotional regulation; in the preschool years on language and behaviour; in middle childhood on academic and social functioning; in adolescence on mood and risk behaviour.","Antenatal maternal depression and anxiety affect the child independently of postnatal depression, through fetal programming mechanisms involving cortisol, which is why treating antenatal illness matters in its own right.","Postnatal depression is associated with insecure attachment, poorer cognitive and language development, and increased behavioural problems, mediated substantially through the quality of interaction rather than the diagnosis itself.","The mediating role of interaction is the crucial point: where interaction is preserved, the effect on the child is much reduced, which makes interaction the treatment target.","Treating maternal depression does not automatically improve child outcomes. Interventions must address the interaction directly, and this is a well-replicated and frequently ignored finding.","Parental psychosis affects children through disrupted care, frightening experiences, and sometimes direct inclusion in delusional systems, which is a specific safeguarding concern.","Parental personality disorder, particularly with emotional instability, is associated with inconsistent care and with role reversal in which the child becomes the carer.","Young carers are a specific and largely invisible group. Around 800,000 young carers are estimated in the UK, many caring for a parent with mental illness, with effects on education, peer relationships and their own mental health.","Protective factors: another consistent adult, a good relationship outside the household, the child's own understanding of the illness, and continuity of school.","Explaining the illness to the child is protective. Children fill silence with self-blame, and age-appropriate explanation reduces that reliably.","Cultural variation affects both aetiology and management: what is regarded as illness, who provides care, whether extended family compensates, and whether services are trusted.","In many cultures extended family absorbs the caring role, which is protective but may conceal the problem from services entirely, and its absence in migrant families removes a protection that was present in the country of origin.","Think family is the practical injunction: every adult mental health assessment should establish whether there are dependent children and what their situation is."],"cards":[["Through what pathways does parental mental illness affect children?","Genetic transmission, prenatal effects including fetal programming through cortisol, disrupted parenting and interaction, and associated social adversity. Parenting and social pathways are modifiable, which is where clinical effort belongs."],["What does the still-face paradigm demonstrate?","That an infant whose caregiver becomes unresponsive for two minutes shows escalating distress, protest and then withdrawal. It demonstrates directly the mechanism by which chronic parental unresponsiveness affects infant emotional regulation."],["Why does treating maternal depression not automatically improve child outcomes?","Because the effect on the child is mediated substantially by the quality of interaction rather than by the diagnosis itself. Interventions must target the parent-infant interaction directly, a well-replicated finding that is frequently ignored in practice."],["What protects children of parents with mental illness?","The presence of another consistent adult, a good relationship outside the household, the child's own age-appropriate understanding of the illness, and continuity of schooling. Explaining the illness is specifically protective because children otherwise fill the silence with self-blame."]],"note":["Around a quarter to a third of adult mental health service users are parents of dependent children.","Children of parents with mental illness have two to three times the risk of psychiatric disorder.","Antenatal depression and anxiety affect the child independently of postnatal illness.","Around 800,000 young carers are estimated in the UK.","Extended family caring is protective but may conceal the problem from services entirely."],"refs":["rutter","nice","bhugra"]};
TEACH["10.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Bad things happening to a child do not damage them in a simple dose-response way. What matters is how bad, how long, how early, whether it happened within a relationship, and whether anyone was there afterwards.","teach":["The Adverse Childhood Experiences studies, Felitti and Anda from 1998, demonstrated a strong graded dose-response relationship between the number of adverse experiences and adult physical and mental health outcomes.","The original ten ACEs: physical, sexual and emotional abuse; physical and emotional neglect; parental separation; domestic violence; household mental illness; household substance misuse; and household member imprisonment.","Four or more ACEs is the conventional threshold, associated with substantially increased risk of depression, suicide attempt, substance misuse, ischaemic heart disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, cancer and early death.","The ACE framework has been criticised: it is retrospective, the categories are crude, it takes no account of severity, timing or protective factors, and it should not be used to screen or predict at individual level.","That criticism matters practically. ACE scores describe populations well and individuals badly, and using them as a risk score is a misuse.","Maternal deprivation: Bowlby's original claim that separation from the mother caused delinquency was overstated. Rutter's Maternal Deprivation Reassessed distinguished privation, the failure ever to form an attachment, from deprivation, the loss of one already formed.","Rutter showed that the damaging factor was disruption and discord rather than separation itself, and that many effects were reversible with subsequent good care.","The English and Romanian Adoptees study followed children adopted from Romanian institutions and is the key evidence: duration of deprivation before adoption predicted outcome, with a threshold around six months.","Children adopted before six months largely caught up. Those deprived beyond six months showed persisting deficits into adulthood, including quasi-autistic features, disinhibited social engagement, inattention and cognitive impairment.","That finding supports a sensitive period rather than a critical period: recovery is possible but becomes progressively less complete with longer deprivation.","Child abuse effects: sexual abuse is associated with depression, PTSD, self-harm, substance misuse, eating disorders, personality disorder, revictimisation and psychosis.","The association between childhood adversity and psychosis is now robust and dose-dependent, and has changed how psychosis aetiology is taught.","Emotional abuse and neglect are as damaging as physical abuse in most outcome studies, and are far less often identified or acted upon.","Neglect is the commonest form of maltreatment and the one with the strongest effects on brain development, and it is under-represented in safeguarding activity relative to its frequency.","Chronic and life-threatening illness in childhood affects development through school absence, restricted peer contact, dependency, altered body image, and parental anxiety and overprotection.","Effects on siblings of a chronically ill child are real and rarely addressed.","Resilience is not a trait the child either has or lacks. It arises from the child, the family and the wider environment together, and the single most consistent protective factor is at least one stable, supportive relationship with a responsive adult.","Steeling effects: manageable adversity with support can increase later resilience, whereas overwhelming adversity does not. The distinction is the presence of support and the dose."],"cards":[["What did the ACE studies show, and what are their limitations?","A strong graded dose-response relationship between the number of adverse childhood experiences and adult physical and mental health outcomes, with four or more the conventional threshold. Limitations: retrospective recall, crude categories, no account of severity, timing or protective factors, and poor individual-level prediction, so ACE scores should not be used to screen or predict for individuals."],["Distinguish privation from deprivation.","Rutter's distinction: privation is the failure ever to form an attachment; deprivation is the loss or disruption of an attachment already formed. Rutter showed that the damaging factor was disruption and discord rather than separation itself, and that many effects were reversible with subsequent good care."],["What did the English and Romanian Adoptees study demonstrate?","That the duration of institutional deprivation before adoption predicted outcome, with a threshold around six months. Children adopted before six months largely caught up, whereas those deprived beyond six months showed persisting deficits into adulthood including quasi-autistic features, disinhibited social engagement, inattention and cognitive impairment. This supports a sensitive rather than a critical period."],["Which form of maltreatment is commonest and most damaging to brain development?","Neglect, which is the commonest form of maltreatment, has the strongest effects on brain development, and is under-represented in safeguarding activity relative to its frequency. Emotional abuse and neglect are as damaging as physical abuse in most outcome studies."],["What is the most consistent protective factor in childhood adversity?","At least one stable, supportive relationship with a responsive adult. Resilience arises from the child, family and wider environment together rather than being a fixed trait the child either possesses or lacks."]],"note":["The original ten ACEs cover abuse, neglect and five categories of household dysfunction.","The association between childhood adversity and psychosis is robust and dose-dependent.","Chronic childhood illness affects development through absence, dependency, body image and parental overprotection.","Effects on siblings of a chronically ill child are real and rarely addressed.","Steeling effects require manageable adversity with support; overwhelming adversity does not produce them."],"refs":["rutter","nice","companion"]};
TEACH["10.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Child mental health services are organised in tiers, from a teacher noticing something to an inpatient unit, and most of the country's problems are in the gaps between tiers rather than within them.","teach":["The historical four-tier CAMHS model: Tier 1, universal services including schools, general practice and health visitors; Tier 2, targeted individual specialists; Tier 3, specialist multidisciplinary community teams; Tier 4, highly specialist inpatient and day services.","The tier model has been formally superseded by the THRIVE framework, which groups children by need rather than by service level: Thriving, Getting Advice, Getting Help, Getting More Help, and Getting Risk Support."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","rutter","companion"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.4"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Every doctor who sees children has a duty here, and it is not to investigate. It is to notice, to record exactly what you saw and heard, and to tell the right people the same day.","teach":["Four categories of abuse in UK statutory guidance: physical abuse, emotional abuse, sexual abuse and neglect. Working Together to Safeguard Children is the statutory guidance in England.","Neglect is the commonest category, accounting for the largest share of child protection plans, and includes failure to provide food, clothing, shelter, supervision, medical care and emotional responsiveness.","Emotional abuse is the hardest to evidence and the most frequently present alongside the others. It rarely occurs alone but rarely occurs in isolation from the rest either.","Fabricated or induced illness, formerly Munchausen syndrome by proxy, is a form of abuse in which a carer fabricates or induces illness in a child. It is managed as child abuse through safeguarding, not as a parental diagnosis.","Child sexual exploitation and county lines criminal exploitation are recognised forms of abuse, and the young person frequently does not perceive themselves as a victim, which is part of the grooming.","Female genital mutilation carries a mandatory reporting duty in England and Wales: regulated professionals must report known cases in under-18s to the police.","Physical indicators: injuries inconsistent with the explanation or with the child's developmental stage; injuries in unusual sites such as the ears, neck, buttocks and inner thighs; bruising in a non-mobile infant; and delayed presentation.","Bruising in a child who is not independently mobile is the single most important physical sign and warrants immediate child protection referral.","Torn frenulum, retinal haemorrhage, metaphyseal fractures, posterior rib fractures and multiple fractures of different ages are strongly associated with inflicted injury.","Behavioural indicators: developmental delay, failure to thrive, frozen watchfulness, indiscriminate friendliness, sexualised behaviour inappropriate for age, self-harm, and abrupt behavioural change.","Sexualised behaviour outside the normal developmental range is a specific indicator warranting referral rather than observation.","Risk factors: parental mental illness, substance misuse, domestic abuse, parental learning disability, social isolation, and disability in the child. The toxic trio of domestic abuse, parental substance misuse and parental mental illness is the recurring combination in serious case reviews.","Disabled children are abused at substantially higher rates than non-disabled children and are less likely to be believed and less likely to secure a prosecution.","What to do: recognise, record, refer, and do not delay. Refer to children's social care the same day where there is a concern, and to the police where a crime may have been committed.","Do not investigate. Do not repeatedly question the child, do not attempt to establish whether abuse occurred, and do not conduct an intimate examination unless competent to do so.","Record verbatim what the child said, in their words, with the question you asked, the date, the time and who was present. Use body maps for injuries.","Consent and confidentiality: safeguarding overrides confidentiality. Inform parents unless doing so would place the child at increased risk, would impede an investigation, or the concern relates to sexual abuse or fabricated illness.","The child's welfare is paramount under the Children Act 1989, section 1.","Section 47 requires the local authority to investigate where there is reasonable cause to suspect significant harm. Section 17 covers children in need who are not suffering significant harm.","Emergency protection orders, police protection powers lasting 72 hours, and care and supervision orders are the legal mechanisms available.","The recurring lesson of every serious case review is the same: information was held in separate agencies and was never assembled into one picture."],"cards":[["Name the four categories of abuse and identify the commonest.","Physical abuse, emotional abuse, sexual abuse and neglect. Neglect is the commonest and accounts for the largest share of child protection plans. Working Together to Safeguard Children is the statutory guidance in England."],["What is the single most important physical sign of possible abuse?","Bruising in a child who is not independently mobile, which warrants immediate child protection referral. Torn frenulum, retinal haemorrhage, metaphyseal fractures, posterior rib fractures and multiple fractures of different ages are also strongly associated with inflicted injury."],["What should a clinician do, and not do, when abuse is suspected?","Recognise, record and refer the same day to children's social care, and to police where a crime may have been committed. Do not investigate, do not question the child repeatedly, and do not attempt to establish whether abuse occurred. Record verbatim what the child said in their own words, with the question asked, date, time and who was present."],["What is the toxic trio?","Domestic abuse, parental substance misuse and parental mental illness, the combination that recurs most frequently in serious case reviews of child deaths and serious harm."],["When should parents not be informed of a safeguarding referral?","Where informing them would place the child at increased risk, would impede a police or social care investigation, or where the concern relates to sexual abuse or to fabricated or induced illness."]],"note":["The child's welfare is paramount under section 1 of the Children Act 1989.","Section 47 triggers investigation where there is reasonable cause to suspect significant harm; section 17 covers children in need.","Female genital mutilation carries a mandatory reporting duty for known cases in under-18s.","Disabled children are abused at substantially higher rates and are less likely to be believed.","Every serious case review finds information held separately and never assembled into one picture."],"refs":["rutter","eastman","nice"]};
TEACH["10.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Children express distress through their bodies more than adults do, because the body is the vocabulary available before the emotional one develops. A stomach ache before school is not a lie.","teach":["Somatic presentation of psychological distress is the norm in childhood, not the exception, and becomes less prominent as emotional vocabulary develops.","Recurrent abdominal pain affects a large minority of school-age children, and organic pathology is found in a small minority. It is frequently associated with anxiety in the child and in the parent."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","nice","lishman"],"n":{"teach":18,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Nothing in child psychiatry has one cause. What matters is how the child's own make-up meets what happens around them, and the same event does very different things to different children.","teach":["Aetiology in child psychiatry is multifactorial and probabilistic. Single-cause explanations are always wrong, and the useful question is how factors combine.","Individual factors: genetic vulnerability, temperament, intelligence, physical health, neurodevelopmental status, and language ability."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","gross","companion"],"n":{"teach":19,"cards":6,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.7"] = {"free":false,"simple":"For most childhood problems the treatment is not given to the child. It is given to the parents, or the school, or the family, because that is where the child lives.","teach":["The general principle in younger children is to intervene through the environment: parents, school and family. Direct individual therapy becomes progressively more appropriate with age.","Parent training programmes are the first-line intervention for conduct problems in children under about eleven. NICE recommends group-based parent training as first-line."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","rutter","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":19,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.9"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Some childhood disorders end in childhood and some become adult disorders under a different name. Knowing which is which decides whether a fifteen-year-old needs a service at eighteen.","teach":["Homotypic continuity means the same disorder persists into adulthood. Heterotypic continuity means a childhood disorder predicts a different adult disorder.","ADHD shows strong homotypic continuity. Around two-thirds have persisting symptoms into adulthood, and around 15 per cent continue to meet full criteria, with hyperactivity declining more than inattention."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","nice","companion"],"n":{"teach":18,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.10"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Admitting a child to a psychiatric unit is a serious intervention with real costs, and the bar should be high. Most of the time the answer is to change what is happening around them rather than to remove them from it.","teach":["General principle: use the least restrictive effective intervention, delivered as close to home as possible, with the child remaining in education and family life wherever it is safe.","Indications for medication generally: significant impairment, persistence despite environmental and psychological intervention, and a disorder for which medication has established evidence in this age group."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","eastman","rutter"],"n":{"teach":19,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.1.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Attachment disorder is rare, and insecure attachment is common. Confusing the two is the single most frequent error in child mental health, and it leads to children being labelled when nothing is wrong with them.","teach":["Insecure attachment is a normal variation affecting around 35 to 40 per cent of children in the general population. It is not a disorder.","Attachment disorder is rare and requires pathogenic care: persistent disregard of the child's emotional or physical needs, or repeated changes of primary caregiver preventing the formation of stable attachments.","The distinction is fundamental: insecure attachment is a pattern of relating; attachment disorder is a psychiatric disorder arising from grossly inadequate care.","Prevalence in the general population is well under 1 per cent, and probably closer to 0.1 per cent for reactive attachment disorder.","Prevalence rises sharply in maltreated and institutionalised populations: around 20 to 40 per cent in institutionally reared children, and around 10 to 20 per cent in maltreated children in care.","Looked-after children are the group in whom these diagnoses are genuinely relevant, and even there most do not have an attachment disorder.","Disinhibited social engagement disorder is commoner than reactive attachment disorder in previously institutionalised children.","Attachment disorder is substantially over-diagnosed in UK practice, particularly in looked-after and adopted children, where behavioural difficulty is attributed to attachment without meeting criteria.","Over-diagnosis has consequences: it directs attention away from treatable comorbidity such as ADHD, language disorder or PTSD, and it can be used to explain away a placement failure."],"cards":[["Distinguish insecure attachment from attachment disorder.","Insecure attachment is a normal variation in the pattern of relating, affecting around 35 to 40 per cent of children in the general population, and is not a disorder. Attachment disorder is a rare psychiatric disorder, well under 1 per cent in the general population, requiring a history of pathogenic care through persistent neglect or repeated caregiver change."],["In which populations is attachment disorder genuinely prevalent?","Institutionally reared children, at around 20 to 40 per cent, and maltreated children in care, at around 10 to 20 per cent. Even in these groups, most children do not have an attachment disorder, and the diagnosis is substantially over-applied in UK practice."],["What harm follows over-diagnosis of attachment disorder?","It directs attention away from treatable comorbid conditions such as ADHD, developmental language disorder and PTSD, and can be used to explain away a placement failure rather than examine it."]],"note":["Pathogenic care is a diagnostic requirement, not an inference from the child's behaviour.","Disinhibited social engagement disorder is commoner than reactive attachment disorder after institutional care.","Looked-after children are the population in whom these diagnoses are genuinely relevant.","Insecure attachment describes a pattern of relating, not a disorder."],"refs":["rutter","icd11","nice"]};
TEACH["10.8.1.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Attachment is a survival system, not an emotional luxury. A frightened baby who cannot get comfort from the person they need is in an impossible situation, and the disorder is what results.","teach":["Bowlby grounded attachment in ethology rather than in drive theory: the child's tie to the caregiver is a primary biological system that promotes proximity and therefore survival.","The attachment behavioural system is activated by threat, illness, fatigue and separation, and deactivated by proximity and comfort. It exists to keep the immature organism near protection."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","gross","icd11"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.1.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"One child never seeks comfort from anyone, even when hurt. The other will go off with any stranger without looking back. Both come from the same failure of early care and they look nothing alike.","teach":["Reactive attachment disorder: a consistent pattern of inhibited, emotionally withdrawn behaviour towards caregivers, with the child rarely seeking or responding to comfort when distressed.","Additional features of reactive attachment disorder: minimal social and emotional responsiveness, limited positive affect, and unexplained episodes of irritability, sadness or fearfulness during non-threatening interactions with caregivers."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["icd11","dsm5","rutter"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["10.8.1.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The treatment is a stable, sensitive, available caregiver, sustained over years. There is no therapy that substitutes for that, and there are several that actively harm.","teach":["The primary intervention is the provision of stable, sensitive, responsive caregiving. Everything else is secondary to securing and supporting that.","For a child in care this means placement stability above all. Repeated placement moves are the ongoing continuation of the aetiology."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","rutter","gabbard"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.1.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Good enough care afterwards repairs a great deal, and the earlier it arrives the more it repairs. But some things, particularly the indiscriminate friendliness, tend to stay.","teach":["Outcome depends principally on the duration and severity of the adverse care and on the quality and stability of what follows.","Reactive attachment disorder features improve substantially with adequate caregiving, and in the Romanian adoptee cohort largely resolved after placement in good adoptive homes."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","nice","companion"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["10.8.2.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Conduct disorder is the commonest reason a child is referred to mental health services, and it is far commoner in poor families, which tells you a good deal about where it comes from.","teach":["Conduct disorder is the commonest psychiatric disorder of childhood and the commonest reason for referral to child mental health services.","Prevalence is around 5 per cent of children aged 5 to 16 in UK surveys, with oppositional defiant disorder adding further cases in younger children.","Male to female ratio is around three or four to one in childhood, narrowing substantially in adolescence as female rates rise.","The narrowing sex ratio in adolescence reflects both later onset in girls and different expression, with girls showing more relational and covert antisocial behaviour.","There is a very strong socioeconomic gradient. Prevalence in the lowest income quintile is several times that in the highest, the steepest social gradient of any childhood psychiatric disorder.","Rates are raised in looked-after children, in children excluded from school, in those with intellectual disability, and in the youth justice population where prevalence is very high.","Onset is bimodal: childhood-onset before age ten, which is more often male, more neurodevelopmentally loaded and more persistent; and adolescent-onset, which is more equal by sex and more often desists.","The lifetime economic cost is very high, with the costs of childhood conduct disorder to public services by adulthood estimated at around ten times those of children without it.","Only a minority of children with conduct disorder reach specialist services, and treatment is available and effective, which makes this the largest untreated burden in child psychiatry."],"cards":[["Give the prevalence, sex ratio and social gradient of conduct disorder.","Around 5 per cent of children aged 5 to 16 in UK surveys, with a male to female ratio of around three or four to one in childhood narrowing in adolescence. It has the steepest socioeconomic gradient of any childhood psychiatric disorder, with prevalence in the lowest income quintile several times that in the highest."],["Describe the bimodal onset of conduct disorder.","Childhood-onset before age ten, more often male, more neurodevelopmentally loaded and considerably more persistent; and adolescent-onset, with a more equal sex ratio, more peer-driven and more likely to desist in early adulthood."]],"note":["Conduct disorder is the commonest childhood psychiatric disorder and the commonest reason for referral.","Girls show more relational and covert antisocial behaviour, contributing to under-recognition.","Prevalence is very high in the youth justice population and raised in looked-after and excluded children.","Lifetime public service costs are around ten times those of unaffected children.","Only a minority reach specialist services despite effective treatment being available."],"refs":["rutter","nice"]};
TEACH["10.8.2.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Conduct disorder is what happens when a difficult child meets a family and a neighbourhood that cannot manage difficulty. Neither half alone reliably produces it.","teach":["Heritability is moderate, around 40 to 50 per cent, and is higher for childhood-onset and for the callous-unemotional subgroup, where estimates approach 70 to 80 per cent.","Callous-unemotional traits, comprising lack of remorse, lack of empathy, shallow affect and unconcern about performance, mark a highly heritable and distinct subgroup with a different aetiological pathway."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","semgen","companion"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.2.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Conduct disorder means a repeated pattern of behaviour that violates other people's rights or major social rules. Not one incident, not being difficult, but a pattern over at least a year.","teach":["ICD-11 conduct-dissocial disorder requires a repetitive and persistent pattern of behaviour violating the basic rights of others or major age-appropriate societal norms, rules or laws, over at least twelve months.","DSM-5 groups the criteria into four categories: aggression to people and animals; destruction of property; deceitfulness or theft; and serious violation of rules.","Three or more criteria in twelve months with at least one in the past six months is required in DSM-5.","Oppositional defiant disorder is the milder and typically earlier condition: angry or irritable mood, argumentative and defiant behaviour, and vindictiveness, directed particularly at authority figures, without the serious rights violations of conduct disorder.","ICD-11 restructured oppositional defiant disorder with a specifier for chronic irritability and anger, which corresponds broadly to DSM-5's disruptive mood dysregulation disorder.","Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder was introduced in DSM-5 for children with severe recurrent temper outbursts and persistently irritable mood between them, explicitly to reduce the over-diagnosis of paediatric bipolar disorder in the United States.","That origin should be known: the diagnosis was created to solve a diagnostic inflation problem rather than because a new disorder had been discovered.","The limited prosocial emotions specifier in DSM-5 identifies the callous-unemotional subgroup, requiring two of four features persistently over twelve months in multiple settings.","Age of onset specifiers: childhood-onset before age ten and adolescent-onset, with the childhood-onset group having more aggression, more neurodevelopmental comorbidity and worse prognosis.","Severity in ICD-11 and DSM-5 is graded, and the setting matters: behaviour confined to the home has different implications from behaviour across home, school and community.","Comorbidity is the rule: ADHD in a large proportion, developmental language disorder, specific learning difficulties, depression, anxiety, PTSD, and substance misuse in adolescence.","Depression and anxiety in conduct disorder are frequently overlooked because the behaviour dominates the presentation, and irritability may be depressive rather than oppositional.","Always assess for language and learning difficulty. A child who cannot understand instructions or express themselves will appear defiant, and this is a common misattribution.","Always assess for maltreatment, domestic abuse and neglect. Conduct problems are a common presentation of an unsafe home, and the child is often protecting the family from disclosure.","Assessment requires information from home and school, since behaviour confined to one setting suggests something about that setting rather than about the child.","Instruments: the Strengths and Difficulties Questionnaire is the standard screening measure with parent, teacher and self-report versions; the Child Behaviour Checklist and Conners rating scales are also used."],"cards":[["Give the DSM-5 categories of conduct disorder criteria.","Aggression to people and animals; destruction of property; deceitfulness or theft; and serious violation of rules. Three or more criteria within twelve months with at least one in the past six months are required."],["What is disruptive mood dysregulation disorder and why was it introduced?","A DSM-5 diagnosis for children with severe recurrent temper outbursts out of proportion to provocation, with persistently irritable or angry mood between outbursts. It was introduced explicitly to reduce the over-diagnosis of paediatric bipolar disorder in the United States rather than because a new disorder had been identified."],["Distinguish oppositional defiant disorder from conduct disorder.","Oppositional defiant disorder involves angry or irritable mood, argumentative and defiant behaviour and vindictiveness, typically directed at authority figures, without the serious violations of others' basic rights, aggression, destruction, deceit and rule-breaking that define conduct disorder."],["What must always be assessed in a child presenting with conduct problems?","Developmental language and learning difficulty, since a child who cannot follow or express language appears defiant; and maltreatment, domestic abuse and neglect, since conduct problems are a common presentation of an unsafe home. Information from both home and school is required, as behaviour confined to one setting indicates something about that setting."]],"note":["ICD-11 conduct-dissocial disorder requires a pattern over at least twelve months.","The limited prosocial emotions specifier identifies the callous-unemotional subgroup.","Childhood-onset before age ten carries more aggression, more comorbidity and worse prognosis.","Depression and anxiety are frequently overlooked because behaviour dominates the presentation.","The Strengths and Difficulties Questionnaire is the standard screening measure."],"refs":["icd11","dsm5","rutter"]};
TEACH["10.8.2.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The treatment is delivered to the parents, not to the child, and it works. Sitting a nine-year-old down for individual therapy about their behaviour is one of the least effective things available.","teach":["NICE CG158 recommends group-based parent training programmes as first-line for children aged 3 to 11 with conduct problems, with individual parent training where group is unsuitable.","Parent training has strong evidence with effect sizes around 0.5 or above, and effects are maintained at follow-up where programmes are delivered with fidelity."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","rutter","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":19,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.2.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"About half grow out of it and about half do not, and the ones who do not have almost always started young. Where it persists it shapes the whole of adult life, not just the criminal part.","teach":["Around 40 to 50 per cent of children with conduct disorder develop antisocial personality disorder in adulthood, and almost all adults with antisocial personality disorder had childhood conduct disorder.","Age of onset is the strongest predictor. Childhood-onset conduct disorder persists in the majority; adolescent-onset largely desists in early adulthood."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","nice","companion"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["10.8.3.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"ADHD affects around one child in twenty. Diagnosis rates have risen sharply, which people take as proof of over-diagnosis, when the evidence points mostly the other way for girls and adults.","teach":["Prevalence in children is around 5 per cent worldwide on meta-analysis, with remarkable consistency across countries once the same criteria are applied.","That international consistency is important: it argues against ADHD being a culturally constructed diagnosis, since prevalence estimates converge when diagnostic method is held constant.","UK diagnosed prevalence is considerably lower than 5 per cent, indicating under-diagnosis rather than over-diagnosis at population level.","Male to female ratio in clinical samples is around three or four to one, but in community samples is closer to two to one, indicating substantial under-recognition in girls.","Girls present more often with predominantly inattentive presentation, which is less disruptive and therefore less often referred, and are diagnosed later.","Adult prevalence is around 2.5 per cent, with around two-thirds of childhood cases having persisting symptoms and around 15 per cent continuing to meet full criteria.","Referral rates for adult ADHD assessment have risen very sharply since around 2020, producing waiting lists of several years in many areas.","Prevalence is markedly raised in specific populations: prison populations at several times the community rate, looked-after children, children with intellectual disability, and children with epilepsy.","Socioeconomic gradient exists but is less steep than for conduct disorder.","Comorbidity is the rule rather than the exception, with around two-thirds having at least one comorbid disorder."],"cards":[["What is the prevalence of ADHD and what does its international consistency show?","Around 5 per cent in children worldwide on meta-analysis, with prevalence estimates converging across countries when the same diagnostic criteria are applied. This consistency argues against ADHD being a culturally constructed diagnosis."],["Why is ADHD under-recognised in girls?","Because girls more often show the predominantly inattentive presentation, which is less disruptive in the classroom and therefore less often referred. The clinical sex ratio of three or four to one falls to around two to one in community samples, indicating substantial under-diagnosis."],["Is ADHD over-diagnosed in the UK?","Diagnosed prevalence in the UK is considerably lower than the 5 per cent found in epidemiological studies, indicating under-diagnosis at population level, though individual over-diagnosis can occur where assessment is inadequate."]],"note":["Adult prevalence is around 2.5 per cent, with two-thirds of childhood cases having persisting symptoms.","Prison prevalence is several times the community rate.","Adult assessment referrals have risen sharply since around 2020, producing multi-year waiting lists.","Around two-thirds of children with ADHD have at least one comorbid disorder."],"refs":["nice","rutter","companion"]};
TEACH["10.8.3.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"ADHD is one of the most heritable conditions in psychiatry, more so than most physical diseases people accept as genetic. Parenting does not cause it, though parenting affects how much damage it does.","teach":["Heritability is around 75 to 80 per cent in twin studies, among the highest of any psychiatric disorder and higher than for most conditions accepted as biological.","Genetic architecture is polygenic, with common variants of small effect and a contribution from rare copy number variants. Genome-wide association studies have identified multiple loci."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semgen","stahl","rutter"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.3.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Three things define it: cannot sustain attention, cannot sit still, cannot wait. All children do these things; in ADHD they happen more, everywhere, and they cost the child something real.","teach":["Two symptom domains: inattention and hyperactivity-impulsivity. Six or more symptoms in either domain in children, or five or more in those aged 17 and over.","Inattention symptoms: failure to attend to detail, difficulty sustaining attention, not seeming to listen, not following through, difficulty organising, avoidance of sustained mental effort, losing things, distractibility and forgetfulness.","Hyperactivity-impulsivity symptoms: fidgeting, leaving seat, running or climbing inappropriately, inability to play quietly, being on the go, talking excessively, blurting answers, difficulty waiting turn and interrupting.","Three presentations: predominantly inattentive, predominantly hyperactive-impulsive, and combined. Presentation can change over development in the same individual.","DSM-5 requires several symptoms before age 12, raised from age 7 in DSM-IV to reflect that inattentive presentations are often identified later.","Symptoms must be present in two or more settings, must interfere with functioning, and must not be better explained by another disorder.","The two-settings requirement is essential and is the commonest point at which inadequate assessment fails. School information is mandatory.","Impairment, not symptom count, is what makes it a disorder. A child with symptoms who is functioning well does not have ADHD.","ICD-11 uses attention deficit hyperactivity disorder with the same broad structure, having abandoned the narrower ICD-10 hyperkinetic disorder which required all three of inattention, hyperactivity and impulsivity.","That change matters historically: ICD-10 hyperkinetic disorder was much more restrictive, which is why older UK prevalence figures are lower than American ones.","Presentation changes with development: hyperactivity is most evident in early childhood, declines through adolescence, and in adults appears as inner restlessness rather than overt overactivity.","Inattention persists and becomes the dominant impairment in adolescence and adulthood, affecting academic work, employment and organisation.","Emotional dysregulation is not in the diagnostic criteria but is present in a large proportion and is a major source of impairment, with low frustration tolerance and rapid intense emotional responses.","Many clinicians regard emotional dysregulation as a core feature that the criteria omit, and it predicts impairment independently of the core symptoms.","Comorbidity: oppositional defiant disorder and conduct disorder in a large proportion, specific learning difficulties, developmental coordination disorder, autism, tics, anxiety, depression, and sleep disorder.","Autism and ADHD may now be diagnosed together, which DSM-IV prohibited, and co-occurrence is common.","Assessment: developmental history, information from school using rating scales, observation, physical examination including cardiovascular assessment, height and weight, and consideration of differential diagnoses.","Rating scales support but never establish the diagnosis: Conners, SNAP-IV, the Strengths and Difficulties Questionnaire, and in adults the DIVA structured interview.","Differential diagnosis: hearing impairment, developmental language disorder, absence seizures, sleep disorder including sleep apnoea, anxiety, trauma, attachment difficulty, intellectual disability, and hyperthyroidism.","Sleep disorder deserves specific mention. Obstructive sleep apnoea in a child produces inattention and hyperactivity, and adenotonsillectomy resolves it."],"cards":[["State the diagnostic requirements for ADHD.","Six or more symptoms of inattention or of hyperactivity-impulsivity, or five or more in those aged 17 and over, present for at least six months, with several symptoms before age 12, present in two or more settings, causing clear functional impairment, and not better explained by another disorder."],["How does the presentation change across development?","Hyperactivity is most evident in early childhood, declines through adolescence, and in adults appears as inner restlessness rather than overt overactivity. Inattention persists and becomes the dominant source of impairment in adolescence and adulthood."],["What is emotional dysregulation in ADHD?","Low frustration tolerance with rapid, intense emotional responses, present in a large proportion of those with ADHD. It is not in the diagnostic criteria but predicts impairment independently of the core symptoms, and many clinicians regard it as a core feature the criteria omit."],["Which differential diagnoses must be excluded?","Hearing impairment, developmental language disorder, absence seizures, sleep disorder including obstructive sleep apnoea, anxiety, trauma, attachment difficulty, intellectual disability and hyperthyroidism. Obstructive sleep apnoea deserves specific attention, since it produces inattention and hyperactivity resolved by adenotonsillectomy."],["Why is school information mandatory?","Because symptoms must be present in two or more settings to meet criteria, and reliance on parental report alone is the commonest point at which inadequate assessment fails."]],"note":["DSM-5 raised the age of onset threshold from 7 to 12 years.","ICD-11 abandoned the narrower ICD-10 hyperkinetic disorder, which required all three symptom clusters.","Autism and ADHD may now be diagnosed together, which DSM-IV prohibited.","Rating scales such as Conners, SNAP-IV and DIVA support but never establish the diagnosis.","Impairment rather than symptom count is what makes it a disorder."],"refs":["icd11","dsm5","nice"]};
TEACH["10.8.3.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Stimulants work better than almost anything else in psychiatry. The effect size is large, the response is fast, and the main risks are appetite, sleep and growth, all of which are monitorable.","teach":["NICE NG87: for children under 5, parent training first-line, and medication only after specialist advice. For children aged 5 and over, offer medication where symptoms cause persistent significant impairment after environmental modification.","Environmental modification comes first at every age: seating, task structure, movement breaks, chunked instructions, and reduced distraction."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","stahl"],"n":{"teach":21,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.3.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"ADHD does not go away for most people, and untreated it costs them accidents, jobs, relationships and, in a substantial number, prison. Treated, most of those risks come down measurably.","teach":["Around two-thirds have persisting symptoms into adulthood and around 15 per cent continue to meet full diagnostic criteria, with hyperactivity declining more than inattention.","Persistence is predicted by symptom severity, comorbidity particularly conduct disorder, and family history."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","rutter","companion"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["10.8.4.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Anxiety disorders are the commonest mental health problem in childhood, and the most likely to be missed, because an anxious child is quiet and a quiet child is not a problem in a classroom of thirty.","teach":["Anxiety disorders are the commonest group of psychiatric disorders in children and adolescents, with prevalence estimates around 6 to 8 per cent at any one time.","Lifetime prevalence by adolescence is considerably higher, of the order of 15 to 30 per cent depending on definition.","Female predominance emerges in adolescence, as with depression, having been more equal in early childhood.","Age of onset varies systematically by disorder: separation anxiety and specific phobia in early childhood; social anxiety disorder in early adolescence with a median around 13; panic disorder in mid to late adolescence; generalised anxiety disorder across childhood and adolescence.","Separation anxiety disorder is the commonest anxiety disorder in younger children, affecting around 3 to 4 per cent.","Specific phobia is very common, with mild fears near-universal in childhood and clinically significant phobia affecting around 5 per cent.","Selective mutism is rare, affecting under 1 per cent, and is classified as an anxiety disorder in both current systems.","OCD affects around 1 to 2 per cent of children and adolescents, with a bimodal onset and an early peak around age 10 that is male-predominant and more often tic-associated.","Around half of adult OCD begins in childhood or adolescence, which makes childhood OCD a substantial contributor to lifetime burden.","Under-detection is the dominant epidemiological problem. Internalising disorders do not disrupt classrooms, so they are not referred, and average delay to treatment is measured in years.","Comorbidity is high: multiple anxiety disorders together, depression, ADHD, autism and, in OCD, tic disorders."],"cards":[["Which is the commonest group of childhood psychiatric disorders?","Anxiety disorders, with point prevalence around 6 to 8 per cent and lifetime prevalence by adolescence of the order of 15 to 30 per cent depending on definition."],["Give the typical ages of onset across the childhood anxiety disorders.","Separation anxiety disorder and specific phobia in early childhood; social anxiety disorder in early adolescence with a median around age 13; panic disorder in mid to late adolescence; and generalised anxiety disorder across childhood and adolescence."],["Why are childhood anxiety disorders under-detected?","Because internalising disorders do not disrupt the classroom, so anxious children are not referred while disruptive children are. Average delay to treatment is measured in years."]],"note":["Separation anxiety disorder is the commonest anxiety disorder in younger children at 3 to 4 per cent.","OCD affects around 1 to 2 per cent, with an early male-predominant tic-associated peak around age 10.","Around half of adult OCD begins in childhood or adolescence.","Selective mutism affects under 1 per cent and is classified as an anxiety disorder.","Female predominance emerges in adolescence, having been more equal in early childhood."],"refs":["nice","rutter","icd11"]};
TEACH["10.8.4.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Anxious children usually have an anxious parent, and the transmission is not mainly genetic. It is watching what the adult treats as dangerous, and being helped to avoid it.","teach":["Heritability of childhood anxiety is moderate, around 30 to 40 per cent, lower than for ADHD or autism, leaving substantial room for environmental transmission.","What is inherited is largely a general vulnerability, best described as behavioural inhibition or trait neuroticism, rather than a specific disorder."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","gross","nice"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.4.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Anxious children rarely say they are anxious. They say their tummy hurts, they will not go to school, they cannot sleep, and they will not leave their mother, and each of those gets treated as a separate problem.","teach":["Presentation is developmentally shaped: younger children present with somatic complaints, clinging, tantrums, crying and refusal; older children can report worry and physical symptoms.","Somatic presentation is the norm in younger children, with abdominal pain and headache the commonest complaints, and repeated medical presentation is common before anxiety is recognised.","Separation anxiety disorder: developmentally excessive fear of separation from attachment figures, with distress on separation, worry about harm befalling them, refusal to go to school or sleep alone, nightmares about separation, and somatic complaints on separation.","Separation anxiety is developmentally normal between about 8 months and 3 years. It becomes a disorder when it is excessive for the developmental stage, persistent and impairing.","School refusal is frequently the presenting problem and may arise from separation anxiety, social anxiety, generalised anxiety, bullying, or depression, and the underlying reason determines treatment.","Generalised anxiety disorder in children: excessive worry across multiple domains, frequently about performance, safety, health and catastrophe, with reassurance seeking, perfectionism and physical symptoms.","Children with generalised anxiety often present as unusually well behaved and conscientious, which delays recognition further.","Social anxiety disorder: fear of scrutiny and negative evaluation, with avoidance of speaking in class, eating in front of others, using public toilets, and joining groups. It must be distinguished from autism, where the difficulty is in understanding rather than in fear of evaluation.","Selective mutism: consistent failure to speak in specific social situations where speech is expected, despite speaking normally elsewhere, for at least a month beyond the first month of school.","Selective mutism is an anxiety disorder, not oppositionality or a language disorder, and treating it as refusal makes it worse.","Specific phobia in children: fear may be expressed as crying, tantrums, freezing or clinging, and the child need not recognise the fear as excessive, unlike adults.","Panic disorder is uncommon before adolescence, and panic attacks in younger children are more often situationally bound to a phobic stimulus.","OCD in children: obsessions of contamination, harm, symmetry and forbidden thoughts; compulsions of washing, checking, ordering, repeating and reassurance seeking.","Children may not report obsessions, either through embarrassment or because they lack the vocabulary, and the compulsions may be all that is visible.","Family involvement in rituals is very common in paediatric OCD, with parents required to answer questions, participate in washing routines or provide reassurance, and this must be asked about directly.","Insight is more variable in children and the requirement that the person recognise the thoughts as excessive is relaxed in children.","Assessment: developmental history, information from school, direct interview with the child alone as well as with parents, and specific enquiry about bullying and about family accommodation.","Instruments: the Spence Children's Anxiety Scale, the Screen for Child Anxiety Related Emotional Disorders, and for OCD the Children's Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale.","Always see the young person alone. Anxiety about disclosure in front of parents is common, and bullying and abuse are rarely disclosed with a parent present."],"cards":[["How does anxiety present in younger children?","Somatically and behaviourally rather than through reported worry: abdominal pain, headache, clinging, tantrums, crying, sleep difficulty and refusal. Repeated medical presentation is common before anxiety is recognised."],["What is selective mutism and how must it be understood?","Consistent failure to speak in specific social situations where speech is expected, despite normal speech elsewhere, lasting at least a month beyond the first month of school. It is an anxiety disorder, not oppositionality or a language disorder, and treating it as deliberate refusal makes it worse."],["Distinguish social anxiety disorder from autism in a child.","In social anxiety the child understands social expectations and fears negative evaluation, and is typically socially competent in comfortable settings. In autism the difficulty is in understanding and processing social interaction itself, is present across all settings including with close family, and is accompanied by restricted repetitive behaviours and sensory features."],["What is distinctive about OCD presentation in children?","Children may not report obsessions, through embarrassment or lack of vocabulary, so only the compulsions may be visible. Family involvement in rituals is very common, with parents required to answer questions, participate in washing or provide reassurance, and must be asked about directly. Insight requirements are relaxed in children."],["Why must a young person be seen alone?","Because anxiety about disclosing in front of parents is common, and bullying, abuse and the true content of obsessions are rarely disclosed with a parent present."]],"note":["Separation anxiety is developmentally normal between about 8 months and 3 years.","School refusal may arise from separation anxiety, social anxiety, bullying or depression, and the cause determines treatment.","Children with generalised anxiety often present as unusually conscientious, delaying recognition.","Panic disorder is uncommon before adolescence.","SCAS, SCARED and CY-BOCS are the standard instruments."],"refs":["icd11","nice","rutter"]};
TEACH["10.8.4.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"CBT works for anxious children and it works better when the parent is taught to stop protecting the child from the thing they are frightened of. That is uncomfortable advice to give and it is the effective part.","teach":["CBT is first-line for childhood anxiety disorders, with strong evidence, delivered individually, in groups, or through parents.","Around 60 per cent of children are free of their primary anxiety disorder after CBT, and around half are free of all anxiety disorders."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","rutter"],"n":{"teach":20,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.4.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Childhood anxiety does not usually resolve on its own, and untreated it is the strongest single predictor of adult depression. Treated, most children get substantially better and stay better.","teach":["Untreated childhood anxiety disorders show substantial persistence, with a majority still meeting criteria for an anxiety disorder or having developed another disorder at follow-up.","Homotypic continuity: childhood anxiety predicts adult anxiety, with the same or a different anxiety disorder."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","rutter","companion"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["10.8.5.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Depression is rare before puberty and common after it, and the sex difference appears at exactly the same point. Something about adolescence changes the picture completely.","teach":["Prepubertal depression is uncommon, with prevalence around 1 to 2 per cent, and the sex ratio is approximately equal or slightly male-predominant.","Adolescent depression is common, with point prevalence around 4 to 5 per cent and cumulative prevalence by age 18 of the order of 15 to 20 per cent.","The female predominance of two to one emerges during adolescence, between roughly ages 12 and 15, and this timing is one of the most consistent findings in developmental epidemiology.","Explanations for the emerging sex difference include hormonal change, differential response to interpersonal stress, rumination style, body image and pubertal timing, and none is established alone.","Early pubertal timing in girls is associated with raised depression risk, and the association is mediated substantially by social factors rather than by hormones directly.","Rates of adolescent depression and anxiety have risen in UK and international surveys since around 2010, with the rise concentrated in girls.","Explanations for the rise are contested: social media and smartphone use, academic pressure, reduced unstructured time, economic conditions, and increased help-seeking and recognition have all been proposed.","The social media explanation is popular and the evidence is genuinely mixed, with small average associations that may be causal in a subgroup, and honest teaching states that rather than asserting a cause.","Bipolar disorder in children is rare. Prepubertal mania is very rare in UK practice, and the substantially higher US diagnostic rates in the 1990s and 2000s reflected diagnostic practice rather than epidemiology.","That diagnostic divergence led to the creation of disruptive mood dysregulation disorder in DSM-5 specifically to capture the children being labelled bipolar.","Adolescent-onset bipolar disorder does occur, with onset typically in late adolescence, and around a fifth to a quarter of bipolar disorder begins before age 18.","Self-harm in adolescents is common, with around 10 per cent reporting self-harm in community surveys and higher rates in girls, and hospital presentation rates have risen sharply."],"cards":[["When does the female predominance in depression emerge?","During adolescence, between roughly ages 12 and 15. Prepubertal depression has an approximately equal sex ratio, and the two to one female predominance emerges with puberty, which is one of the most consistent findings in developmental epidemiology."],["What is the status of prepubertal bipolar disorder?","Very rare in UK practice. The substantially higher United States diagnostic rates in the 1990s and 2000s reflected diagnostic practice rather than true epidemiology, and disruptive mood dysregulation disorder was created in DSM-5 specifically to capture the children who were being labelled bipolar."],["What is the evidence on rising adolescent depression rates?","Rates have risen in UK and international surveys since around 2010, concentrated in girls. Proposed explanations include social media and smartphone use, academic pressure, reduced unstructured time, economic conditions and increased recognition. The social media evidence is genuinely mixed, with small average associations that may be causal in a subgroup."]],"note":["Prepubertal depression prevalence is around 1 to 2 per cent; adolescent point prevalence around 4 to 5 per cent.","Cumulative prevalence by age 18 is of the order of 15 to 20 per cent.","Early pubertal timing in girls raises depression risk, mediated largely by social factors.","Around a fifth to a quarter of bipolar disorder begins before age 18.","Around 10 per cent of adolescents report self-harm in community surveys."],"refs":["nice","rutter","companion"]};
TEACH["10.8.5.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Adolescent depression usually has a reason, and the reason is usually about other people. Loss, rejection, bullying and family conflict do most of the work, on top of a temperament that was already vulnerable.","teach":["Heritability of adolescent depression is around 40 per cent, similar to adult depression, and rises through adolescence into adulthood.","Genetic effects increase with age across adolescence while shared environmental effects decline, which is a general pattern in developmental behaviour genetics."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","gross","nice"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.5.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"A depressed adolescent is often irritable rather than sad, and everyone reads it as attitude. Ask about whether anything is still enjoyable, and about sleep, and the picture usually becomes clear quickly.","teach":["The criteria are the same as in adults, with two developmental modifications: irritable mood may substitute for depressed mood in children and adolescents, and failure to make expected weight gain may substitute for weight loss.","Irritability rather than sadness is the commonest mood presentation in adolescents, and it is routinely misread as ordinary teenage behaviour or as oppositionality.","Anhedonia is the most useful discriminating symptom. Ask what the young person used to enjoy and whether they still enjoy it, and the answer separates depression from ordinary adolescent moodiness.","Presentation varies with age: younger children show somatic complaints, separation anxiety, irritability and behavioural change; adolescents show more classical presentations with hopelessness, guilt and suicidal thinking.","Somatic complaints, school refusal, deteriorating academic performance, social withdrawal and substance misuse are all common presenting features.","Comorbidity is very high: anxiety disorders, conduct disorder, ADHD, substance misuse and eating disorders.","Self-harm is common in depressed adolescents and is not the same as suicidal intent, though it substantially raises suicide risk and both must be assessed separately.","Assess self-harm and suicidal ideation directly, alone, in every case. Asking does not increase risk, and adolescents frequently disclose to a clinician what they have told nobody else.","Psychotic depression occurs in adolescents and is under-recognised. Hallucinations should be asked about specifically, since young people rarely volunteer them.","Distinguish normal adolescent moodiness: normal mood change is reactive, brief and does not impair functioning, whereas depression is persistent, pervasive and impairing.","Bipolar disorder in adolescence: mania presents with elation or irritability, reduced sleep need, grandiosity, increased goal-directed activity and risk-taking, and must be distinguished from ADHD.","The key discriminators from ADHD are episodicity, elevated mood, reduced need for sleep as opposed to difficulty sleeping, and grandiosity. ADHD is chronic and non-episodic.","Reduced need for sleep, meaning the young person sleeps less and feels fine, is the single most useful discriminator from ADHD, where the child is tired.","Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder in DSM-5: severe recurrent temper outbursts inconsistent with developmental level, occurring three or more times weekly, with persistently irritable or angry mood between outbursts, onset before age 10 and diagnosis between 6 and 18.","ICD-11 handles the same presentation through an oppositional defiant disorder specifier with chronic irritability and anger, rather than as a separate diagnosis.","Assessment: interview the young person alone and with parents, obtain school information, assess self-harm and suicide, screen for substance misuse, and enquire about bullying and abuse.","Instruments: the Mood and Feelings Questionnaire is the standard measure in this age group; the Children's Depression Rating Scale Revised is clinician-administered.","Physical assessment: exclude hypothyroidism, anaemia, glandular fever, vitamin D deficiency and substance use, and consider chronic illness and its treatment."],"cards":[["How do the diagnostic criteria for depression differ in children and adolescents?","Irritable mood may substitute for depressed mood, and failure to make expected weight gain may substitute for weight loss. Otherwise the criteria are the same as in adults."],["What is the most useful discriminating symptom in adolescent depression?","Anhedonia. Asking what the young person used to enjoy and whether they still enjoy it separates depression from ordinary adolescent moodiness more reliably than asking about sadness, since irritability rather than sadness is the commonest mood presentation."],["How is adolescent mania distinguished from ADHD?","By episodicity, since ADHD is chronic and non-episodic; by elevated or expansive mood; by grandiosity; and above all by reduced need for sleep, in which the young person sleeps less and feels well, as opposed to the difficulty sleeping and consequent tiredness seen in ADHD."],["What is disruptive mood dysregulation disorder in DSM-5?","Severe recurrent temper outbursts inconsistent with developmental level occurring three or more times weekly, with persistently irritable or angry mood between outbursts, onset before age 10 and diagnosis made between ages 6 and 18. ICD-11 handles the same presentation through an oppositional defiant disorder specifier with chronic irritability and anger."],["What must always be assessed in a depressed adolescent?","Self-harm and suicidal ideation, asked directly and with the young person seen alone, since asking does not increase risk and adolescents frequently disclose to a clinician what they have told nobody else. Psychotic symptoms should also be asked about specifically."]],"note":["Irritability is routinely misread as ordinary teenage behaviour or oppositionality.","Self-harm is common in adolescent depression and is distinct from suicidal intent but raises suicide risk.","The Mood and Feelings Questionnaire is the standard measure in this age group.","Exclude hypothyroidism, anaemia, glandular fever, vitamin D deficiency and substance use.","Normal adolescent mood change is reactive, brief and non-impairing."],"refs":["nice","icd11","rutter"]};
TEACH["10.8.5.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Talking therapy first, fluoxetine second, and only fluoxetine, because it is the one antidepressant that has been shown to do more good than harm in this age group.","teach":["NICE NG134 for depression in children and young people: for mild depression, digital CBT, group CBT, group IPT or group non-directive supportive therapy, with watchful waiting for two weeks first.","Antidepressants should not be offered for mild depression in children and young people."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","rutter"],"n":{"teach":20,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.5.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Most adolescent depressive episodes end within a year, and most of those young people have another one. The illness that starts in adolescence is usually the illness they will have as an adult.","teach":["Median episode duration in adolescent depression is around seven to nine months, with the great majority remitting within two years.","Recurrence is high: around 40 to 60 per cent have a further episode within two to five years, and around two-thirds have a further episode in adulthood."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","rutter","companion"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.6.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Psychosis before puberty is rare enough that most psychiatrists never see it. From about fifteen onwards the rate climbs steeply, which is why early intervention services start where they do.","teach":["Childhood-onset schizophrenia, defined as onset before age 13, is very rare, with prevalence of the order of 1 in 10,000 or less.","Rates rise steeply through adolescence. Around 4 per cent of schizophrenia has onset before 15, and around 20 per cent before 20.","Peak incidence is in late adolescence and early adulthood, earlier in males by around three to five years.","Psychotic experiences, as distinct from psychotic disorder, are common in the general child and adolescent population, reported by around 5 to 15 per cent depending on age and method.","That distinction matters greatly: hearing a voice or having an odd belief is common in adolescence and most of those young people never develop a psychotic disorder.","Psychotic experiences in adolescence do carry raised risk of later psychotic disorder, of the order of a fourfold increase, but the absolute risk remains low.","Psychotic experiences are also a general marker of psychopathology, predicting depression, anxiety, self-harm and suicide as much as they predict psychosis.","Risk is markedly raised in adolescents with a family history, with 22q11.2 deletion syndrome, and in those with childhood adversity or cannabis use.","Ultra-high risk or at-risk mental state criteria identify young people with attenuated psychotic symptoms, brief intermittent psychotic symptoms, or genetic risk with functional decline.","Transition rates from ultra-high risk states to psychosis have fallen over time, from around 40 per cent in early cohorts to around 15 to 20 per cent at two years in recent samples, probably through earlier and broader identification.","That falling transition rate is important, because interventions justified on the earlier figures now treat a group in whom the majority will not develop psychosis."],"cards":[["How common is psychosis in childhood and adolescence?","Childhood-onset schizophrenia before age 13 is very rare, of the order of 1 in 10,000 or less. Around 4 per cent of schizophrenia has onset before 15 and around 20 per cent before 20, with incidence rising steeply through late adolescence."],["Distinguish psychotic experiences from psychotic disorder in young people.","Psychotic experiences such as hearing a voice or holding an odd belief are common in adolescence, reported by around 5 to 15 per cent, and most of those young people never develop a psychotic disorder. They raise the risk of later psychotic disorder around fourfold but the absolute risk remains low, and they predict depression, anxiety and self-harm as much as psychosis."],["What has happened to transition rates from ultra-high risk states?","They have fallen from around 40 per cent in early cohorts to around 15 to 20 per cent at two years in recent samples, probably through earlier and broader identification. This matters because interventions justified on the earlier figures now treat a group in whom the majority will not develop psychosis."]],"note":["Peak incidence is in late adolescence and early adulthood, earlier in males by three to five years.","22q11.2 deletion syndrome carries a schizophrenia risk of around a quarter to a third.","Ultra-high risk criteria cover attenuated symptoms, brief intermittent symptoms, and genetic risk with functional decline.","Childhood adversity and cannabis use raise risk in adolescents as in adults."],"refs":["nice","rutter","morgan"]};
TEACH["10.8.6.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The earlier psychosis starts, the more it looks like a developmental problem and the less it looks like an illness that arrived. Childhood-onset cases carry more genetic loading and more early developmental abnormality.","teach":["Childhood-onset schizophrenia has greater genetic loading than adult-onset disease, with higher rates of family history and a higher burden of rare copy number variants.","Rare copy number variants including 22q11.2 deletion are substantially over-represented in childhood-onset cases, and genetic testing including microarray is indicated in very early onset psychosis."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semgen","morgan","lishman"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["10.8.6.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"In a young person the illness usually announces itself by things falling apart rather than by clear symptoms. The grades drop, the friendships go, the person becomes odd, and the voices come later.","teach":["Presentation in young people is dominated by functional decline and negative symptoms, with positive symptoms often appearing later and less prominently than in adults.","The prodrome is longer and more insidious in younger onset, with social withdrawal, declining academic performance, odd beliefs, perceptual abnormalities, reduced self-care and loss of friendships."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oyebode","nice","rutter"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.6.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The same drugs are used at lower doses, and young people get the side effects far more readily than adults do, particularly the weight gain, which can be dramatic and permanent.","teach":["NICE NG155 and CG155: for young people with first-episode psychosis, offer oral antipsychotic medication in conjunction with family intervention and individual CBT.","Psychological intervention is not optional or secondary. Both family intervention and CBT for psychosis are recommended alongside medication from the outset."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","rutter"],"n":{"teach":20,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.6.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The younger psychosis starts, the worse it tends to go, because it interrupts the years when a person is supposed to be building everything else. But early treatment changes that, which is the whole argument for these services.","teach":["Earlier age of onset predicts poorer outcome across symptomatic, cognitive and functional domains, and childhood-onset schizophrenia has the worst prognosis of any onset group.","The reason is developmental: psychosis in adolescence interrupts education, peer relationships and identity formation at the point when those are being established."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","rutter","companion"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.7.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Eating disorders start in adolescence more than at any other time of life, and the number of young people presenting rose sharply during and after the pandemic in every country that measured it.","teach":["Adolescence is the peak period of onset for eating disorders. Anorexia nervosa peaks between around 15 and 19; bulimia nervosa slightly later, in the late teens and early twenties.","Point prevalence of anorexia nervosa in adolescent girls is around 0.3 to 1 per cent, with a larger group having subthreshold presentations.","Bulimia nervosa is less common in adolescence than in young adulthood, since onset is later.","Avoidant restrictive food intake disorder, ARFID, is specifically a childhood presentation and is common in children with autism, anxiety and sensory sensitivity.","ARFID was introduced in DSM-5 and ICD-11 for restrictive eating without weight or shape concern, replacing the older feeding disorder of infancy and childhood.","The sex ratio is around ten to one female to male in clinical samples, though boys are substantially under-diagnosed and present later, often after greater weight loss.","Presentations rose markedly in adolescents during and after the COVID-19 pandemic, reported across multiple countries, with increases in both referrals and admissions and greater severity at presentation.","Younger children present with eating disorders, including under-13s, and this group has particular medical vulnerability because of lower reserves and ongoing growth requirements.","Higher risk groups: those in weight-dependent sports and dance, those with type 1 diabetes, and those with a family history.","Prevalence in type 1 diabetes is around twice that in the general population, and insulin omission for weight control is common and specifically dangerous."],"cards":[["When do eating disorders peak in onset?","Adolescence, with anorexia nervosa peaking between around 15 and 19 and bulimia nervosa slightly later in the late teens and early twenties. Adolescence is the peak period of onset for the eating disorders as a group."],["What is ARFID?","Avoidant restrictive food intake disorder, introduced in DSM-5 and ICD-11 for restrictive eating without weight or shape concern, arising from sensory sensitivity, lack of interest in food or fear of aversive consequences. It is specifically a childhood presentation and is common in children with autism, anxiety and sensory sensitivity."],["What happened to adolescent eating disorder presentations during the pandemic?","Referrals and admissions rose markedly across multiple countries during and after the COVID-19 pandemic, with greater severity at presentation."]],"note":["Point prevalence of anorexia nervosa in adolescent girls is around 0.3 to 1 per cent.","Boys are substantially under-diagnosed and present later, often after greater weight loss.","Children under 13 present with eating disorders and have particular medical vulnerability.","Prevalence in type 1 diabetes is around twice the general population rate."],"refs":["nice","icd11","rutter"]};
TEACH["10.8.7.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The strongest aetiological finding in the last decade was genetic, and it was that anorexia has as much to do with metabolism as with psychology. That was not what anyone expected.","teach":["Heritability of anorexia nervosa is around 50 to 60 per cent, considerably higher than was assumed when it was regarded as a sociocultural disorder.","The 2019 genome-wide association study identified eight loci and found genetic correlations both with psychiatric traits, particularly OCD, anxiety and depression, and with metabolic and anthropometric traits including insulin-glucose metabolism and body mass index."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","semgen","rutter"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.7.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"In a growing child, weight loss is not the only measure and often not the first. A child who stops gaining weight, or falls off their growth centile, is in trouble even if the number on the scale has not moved.","teach":["Failure to make expected weight gain, rather than weight loss, may be the presenting feature in a growing child, and growth charts are essential.","Weight and BMI must be interpreted against growth centiles and against the child's own trajectory, using BMI centile or percentage median BMI rather than absolute BMI.","A child may be at high physical risk at a BMI that would be unremarkable in an adult, because reserves are smaller and requirements for growth are ongoing.","Rate of weight loss matters as much as absolute weight, and rapid loss in a child is dangerous even from a higher starting point.","Presentation may lack explicit weight and shape concern, particularly in younger children, and ICD-11 and DSM-5 both permit diagnosis without fat phobia.","Younger children more often give somatic explanations such as feeling full, stomach ache or fear of vomiting, and may not articulate a wish to be thin.","Physical signs: bradycardia, hypotension with postural drop, hypothermia, acrocyanosis, lanugo, dry skin, hair loss, and delayed capillary refill.","Bradycardia in a child is a serious sign and the thresholds are age-dependent, with a heart rate below 40 to 50 depending on age indicating high risk.","Growth and pubertal effects: growth retardation, delayed or arrested puberty, primary or secondary amenorrhoea, and reduced peak bone mass accrual.","Bone effects are particularly serious in adolescence because peak bone mass is accrued during this period and is not fully recoverable, so duration of amenorrhoea is a key clinical variable.","Biochemistry: hypokalaemia especially with purging, hyponatraemia, hypophosphataemia, hypoglycaemia which is particularly dangerous in children, and deranged liver function.","Hypoglycaemia is a specific paediatric danger, since children have smaller glycogen reserves, and it can be fatal.","Assessment framework: MEED, Medical Emergencies in Eating Disorders, published in 2022, replaced MARSIPAN and Junior MARSIPAN and covers all ages.","MEED risk parameters include BMI or percentage median BMI, rate of weight loss, heart rate, blood pressure and postural drop, temperature, muscle power on the sit up squat stand test, biochemistry and ECG.","The SUSS test, sit up squat stand, assesses proximal myopathy and is quick, reproducible and informative in children.","ARFID presentation: restriction based on sensory characteristics of food, lack of interest in eating, or fear of aversive consequences such as choking or vomiting, without weight or shape concern.","ARFID must be distinguished from anorexia nervosa by the absence of weight and shape concern, and from normal picky eating by the presence of nutritional deficiency, weight faltering, dependence on supplements or marked psychosocial impairment.","Bulimia nervosa in adolescents presents as in adults, with binge eating and compensatory behaviour, and normal weight means it is frequently concealed for years.","Always ask about diabetes and insulin omission, exercise, laxatives, diet pills and vomiting, since none is volunteered."],"cards":[["How is weight assessed differently in a child or adolescent?","Against growth centiles and the child's own trajectory, using BMI centile or percentage median BMI rather than absolute BMI, since failure to make expected weight gain may be the presenting feature in a growing child. A child may be at high physical risk at a BMI that would be unremarkable in an adult."],["What is MEED and what does it assess?","Medical Emergencies in Eating Disorders, published in 2022, replacing MARSIPAN and Junior MARSIPAN and covering all ages. It assesses BMI or percentage median BMI, rate of weight loss, heart rate, blood pressure and postural drop, temperature, muscle power on the sit up squat stand test, biochemistry and ECG."],["Why are bone effects particularly serious in adolescent anorexia nervosa?","Because peak bone mass is accrued during adolescence and is not fully recoverable later, so amenorrhoea during this period causes permanent deficit. Duration of amenorrhoea is therefore a key clinical variable."],["How is ARFID distinguished from anorexia nervosa and from picky eating?","From anorexia nervosa by the absence of any weight or shape concern, restriction instead arising from sensory characteristics, lack of interest in eating, or fear of aversive consequences. From normal picky eating by the presence of nutritional deficiency, weight faltering, dependence on supplements, or marked psychosocial impairment."],["Which biochemical abnormality is a specific paediatric danger?","Hypoglycaemia, because children have smaller glycogen reserves than adults and it can be rapidly fatal."]],"note":["Bradycardia thresholds are age-dependent; a rate below 40 to 50 indicates high risk depending on age.","Both ICD-11 and DSM-5 permit diagnosis without fat phobia.","Younger children often give somatic explanations rather than expressing a wish to be thin.","The sit up squat stand test assesses proximal myopathy and is quick and reproducible.","Ask specifically about insulin omission, exercise, laxatives, diet pills and vomiting."],"refs":["nice","maudphys","icd11"]};
TEACH["10.8.7.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"For a young person with anorexia the treatment is to put the parents back in charge of eating, temporarily and with support, and then hand control back as the young person recovers. It works better than anything else available.","teach":["NICE NG69: anorexia-nervosa-focused family therapy, FT-AN, is first-line for children and young people, and has the strongest evidence of any treatment in this age group.","FT-AN is delivered in three phases: first, parents take charge of refeeding with the therapist supporting them; second, control of eating is gradually returned to the young person; third, adolescent development and relapse prevention are addressed."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudphys","rutter"],"n":{"teach":19,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.7.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Adolescent anorexia has a much better outlook than adult anorexia, and the reason is largely that it is caught earlier and the family is still there to help. Duration before treatment is the thing that matters most.","teach":["Adolescent-onset anorexia nervosa has substantially better outcomes than adult-onset disease, with recovery rates of the order of 60 to 70 per cent at long-term follow-up.","The better prognosis reflects shorter duration of illness at treatment, availability of family involvement, and greater physiological resilience."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","rutter","maudphys"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.8.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Autism diagnoses have risen enormously and the reason is mostly that the definition widened and recognition improved, not that more children became autistic.","teach":["Current prevalence estimates in children are around 1 to 2 per cent in the UK and internationally, with some recent studies reporting higher figures.","Recorded prevalence has risen dramatically since the 1970s, when estimates were around 4 per 10,000, a rise of one to two orders of magnitude.","The rise is attributable principally to broadening diagnostic criteria, the introduction of the spectrum concept, diagnostic substitution from intellectual disability, increased awareness, and service and educational incentives to diagnose.","Diagnostic substitution is well demonstrated: as autism diagnoses rose, diagnoses of intellectual disability and of language disorder fell correspondingly in the same populations.","There is no credible evidence of a true epidemic. Studies applying consistent criteria across time find much more stable rates.","The male to female ratio in clinical samples is around three or four to one, falling to around three to one or lower in careful population screening studies, indicating substantial under-diagnosis in girls.","Girls are diagnosed later and require greater impairment to receive a diagnosis, partly through camouflaging and partly through diagnostic criteria and instruments developed on male samples.","Camouflaging or masking, the effortful suppression of autistic behaviours and conscious imitation of social behaviour, is commoner in girls and women and delays diagnosis at substantial psychological cost.","Around a third to a half of autistic people have co-occurring intellectual disability, though this proportion has fallen as diagnosis has broadened to include more able individuals.","Autism is markedly over-represented in certain genetic conditions: fragile X, tuberous sclerosis, 22q11.2 deletion and Rett syndrome.","Adult diagnosis rates have risen sharply, with long waiting lists, as adults who were missed in childhood present for assessment.","The MMR vaccine does not cause autism. The 1998 Wakefield paper was fraudulent, was retracted in 2010, Wakefield was struck off, and multiple large studies have found no association."],"cards":[["Why have autism diagnosis rates risen so dramatically?","Principally through broadening diagnostic criteria, introduction of the spectrum concept, diagnostic substitution from intellectual disability and language disorder, increased awareness, and service and educational incentives to diagnose. Studies applying consistent criteria across time find much more stable rates, and there is no credible evidence of a true epidemic."],["Why is autism under-diagnosed in girls?","Because of camouflaging or masking, the effortful suppression of autistic behaviours and conscious imitation of social behaviour, which is commoner in girls; and because diagnostic criteria and instruments were developed largely on male samples. Girls are diagnosed later and require greater impairment to receive a diagnosis."],["What is the position on MMR and autism?","The MMR vaccine does not cause autism. The 1998 Wakefield paper was fraudulent, was retracted in 2010, and Wakefield was removed from the medical register. Multiple large epidemiological studies have found no association."]],"note":["Current prevalence estimates are around 1 to 2 per cent in children.","Diagnostic substitution is demonstrated: intellectual disability and language disorder diagnoses fell as autism diagnoses rose.","Around a third to a half of autistic people have co-occurring intellectual disability.","Autism is over-represented in fragile X, tuberous sclerosis, 22q11.2 deletion and Rett syndrome.","Adult diagnosis rates have risen sharply with long waiting lists."],"refs":["rutter","nice","semgen"]};
TEACH["10.8.8.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Autism is among the most heritable conditions in psychiatry. It is not caused by parenting, it is not caused by vaccines, and the search for a single cause has failed because there are hundreds.","teach":["Heritability is high, around 70 to 90 per cent in twin studies, with monozygotic concordance around 60 to 90 per cent and dizygotic considerably lower.","Sibling recurrence risk is around 10 to 20 per cent, substantially above the population rate, and higher where the affected sibling is female."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","semgen","gross"],"n":{"teach":18,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.8.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Two things define autism: difficulty with the back-and-forth of social interaction, and a need for sameness with intense narrow interests. Both must have been there from early childhood, even if nobody noticed until later.","teach":["DSM-5 and ICD-11 both require two domains: persistent deficits in social communication and social interaction, and restricted repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities.","DSM-5 collapsed the previous separate diagnoses of autistic disorder, Asperger's disorder and pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified into a single autism spectrum disorder.","Asperger's syndrome no longer exists as a separate diagnosis in either system, though many people continue to use the term for themselves and this should be respected.","Social communication domain: deficits in social-emotional reciprocity, in nonverbal communicative behaviours, and in developing, maintaining and understanding relationships.","Restricted repetitive domain: stereotyped or repetitive movements or speech; insistence on sameness and inflexible adherence to routine; highly restricted fixated interests of abnormal intensity or focus; and hyper- or hyporeactivity to sensory input.","Sensory features were added as a criterion in DSM-5, having previously been regarded as associated rather than diagnostic, and they are a major source of impairment.","Sensory sensitivity is frequently the most disabling feature in daily life and is the one most amenable to environmental adaptation.","Symptoms must be present in early developmental period, though they may not become fully manifest until social demands exceed capacity, which is why diagnosis can be made in adolescence or adulthood.","The late-manifestation clause is important: a person may be diagnosed at 30 and still have had autism since early childhood, and this is not a contradiction.","Severity specifiers in DSM-5 describe the level of support required, graded across each domain separately.","Specifiers for co-occurring intellectual impairment and language impairment are used, since these substantially alter presentation and needs.","Presentation in girls: more socially motivated, better superficial social skills through learning and imitation, interests that are more socially acceptable in content though equally intense, and greater camouflaging.","Camouflaging is effortful and exhausting, is associated with anxiety, depression and autistic burnout, and its cost is frequently invisible until the person collapses.","Comorbidity is the rule: ADHD in a large proportion, anxiety disorders in around 40 per cent, depression, epilepsy in around 20 to 30 per cent particularly with intellectual disability, sleep disorder, and gastrointestinal problems.","Autism and ADHD may be diagnosed together, which DSM-IV prohibited and DSM-5 permits, and co-occurrence is common.","Catatonia occurs in autism, particularly in adolescence, presenting as progressive slowing, increased prompt dependence, and loss of function, and it is treatable and frequently missed.","Assessment: developmental history, direct observation, information from school, and where used, structured instruments such as ADI-R for the parent interview and ADOS-2 for observation.","ADOS and ADI-R support but do not establish the diagnosis, which remains clinical, and NICE recommends multidisciplinary assessment.","Differential diagnosis: developmental language disorder, intellectual disability, social anxiety disorder, selective mutism, attachment disorder, schizoid personality, and severe deprivation producing quasi-autistic features."],"cards":[["State the two diagnostic domains of autism.","Persistent deficits in social communication and social interaction, comprising deficits in social-emotional reciprocity, in nonverbal communicative behaviour, and in developing and maintaining relationships; and restricted repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities, comprising stereotyped movements or speech, insistence on sameness, highly restricted fixated interests, and hyper- or hyporeactivity to sensory input."],["What happened to Asperger's syndrome?","It was removed as a separate diagnosis in DSM-5 and ICD-11, collapsed with autistic disorder and pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified into a single autism spectrum disorder. Many people continue to use the term for themselves and this should be respected."],["How does autism present differently in girls?","With greater social motivation, better superficial social skills acquired through learning and imitation, interests that are more socially acceptable in content though equally intense in focus, and greater camouflaging. Camouflaging is effortful and exhausting, is associated with anxiety, depression and autistic burnout, and its cost is frequently invisible."],["Why can autism be diagnosed for the first time in adulthood?","Because the criteria require symptoms to be present in the early developmental period but allow that they may not become fully manifest until social demands exceed the person's capacity. A person diagnosed at 30 has had autism since early childhood."],["What is catatonia in autism?","A presentation, particularly in adolescence, of progressive slowing, increased prompt dependence, mutism, posturing and loss of previously acquired function. It is treatable with benzodiazepines and ECT and is frequently missed, being attributed to deterioration in the autism itself."]],"note":["Sensory features were added as a diagnostic criterion in DSM-5 and are frequently the most disabling feature.","Autism and ADHD may now be diagnosed together, which DSM-IV prohibited.","Epilepsy occurs in around 20 to 30 per cent, particularly with intellectual disability.","ADI-R and ADOS-2 support but do not establish the diagnosis, which remains clinical.","Severe deprivation can produce quasi-autistic features, as shown in the Romanian adoptees."],"refs":["icd11","dsm5","nice"]};
TEACH["10.8.8.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There is no treatment for autism itself and there does not need to be. What helps is adapting the environment, teaching communication, supporting the family, and treating the anxiety and other conditions that come with it.","teach":["There is no medication for the core features of autism, and this should be said plainly at diagnosis rather than allowing families to search for one.","The aims of intervention are communication, functional skills, adaptation of the environment, family understanding and support, and treatment of co-occurring conditions."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","rutter","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":20,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.8.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Autism does not go away, and it does not need to. What changes is how well the person's life fits them, and that depends far more on what is around them than on the autism itself.","teach":["Autism is lifelong. Core features persist, though presentation changes with development, learning and compensation.","A minority, of the order of 5 to 20 per cent in some cohorts, no longer meet diagnostic criteria in later childhood or adulthood, described as optimal outcome, though most retain some difficulties."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","nice","companion"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.9.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Most adolescents who try drugs stop on their own. The ones who do not are usually the ones who started youngest and who had other problems first.","teach":["Experimentation in adolescence is common and most of it does not progress to a disorder. Around a third to a half of 15-year-olds in UK surveys report having tried alcohol.","Alcohol use in UK adolescents has declined substantially since the early 2000s, a sustained and under-reported trend seen across many high-income countries."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","nice","rutter"],"n":{"teach":11,"cards":2,"note":4}};
TEACH["10.8.9.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The adolescent brain is built to seek reward and not yet built to stop itself, and drugs arrive exactly in that gap. That is why starting young matters so much more than starting at thirty.","teach":["The developmental mismatch model: limbic reward systems mature earlier than prefrontal control systems, producing a period of heightened reward sensitivity with immature regulation.","This mismatch explains adolescent risk-taking generally and makes adolescence a period of specific vulnerability to substances."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","rutter","morgan"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.9.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Adolescents rarely present saying they have a drug problem. They present through school, the police, an overdose, or a parent who found something, and the substance use is discovered rather than reported.","teach":["Presentation is rarely self-referred. Adolescents present through school concerns, criminal justice contact, parental discovery, acute intoxication, or an unrelated health problem.","Ask every adolescent about substance use as part of routine assessment, in a non-judgemental way, alone and with confidentiality explained."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","nice","rutter"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":4}};
TEACH["10.8.9.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Treatment for young people is mostly family work and practical help with school and housing, not detox and not medication. The evidence supports working with the system the young person lives in.","teach":["Family-based interventions have the strongest evidence in adolescent substance misuse: multidimensional family therapy, functional family therapy, brief strategic family therapy and multisystemic therapy.","The common element is working with the family and the wider system rather than treating the young person in isolation, which distinguishes adolescent from adult addiction treatment."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","semaddict","rutter"],"n":{"teach":19,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.9.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Most young people who use substances stop as they take on adult roles. The ones who do not are usually those who started earliest, had a conduct disorder first, and lost their education along the way.","teach":["Most adolescent substance use does not persist. Natural maturing out occurs as young people take on employment, partnership and parenting roles.","The transition to adult roles is the mechanism of desistance, which is the same finding as in the offending literature and reflects the same process."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","rutter","nice"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["10.8.10.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Tics are common in childhood and Tourette syndrome is not. Most tics appear around seven, get worse around eleven, and fade by the early twenties.","teach":["Transient tics are common in childhood, affecting up to 20 per cent of children at some point, and most resolve within a year.","Chronic tic disorder, lasting more than a year, affects around 1 to 3 per cent of children."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","nice","oyebode"],"n":{"teach":11,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["10.8.10.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Tics come from a circuit connecting the deep motor structures of the brain to the frontal lobes, the same circuit involved in OCD. That shared circuit is why the two conditions travel together.","teach":["Heritability is high, around 60 to 80 per cent in twin studies, with a strong familial pattern and shared familial transmission with OCD.","Genetic architecture is polygenic with contributions from rare variants. No single gene accounts for more than a small proportion."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lishman","rutter","oyebode"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.10.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A tic is a sudden, rapid, repeated movement or sound that the person can hold in for a while but not forever. Coprolalia, the swearing everyone associates with Tourette's, occurs in only about one in ten.","teach":["Definition: sudden, rapid, recurrent, non-rhythmic motor movements or vocalisations.","Simple motor tics: eye blinking, facial grimacing, head jerking, shoulder shrugging, abdominal tensing. Complex motor tics: touching, tapping, jumping, echopraxia and copropraxia."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oyebode","lishman","nice"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.10.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Most children with tics need nothing but an explanation. When treatment is needed, the first choice is a behavioural therapy that teaches the child to do something incompatible with the tic when the urge arrives.","teach":["Psychoeducation is the first intervention and is sufficient for many. Explaining the natural history, the premonitory urge, the waxing and waning, and the expected decline after early adolescence changes how families and schools respond.","School liaison is a core intervention: allowing the child to leave the room to tic, avoiding drawing attention, permitting extra time, and preventing punishment for tics."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","rutter"],"n":{"teach":19,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.10.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"This is one of the few conditions in child psychiatry where you can honestly tell a family that it usually gets better on its own. Most tics fade in the late teens.","teach":["Tics follow a characteristic course: onset around six to seven, worsening to peak severity around ten to twelve, then progressive improvement through adolescence.","By early adulthood around a third are tic-free, around a third have minimal tics, and around a third have persisting tics, with only a small minority having severe persisting tics."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","nice","lishman"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.11.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"This heading catches the conditions that do not fit elsewhere: elimination disorders, sleep problems, selective mutism, self-harm and the neurodevelopmental conditions that get overlooked.","teach":["Nocturnal enuresis affects around 15 to 20 per cent of five-year-olds and around 5 per cent of ten-year-olds, with spontaneous resolution of around 15 per cent per year.","Encopresis affects around 1 to 2 per cent of school-age children, is more common in boys, and is usually secondary to chronic constipation with overflow."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","rutter","icd11"],"n":{"teach":12,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["10.8.11.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Bedwetting is usually a matter of a bladder and a brain that have not yet learned to talk to each other at night. It is not laziness, it is not psychological, and punishing a child for it makes everything worse.","teach":["Nocturnal enuresis aetiology has three components: nocturnal polyuria from reduced overnight vasopressin secretion, bladder overactivity with reduced functional capacity, and a high arousal threshold preventing waking.","The high arousal threshold is the key element: these children do not fail to notice a full bladder, they fail to wake, and they are frequently unusually deep sleepers."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","rutter","icd11"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.11.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"These conditions are recognised by asking the right question rather than by finding a sign. Ask about the toilet, ask about sleep, ask about school, and ask about self-harm, and most of it comes out.","teach":["Enuresis: diagnosed from age five, requiring involuntary voiding at least twice weekly for at least three months in ICD-11.","Distinguish primary from secondary, monosymptomatic from non-monosymptomatic, and nocturnal from diurnal, since each determines management."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","rutter","icd11"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.11.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Most of these respond to simple, practical, well-evidenced measures. An alarm for bedwetting, laxatives for soiling, a routine for sleep, and a speech therapist for language do more than most psychiatric interventions.","teach":["Enuresis, NICE CG111: explain the mechanism, address constipation, optimise fluid intake distributed through the day, and stop punishing or rewarding dryness alone.","Reward systems should reward behaviours the child controls, such as drinking adequately during the day and using the toilet before bed, not dry nights, which the child cannot control."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","rutter"],"n":{"teach":20,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["10.8.11.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Most of these get better. Bedwetting stops, soiling stops, sleep settles, and language improves with help. The one that does not simply resolve is self-harm, which predicts real risk later.","teach":["Nocturnal enuresis has a spontaneous remission rate of around 15 per cent per year, so most children become dry without treatment, though treatment shortens the course substantially.","Around 1 to 2 per cent of adults have persistent nocturnal enuresis, so a small group does not resolve."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","rutter","companion"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["11.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Every drug of misuse ends up doing one thing: raising dopamine in a small structure behind the eyes called the nucleus accumbens. They take completely different routes to get there, and the route is what determines the withdrawal, the overdose and the treatment.","teach":["The common final pathway is the mesolimbic dopamine system, from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens. Every substance of misuse increases dopamine there, directly or indirectly.","Alcohol: potentiates GABA-A and inhibits NMDA glutamate receptors. Chronic use downregulates GABA and upregulates NMDA, so withdrawal is a glutamate-driven hyperexcitable state. That single fact explains withdrawal seizures, delirium tremens, and why benzodiazepines are the treatment.","Alcohol is metabolised by alcohol dehydrogenase to acetaldehyde, then by aldehyde dehydrogenase to acetate. Disulfiram inhibits the second step, so acetaldehyde accumulates and the person feels poisoned.","Zero-order kinetics: alcohol is eliminated at a fixed rate of roughly one unit per hour regardless of concentration, because the enzymes are saturated.","UK epidemiology: around one in five adults drinks above the low-risk guideline of fourteen units a week for both sexes. Alcohol dependence affects a much smaller proportion, in the low single figures per cent.","Cannabis: delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol is a partial agonist at CB1 receptors. Cannabidiol is not intoxicating and may be mildly antipsychotic. The THC to CBD ratio has risen sharply in illicit cannabis over three decades.","High-potency cannabis is associated with a substantially raised odds of psychotic disorder in case-control work, notably the South London studies, with a dose-response relationship. Causality remains contested but the association is robust.","Synthetic cannabinoids are full CB1 agonists, unlike THC, which is why they produce far more severe toxicity including seizures, psychosis and death.","Stimulants: cocaine blocks reuptake of dopamine, noradrenaline and serotonin. Amphetamines additionally reverse the dopamine transporter and displace vesicular stores, producing a larger and longer release.","Cocaine's short half-life, around one hour, drives compulsive redosing and binge patterns. Crack, the freebase, is smoked, reaches the brain in seconds, and is more addictive for that reason alone.","Cocaine causes coronary vasospasm and myocardial infarction in young people with normal arteries. Beta-blockers are traditionally avoided in acute cocaine chest pain because of unopposed alpha stimulation; benzodiazepines are first line.","Phentermine, diethylpropion and pemoline are older stimulant-type agents. Pemoline was withdrawn for hepatotoxicity; the anorectic stimulants carry cardiovascular and pulmonary hypertension risks.","Hallucinogens: LSD, psilocybin and mescaline are agonists at 5-HT2A receptors. They produce tolerance rapidly, cause no physical dependence and no withdrawal syndrome.","Hallucinogen persisting perception disorder is the recurrence of perceptual disturbance long after the drug has cleared, and is a recognised ICD and DSM diagnosis.","Solvents: toluene, butane and related volatile substances are lipid-soluble CNS depressants with rapid onset and offset. Sudden sniffing death is due to sensitisation of the myocardium to catecholamines, producing arrhythmia.","Nitrites, poppers, are vasodilators rather than CNS depressants. Their specific hazard is methaemoglobinaemia, and they are lethal in combination with phosphodiesterase inhibitors.","MDMA releases serotonin massively through transporter reversal, with dopamine and noradrenaline release as well. Its dangers are hyperthermia, hyponatraemia from excess water intake with SIADH, and serotonin syndrome.","Benzodiazepines are positive allosteric modulators at GABA-A, increasing the frequency of chloride channel opening. Barbiturates increase channel opening duration and, unlike benzodiazepines, open the channel directly at high dose, which is why barbiturate overdose is lethal and benzodiazepine overdose usually is not.","Opiates: agonists at mu, kappa and delta receptors. Mu mediates analgesia, euphoria, respiratory depression and dependence. Respiratory depression is the cause of death in overdose, and the pinpoint pupil is the diagnostic sign.","Heroin, diamorphine, is a prodrug, more lipid-soluble than morphine and therefore faster into the brain. Methadone is a full mu agonist with a long half-life; buprenorphine is a partial agonist with a ceiling on respiratory depression and very high receptor affinity."],"cards":[["What is the common final pathway for all substances of misuse?","Increased dopamine transmission in the mesolimbic pathway from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens, reached by different mechanisms for each substance."],["Explain the neurochemistry of alcohol withdrawal.","Chronic alcohol potentiates GABA-A and inhibits NMDA receptors. The brain compensates by downregulating GABA and upregulating NMDA. On withdrawal the unopposed glutamatergic drive produces a hyperexcitable state causing tremor, seizures and delirium tremens, which is why benzodiazepines are the treatment."],["How do benzodiazepines and barbiturates differ at the GABA-A receptor, and why does it matter?","Benzodiazepines increase the frequency of chloride channel opening and require GABA to be present. Barbiturates increase the duration of opening and at high dose open the channel directly without GABA. This absence of a ceiling makes barbiturate overdose lethal where benzodiazepine overdose alone usually is not."],["Contrast methadone and buprenorphine pharmacologically.","Methadone is a full mu agonist with a long half-life and no ceiling on respiratory depression. Buprenorphine is a partial mu agonist with very high receptor affinity and a ceiling effect on respiratory depression, making it safer in overdose but capable of precipitating withdrawal if given too early."],["What is sudden sniffing death?","Fatal cardiac arrhythmia in volatile substance misuse, caused by sensitisation of the myocardium to catecholamines, typically precipitated by sudden exertion or fright during or shortly after inhalation."]],"note":["Alcohol follows zero-order kinetics, eliminated at approximately one unit per hour.","Disulfiram inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase, causing acetaldehyde accumulation.","UK low-risk drinking guideline is fourteen units per week for both sexes.","THC is a partial CB1 agonist; synthetic cannabinoids are full agonists and far more toxic.","Cocaine has a half-life of about one hour, driving binge redosing.","Hallucinogens produce rapid tolerance, no physical dependence and no withdrawal syndrome.","MDMA deaths result from hyperthermia, hyponatraemia with SIADH, and serotonin syndrome.","Pinpoint pupils and respiratory depression are the signs of opioid overdose."],"refs":["semaddict","stahl","addmed"]};
TEACH["11.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There is a long argument in addiction medicine about whether you should give a person the drug they are addicted to. One side says it keeps them alive and in contact. The other says it keeps them addicted. The evidence sides fairly clearly with the first, and the law sits somewhere in the middle.","teach":["Harm reduction accepts that some people will continue to use and works to reduce the damage: needle and syringe programmes, take-home naloxone, opioid substitution, drug checking, and supervised consumption.","Abstinence-oriented treatment aims at cessation. The two are not opposites; a good service offers both and moves people between them."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["addmed","bnf","semaddict"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":6}};
TEACH["11.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"There is a difference between someone whose drinking is causing problems and someone whose body now needs alcohol to work normally. The first is a problem drinker. The second is dependent, and stopping suddenly can kill them.","teach":["Edwards and Gross described the alcohol dependence syndrome in 1976, and its seven elements remain the framework for both ICD and DSM.","The seven elements: narrowing of the drinking repertoire; salience of drink-seeking behaviour; increased tolerance; repeated withdrawal symptoms; relief or avoidance of withdrawal by further drinking; subjective compulsion to drink; and reinstatement after abstinence.","ICD-11 requires two of three core features over twelve months: impaired control, increasing priority given to substance use, and physiological features of tolerance or withdrawal.","Problem drinking is defined by consequence rather than by physiology: harm to health, relationships, work or the law, without necessarily any dependence.","UK guidance: low risk is up to fourteen units per week for both sexes, spread over three or more days, with several drink-free days. Increasing risk and higher risk categories sit above this.","One UK unit is 10 millilitres, or 8 grams, of pure alcohol. Units equal volume in litres multiplied by percentage ABV.","Screening: AUDIT is the fully validated ten-item instrument, with a score of eight or more indicating hazardous drinking. AUDIT-C is the three-item consumption subset used in primary care. CAGE is brief but insensitive to hazardous drinking short of dependence.","Alcohol-related physical disability: fatty liver, alcoholic hepatitis, cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma; pancreatitis; cardiomyopathy; hypertension; peripheral neuropathy; and increased risk of oropharyngeal, oesophageal, breast and colorectal cancer.","Alcohol-related brain damage: Wernicke's encephalopathy, Korsakoff's syndrome, cerebellar degeneration, central pontine myelinolysis, Marchiafava-Bignami disease and alcohol-related dementia.","Wernicke's triad is confusion, ataxia and ophthalmoplegia, and all three are present in only a small minority. Treat on suspicion. It is a clinical diagnosis and untreated it progresses to Korsakoff's in most survivors.","Parenteral thiamine is required in suspected Wernicke's because oral absorption is unreliable in this population. Never give glucose before thiamine, since a carbohydrate load can precipitate the encephalopathy.","Withdrawal timeline: tremor, sweating, nausea and anxiety from six to twelve hours; seizures typically at twelve to forty-eight hours; alcoholic hallucinosis around twelve to twenty-four hours; delirium tremens characteristically at forty-eight to seventy-two hours.","Alcoholic hallucinosis is usually auditory, occurs in clear consciousness, and is distinct from delirium tremens, which involves clouded consciousness and typically visual hallucinations.","Delirium tremens carries appreciable mortality even when treated, historically much higher, and is a medical emergency.","Detoxification regimens use a long-acting benzodiazepine, chlordiazepoxide in the UK, either fixed-dose reducing or symptom-triggered against a scale such as CIWA-Ar. Symptom-triggered regimens use less total drug and shorten admission.","In significant hepatic impairment, lorazepam or oxazepam are preferred, being metabolised by glucuronidation without oxidative first-pass metabolism.","Inpatient detoxification is indicated for a history of withdrawal seizures or delirium tremens, very high consumption typically above thirty units daily, significant comorbid physical or psychiatric illness, and lack of any social support. Otherwise community detoxification is safe and preferred."],"cards":[["Name the seven elements of the alcohol dependence syndrome.","Narrowing of the drinking repertoire; salience of drink-seeking behaviour; increased tolerance; repeated withdrawal symptoms; relief or avoidance of withdrawal by further drinking; subjective compulsion to drink; and reinstatement after a period of abstinence. Described by Edwards and Gross in 1976."],["Give the timeline of alcohol withdrawal.","Tremor, sweating, nausea and anxiety at six to twelve hours; seizures at twelve to forty-eight hours; alcoholic hallucinosis at twelve to twenty-four hours; and delirium tremens characteristically at forty-eight to seventy-two hours."],["State Wernicke's triad and the rule about glucose.","Confusion, ataxia and ophthalmoplegia, all three present in only a minority, so treatment is given on suspicion. Parenteral thiamine must be given before any glucose load, since carbohydrate can precipitate the encephalopathy."],["Which benzodiazepines are preferred in hepatic impairment and why?","Lorazepam and oxazepam, because they are metabolised by glucuronidation and do not undergo oxidative hepatic metabolism, so they do not accumulate in liver disease."],["What are the indications for inpatient rather than community alcohol detoxification?","A history of withdrawal seizures or delirium tremens, very high consumption typically above thirty units daily, significant comorbid physical or psychiatric illness, and absence of social support."]],"note":["One UK unit is 10 millilitres or 8 grams of pure alcohol.","AUDIT is ten items; a score of eight or more indicates hazardous drinking. AUDIT-C is the three-item consumption subset.","Alcoholic hallucinosis occurs in clear consciousness and is usually auditory; delirium tremens involves clouded consciousness and visual hallucinations.","Symptom-triggered detoxification against CIWA-Ar uses less total benzodiazepine than fixed-dose regimens.","Chlordiazepoxide is the standard UK detoxification agent.","Untreated Wernicke's encephalopathy progresses to Korsakoff's syndrome in most survivors."],"refs":["semaddict","lishman","nice","maudsley"]};
TEACH["11.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"People do not start using drugs because they want to be addicted. They start because it does something for them that nothing else was doing, and they continue for reasons that change completely once dependence has set in. The reason for starting and the reason for continuing are almost never the same reason.","teach":["Initiation is driven by availability, peer group, curiosity, and the social meaning of the substance. Continuation is driven by reinforcement, both positive, the reward, and negative, the relief of withdrawal and distress.","The distinction between liking and wanting is central. Robinson and Berridge's incentive sensitisation theory holds that with repeated use dopaminergic wanting sensitises while opioid-mediated liking does not, producing intense craving for a drug that is no longer enjoyed."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","addmed","bhugra"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":4,"note":6}};
TEACH["11.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A great many people with serious mental illness also use drugs, and each condition makes the other worse. Services have historically dealt with this by sending the person back and forth between two teams, each saying the other should go first.","teach":["Dual diagnosis, or comorbidity, is the co-occurrence of substance misuse with another mental disorder. It is the norm rather than the exception in severe mental illness.","Rates: around half of people with a psychotic illness have a lifetime substance use disorder, several times the general population rate. Rates in personality disorder and bipolar disorder are also high."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","addmed","nice"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":6}};
TEACH["11.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Assessing a drug user is mostly arithmetic and honesty: what, how much, how often, by what route, since when, and what happens when they stop. Get that wrong and every treatment decision downstream is wrong too.","teach":["The core history: substance by substance, the quantity, the frequency, the route, the duration, the time of last use, the pattern across a typical day, and the money involved.","Route matters more than quantity for risk. Injecting brings overdose, blood-borne virus, endocarditis and thrombosis; smoking brings respiratory harm; intranasal brings septal damage."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["addmed","maudsley","nice"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["11.7"] = {"free":false,"simple":"With alcohol the assessment has one extra question that everything else hangs on: if this person stops today, will they have a seizure. That answer decides where they are treated, how fast, and whether they can be sent home.","teach":["History: units per day and per week, pattern across the day, time of the first drink, time of the last drink, and the longest recent period of abstinence and what happened during it.","Morning drinking, relief drinking and drinking to prevent shakes are the practical markers of physiological dependence and matter more than the total quantity."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","semaddict","addmed"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["11.8"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Prevention campaigns that tell young people drugs are terrible do not work, and some make things worse. What works is less dramatic: making the drug harder to get, more expensive, and less normal.","teach":["The evidence hierarchy in prevention is close to inverted against public expectation. Population-level structural measures work; individual fear-based education largely does not.","Price and availability are the strongest levers. Minimum unit pricing, taxation, restricted licensing hours and reduced outlet density all reduce consumption and harm."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","bhugra","nice"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":3,"note":6}};
TEACH["11.9"] = {"free":false,"simple":"You can become addicted to something you never swallow. Gambling is the one everyone agrees on, because it produces the same craving, the same tolerance, the same withdrawal and the same brain changes as a drug does, without a substance anywhere in the story.","teach":["Gambling disorder is the only behavioural addiction with full status in both systems. DSM-5 moved it in 2013 from the impulse control disorders into Substance-Related and Addictive Disorders, the first non-substance addiction so classified.","The justification was empirical: shared clinical features of tolerance, withdrawal, craving and loss of control, shared genetics, shared mesolimbic dopamine involvement, and shared response to treatment."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["icd11","dsm5","semaddict","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":6}};
TEACH["11.10"] = {"free":true,"simple":"If you tell someone why they should stop drinking, they will explain why they cannot. If you ask them why they might want to, they explain it to themselves, and people believe themselves. That reversal is the whole of motivational interviewing.","teach":["Motivational interviewing was developed by William Miller and Stephen Rollnick. It is a collaborative conversation style for strengthening a person's own motivation and commitment to change.","The founding observation was that therapist style predicted client outcome. Confrontational counselling produced more resistance and worse drinking outcomes; empathic counselling produced better ones.","Ambivalence is treated as normal, not as denial or pathology. Almost everyone with a substance problem simultaneously wants to change and wants to continue.","The spirit of MI has four elements: partnership, acceptance, compassion and evocation. Evocation is the crucial one: the motivation is drawn out of the person rather than installed by the clinician.","The four processes, in order: engaging, focusing, evoking and planning. They are recursive rather than strictly sequential.","The core skills, OARS: Open questions, Affirmations, Reflective listening, and Summaries.","Change talk is any statement by the person favouring change, and it predicts actual behaviour change. Sustain talk favours the status quo.","The DARN-CAT taxonomy of change talk: preparatory change talk is Desire, Ability, Reason and Need; mobilising change talk is Commitment, Activation and Taking steps. Mobilising change talk is the stronger predictor.","The righting reflex is the clinician's instinct to correct and to argue for change. Following it reliably produces sustain talk, because the person then argues the other side.","Rolling with resistance, later reframed as working with discord, means not opposing it directly. Reflect it, reframe it, or shift focus.","Developing discrepancy between present behaviour and the person's own stated values or goals is the engine of change. The discrepancy must be the person's own, not the clinician's.","Supporting self-efficacy is a core task, since a person who wants to change but believes they cannot will not attempt it. Past successes, however small, are the material.","MI is often paired with Prochaska and DiClemente's stages of change: precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action, maintenance, and relapse as part of the cycle rather than as failure. The two models are independent and the stage model is empirically contested.","MI evidence: effective in alcohol and drug use, and in medication adherence and health behaviour change generally. Effect sizes are modest but it is brief, teachable and generalisable.","Adaptations: brief MI in emergency and primary care; motivational enhancement therapy, the four-session manualised version tested in Project MATCH; and MI combined with CBT or with contingency management.","Project MATCH found broadly equivalent outcomes for motivational enhancement therapy, CBT and twelve-step facilitation, despite the shorter duration of the motivational arm.","MI is a style, not a technique set. Delivered as a checklist of questions without the underlying spirit it does not work, and this is the commonest failure in practice."],"cards":[["What are the four processes and the four elements of spirit in motivational interviewing?","Processes: engaging, focusing, evoking and planning. Spirit: partnership, acceptance, compassion and evocation."],["What does OARS stand for?","Open questions, Affirmations, Reflective listening, and Summaries: the four core micro-skills of motivational interviewing."],["Give the DARN-CAT taxonomy of change talk.","Preparatory change talk is Desire, Ability, Reason and Need. Mobilising change talk is Commitment, Activation and Taking steps. Mobilising change talk is the stronger predictor of actual behaviour change."],["What is the righting reflex and why does it matter?","The clinician's instinct to correct the person and argue for change. Acting on it predictably elicits sustain talk, because the person takes up the opposing side of the argument, so the intervention produces the reverse of its intention."],["What did Project MATCH find?","Broadly equivalent outcomes for motivational enhancement therapy, cognitive behavioural coping skills therapy and twelve-step facilitation, despite motivational enhancement therapy being delivered in only four sessions."]],"note":["Miller and Rollnick developed MI; the founding observation was that therapist style predicted client drinking outcome.","Ambivalence is treated as normal rather than as denial.","Discrepancy must be between the person's behaviour and their own values, not the clinician's.","Prochaska and DiClemente's stages of change are a separate and empirically contested model.","Motivational enhancement therapy is the four-session manualised adaptation.","MI delivered as technique without spirit does not work."],"refs":["semaddict","addmed","nice"]};
TEACH["12.1.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Most people with mental illness never commit a crime, and most crime is committed by people who are perfectly well. The overlap is small. It is also the part everybody reads about, so you have to know it accurately.","teach":["The baseline first: the population attributable risk of violence from mental illness is small, of the order of five per cent or less. Substance misuse contributes far more, and being young and male contributes more still.","People with severe mental illness are considerably more likely to be victims of violence than perpetrators of it, and this is consistently found and rarely reported.","Homicide: the National Confidential Inquiry into Suicide and Safety in Mental Health reports that around one in ten homicide perpetrators in the UK have been in contact with mental health services in the preceding year, and a smaller proportion have symptoms of mental illness at the time.","Homicide followed by suicide is a distinct group, more often involving depression, more often within families, and more often involving a male perpetrator killing a partner or children.","Infanticide, under the Infanticide Act 1938 in England and Wales, applies where a mother kills her own child under twelve months while the balance of her mind is disturbed by not having recovered from childbirth or from lactation. It reduces murder to a manslaughter-equivalent offence.","Filicide classification, Resnick: altruistic, acutely psychotic, unwanted child, accidental or fatal maltreatment, and spouse revenge. Altruistic filicide, killing to spare the child suffering, is the commonest in psychiatric populations.","Neonaticide, killing within the first day, differs from other filicide: typically a young mother, concealed pregnancy, unassisted delivery, and a low rate of major mental illness.","Sexual offences: the great majority are committed by people without major mental illness. Paraphilic disorders, intellectual disability and, less commonly, disinhibition from organic disorder are the psychiatric associations.","Arson: strong association with intellectual disability, alcohol misuse, and personality disorder. Pyromania as a diagnosis is rare and should not be assumed. Motives include revenge, excitement, concealment of another crime, cry for help, and financial gain.","Fire-setting has one of the highest recidivism concerns of any offence category, and repeat fire-setting with no clear instrumental motive is a specific forensic concern.","Criminal damage frequently accompanies psychotic illness, intoxication and intellectual disability, and is often the offence that first brings a person into forensic services.","Shoplifting has psychiatric associations with depression in older adults, with eating disorders, with dementia and with intellectual disability. Kleptomania is a genuine but rare diagnosis requiring the absence of any use or monetary motive for the stolen items.","The offence type shapes the assessment. Sexual and violent offences require structured risk assessment; acquisitive offending rarely does."],"cards":[["What is the population attributable risk of violence from mental illness?","Small, of the order of five per cent or less. Substance misuse contributes far more, and demographic factors such as being young and male contribute more still. People with severe mental illness are more often victims of violence than perpetrators."],["State the Infanticide Act 1938 provision.","Where a mother kills her own child aged under twelve months while the balance of her mind is disturbed by reason of not having fully recovered from childbirth or from the effect of lactation, the offence is reduced from murder to an infanticide offence carrying the sentencing flexibility of manslaughter."],["Give Resnick's classification of filicide.","Altruistic, in which the parent kills to spare the child suffering; acutely psychotic; unwanted child; accidental or fatal maltreatment; and spouse revenge. Altruistic filicide is the commonest in psychiatric populations."],["What are the psychiatric associations of arson?","Intellectual disability, alcohol misuse and personality disorder. Motives include revenge, excitement, concealment of another offence, a cry for help, and financial gain. Pyromania is a rare diagnosis and should not be assumed."]],"note":["Around one in ten UK homicide perpetrators have had mental health service contact in the preceding year.","Homicide-suicide more often involves depression and occurs within families.","Neonaticide typically involves a young mother, concealed pregnancy and low rates of major mental illness.","Most sexual offending is committed by people without major mental illness.","Kleptomania requires the absence of any use or monetary motive for the stolen items.","The National Confidential Inquiry is the principal UK data source in this area."],"refs":["puriforen","eastman","forrester"]};
TEACH["12.1.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Different illnesses carry different risks, and the differences are specific rather than general. Schizophrenia raises the risk of violence a little; schizophrenia with untreated stimulant use raises it a lot. The detail is where the exam lives.","teach":["Substance misuse is the single largest psychiatric contributor to offending, larger than any illness category, and it multiplies the risk attached to every other diagnosis.","Alcohol is involved in a very large proportion of violent offences, acting through disinhibition, impaired judgement and myopic attention to immediate provocation."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["puriforen","forrester","lishman"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["12.1.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A few named syndromes come up in forensic psychiatry far more often than their rarity would suggest, because each has a specific and dangerous relationship to violence. Learn the names, the definition, and the risk attached to each.","teach":["Morbid jealousy, also called delusional jealousy or the Othello syndrome, is the delusional belief that a partner is unfaithful. It is a symptom, not a diagnosis, and can arise in delusional disorder, schizophrenia, affective disorder, alcohol dependence and organic states.","Morbid jealousy carries a genuinely high risk of serious violence and homicide directed at the partner, and it is among the most dangerous presentations in general psychiatry."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["puriforen","forrester","oyebode"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["12.1.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The same offence, committed by two people with the same illness, produces very different journeys depending on who they are. That is not a claim about fairness in the abstract. It is a measurable feature of the system, and you are expected to know where the differences fall.","teach":["Young offenders: the age of criminal responsibility in England, Wales and Northern Ireland is ten, among the lowest in Europe. It is twelve in Scotland, raised from eight in 2021.","Doli incapax, the rebuttable presumption that a child aged ten to thirteen was incapable of crime, was abolished in England and Wales by the Crime and Disorder Act 1998."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["forrester","eastman","bhugra"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":6}};
TEACH["12.1.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Forensic psychiatry is not only about the person in the dock. The person who was harmed also has a psychiatry, and when money is attached to their symptoms the assessment becomes considerably harder.","teach":["Victimisation is a major risk factor for psychiatric disorder, and people with severe mental illness are victimised at much higher rates than the general population, particularly people with psychosis and intellectual disability.","PTSD requires exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence, with re-experiencing, avoidance, negative alterations in cognition and mood, and hyperarousal, in DSM-5."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["forrester","puriforen","icd11"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["12.2.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A psychiatrist meets the criminal justice system at three moments, and the question is different each time. At arrest: is this person well enough to be interviewed. Before trial: are they well enough to be tried. After conviction: what should happen to them now.","teach":["At the police station the questions are fitness to be detained, fitness to be interviewed, and whether an appropriate adult is required.","Fitness to be interviewed asks whether the person's condition would make their responses unreliable, or whether interviewing would cause significant harm to their health. It is not the same as capacity."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["eastman","forrester","puriforen"],"n":{"teach":12,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["12.2.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"There are two completely different questions the law asks about a defendant's mind, and mixing them up is the classic error. One is about now: can they follow their trial. The other is about then: what was in their mind when they did it.","teach":["Fitness to plead concerns the defendant's state at the time of trial. Criminal responsibility concerns the state at the time of the offence. They are independent: a person may be fit to plead and not responsible, or unfit and fully responsible.","Fitness to plead in England and Wales rests on R v Pritchard, 1836, as developed in later cases. The defendant must be able to plead, understand the course of proceedings, instruct a lawyer, challenge a juror, and understand the evidence.","The Pritchard criteria are widely criticised as setting the threshold too low and taking no account of decision-making capacity. The Law Commission recommended replacement in 2016 with a capacity-based test; the recommendation has not been enacted.","Procedure: unfitness is determined by a judge on the evidence of two registered medical practitioners, at least one approved under section 12. If found unfit, a trial of the facts follows to determine whether the defendant did the act.","Disposal after a finding of unfitness and a trial of the facts: a hospital order, with or without restriction, a supervision order, or an absolute discharge, under the Criminal Procedure (Insanity) Act 1964 as amended in 1991 and 2004.","Mutism and deafness: the court must first determine whether the defendant is mute of malice or mute by visitation of God. Deafness alone does not make a person unfit provided communication can be enabled with an intermediary and registered interpreter.","Criminal responsibility requires actus reus, the guilty act, and mens rea, the guilty mind. Psychiatric evidence in defences generally bears on mens rea.","The insanity defence rests on the M'Naghten Rules, 1843: at the time of the act the defendant was labouring under such a defect of reason, from disease of the mind, as not to know the nature and quality of the act, or, if he did know it, not to know that what he was doing was wrong.","M'Naghten is narrow and rarely used. Knowing an act is wrong means knowing it is legally wrong, so a person who knows the law forbids the act but believes it morally justified by a delusion still fails the test.","Disease of the mind is a legal, not a medical, concept. It has been held to include epilepsy, hyperglycaemia in diabetes, arteriosclerosis and sleepwalking, which is why the case law appears strange to clinicians.","The special verdict is not guilty by reason of insanity. Since 1991 the court has had a range of disposals rather than mandatory hospitalisation, other than for murder.","Diminished responsibility is a partial defence to murder only, reducing it to manslaughter. Section 2 of the Homicide Act 1957 as substituted by section 52 of the Coroners and Justice Act 2009.","The current diminished responsibility test has four limbs: an abnormality of mental functioning; arising from a recognised medical condition; which substantially impaired the ability to understand the nature of the conduct, to form a rational judgement, or to exercise self-control; and which provides an explanation for the killing.","The 2009 reformulation deliberately tied the defence to a recognised medical condition and introduced the causal requirement, replacing the older and vaguer abnormality of mind formula.","Automatism is action without conscious volition. Insane automatism arises from an internal cause, a disease of the mind, and yields the special verdict. Non-insane automatism arises from an external cause and yields a complete acquittal.","The internal-external distinction produces the notorious result that a hypoglycaemic diabetic who has taken insulin, an external cause, may receive a full acquittal, while a hyperglycaemic diabetic who has not, an internal cause, may be found insane.","Amnesia for the offence does not by itself make a person unfit to plead, and it is not a defence. It is common, frequently associated with intoxication or with high emotional arousal, and is often exaggerated.","Intoxication: voluntary intoxication is not a defence to crimes of basic intent. It may negate the specific intent required for offences such as murder, reducing the offence rather than excusing it."],"cards":[["State the Pritchard criteria for fitness to plead.","The defendant must be able to enter a plea, understand the course of proceedings, instruct a legal representative, challenge a juror, and understand the evidence. Criticised for setting the threshold too low and for ignoring decision-making capacity; the Law Commission recommended a capacity-based replacement in 2016, not enacted."],["State the M'Naghten Rules.","That at the time of committing the act the accused was labouring under such a defect of reason, from disease of the mind, as not to know the nature and quality of the act he was doing, or, if he did know it, that he did not know that what he was doing was wrong. Wrong means legally wrong."],["Give the four limbs of the current diminished responsibility test.","An abnormality of mental functioning; arising from a recognised medical condition; which substantially impaired the ability to understand the nature of the conduct, to form a rational judgement, or to exercise self-control; and which provides an explanation for the killing. Homicide Act 1957 section 2 as substituted by the Coroners and Justice Act 2009."],["Distinguish insane from non-insane automatism.","Insane automatism arises from an internal cause amounting in law to a disease of the mind and results in the special verdict of not guilty by reason of insanity. Non-insane automatism arises from an external cause and results in a complete acquittal."],["Is amnesia for the offence a defence, or a bar to trial?","Neither. Amnesia does not by itself render a defendant unfit to plead and is not a defence. It is common, frequently associated with intoxication or high emotional arousal, and is often exaggerated."]],"note":["Fitness to plead concerns the time of trial; criminal responsibility concerns the time of the offence.","Unfitness is determined by a judge on the evidence of two doctors, one section 12 approved.","A finding of unfitness is followed by a trial of the facts.","Disease of the mind is a legal concept and has included epilepsy, hyperglycaemia and sleepwalking.","Diminished responsibility is a partial defence to murder only.","Voluntary intoxication is no defence to crimes of basic intent but may negate specific intent."],"refs":["eastman","puriforen","forrester"]};
TEACH["12.2.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Once someone is convicted the court has a menu, and the psychiatric options on it are specific sections with specific criteria. Knowing which section does what is straightforward marks.","teach":["Section 37, the hospital order, is the principal psychiatric disposal. The court must be satisfied on the evidence of two doctors that the person has a mental disorder of a nature or degree making hospital treatment appropriate, and that appropriate treatment is available.","A section 37 hospital order lasts six months initially, is renewable, and carries no requirement that the person serve any part of a sentence. It is a disposal instead of punishment, not alongside it."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["eastman","forrester"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["12.2.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A court report is not a clinical letter with the addressee changed. It answers a question a lawyer asked, in an order a judge can follow, with the reasoning visible so that it can be attacked. If your reasoning cannot be attacked, you have not shown it.","teach":["Establish the instruction first. Who instructs you, what question they are asking, and by when. Write to the question asked, and if the question is wrong say so and answer the right one as well.","The duty of the expert is to the court and overrides any obligation to the instructing party. State this in the report, as the Criminal Procedure Rules require."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["eastman","puriforen","forrester"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["12.2.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"In the witness box you are not defending your patient and you are not defending yourself. You are answering a question, accurately, in front of someone paid to make you look wrong. The composure comes from having prepared, not from being clever.","teach":["Two roles. A professional witness gives evidence of fact from their own clinical involvement. An expert witness gives an opinion and may comment on matters they were not involved in.","Only an expert may give opinion evidence. A treating clinician called as a professional witness who begins offering opinions on matters beyond their involvement is exceeding their role."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["eastman","forrester"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["12.3.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Security in a forensic hospital is not just locks. It is three things working together, and if you get the balance wrong you either have an unsafe ward or a ward so controlled that nobody can recover on it.","teach":["The three elements of security are physical, procedural and relational, and they must be balanced. Over-reliance on any one produces failure.","Physical security is the built environment: perimeter fences, locked doors, airlocks, unbreakable glass, alarm systems, closed circuit television, and controlled access to items."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["forrester","puriforen","nice"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["12.3.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A forensic service exists to do two things at once that pull against each other: treat an illness and manage a risk. Everything about how it is built follows from having to do both.","teach":["The core components: assessment including court and prison work, inpatient care at graded levels of security, forensic community teams, and a pathway between them in both directions.","The multidisciplinary team is larger than in general psychiatry and includes forensic psychology, occupational therapy, social work, substance misuse expertise, and often a dedicated speech and language therapist."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["forrester","puriforen","oxid"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["12.3.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Prison holds a greater concentration of mental illness than any hospital in the country, and treats it with a fraction of the resource. That sentence is the whole of prison psychiatry.","teach":["Prevalence in prisons is far above the community rate across almost every category. The landmark UK survey by Singleton and colleagues in 1998 remains the reference point.","Personality disorder is present in the majority of prisoners on structured assessment, with antisocial personality disorder predominating in men."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["forrester","puriforen","semaddict"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["12.3.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Risk assessment is not fortune telling and it is not a number. It is a description of the circumstances in which this particular person becomes dangerous, so that those circumstances can be watched for and interrupted.","teach":["Three approaches. Unstructured clinical judgement, which performs barely better than chance. Actuarial prediction, which uses fixed statistical predictors and forbids clinical override. Structured professional judgement, which uses empirically derived items to guide, but not to determine, a clinical formulation.","Structured professional judgement is the recommended approach in UK practice, combining the reliability of a defined item set with the flexibility to formulate."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["puriforen","forrester","nice"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["12.3.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The most dangerous moment in a forensic pathway is the day someone leaves. Everything that held the risk in place, the structure, the supervision, the routine, the absence of drugs, disappears at once, and the person has to manage without it.","teach":["Conditional discharge under section 42 or by a tribunal applies to restricted patients. Conditions typically cover residence, supervision by a responsible clinician and a social supervisor, treatment compliance, abstinence from substances, and exclusion zones.","Recall to hospital may be ordered by the Secretary of State for Justice at any time and requires no fresh Mental Health Act assessment. This is the defining feature of conditional discharge."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["eastman","forrester","puriforen"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["12.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Human rights law is not a background principle in psychiatry. It is the reason particular things are done in particular ways: why detention must be reviewable, why seclusion has time limits, and why a person must be told why they are being held.","teach":["The Human Rights Act 1998 brought the European Convention on Human Rights into UK law, making Convention rights enforceable in domestic courts and requiring public authorities, including NHS trusts, to act compatibly with them.","Article 2, the right to life, imposes both a negative duty not to take life and a positive operational duty to protect life where the authority knew or ought to have known of a real and immediate risk."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["eastman","forrester","puriforen"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["13.1.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"For most of the twentieth century the answer to intellectual disability was a building far from town. The idea that replaced it was simple and radical: give people the same patterns of ordinary life that everyone else has. Not special care. Ordinary life.","teach":["Normalisation was formulated by Bengt Nirje in Sweden in 1969: making available to people with intellectual disability the patterns and conditions of everyday life which are as close as possible to the norms of mainstream society.","Nirje's eight patterns include a normal rhythm of the day, a normal rhythm of the week, a normal rhythm of the year, the normal experiences of the life cycle, normal respect for choices, normal sexual patterns, normal economic standards, and normal environmental standards.","Wolf Wolfensberger took the idea to North America and later reframed it as Social Role Valorisation in 1983: the goal is to enable people to occupy socially valued roles, because valued roles bring the good things of life with them.","Wolfensberger's argument was that devalued people are treated badly not because of their impairment but because of the role they are cast in, and that changing the role changes the treatment.","The social model of disability distinguishes impairment, the functional limitation, from disability, the disadvantage created by how society is organised. On this account a wheelchair user is disabled by stairs, not by legs.","The five service accomplishments, from O'Brien: community presence, choice, competence, respect, and community participation. These became the practical audit tool for services.","The scandals drove the policy. Ely Hospital, Cardiff, 1969, exposed by a whistleblower and investigated by Howe, was the first major UK inquiry and led directly to the Hospital Advisory Service.","Better Services for the Mentally Handicapped, 1971, was the first white paper to set targets for reducing hospital places and expanding community provision.","An Ordinary Life, King's Fund, 1980, set out the model of ordinary housing in ordinary streets with support brought to the person, rather than the person brought to the institution.","Valuing People, 2001, was the first white paper in thirty years, built on four principles: rights, independence, choice and inclusion. Valuing People Now followed in 2009.","Person-centred planning replaced service-led assessment: the plan starts from what the person wants their life to be, and the service is arranged around it rather than the reverse.","Direct payments and personal budgets give the person or their family control of the money, and were intended to make choice real rather than notional.","Winterbourne View, exposed by Panorama in 2011, showed systematic abuse in a private assessment and treatment unit and produced the Transforming Care programme, aimed at closing such units and moving people into community support.","Transforming Care has repeatedly missed its targets. The number of people with learning disability and autism in inpatient units has fallen far more slowly than planned, and this is a live policy failure rather than history.","Deinstitutionalisation is not itself a treatment. Where community support was properly funded, outcomes improved substantially. Where it was not, people moved from one poor setting to another."],"cards":[["Define normalisation and name its originator.","The making available to people with intellectual disability of the patterns and conditions of everyday life which are as close as possible to the norms of mainstream society. Formulated by Bengt Nirje in Sweden in 1969."],["What is Social Role Valorisation?","Wolfensberger's 1983 reformulation of normalisation, holding that the goal is to enable devalued people to occupy socially valued roles, because valued social roles bring with them the good things of life, and because devaluation rather than impairment drives poor treatment."],["Name O'Brien's five service accomplishments.","Community presence, choice, competence, respect, and community participation."],["What was Winterbourne View and what did it produce?","A private assessment and treatment unit where systematic abuse of people with learning disability was exposed by BBC Panorama in 2011. It produced the Transforming Care programme, aimed at closing such units in favour of community support, which has repeatedly missed its targets."],["Distinguish impairment from disability in the social model.","Impairment is the functional limitation in the individual. Disability is the disadvantage created by the way society is organised. On this account a person is disabled by inaccessible arrangements rather than by their impairment."]],"note":["Ely Hospital inquiry, 1969, was the first major UK scandal and led to the Hospital Advisory Service.","Better Services for the Mentally Handicapped, 1971, first set hospital reduction targets.","An Ordinary Life, King's Fund, 1980, set out ordinary housing with support brought to the person.","Valuing People, 2001: rights, independence, choice and inclusion.","Person-centred planning starts from the person's own goals rather than service categories.","Deinstitutionalisation improves outcome only where community support is adequately funded."],"refs":["oxid","semid","companion"]};
TEACH["13.1.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There is a long argument about whether people with intellectual disability should have their own psychiatric services or use the ordinary ones. The honest answer is that mainstream services usually cannot adjust enough, and specialist services are usually too small to cover everything.","teach":["The case for specialist services: expertise in atypical presentation, in communication, in behavioural phenotypes, in epilepsy and in polypharmacy; and the demonstrable failure of mainstream services to make reasonable adjustments.","The case for mainstream services: normalisation implies using the same services as everyone else, specialist services can become segregated and hard to leave, and separate provision risks lower standards through invisibility."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxid","semid","nice"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["13.2.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Roughly one person in fifty has an intellectual disability, but only a fraction of those are known to services. And among those who are, mental illness is two to three times commoner than in everyone else, for reasons that are mostly not mysterious.","teach":["Definition requires three elements: significant impairment of intellectual functioning, significant impairment of adaptive or social functioning, and onset during the developmental period.","All three are required. A person with a low IQ who functions well socially does not meet the definition, and an acquired impairment in adult life is not intellectual disability.","IQ bands, approximate: mild 50 to 69, moderate 35 to 49, severe 20 to 34, profound below 20. IQ alone never establishes the diagnosis, and ICD-11 explicitly de-emphasises it in favour of functioning.","Prevalence: administrative prevalence, those known to services, runs at around 0.5 per cent in the UK. True prevalence including unidentified mild disability is estimated at around 2 per cent.","Mild intellectual disability accounts for the great majority, around 85 per cent of all cases, and is disproportionately associated with social deprivation.","Severe and profound intellectual disability is more evenly distributed across social classes, because it is more often caused by identifiable biological pathology rather than by the interaction of polygenic and environmental factors.","Life expectancy is reduced and falls further with increasing severity, though it has risen substantially over recent decades, particularly in Down syndrome.","Psychiatric comorbidity: rates of mental disorder are approximately two to three times the general population rate, with estimates varying widely by case definition and by whether challenging behaviour is counted.","Epilepsy affects a large minority, and prevalence rises with severity, reaching around half in profound disability against around one per cent in the general population.","Autism co-occurs in a substantial proportion, rising with severity of disability, and ADHD is also markedly over-represented.","Schizophrenia is around three times more common than in the general population; bipolar disorder and depression are also more common.","Why the rates are raised: shared biological aetiology, epilepsy and its treatment, sensory impairment, communication difficulty, low self-esteem and repeated failure, restricted social networks, poverty, and very high rates of abuse and bullying.","Life events matter and are under-recognised. Bereavement in particular is frequently concealed from people with intellectual disability, who are then excluded from funerals and rituals and present later with behavioural change.","Abuse is a major aetiological factor. People with intellectual disability experience physical, sexual and financial abuse at far higher rates than the general population and are far less likely to be believed or to secure a prosecution.","Diagnostic overshadowing suppresses recorded rates: symptoms are attributed to the disability rather than recognised as a treatable illness, so real prevalence is probably higher than measured prevalence."],"cards":[["State the three diagnostic requirements for intellectual disability.","Significant impairment of intellectual functioning; significant impairment of adaptive or social functioning; and onset during the developmental period. All three are required, and IQ alone never establishes the diagnosis."],["Give the administrative and true prevalence of intellectual disability, and the distribution by severity.","Administrative prevalence, those known to services, is around 0.5 per cent in the UK; true prevalence including unidentified mild disability is estimated at around 2 per cent. Mild disability accounts for around 85 per cent of cases."],["Why is psychiatric disorder more common in intellectual disability?","Shared biological aetiology, epilepsy and antiepileptic treatment, sensory impairment, communication difficulty, repeated experience of failure, restricted social networks, poverty, and very high rates of abuse and bullying. Recorded rates are further affected by diagnostic overshadowing."],["What is the relationship between severity of disability and epilepsy prevalence?","Epilepsy prevalence rises with severity, from around one per cent in the general population to approximately half of people with profound intellectual disability."],["How does the social class distribution differ between mild and severe intellectual disability?","Mild intellectual disability is disproportionately associated with social deprivation. Severe and profound disability is distributed more evenly across social classes because it more often arises from identifiable biological pathology."]],"note":["IQ bands: mild 50 to 69, moderate 35 to 49, severe 20 to 34, profound below 20.","ICD-11 de-emphasises IQ in favour of functional assessment.","Schizophrenia is around three times more common than in the general population.","Bereavement is frequently concealed from people with intellectual disability, who then present with behavioural change.","Abuse rates are far higher and prosecution rates far lower than in the general population."],"refs":["oxid","semid","icd11"]};
TEACH["13.2.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Causes divide into what happened before birth, during birth, and after. In severe disability a cause is usually found; in mild disability it usually is not, and the honest answer is that most mild disability is the ordinary bottom end of a normal distribution plus disadvantage.","teach":["Classification by timing: prenatal, perinatal and postnatal. Prenatal causes predominate, particularly in severe disability.","A specific cause is identified in the great majority of severe cases and in a minority of mild cases. Chromosomal microarray is now the first-line genetic investigation, superseding karyotype."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semgen","oxid","semid"],"n":{"teach":18,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["13.3.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"If your only tool is asking questions, you will get almost nothing. The assessment has to be rebuilt around what this person can actually do: shorter sentences, longer silences, concrete words, pictures, and a great deal of information from the people who know them.","teach":["The first task is to establish the person's communication level, not to take a history. Ask the carer how the person communicates, what they understand, and what they use to make themselves understood.","Practical adjustments: allow much longer, meet in a familiar place, reduce distraction, and see the person alone as well as with the carer, since abuse and unhappiness are rarely disclosed in front of the person being complained about.","Language: short sentences, one idea at a time, concrete rather than abstract, active rather than passive voice, no metaphor, and no double negatives.","Allow long pauses. Processing time is longer, and filling the silence with a second question guarantees you get an answer to neither.","Avoid leading questions and yes or no questions, both of which produce acquiescence rather than information. Acquiescence, the tendency to agree, is a well-documented response bias in this population.","Nay-saying and recency effects are also documented: some people consistently disagree, and many people choose the last option offered. Vary the order of options to test this.","Use open questions where possible, then multiple choice with the options varied, then closed questions only as a last resort. Cross-check important answers by asking differently later.","Non-verbal tools: photographs, symbols, Talking Mats, drawings, body maps for pain and for abuse, and easy-read materials.","Communication systems in use include Makaton, a signing and symbol system, and PECS, the Picture Exchange Communication System. Objects of reference are used at profound levels.","Intensive interaction is an approach for people at the earliest communication levels, using imitation and responsiveness to build the foundations of interaction rather than to transmit content.","Collateral history is not supplementary here, it is central. Establish what has changed, when it changed, and what else changed around the same time.","Ask the carer for a description of behaviour rather than an interpretation. He is aggressive is an interpretation; he hit out when the television was turned off is a description you can work with.","Always assess physical health, pain, constipation, dental disease, otitis, reflux, and sensory impairment as part of a psychiatric assessment. Behavioural change is frequently a report of pain in someone who cannot report pain.","Consider medication, including recent changes and antiepileptics, and consider seizures, including non-convulsive status, in any acute change in presentation.","Capacity is assessed as in any other person, decision by decision, with all practicable support given first. Intellectual disability never establishes incapacity.","Supported decision-making means providing the information in a usable form and giving the time and support to weigh it, before concluding anything about capacity."],"cards":[["What is acquiescence and how do you counter it?","The well-documented tendency of people with intellectual disability to agree with the interviewer regardless of the content of the question. Counter it by avoiding leading and yes-or-no questions, using open questions, offering multiple choice with the order of options varied, and cross-checking important answers by asking differently later."],["Name four practical communication adjustments in assessment.","Allow substantially longer for the appointment; use short concrete sentences with one idea at a time; allow long pauses for processing without filling the silence; and use non-verbal aids such as photographs, symbols, Talking Mats or body maps."],["Why must the person be seen alone as well as with the carer?","Because abuse, unhappiness and complaints about care are rarely disclosed in the presence of the person being complained about, who is frequently the carer accompanying them."],["What must always be excluded before attributing behavioural change to psychiatric illness?","Physical causes: pain of any origin, constipation, dental disease, otitis, reflux, sensory impairment, medication change, and seizures including non-convulsive status. Behavioural change is often the only available report of pain."],["How should a carer be asked about behaviour?","For a description rather than an interpretation. He hit out when the television was turned off is usable data; he is aggressive is not."]],"note":["Establish the communication level before taking any history.","Nay-saying and recency effects also affect responses; vary the order of options offered.","Makaton is a signing and symbol system; PECS is the Picture Exchange Communication System.","Intensive interaction builds the foundations of interaction at the earliest communication levels.","Capacity is decision-specific; intellectual disability never establishes incapacity."],"refs":["oxid","semid","nice"]};
TEACH["13.3.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The single most important idea in this specialty has an ugly name and a simple meaning. Diagnostic overshadowing is what happens when a doctor sees the disability and stops looking, so a treatable illness is written off as just how he is.","teach":["Diagnostic overshadowing is the attribution of symptoms to the intellectual disability itself rather than to a coexisting and treatable disorder. It is the commonest diagnostic error in this population and it applies to physical illness as much as to psychiatric illness.","Presentation changes with the level of disability. In mild disability, presentation resembles the general adult picture. In severe and profound disability, psychiatric illness presents almost entirely through observed behaviour."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semid","oxid","icd11"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["13.3.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Instead of asking what is wrong with this behaviour, behavioural psychology asks what the behaviour is for. Almost every persistent behaviour is achieving something, and once you know what, you can teach a better way to achieve the same thing.","teach":["Functional analysis rests on the ABC framework: Antecedents, what happened before; Behaviour, described precisely; and Consequences, what followed and what the person obtained or avoided.","The four established functions of behaviour: escape or avoidance of demand, attention, tangible gain, and sensory or automatic reinforcement."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semid","oxid","nice"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["13.3.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Everything psychiatry has works here, but everything needs adapting. The bigger problem is the opposite one: for decades, the main treatment offered has been an antipsychotic given to someone with no psychiatric diagnosis at all, simply because their behaviour was difficult.","teach":["The over-medication problem is the central prescribing issue in this specialty. A large proportion of people with intellectual disability receive psychotropic medication, and a substantial share of those have no corresponding psychiatric diagnosis.","STOMP, launched in 2016, is the national campaign to stop over-medication of people with a learning disability, autism or both. STAMP is its counterpart for children and young people."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","semid","oxid"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["13.3.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Some genetic conditions come with a predictable pattern of behaviour, as reliably as they come with a facial appearance. Knowing the pattern tells you what to expect, what to screen for, and what not to blame on the person.","teach":["A behavioural phenotype is the characteristic pattern of behavioural, cognitive and psychiatric features associated with a specific genetic disorder, occurring at a rate significantly above chance.","The phenotype is probabilistic, not deterministic. It raises the likelihood of a feature; it does not guarantee it, and it does not license disregarding the individual."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semgen","oxid","semid"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["13.3.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"When a child is born with a disability, the family receives two things at once: a child they love, and the loss of the child they had expected. Both are real, and pretending only the first exists is one of the ways professionals make things worse.","teach":["Chronic sorrow, described by Olshansky in 1962, is the enduring and recurrent grief experienced by parents of a child with a disability. It is a normal response, not pathological, and it recurs at developmental milestones the child does not reach.","The recurrence pattern matters clinically. Grief returns when peers start school, pass exams, leave home, marry or have children, and a parent presenting distressed at such a moment is not relapsing but responding."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxid","semid","bhugra"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":6}};
TEACH["13.3.7"] = {"free":false,"simple":"An offender with intellectual disability is usually a person who did not understand, was used by someone else, or could not stop themselves in time. That is not an excuse, but it changes the assessment, the interview, the trial and the treatment.","teach":["Prevalence in the criminal justice system is contested. Screened rates in prison are far higher than the community rate, but formally diagnosed intellectual disability is uncommon, and much of the excess is borderline intellectual functioning, IQ 70 to 85, which falls outside the diagnosis and outside services.","The borderline group is the practical problem: too able for learning disability services, too impaired to manage mainstream processes, and consequently unsupported everywhere."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxid","forrester","puriforen"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["2.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Is a person built by their genes or by what happens to them? The honest answer is that the question is wrong. Genes do not make a person, they make a person who reacts to things in a particular way, and then things happen. A shy child and a bold child raised in the same house are not raised in the same house, because they pull different reactions out of the same parents.","teach":["Stage theories say development moves through a fixed order of qualitatively different steps. Continuity theories say it is gradual and quantitative. Piaget and Freud are stage theorists; learning theory is continuous.","Critical period: a window during which input is required or the capacity is lost permanently. Sensitive period: a window in which learning is easiest but not the only chance. Most human development uses sensitive rather than critical periods.","Gene-environment interaction: the same genotype produces different outcomes in different environments. Gene-environment correlation: genes influence which environments you end up in. Passive, evocative and active are the three types.","Heritability of IQ rises with age, from around 0.4 in childhood to 0.6 to 0.8 in adulthood. That is counter-intuitive and examinable: as people gain autonomy they select environments that match their genotype.","Early versus later adversity. Early adversity has larger effects, but the Romanian adoptee studies show that recovery is substantial if the environment changes early enough, and that persistent deficits follow deprivation beyond about six months.","Freud's psychosexual stages: oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital. Historically essential, empirically unsupported. Know them because they are asked.","The developmental framework matters clinically because the same trauma has different consequences at different ages, and the presentation of any disorder is shaped by the developmental stage it lands in."],"cards":[["Distinguish a critical from a sensitive period.","A critical period is a window in which input is essential; the capacity is permanently lost if it is missed. A sensitive period is a window of maximal responsiveness, after which learning is harder but still possible."],["Name the three types of gene-environment correlation.","Passive (parents provide both genes and environment), evocative (the child's genotype evokes responses from others), active (the child selects environments matching their genotype, also called niche-picking)."],["Why does the heritability of IQ increase with age?","As individuals gain autonomy they increasingly select and shape environments consistent with their genotype, so genetic variance accounts for a larger proportion of phenotypic variance."]],"note":["Heritability is a population statistic, not a personal probability, and it changes when the environment changes.","Freud's psychosexual stages: oral 0 to 1, anal 1 to 3, phallic 3 to 6, latency 6 to puberty, genital from puberty.","Equifinality: different starting points lead to the same outcome. Multifinality: the same starting point leads to different outcomes. Both are core developmental psychopathology concepts.","The English and Romanian Adoptees study, Rutter: deprivation beyond six months predicted persistent deficits including quasi-autistic features, disinhibited attachment and inattention.","Maturational tasks: each stage presents a task whose resolution shapes the next. Erikson formalised this across the whole lifespan."],"refs":["gross","rutter","noptp"]};
TEACH["2.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"To find out how people change over a lifetime you have two choices. Test lots of people of different ages today, which is quick but compares people born in different decades. Or follow the same people for forty years, which is slow, expensive, and the only way to answer the question properly.","teach":["Cross-sectional: different age groups measured at one time. Fast and cheap. Confounded by cohort effects, because a 70-year-old today had a different education from a 20-year-old.","Longitudinal: the same individuals followed over time. Separates true change from cohort effect. Costly, slow, and vulnerable to attrition and practice effects."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","rutter","kirkwood"],"n":{"teach":6,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["2.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Bowlby noticed that a child taken from its mother does not simply become sad. It protests loudly, then gives up, then goes quiet and easy to manage, and the quiet stage is the worst sign of the three. Ainsworth then built a room with a door and found that the useful moment is not the leaving. It is what the child does when the mother comes back.","teach":["Attachment is a biologically driven system whose function is protection through proximity to a caregiver under threat. It is not the same as love, and not the same as dependency.","Robertson and Bowlby's phases of separation: protest, despair, detachment.","The Strange Situation, Ainsworth, twelve to eighteen months, eight episodes over about twenty minutes. Classification comes from the reunion, not the separation.","Secure (B), around 60 to 65 per cent of low-risk samples. Insecure-avoidant (A). Insecure-ambivalent or resistant (C). Disorganised (D), added by Main and Solomon.","The condition for security is sensitive responsiveness: reading the signal, reading it correctly, and responding promptly and appropriately. Quantity of contact matters far less than accuracy of reading.","Disorganised attachment shows contradictory approach and avoidance, freezing or apprehension towards the caregiver. It is associated with frightening or frightened caregiver behaviour, and with maltreatment, and it predicts later psychopathology more strongly than the organised insecure patterns.","The internal working model is the expectation of self and other carried forward, which is how early attachment reaches into adult relationships and into the therapeutic alliance.","Failure to form any selective attachment, as in institutional deprivation, produces reactive attachment disorder or disinhibited social engagement disorder."],"cards":[["Name the four Strange Situation classifications and the approximate proportion secure.","Secure (B), insecure-avoidant (A), insecure-ambivalent or resistant (C), disorganised (D). Around 60 to 65 per cent are secure in low-risk samples."],["What did Harlow's rhesus monkey work demonstrate?","Infant monkeys preferred a cloth surrogate providing contact comfort over a wire surrogate that fed them. Attachment is not driven by feeding."],["Name the Adult Attachment Interview classifications.","Autonomous or secure, dismissing, preoccupied, and unresolved or disorganised. Coding depends on the coherence of the narrative, not the content of the childhood described."]],"note":["Bowlby's maternal deprivation hypothesis, and his 44 juvenile thieves study, are historically central and methodologically weak. Rutter's Maternal Deprivation Reassessed separated privation from deprivation.","Monotropy, Bowlby's claim of one privileged attachment figure, is not supported. Children form a hierarchy of attachments.","Cross-cultural variation: more avoidant classifications in Germany, more resistant in Japan and Israel. The instrument is not culture-free.","Attachment classification in infancy predicts later outcomes modestly, not deterministically. Continuity is around 70 per cent in stable circumstances and falls sharply where circumstances change.","Mentalisation-based treatment and much of the borderline personality disorder literature descend directly from this work."],"refs":["gross","rutter","noptp"]};
TEACH["2.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Families are systems, not collections of individuals. Push on one part and something moves somewhere else. A child who starts refusing school may be doing the family's arguing for it, and treating only the child will not work, because the child is not where the problem lives.","teach":["Baumrind's parenting styles cross two dimensions, demandingness and responsiveness: authoritative (high both, best outcomes), authoritarian (high demand, low warmth), permissive (low demand, high warmth), neglectful (low both, worst outcomes).","Parenting practices, what parents actually do, predict outcomes better than parental attitudes, what parents say they believe."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","gross","noptp"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["2.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Some babies are easy from the first week and some are not, and no parent chose either. What matters is not the baby's temperament on its own but whether it fits what the family can offer. A very active child in a family that loves quiet is a poor fit, and it is the fit that predicts trouble, not the child.","teach":["Thomas and Chess, New York Longitudinal Study from 1956, nine temperament dimensions: activity level, rhythmicity, approach or withdrawal, adaptability, intensity of reaction, threshold of responsiveness, quality of mood, distractibility, attention span and persistence.","Three categories: easy (about 40 per cent), difficult (about 10 per cent), slow to warm up (about 15 per cent). The remainder do not fit a category."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","gross","noptp"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["2.6"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Pour the same juice into a taller thinner glass and a four-year-old will tell you there is now more of it. She is not being silly. She can only hold one dimension in her head at once, so she sees taller and stops. Two years later the same child will look at you as though the question is beneath her. Nothing was taught in between. The machinery arrived.","teach":["Piaget's four stages: sensorimotor 0 to 2, preoperational 2 to 7, concrete operational 7 to 11, formal operational 11 and over.","Sensorimotor: the world is known through action. Object permanence around eight to nine months. Ends with symbolic thought and deferred imitation.","Preoperational: egocentrism, animism, centration, irreversibility, and failure of conservation. The three mountains task demonstrates egocentrism.","Concrete operational: conservation, reversibility, seriation, classification, and transitive inference, but tied to concrete material.","Formal operational: abstract and hypothetico-deductive reasoning. Not universally attained.","Assimilation fits new information into an existing schema. Accommodation changes the schema to fit the information. Equilibration drives the alternation between them.","Vygotsky's alternative: development is social. The zone of proximal development is the gap between what a child can do alone and with help, and scaffolding is how it is crossed. For Vygotsky language drives thought; for Piaget thought drives language.","The clinical payoff. A seven-year-old cannot reason hypothetically about a treatment they have not experienced, so consent conversations must be concrete. And preoperational egocentrism is why children blame themselves for parental illness, divorce and death."],"cards":[["Name Piaget's four stages with ages.","Sensorimotor 0 to 2, preoperational 2 to 7, concrete operational 7 to 11, formal operational 11 and over."],["Define assimilation and accommodation.","Assimilation incorporates new information into an existing schema. Accommodation modifies the schema to fit new information that will not assimilate."],["What is the zone of proximal development?","Vygotsky: the distance between what a child can achieve independently and what they can achieve with guidance from a more capable other. Scaffolding is the support that closes it."]],"note":["Piaget underestimated infants. Baillargeon's violation-of-expectation studies show rudimentary object permanence well before eight months.","Conservation of number is acquired before mass, then weight, then volume. This ordering is called horizontal decalage and is a problem for a strict stage theory.","Egocentrism is not selfishness. It is inability to take another's perspective, and it is distinct from theory of mind, which arrives around four.","The bio-psychosocial model, Engel 1977, is the framework the syllabus names alongside Piaget: biological, psychological and social factors interacting at every level.","Information-processing accounts explain cognitive development as gradual increases in processing speed, working memory capacity and strategy use, without stages."],"refs":["gross","rutter","noptp"]};
TEACH["2.7"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Nobody teaches a child grammar, and yet by four they use it better than most adults can explain it. Children say things they have never heard, like sheeps and goed, which proves they are not copying. They have worked out a rule and are applying it too well.","teach":["The sequence: cooing from about six weeks; babbling from about six months, initially universal then narrowing to the ambient language; first words around twelve months; two-word telegraphic speech at eighteen to twenty-four months.","Vocabulary: around 50 words at 18 months, around 200 at two years, with a vocabulary spurt between. By five, several thousand words."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","rutter"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["2.8"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Watch children in a nursery and you can put an age on them without asking. At two they play beside each other, not with each other. At three they start swapping toys. At four they build something together with roles and rules. A child stuck two steps behind that sequence is telling you something.","teach":["Parten's stages of play: unoccupied, solitary, onlooker, parallel, associative, cooperative. Parallel play around two, associative around three, cooperative from about three to four.","Play develops in parallel with cognition: sensorimotor play, then symbolic or pretend play from about eighteen months, then games with rules from about six."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","gross"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["2.9"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Ask a six-year-old why stealing is wrong and she will say because you get told off. Ask a sixteen-year-old and she will say because it is somebody else's. Both said stealing is wrong. They did not say the same thing, and Kohlberg's point was that the reasoning matters far more than the verdict.","teach":["Kohlberg used moral dilemmas, most famously Heinz and the drug, and scored the reasoning rather than the answer.","Preconventional level: stage 1 punishment and obedience; stage 2 instrumental exchange, what is in it for me."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","rutter"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["2.10"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Small children are afraid of things you can see. Bigger children are afraid of things they can imagine. That shift, from the dark and dogs to being laughed at and failing, tracks the arrival of the ability to think about the future, and it explains why the same reassurance stops working around eight.","teach":["Normative fears by age: loud noises and loss of support in infancy; strangers from six to eight months; separation peaking at ten to eighteen months; animals, the dark and imaginary creatures in the preschool years; injury, illness and death in middle childhood; social evaluation and performance in adolescence.","A fear becomes a disorder when it is out of proportion, persistent, and impairing. Developmental appropriateness is the first question, not the last."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","gross","nice"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["2.11"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Sex, gender and orientation are three different things and get muddled constantly. Sex is biological. Gender identity is the sense of who you are. Sexual orientation is who you are drawn to. A person can vary on any one of the three independently of the other two.","teach":["Biological sex is determined by chromosomes, gonads, hormones and anatomy. Gender identity is the internal sense of one's gender. Gender role is the socially expected behaviour. Sexual orientation is the pattern of attraction. These are independent dimensions.","Gender identity is generally established by around two to three years, gender stability by four, and gender constancy, understanding that gender does not change with appearance, by around six to seven."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","noptp","icd11"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["2.12"] = {"free":true,"simple":"An adolescent brain is a car whose accelerator is fitted years before the brakes. The reward system matures early and the part that says wait a moment matures late, into the mid-twenties. Most of what parents complain about lives in that gap, and it is developmental, not moral.","teach":["Neurodevelopment: limbic and reward systems mature early, prefrontal control late. Synaptic pruning and continuing myelination proceed through adolescence into the mid-twenties, caudal to rostral.","The consequence is heightened reward sensitivity, greater risk-taking in the presence of peers specifically, and immature response inhibition. Adolescents are not poor at assessing risk in the abstract; they discount it in the moment and among peers.","Erikson's task is identity versus role confusion. Marcia operationalised it into four identity statuses, from crossing exploration with commitment: diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium, achievement.","Conflict with parents is normative but usually mild and about everyday matters. Coleman's focal theory: adolescents deal with one issue at a time, and difficulty arises when several converge.","Storm and stress, Hall's original claim, is not supported as a universal. Most adolescents negotiate the period without significant turmoil, which matters because normalising real disorder as teenage moodiness is a common and dangerous error.","Puberty timing interacts with psychological outcome. Early maturation in girls carries the clearest risk.","Epidemiology: median age of onset for any mental disorder is in the mid-teens, and around three quarters of adult disorders begin before twenty-four. Adolescence is when psychiatry's caseload is created.","Distinguishing normal from abnormal: look for functional impairment, persistence, pervasiveness across settings, and departure from the young person's own baseline."],"cards":[["What neurodevelopmental asymmetry explains adolescent risk-taking?","Early maturation of limbic and reward circuitry ahead of prefrontal regulatory maturation, which continues into the mid-twenties, together with synaptic pruning and continuing myelination."],["Name Marcia's four identity statuses.","Identity diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium, and identity achievement, derived from crossing exploration with commitment."],["When do most adult psychiatric disorders begin?","Median onset in the mid-teens, with around 75 per cent of lifetime disorders beginning before age 24."]],"note":["Risk-taking in adolescence rises specifically in the presence of peers; this is demonstrable experimentally and does not occur to the same degree in adults.","Sleep phase delays physiologically in adolescence, which is why early school start times are a genuine health question.","Hall coined storm and stress in 1904. Offer's work showed most adolescents report stable self-image.","Self-harm peaks in adolescence, more commonly in girls; completed suicide is more common in boys.","Transition from CAMHS to adult services at 18 is a recognised point of disengagement and risk."],"refs":["rutter","gross","noptp"]};
TEACH["2.13"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Adult life is a series of jobs nobody trains you for: choosing a partner, keeping one, raising a child, being ill, and losing people. Each one demands a different version of you, and psychiatric illness in adulthood very often arrives at the seam between two of them.","teach":["Erikson's adult stages: intimacy versus isolation in young adulthood, generativity versus stagnation in middle adulthood, ego integrity versus despair in later life.","Pairing: assortative mating is strong for education, values and psychiatric disorder. Marriage is associated with better mental health outcomes for men more than for women, and the direction of causation is contested."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","noptp","icd11"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["2.14"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Pregnancy and the year after are the highest-risk period in a woman's psychiatric life, and the most likely to be missed, because everybody assumes exhaustion and tearfulness are just what having a baby is like. The illness that must never be missed comes on fast, in the first two weeks, and looks like confusion as much as like mania.","teach":["Physiological: large shifts in oestrogen and progesterone, an abrupt fall after delivery, thyroid change, sleep disruption and pain.","Baby blues: 50 to 80 per cent of mothers, days three to ten, tearfulness and lability, self-limiting. Not an illness and not treated.","Postnatal depression: around 10 to 15 per cent, usually within the first three months. Screened with the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale, ten items, self-rated. It is a screening tool, not a diagnosis.","Postpartum psychosis: one to two per thousand deliveries, onset usually within the first two weeks, often abrupt. Confusion, perplexity, mood lability, delusions frequently concerning the infant. It is a psychiatric emergency requiring admission, ideally to a mother and baby unit.","The single strongest risk factor for postpartum psychosis is bipolar disorder, raising risk more than twentyfold, and a previous episode of postpartum psychosis carries a recurrence risk of roughly one in two.","Suicide is a leading cause of maternal death in the UK, and the deaths characteristically occur by violent means in women who were not thought to be at high risk.","Prescribing: every decision is a comparison, never a choice between risk and no risk. Untreated severe illness harms mother and infant. Sodium valproate is contraindicated in women of childbearing potential outside the Pregnancy Prevention Programme. Lithium carries cardiac malformation risk and requires specialist counselling and level monitoring across pregnancy.","Maternal depression affects infant development through reduced responsiveness and disrupted interaction. Treating the mother is an intervention for the child."],"cards":[["Give the frequency and onset of baby blues, postnatal depression and postpartum psychosis.","Baby blues: 50 to 80 per cent, days three to ten, self-limiting. Postnatal depression: 10 to 15 per cent, usually within three months. Postpartum psychosis: 1 to 2 per 1000, usually within two weeks."],["What is the single strongest risk factor for postpartum psychosis?","Bipolar disorder, raising risk more than twentyfold. A previous postpartum psychosis carries a recurrence risk of roughly 50 per cent."],["Which antidepressant is generally preferred in breastfeeding?","Sertraline, which has low milk transfer. Paroxetine is also low. Fluoxetine has a long half-life and accumulates in the infant."]],"note":["The Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale omits somatic items because they are confounded by normal postnatal physiology.","Mother and baby units exist so that admission does not separate mother and infant. Separation is itself a harm.","Neonatal adaptation syndrome after third-trimester SSRI exposure: jitteriness, respiratory distress, feeding difficulty, usually resolving within two weeks.","MBRRACE-UK is the source for UK maternal mortality data, including psychiatric causes.","Perinatal mental illness is a NICE guideline topic (CG192) and a recurring exam theme."],"refs":["nice","maudsley","semgen"]};
TEACH["2.15"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Identity is the answer to who am I, and it is not answered once. You settle it in your twenties, and then a job ends or a child leaves or a diagnosis arrives and the question reopens. That reopening is normal. It is only a crisis if nothing else in the person's life is holding steady.","teach":["Erikson: identity versus role confusion in adolescence, then intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation, and ego integrity versus despair.","Marcia's four identity statuses: diffusion, no exploration and no commitment; foreclosure, commitment without exploration, usually inherited from parents; moratorium, active exploration without commitment; achievement, commitment following exploration."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","bhugra","noptp"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["2.16"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Ageing does not take the mind away evenly. What you know keeps growing. Your vocabulary at seventy is better than it was at thirty. What slows is how fast you can work something out that you have never seen before, and how quickly you can find a word you definitely know. Mistaking the second for dementia is one of the commonest errors in old age psychiatry.","teach":["Crystallised intelligence, accumulated knowledge and vocabulary, is preserved or improves into the seventies. Fluid intelligence, novel problem solving and processing speed, declines from early adulthood.","Normal ageing: slower processing, mild retrieval difficulty with intact recognition, preserved semantic memory, mild reduction in divided attention. Free recall falls more than cued recall, and cueing helps. In dementia, cueing does not help.","Brain volume falls by roughly 0.2 to 0.5 per cent per year from middle age, greatest in prefrontal cortex and hippocampus, with ventricular enlargement and white matter change.","Cross-sectional studies exaggerate decline compared with longitudinal studies because of cohort effects in education and nutrition. This is a repeat examinable point.","Personality is broadly stable in later life. Small mean shifts are described: slight increases in agreeableness and conscientiousness, slight decrease in openness. Marked personality change in old age is a symptom, most often of frontotemporal dementia, and must not be attributed to ageing.","Socioemotional selectivity theory, Carstensen: as time horizons shorten, emotionally meaningful goals are prioritised, social networks are pruned deliberately, and emotional wellbeing is often preserved or improved. This is the positivity effect.","Disengagement theory, that withdrawal is natural and adaptive, is largely discredited. Activity theory and continuity theory have better support.","Social and economic context: retirement, reduced income, loss of role and status, poor housing, sensory impairment, and ageism. Loneliness is an independent predictor of mortality and cognitive decline."],"cards":[["How do crystallised and fluid intelligence differ in ageing?","Crystallised intelligence, knowledge and vocabulary, is preserved or rises into the seventies. Fluid intelligence, novel problem solving and processing speed, declines from early adulthood."],["How does normal age-related memory change differ from dementia?","In normal ageing, free recall declines but recognition and cued recall are preserved, so cueing helps. In dementia the deficit is in encoding and storage, so cueing does not help and recognition is impaired."],["What is socioemotional selectivity theory?","Carstensen: as perceived time remaining shortens, people prioritise emotionally meaningful goals and close relationships over information seeking, which contributes to preserved or improved emotional wellbeing in later life."]],"note":["Marked personality change in later life is not normal ageing and should prompt consideration of frontotemporal dementia, frontal lesion or depression.","Sensory impairment is a major and correctable contributor to apparent cognitive decline and to late-onset psychosis. Check hearing and vision before diagnosing anything.","Loneliness and social isolation are independently associated with dementia risk, depression and mortality.","The Lancet Commission modifiable dementia risk factors span the life course, including education, hearing loss, hypertension, obesity, smoking, depression, physical inactivity, diabetes, social isolation, alcohol, head injury and air pollution.","Ageism affects clinical decisions: undertreatment of depression and under-investigation of physical symptoms in older people are both documented."],"refs":["oxoldage","gross","noptp"]};
TEACH["2.17"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Genes are not a blueprint that gets built regardless. They are more like a recipe that behaves differently depending on the kitchen. The same gene variant can be an advantage in one environment and a liability in another, which is why asking whether something is genetic or environmental is asking the wrong question.","teach":["Heritability is the proportion of variance in a trait, in a given population under given conditions, attributable to genetic variance. Change the population or the environment and the number changes. It is not a personal probability.","Behavioural genetics separates additive genetic, shared environmental and non-shared environmental variance. For most psychiatric and personality traits, non-shared environment exceeds shared environment, which surprises people every time."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","noptp","rutter"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["2.18"] = {"free":false,"simple":"For most of history the only way to see a developing brain was after it had stopped. Now we can watch a child's brain change year by year without touching it, and the first surprise was that the brain does not simply grow. It builds far too many connections and then deliberately destroys most of them, and that destruction is what makes it work.","teach":["Structural MRI shows grey matter volume peaking in childhood and declining through adolescence, reflecting synaptic pruning, while white matter volume rises steadily through myelination into the twenties.","Maturation proceeds posterior to anterior, with primary sensory and motor cortex maturing first and prefrontal cortex last. This is the anatomical basis of the adolescent accelerator-and-brakes problem."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lynall","rutter"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.1.1.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Push a wire thinner than a hair next to one brain cell and you can hear that single cell talk. It is the most precise recording anyone can make, and it is why almost everything we know about how single neurones code information came from animals rather than people.","teach":["A microelectrode records the action potentials of a single neurone, giving millisecond resolution and single-cell precision.","It is invasive, so human data come only from patients already undergoing electrode implantation, chiefly epilepsy surgery and deep brain stimulation."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lynall"],"n":{"teach":5,"cards":2,"note":4}};
TEACH["3.1.1.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The EEG is a stadium heard from the car park. You cannot pick out one voice, only whether the crowd is roaring together or muttering separately. That makes it useless for locating a small lump and superb for catching a seizure, because a seizure is the whole crowd shouting the same word at once.","teach":["The EEG records summed postsynaptic potentials of cortical pyramidal cells, oriented perpendicular to the surface. It has millisecond temporal resolution and poor spatial resolution.","Frequency bands: delta under 4 Hz, theta 4 to under 8 Hz, alpha 8 to 13 Hz, beta above 13 Hz. Gamma above about 30 Hz."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lynall","lishman"],"n":{"teach":10,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.1.1.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Every scan is a bargain between seeing where and seeing when. Structural MRI gives you an exquisite photograph and tells you nothing about time. EEG gives you the timing to the millisecond and cannot tell you where. Nothing yet does both well, and almost every imaging question is really asking which half the technique gave up.","teach":["Structural MRI: best spatial resolution, around 1 mm, no ionising radiation, superior grey-white differentiation and posterior fossa imaging. First line for structural questions in psychiatry.","CT: faster, cheaper, better for acute haemorrhage and bone. Uses ionising radiation. Use it in the emergency, MRI for the question.","Diffusion tensor imaging measures the directionality of water diffusion, expressed as fractional anisotropy, and maps white matter tracts. Reduced fractional anisotropy is reported in schizophrenia, particularly in frontotemporal connections.","Functional MRI measures the blood oxygen level dependent signal, the ratio of oxygenated to deoxygenated haemoglobin. It is an indirect haemodynamic proxy lagging neural activity by four to six seconds. Good spatial, poor temporal resolution.","PET requires a cyclotron-produced positron-emitting tracer and gives quantitative measures, including receptor occupancy. It is how we know antipsychotics need roughly 60 to 70 per cent D2 occupancy for efficacy.","SPECT uses longer-lived isotopes, is cheaper and more available, and has lower resolution. DaTSCAN is a SPECT ligand for the dopamine transporter, used to separate Lewy body dementia and Parkinson's disease from other causes of parkinsonism and dementia.","MR spectroscopy measures metabolite concentrations without a tracer: N-acetylaspartate as a neuronal marker, choline, creatine as an internal reference, myo-inositol, glutamate and glutamine, and GABA.","Clinical use in psychiatry is to exclude structural, vascular, inflammatory or neoplastic pathology. There is no imaging test that makes a psychiatric diagnosis in an individual."],"cards":[["What does the BOLD signal actually measure?","The ratio of oxygenated to deoxygenated haemoglobin, an indirect haemodynamic correlate of neural activity, lagging it by four to six seconds."],["Which technique has the best spatial resolution, and which the best temporal?","Structural MRI has the best spatial resolution, around one millimetre. EEG and MEG have the best temporal resolution, of the order of milliseconds."],["What is DaTSCAN and when is it used?","A SPECT ligand for the presynaptic dopamine transporter. It is used to distinguish dementia with Lewy bodies and Parkinson's disease from other causes of dementia and parkinsonism, and is a supportive diagnostic feature in DLB."]],"note":["MRI contraindications: ferromagnetic implants, some pacemakers, cochlear implants, intraocular metallic foreign bodies.","FDG-PET in Alzheimer's disease shows temporoparietal hypometabolism; in frontotemporal dementia, frontal and anterior temporal hypometabolism.","Amyloid PET is now available and shows amyloid deposition, which is present in a substantial minority of cognitively normal older people, so a positive scan does not diagnose Alzheimer's disease.","Reverse inference, concluding a mental process occurred because a region lit up, is the standard logical error in reading fMRI papers.","MEG detects magnetic fields from neuronal currents, with EEG-like temporal and better spatial resolution, but is expensive and scarce."],"refs":["agrawal","lynall","oxneuropsy"]};
TEACH["3.1.1.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"If you want to know what chemicals are actually floating around in a living brain, you have to go and take a sample. Microdialysis threads a fine tube with a semi-permeable membrane into the tissue, washes fluid past it, and collects what crosses over.","teach":["A probe with a semi-permeable membrane is implanted and perfused with a physiological solution. Molecules diffuse down their concentration gradient into the perfusate and are collected for assay.","It measures extracellular concentrations of neurotransmitters and metabolites in the awake behaving animal, with temporal resolution of minutes."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","stahl"],"n":{"teach":5,"cards":2,"note":3}};
TEACH["3.1.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Watching the brain tells you what happens alongside a thought. Changing the brain tells you what the thought needs. That is why all the interesting causal evidence comes from methods that interfere: lesion it, stimulate it, switch it off with light, and see what breaks.","teach":["Lesion studies are the oldest causal method. Broca, Wernicke, Phineas Gage and HM are all lesion evidence. The limitation is that natural lesions do not respect functional boundaries and reorganisation follows.","Transcranial magnetic stimulation: a magnetic pulse induces a current in cortex. Single-pulse produces a transient virtual lesion; repetitive TMS at high frequency is excitatory and at low frequency inhibitory. rTMS to left dorsolateral prefrontal cortex is licensed and NICE-approved for treatment-resistant depression."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lynall","ecthandbk"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.1.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"You cannot give a mouse schizophrenia. What you can do is produce one measurable piece of it, such as too much startle or too little interest in sugar water, and study that piece. The danger is forgetting which piece you are looking at and claiming you have modelled the illness.","teach":["Three validity criteria: face validity, resembling the human phenotype; construct validity, sharing the underlying mechanism; predictive validity, responding to treatments that work in humans, and not to those that do not. Predictive validity is the most useful and the hardest.","Depression models: forced swim test and tail suspension test measure behavioural despair and have good predictive validity for antidepressants and poor construct validity. Chronic mild stress produces anhedonia, measured by reduced sucrose preference. Learned helplessness, Seligman."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lynall"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["3.1.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Instead of describing what the brain does, you write a set of equations that would produce the same behaviour, then check whether real brains follow them. When they do, you have learned something that no amount of watching could tell you: not what happened, but what rule it was following.","teach":["Computational psychiatry builds formal models of learning and decision-making and fits their parameters to individual behaviour, aiming to convert a symptom into a mechanism.","Reinforcement learning models rest on prediction error, the difference between expected and received reward. Dopamine neurone firing encodes reward prediction error, which is one of the best-established findings in systems neuroscience (Schultz)."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lynall","agrawal"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["3.2.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The brain has two kinds of resident. Neurones do the talking. Glia were dismissed for a century as the packing material that stops the neurones rattling. They are nothing of the sort: they build the insulation, mop up the chemicals, feed the neurones and control what gets in from the blood.","teach":["Neurones: cell body, dendrites receiving input, one axon carrying output. Types include pyramidal projection neurones, using glutamate, and interneurones, mostly using GABA.","Astrocytes: the commonest glial cell. They induce and maintain the blood-brain barrier, buffer extracellular potassium, take up glutamate via excitatory amino acid transporters, store glycogen, and form the third element of the tripartite synapse.","Oligodendrocytes myelinate in the central nervous system, each supplying several axons. Schwann cells myelinate in the peripheral nervous system, one internode each.","Microglia are the resident macrophages, of mesodermal rather than neuroectodermal origin. They prune synapses in development and mediate neuroinflammation.","Ependymal cells line the ventricles. The choroid plexus produces around 500 ml of CSF a day against a total volume of roughly 150 ml, so it turns over three to four times daily.","Cortical layers, six in neocortex: layer I molecular; layer IV granular, the main thalamic input, thickest in sensory cortex; layer V internal pyramidal, the main subcortical output including corticospinal fibres; layer VI projects back to thalamus.","Sensory cortex is granular with a thick layer IV; motor cortex is agranular with a thin layer IV and a prominent layer V. Hippocampal allocortex has three layers.","Betz cells are the giant pyramidal cells of layer V of primary motor cortex."],"cards":[["Which cell myelinates in the CNS and which in the PNS, and how do they differ?","Oligodendrocytes in the CNS, each myelinating segments of several axons. Schwann cells in the PNS, one cell per internode of one axon."],["Name four functions of astrocytes.","Induction and maintenance of the blood-brain barrier; potassium buffering; glutamate uptake and recycling; glycogen storage and metabolic support. They also participate in synaptic transmission as part of the tripartite synapse."],["Which cortical layer receives thalamic input and which provides the main subcortical output?","Layer IV, the internal granular layer, receives thalamic input and is thickest in primary sensory cortex. Layer V, the internal pyramidal layer, provides the main subcortical and corticospinal output."]],"note":["Microglia are derived from yolk sac macrophages, not from neuroectoderm. This is a favourite examination detail.","CSF is produced at about 500 ml per day; total volume around 150 ml; normal pressure 10 to 15 cmH2O.","Glutamate taken up by astrocytes is converted to glutamine and returned to neurones: the glutamate-glutamine cycle.","Neuroinflammation and microglial activation are proposed contributors to depression and schizophrenia; the evidence is associative, not established as causal.","The blood-brain barrier is formed by tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells, induced and maintained by astrocyte end-feet. Circumventricular organs lack it."],"refs":["agrawal","lynall"]};
TEACH["3.2.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"A neurone holds a charge across its skin like a tiny battery, about minus seventy millivolts, kept there by a pump that throws three sodium ions out for every two potassium ions it lets in. Let enough sodium rush back and the whole membrane flips over in a wave that runs to the end of the cell. That wave is a thought's smallest unit.","teach":["Resting membrane potential is about minus 70 mV, set by high resting potassium permeability and maintained by the sodium-potassium ATPase, which moves 3 Na+ out for 2 K+ in and is therefore electrogenic.","The action potential is all-or-none. Depolarisation to threshold opens voltage-gated sodium channels; sodium influx depolarises further, a positive feedback; sodium channels then inactivate and delayed rectifier potassium channels open, repolarising the membrane, with a brief afterhyperpolarisation.","Absolute refractory period, during sodium channel inactivation, prevents a second action potential at any stimulus strength and enforces unidirectional propagation. Relative refractory period follows and requires a larger stimulus.","Saltatory conduction: myelin insulates the internodes and the action potential regenerates at nodes of Ranvier, greatly increasing velocity and reducing metabolic cost. Conduction velocity rises with axon diameter and with myelination.","Synaptic transmission: depolarisation opens voltage-gated calcium channels, calcium entry triggers SNARE-mediated vesicle fusion, and transmitter is released into the cleft.","Excitatory postsynaptic potentials and inhibitory postsynaptic potentials are graded, not all-or-none, and summate temporally and spatially. The axon hillock has the lowest threshold and is where the decision is made.","G-protein coupled receptors are seven-transmembrane metabotropic receptors: Gs raises cAMP, Gi lowers it, Gq activates phospholipase C producing IP3 and diacylglycerol.","Allosteric modulation acts at a site distinct from the orthosteric site. Benzodiazepines are positive allosteric modulators at GABA-A: they cannot open the channel alone, which is why they are far safer in overdose than barbiturates, which can.","Synaptic plasticity: long-term potentiation and depression. Pruning removes excess synapses through childhood and adolescence and is the structural basis of the adolescent grey matter trajectory."],"cards":[["What maintains the resting membrane potential?","High resting permeability to potassium, with the sodium-potassium ATPase moving three sodium ions out for every two potassium ions in, which is electrogenic and maintains the gradients."],["Why does the action potential travel in only one direction?","Because sodium channels behind the wavefront are in the absolute refractory period through inactivation, and cannot be reopened by any stimulus strength."],["Why are benzodiazepines safer than barbiturates in overdose?","Benzodiazepines are positive allosteric modulators that increase the frequency of GABA-A channel opening only in the presence of GABA. Barbiturates increase channel open duration and at high dose can open the channel directly without GABA."]],"note":["Benzodiazepines increase frequency of channel opening; barbiturates increase duration. This distinction is examined repeatedly.","Local anaesthetics and phenytoin act on voltage-gated sodium channels; carbamazepine and lamotrigine stabilise them in the inactivated state.","Tetrodotoxin blocks voltage-gated sodium channels; botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins and prevents vesicle fusion.","Demyelination slows or blocks conduction, which is the basis of symptoms in multiple sclerosis and Guillain-Barre syndrome.","Second messengers: cAMP acting through protein kinase A, IP3 releasing intracellular calcium, DAG activating protein kinase C. Lithium inhibits inositol monophosphatase, the inositol depletion hypothesis."],"refs":["agrawal","stahl","lynall"]};
TEACH["3.2.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"If you can write down what one neurone does as a set of equations, you can wire a thousand of them together in a computer and watch what the network does. Very often the network does something that no single neurone was doing, and that is the point: the behaviour belongs to the circuit, not the cell.","teach":["The Hodgkin-Huxley model describes the action potential in terms of voltage- and time-dependent sodium and potassium conductances. It won the 1963 Nobel Prize and remains the reference biophysical model.","Simplified models trade realism for tractability: the integrate-and-fire neurone sums input and fires at threshold; the Izhikevich model reproduces many firing patterns cheaply."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lynall","agrawal"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["3.3.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Every transmitter in your head is made out of something you ate, in a fixed order, by a queue of enzymes. Tyrosine becomes dopamine becomes noradrenaline becomes adrenaline. Tryptophan becomes serotonin. Learn the order and the enzyme at each arrow and a surprising number of exam questions answer themselves.","teach":["Catecholamine pathway: tyrosine, then tyrosine hydroxylase to L-DOPA, then DOPA decarboxylase to dopamine, then dopamine beta-hydroxylase to noradrenaline, then PNMT to adrenaline. Tyrosine hydroxylase is the rate-limiting step.","Serotonin pathway: tryptophan, then tryptophan hydroxylase to 5-hydroxytryptophan, then decarboxylase to serotonin. Tryptophan hydroxylase is rate-limiting and is not saturated at normal substrate levels, which is why dietary tryptophan depletion lowers brain serotonin and can transiently reverse antidepressant response.","Acetylcholine: choline acetyltransferase combines choline and acetyl-CoA. Choline uptake is rate-limiting. Acetylcholinesterase degrades it in the cleft.","GABA: glutamic acid decarboxylase converts glutamate to GABA and requires pyridoxine, vitamin B6, as a cofactor. GABA transaminase degrades it, and is inhibited by vigabatrin.","Storage: transmitters are packaged into vesicles by vesicular monoamine transporter 2. Reserpine depletes monoamines by blocking VMAT2, and historically caused depression, which is a foundation of the monoamine hypothesis.","Release: depolarisation opens voltage-gated calcium channels, calcium triggers SNARE-mediated fusion, transmitter is released. Calcium influx is the essential coupling step.","Reuptake by transporters terminates the signal for monoamines: SERT, NET and DAT. These are the targets of the SSRIs, SNRIs, tricyclics, cocaine and methylphenidate.","Degradation: monoamine oxidase A prefers serotonin and noradrenaline, MAO-B prefers phenylethylamine, and dopamine is a substrate for both. COMT methylates catecholamines."],"cards":[["What is the rate-limiting enzyme of catecholamine synthesis, and of serotonin synthesis?","Tyrosine hydroxylase for catecholamines, converting tyrosine to L-DOPA. Tryptophan hydroxylase for serotonin, converting tryptophan to 5-hydroxytryptophan."],["Which cofactor does glutamic acid decarboxylase require, and what is the clinical consequence?","Pyridoxine, vitamin B6. Deficiency, including that caused by isoniazid, reduces GABA synthesis and causes seizures, treated with pyridoxine."],["Name the main metabolites of dopamine, noradrenaline and serotonin.","Dopamine: homovanillic acid via COMT and MAO, and DOPAC via MAO. Noradrenaline: MHPG and VMA. Serotonin: 5-HIAA via MAO-A."]],"note":["PNMT, converting noradrenaline to adrenaline, is found in the adrenal medulla and a small number of brainstem neurones. It requires cortisol, which is why it is concentrated in the adrenal.","Reserpine blocks VMAT2 and depletes monoamines. Its association with depression underpinned the monoamine hypothesis.","Low CSF 5-HIAA is associated with impulsivity, violence and suicide, a finding replicated across several populations.","Amphetamine reverses the direction of the dopamine transporter and displaces dopamine from vesicles; cocaine simply blocks reuptake.","Autoreceptors provide negative feedback: 5-HT1A somatodendritic and 5-HT1B terminal for serotonin, D2 for dopamine, alpha-2 for noradrenaline."],"refs":["stahl","agrawal","maudsley"]};
TEACH["3.3.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There are only two ways a chemical message can reach a cell. It can punch a hole in the wall, which is fast and over in milliseconds. Or it can ring a doorbell inside, which is slower and lasts far longer. Holes are ionotropic receptors. Doorbells are metabotropic, and almost all of them work through G proteins.","teach":["Ionotropic receptors are ligand-gated ion channels: fast, milliseconds, direct. Metabotropic receptors are G-protein coupled: slower, seconds to minutes, and act through second messengers.","Glutamate: AMPA, NMDA and kainate are ionotropic; mGluR1 to 8 are metabotropic. AMPA mediates fast excitatory transmission."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["stahl","agrawal","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.3.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"When a metabotropic receptor is switched on, nothing happens at the wall. A message runs into the cell instead, gets amplified enormously, and can end up changing which genes the cell is reading. That is how a drug taken today changes a brain over the following weeks, which is exactly the delay you see with antidepressants.","teach":["G proteins are heterotrimeric, alpha beta gamma. Ligand binding causes GDP-GTP exchange on the alpha subunit and dissociation, and both alpha and beta-gamma act on effectors.","Gs stimulates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP. Gi inhibits it, lowering cAMP. Gq activates phospholipase C, generating IP3 and diacylglycerol."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["stahl","agrawal"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.3.4"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Six chemicals do most of the work. Dopamine handles reward and movement. Serotonin handles mood, sleep and appetite. Noradrenaline handles alertness. Acetylcholine handles memory. GABA is the brake and glutamate is the accelerator. Almost every psychiatric drug you will ever prescribe is nudging one of those six.","teach":["Dopamine. Four pathways: mesolimbic for positive symptoms, mesocortical for negative and cognitive symptoms, nigrostriatal for movement, tuberoinfundibular for prolactin inhibition. Cell bodies in substantia nigra pars compacta (A9) and ventral tegmental area (A10). Receptors D1 to D5. Antipsychotics need roughly 60 to 70 per cent D2 occupancy; extrapyramidal effects above about 80 per cent; hyperprolactinaemia from around 72 per cent.","Serotonin. Cell bodies in the raphe nuclei; rostral raphe project to forebrain, caudal to spinal cord. Fourteen receptor subtypes across seven families. 5-HT1A somatodendritic autoreceptor desensitisation explains the antidepressant delay. 5-HT2A blockade contributes to atypicality; 5-HT2C blockade to appetite and weight gain.","Noradrenaline. Locus coeruleus in the dorsal pons supplies most cortical noradrenaline. Alpha-1 postsynaptic, alpha-2 inhibitory autoreceptor, beta-1 and beta-2. Governs arousal, vigilance and the stress response.","Acetylcholine. Nucleus basalis of Meynert projects to cortex; medial septal nucleus to hippocampus. Loss of nucleus basalis neurones is the cholinergic deficit of Alzheimer's disease and the rationale for cholinesterase inhibitors. Muscarinic blockade impairs memory, which is why anticholinergic burden matters.","GABA. The principal inhibitory transmitter, made from glutamate by glutamic acid decarboxylase. GABA-A ionotropic chloride channel; GABA-B metabotropic. Target of benzodiazepines, barbiturates, alcohol, Z-drugs and many anticonvulsants.","Glutamate. The principal excitatory transmitter. AMPA for fast transmission, NMDA for plasticity, kainate, and metabotropic mGluR. Excitotoxicity through excessive calcium entry contributes to damage in stroke, hypoxia and neurodegeneration.","The glutamate hypothesis of schizophrenia rests on NMDA antagonists such as ketamine and phencyclidine producing positive, negative and cognitive symptoms in healthy volunteers, which dopamine agonists do not."],"cards":[["Name the four dopamine pathways and the clinical consequence of blocking each.","Mesolimbic: antipsychotic effect on positive symptoms. Mesocortical: worsening of negative and cognitive symptoms. Nigrostriatal: extrapyramidal side effects. Tuberoinfundibular: hyperprolactinaemia."],["Where are the cell bodies of the serotonergic, noradrenergic and cortical cholinergic systems?","Serotonin: raphe nuclei of the brainstem. Noradrenaline: locus coeruleus in the dorsal pons. Cortical acetylcholine: nucleus basalis of Meynert in the basal forebrain."],["Why does NMDA antagonism support a glutamate hypothesis of schizophrenia?","Ketamine and phencyclidine reproduce positive, negative and cognitive symptoms in healthy volunteers and worsen symptoms in patients, whereas dopamine agonists reproduce only positive symptoms."]],"note":["Around 80 per cent of nigrostriatal neurones must be lost before parkinsonism becomes apparent.","Aripiprazole is a D2 partial agonist, which is why it can lower prolactin rather than raise it.","Clozapine and quetiapine have low and transient D2 occupancy, the usual explanation for their low extrapyramidal burden.","Alpha-2 blockade by mirtazapine increases both noradrenaline and serotonin release, since alpha-2 heteroreceptors sit on serotonergic terminals.","Excitotoxicity is the rationale for memantine in moderate to severe Alzheimer's disease."],"refs":["stahl","agrawal","maudsley"]};
TEACH["3.3.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Alongside the fast chemical messages there is a slower conversation carried by short protein fragments. These are the molecules that tell you that you are hungry, full, stressed or in pain, and they are the reason a drug developed for diabetes turned out to change what people want to eat.","teach":["Corticotrophin releasing hormone: from the paraventricular nucleus, drives ACTH release and the stress response, and has direct anxiogenic effects. Elevated CSF CRH is reported in depression.","Cholecystokinin: a gut and brain peptide. CCK-4 infusion reliably induces panic attacks in susceptible individuals and is a standard experimental panic challenge."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","stahl","lynall"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.3.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"For thirty years people looked for the schizophrenia gene by picking a chemical they already suspected and testing its gene. Almost nothing replicated. Then researchers stopped guessing, scanned the whole genome without a hypothesis, and the answers that came back were not the ones anybody had picked.","teach":["Candidate gene studies selected genes from an existing hypothesis, typically COMT, DRD2, SLC6A4 and BDNF. Almost none replicated in large samples. This is one of the clearest cautionary tales in psychiatric research.","Genome-wide association studies test millions of variants without prior hypothesis, requiring genome-wide significance at 5 x 10 to the minus 8 and very large samples."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lynall","noptp"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.3.7"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Every drug that people take for pleasure ends up doing the same thing in the same place: raising dopamine in a small structure behind the front of the brain. They arrive by completely different routes, but they all end at the same door, and that is why very different substances produce the same trap.","teach":["The common final pathway is increased dopamine in the nucleus accumbens, part of the mesolimbic projection from the ventral tegmental area.","Cocaine blocks the dopamine, noradrenaline and serotonin transporters, preventing reuptake. Amphetamine additionally reverses the dopamine transporter and displaces dopamine from vesicles, producing much larger release.","Opioids act at mu receptors. They disinhibit ventral tegmental dopamine neurones by inhibiting GABA interneurones. Mu agonism gives analgesia, euphoria, miosis, constipation and respiratory depression.","Alcohol is dirty pharmacology: it potentiates GABA-A, inhibits NMDA, and releases dopamine and endogenous opioids. NMDA inhibition acutely with compensatory upregulation explains withdrawal seizures and delirium tremens.","Cannabis: THC is a partial agonist at CB1, which is presynaptic and inhibits transmitter release. Cannabidiol is not intoxicating and has different, possibly antipsychotic, properties. Dose-related increase in psychosis risk, greatest with high-potency preparations and adolescent onset.","Nicotine acts at nicotinic acetylcholine receptors on ventral tegmental dopamine neurones. Its very rapid delivery by inhalation is central to its addictive potential.","Hallucinogens: LSD, psilocybin and mescaline are 5-HT2A agonists. Ketamine and phencyclidine are NMDA antagonists. MDMA is principally a serotonin releaser with risk of hyperthermia, hyponatraemia and serotonin syndrome.","The mental health consequences follow the pharmacology: stimulant psychosis, cannabis-associated psychosis, opioid-associated depression and cognitive dulling, alcohol-related depression and cognitive impairment, and withdrawal states that mimic anxiety and depression.","Loss of tolerance after a period of abstinence, following prison release or inpatient detoxification, is the commonest setting for fatal opioid overdose. This single fact belongs in every discharge plan."],"cards":[["What is the common final pathway for drugs of abuse?","Increased dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens, via the mesolimbic projection from the ventral tegmental area."],["How do cocaine and amphetamine differ pharmacologically?","Cocaine blocks monoamine reuptake transporters. Amphetamine additionally reverses the dopamine transporter and displaces dopamine from vesicles via VMAT2, producing much greater release."],["Explain the pharmacology of alcohol withdrawal seizures.","Chronic alcohol inhibits NMDA receptors and potentiates GABA-A, producing compensatory NMDA upregulation and GABA-A downregulation. On withdrawal, unopposed glutamatergic excitation lowers seizure threshold."]],"note":["Incentive sensitisation, Robinson and Berridge: wanting and liking dissociate with repeated use. Wanting sensitises while liking does not, which is why craving persists after pleasure has gone.","Cannabis and psychosis: dose-response relationship, greatest risk with daily high-potency use and adolescent onset. Causation is supported but not proven.","Naloxone reverses opioid overdose and has a shorter half-life than most opioids, so repeated dosing or infusion may be needed.","Synthetic cannabinoid receptor agonists are full agonists at CB1, unlike THC, and carry a high risk of severe agitation, psychosis and seizures.","GHB, solvents and nitrous oxide are further examinable substances; nitrous oxide inactivates vitamin B12 and can cause subacute combined degeneration of the cord."],"refs":["stahl","semaddict","maudsley"]};
TEACH["3.4.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"In 1848 an iron bar went through the front of Phineas Gage's skull and he stood up and spoke within minutes. He did not lose speech, movement or memory. He lost judgement, restraint and the person his friends recognised. That accident is the whole argument for functional localisation in one sentence: the brain is not a general-purpose soup.","teach":["Frontal lobe: motor cortex (area 4), premotor and supplementary motor, Broca's area (44, 45) in the dominant hemisphere, frontal eye fields, and prefrontal cortex.","Three prefrontal syndromes: dorsolateral, dysexecutive with poor planning, set-shifting and working memory; orbitofrontal, disinhibition, impulsivity and social misconduct; anterior cingulate or medial, apathy, abulia and akinetic mutism.","Parietal lobe: primary somatosensory cortex (3, 1, 2). Dominant parietal lesion gives Gerstmann syndrome, dysgraphia, dyscalculia, finger agnosia and left-right disorientation. Non-dominant gives hemineglect, dressing and constructional apraxia, and anosognosia.","Temporal lobe: primary auditory cortex, Wernicke's area (22) in the dominant hemisphere, hippocampus and amygdala. Bilateral damage gives Kluver-Bucy syndrome with hyperorality, hypersexuality, placidity, hyperphagia and visual agnosia.","Occipital lobe: primary visual cortex (17). Bilateral lesions give cortical blindness, and Anton syndrome when the patient denies it. Balint syndrome from bilateral parieto-occipital damage gives optic ataxia, ocular apraxia and simultanagnosia.","Cingulate gyrus: anterior for conflict monitoring, effort and emotion; posterior part of the default mode network.","Limbic system: hippocampus, amygdala, cingulate, parahippocampal gyrus, mammillary bodies, fornix, septal nuclei.","Cranial nerves: the classical mnemonic order, with I and II being CNS tracts rather than true peripheral nerves. Olfaction is the only sensory modality that does not relay through the thalamus.","Spinal cord: dorsal columns for fine touch, vibration and proprioception, decussating in the medulla; spinothalamic for pain and temperature, decussating within a segment or two of entry; corticospinal for motor, decussating in the medullary pyramids."],"cards":[["Name the three prefrontal syndromes and their features.","Dorsolateral: dysexecutive, impaired planning, set-shifting and working memory. Orbitofrontal: disinhibition, impulsivity, social misconduct, emotional lability. Anterior cingulate or medial: apathy, abulia, akinetic mutism."],["What is Gerstmann syndrome and where is the lesion?","Dysgraphia, dyscalculia, finger agnosia and left-right disorientation, from a lesion of the dominant parietal lobe at the angular gyrus."],["Name the features of Kluver-Bucy syndrome.","Hyperorality, hypersexuality, placidity or loss of fear, hyperphagia, visual agnosia and hypermetamorphosis, from bilateral temporal lobe damage including the amygdala."]],"note":["Gage's accident was on 13 September 1848, not 1835. His doctor was John Martyn Harlow.","Broca's aphasia: non-fluent, effortful, comprehension relatively preserved, repetition impaired, and the patient is aware and frustrated. Wernicke's: fluent, empty, comprehension impaired, repetition impaired, and insight lost.","Conduction aphasia: fluent speech, preserved comprehension, disproportionately impaired repetition, attributed to arcuate fasciculus damage.","Anosognosia and hemineglect follow non-dominant, typically right, parietal lesions.","Utilisation behaviour and imitation behaviour follow frontal lesions and are examples of environmental dependency."],"refs":["agrawal","lishman","oxneuropsy"]};
TEACH["3.4.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The basal ganglia are the brain's committee for movement, and they work by veto. The default position is do nothing. One route through the committee says yes, another says no, and the balance between them decides whether your hand moves. Damage one route and you get too little movement. Damage the other and you get too much.","teach":["Components: caudate, putamen and globus pallidus, with the subthalamic nucleus and substantia nigra functionally included. Caudate plus putamen is the striatum. Putamen plus globus pallidus is the lentiform nucleus.","The direct pathway facilitates movement, via D1 receptors, and inhibits the internal globus pallidus, releasing the thalamus."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lishman","oxneuropsy"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.4.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Deep in the temporal lobe sit two structures the size of your thumb joint. One turns experience into memory. The other decides whether you should be frightened. Take the first out on both sides and you get a man who can no longer make a new memory. Take the second out and you get a person who cannot learn to be afraid of anything.","teach":["Hippocampal formation: dentate gyrus, cornu ammonis fields CA1 to CA4, and subiculum. The trisynaptic circuit runs entorhinal cortex to dentate gyrus via the perforant path, dentate to CA3 via mossy fibres, CA3 to CA1 via Schaffer collaterals.","CA1 is the most vulnerable to hypoxia and ischaemia, sometimes called Sommer's sector. Hippocampal sclerosis in temporal lobe epilepsy involves CA1 and CA3 loss with dentate preservation."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lishman","oxneuropsy"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.4.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The front of the brain is where you decide not to do something. It is the last part to finish growing, finishing in your mid-twenties, and the first part to go in several diseases. When it fails, the person's intelligence, memory and speech can all be intact, and the person is still unrecognisable.","teach":["Prefrontal cortex divides into dorsolateral, ventromedial and orbitofrontal, plus anterior cingulate medially.","Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (areas 9, 46) supports working memory, planning, set-shifting and abstraction. Hypofrontality on functional imaging during cognitive tasks is a replicated finding in schizophrenia."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lishman","oxneuropsy"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.4.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Grey matter does the thinking and white matter does the talking between the thinkers. Cut the cables and the parts still work but they stop cooperating, which is why some of the strangest neurology comes not from damaged regions but from disconnected ones.","teach":["Corpus callosum: the largest commissure, connecting the hemispheres. Genu, body, splenium, and the rostrum. Section produces disconnection syndromes, including alien hand and inability to name objects felt in the left hand.","Fornix: the main hippocampal output, running to the mammillary bodies. Part of the Papez circuit; damage causes amnesia."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lishman","oxneuropsy"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.4.6"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Four small clusters of cells, none bigger than a grain of rice, send fibres to almost the entire brain. That is why a drug acting on one of them changes mood, sleep, appetite, attention and movement all at once. The side effect list of an antidepressant is not a list of accidents. It is a map of where those fibres go.","teach":["Nigrostriatal: substantia nigra pars compacta (A9) to dorsal striatum. Movement. Blockade gives extrapyramidal side effects; degeneration gives Parkinson's disease.","Mesolimbic: ventral tegmental area (A10) to nucleus accumbens, amygdala and hippocampus. Reward and salience. Overactivity is the standard account of positive psychotic symptoms.","Mesocortical: ventral tegmental area to prefrontal cortex. Cognition and motivation. Underactivity is the standard account of negative and cognitive symptoms, and is why D2 blockade can worsen them.","Tuberoinfundibular: arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus to the median eminence, tonically inhibiting prolactin. Blockade raises prolactin.","Noradrenergic: locus coeruleus in the dorsal pons, supplying most of the cortical, hippocampal and cerebellar noradrenaline. Arousal, vigilance, and the stress response.","Serotonergic: raphe nuclei in the brainstem midline. Rostral raphe, chiefly dorsal and median, project to the whole forebrain; caudal raphe project to the spinal cord and modulate pain.","Basal forebrain cholinergic: nucleus basalis of Meynert to neocortex, medial septal nucleus and diagonal band to hippocampus. Attention and memory encoding. Degeneration underlies the cholinergic deficit in Alzheimer's disease.","Brainstem cholinergic: pedunculopontine and laterodorsal tegmental nuclei, projecting to thalamus. Arousal and REM sleep generation.","Corticofugal glutamate: descending projections from cortex to striatum, thalamus, brainstem and spinal cord. The main excitatory output of the cortex, and the anatomy behind the glutamate hypothesis of schizophrenia."],"cards":[["Name the four dopamine pathways with their origins and destinations.","Nigrostriatal: substantia nigra pars compacta to dorsal striatum. Mesolimbic: ventral tegmental area to nucleus accumbens and limbic structures. Mesocortical: ventral tegmental area to prefrontal cortex. Tuberoinfundibular: arcuate nucleus to median eminence."],["Where does most cortical noradrenaline originate?","The locus coeruleus, in the dorsal pons, a small nucleus of a few thousand neurones projecting diffusely to almost the entire cortex, hippocampus and cerebellum."],["Which two nuclei provide the basal forebrain cholinergic projections and to where?","Nucleus basalis of Meynert to the neocortex, and the medial septal nucleus with the diagonal band of Broca to the hippocampus."]],"note":["Antipsychotics cannot select between dopamine pathways, which is why efficacy and extrapyramidal effects and hyperprolactinaemia travel together.","The locus coeruleus contains only around 15,000 neurones per side in humans, yet supplies the whole cortex.","Locus coeruleus and raphe are among the earliest sites of Lewy and tau pathology, which is why depression and sleep disturbance can precede motor or cognitive signs by years.","Serotonergic projections from the caudal raphe to the dorsal horn are the anatomy behind the analgesic effects of SNRIs and tricyclics.","Histaminergic projections arise from the tuberomammillary nucleus; H1 blockade explains sedation and weight gain."],"refs":["stahl","agrawal","lynall"]};
TEACH["3.5.1.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Two small parts of the hypothalamus argue about whether you are hungry. One says eat, the other says stop. Damage the stop signal in an animal and it eats until it is enormous. That circuit is also where several psychiatric drugs land, which is why weight gain is not a lifestyle failure but a pharmacological effect.","teach":["Lateral hypothalamus drives feeding; lesion causes aphagia. Ventromedial hypothalamus signals satiety; lesion causes hyperphagia and obesity. The arcuate nucleus is the main integrator.","Arcuate nucleus contains two opposing populations: NPY/AgRP neurones which are orexigenic, and POMC/CART neurones which are anorexigenic."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","stahl","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.5.1.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Sleep runs a fixed programme in ninety-minute cycles, with the deep repairing part loaded into the first half of the night and the dreaming into the second. That is why sleepwalking happens before midnight and nightmares happen at five, and why a person acting out their dreams is telling you something serious about the next twenty years.","teach":["Architecture: N1, N2 with K complexes and sleep spindles, N3 slow wave, and REM with low-voltage mixed frequency EEG, sawtooth waves, rapid eye movements and muscle atonia.","A cycle lasts about 90 minutes. REM is 20 to 25 per cent of adult sleep and REM periods lengthen across the night. Slow wave sleep is front-loaded into the first third.","Regulation: the two-process model. Process S is homeostatic sleep pressure building with wakefulness; process C is the circadian drive from the suprachiasmatic nucleus, entrained by light through the retinohypothalamic tract.","Melatonin is synthesised in the pineal gland from serotonin and released in darkness under suprachiasmatic control.","Sleep-wake switching: the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus promotes sleep; the ascending reticular activating system, orexin neurones, and histaminergic tuberomammillary neurones promote wakefulness. Orexin stabilises the switch, and its loss causes narcolepsy.","Sleep deprivation: impaired vigilance and sustained attention, microsleeps, irritability, and at extremes perceptual disturbance. Selective REM deprivation produces REM rebound. Total sleep deprivation has a rapid but transient antidepressant effect.","Depression: reduced REM latency, increased REM density, reduced slow wave sleep, early morning wakening, reduced total sleep time.","Primary sleep disorders: narcolepsy, obstructive sleep apnoea, restless legs syndrome, circadian rhythm disorders, and the parasomnias.","REM sleep behaviour disorder is loss of REM atonia with dream enactment, and is a strong prodromal marker of the synucleinopathies, particularly Lewy body dementia and Parkinson's disease."],"cards":[["In which stage do sleepwalking and night terrors occur, and in which do nightmares?","Sleepwalking and night terrors occur in slow wave sleep, N3, in the first third of the night, with little dream recall. Nightmares occur in REM, later in the night, and are recalled."],["Describe the two-process model of sleep regulation.","Process S is homeostatic sleep pressure accumulating with time awake. Process C is the circadian drive from the suprachiasmatic nucleus. Sleep occurs when S is high and C is low."],["What is lost in narcolepsy with cataplexy and what is the HLA association?","Hypothalamic orexin (hypocretin) neurones, with low CSF orexin. Strong association with HLA-DQB1*06:02."]],"note":["Narcolepsy tetrad: excessive daytime sleepiness, cataplexy, sleep paralysis, hypnagogic hallucinations. Only sleepiness is universal.","Most antidepressants suppress REM. Trazodone, mirtazapine and agomelatine are relative exceptions.","Obstructive sleep apnoea causes daytime somnolence, cognitive impairment and depression, and is regularly misdiagnosed as depression in psychiatric clinics.","Total sleep time and slow wave sleep fall with age; sleep fragments and phase-advances.","CBT for insomnia, not hypnotics, is first-line for chronic insomnia in NICE guidance."],"refs":["agrawal","oxoldage","nice"]};
TEACH["3.5.1.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Sexual function needs three things to work: wanting, physical arousal, and the ability to finish. Different drugs break different ones, which is why the question is never just are you having side effects but which part has stopped, because the answer tells you what to change.","teach":["The sexual response cycle: desire, arousal, orgasm, resolution, with Basson's circular model recognising that desire often follows rather than precedes arousal, particularly in women.","Neurochemistry: dopamine promotes desire and arousal; serotonin, particularly 5-HT2, inhibits it; prolactin inhibits desire; nitric oxide mediates erection; noradrenaline mediates ejaculation."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","noptp","icd11"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.5.1.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Aggression comes in two flavours and they are not the same illness. One is hot: a threat arrives, the alarm goes off, and the person lashes out before thinking. The other is cold: planned, purposeful, unemotional. They have different brain signatures, different prognoses and different treatments, and confusing them is a serious clinical error.","teach":["Reactive or affective aggression: impulsive, emotional, provoked, with high autonomic arousal. Associated with amygdala hyperreactivity and reduced prefrontal, particularly ventromedial and orbitofrontal, regulation.","Instrumental or proactive aggression: planned, goal-directed, low autonomic arousal. Associated with callous-unemotional traits and reduced amygdala responsiveness to distress cues, which is the opposite pattern."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","puriforen","noptp"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.5.1.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Pain is not a signal that arrives from an injury. It is a decision the brain makes about how much danger you are in, using the signal as one input among several. That is why the same injury hurts more when you are frightened, and why chronic pain can be entirely real with no ongoing damage anywhere.","teach":["The ascending pathway: nociceptors, A-delta fibres for fast sharp pain and C fibres for slow burning pain, into the dorsal horn, decussating within one or two segments, ascending in the spinothalamic tract to thalamus and then to somatosensory cortex, insula and anterior cingulate.","Lateral pain system, thalamus to somatosensory cortex, encodes location and intensity. Medial pain system, to anterior cingulate and insula, encodes the unpleasantness. This split explains why opioids and cingulotomy can leave pain present but no longer distressing."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","semliaison","nice"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.5.1.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Give somebody a drug that blocks dopamine and you will quiet the psychosis, but the same block reaches the circuit that steadies movement. Within hours a young man's neck may twist and his eyes roll upwards while he is fully awake inside it. That is not a tantrum and not a seizure, and it is fixed in minutes if you know what it is.","teach":["Motor control has three components: the corticospinal system for execution, the basal ganglia for selection and scaling, and the cerebellum for coordination and error correction.","Basal ganglia balance: D1 direct pathway facilitates movement, D2 indirect pathway inhibits it. D2 blockade shifts the balance towards inhibition, producing parkinsonism."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","stahl","agrawal"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.5.1.7"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Neurones that fire together wire together. Donald Hebb wrote that in 1949 without knowing the mechanism, and when the mechanism was found forty years later it turned out to be almost exactly what he described: a receptor that only opens when both sides are active at the same moment.","teach":["Hebb's rule, 1949: when cell A repeatedly takes part in firing cell B, the efficiency of A in firing B increases.","Long-term potentiation is the cellular form. High-frequency stimulation produces a lasting increase in synaptic strength. Calcium entry through the NMDA receptor initiates it; AMPA receptor insertion into the postsynaptic membrane maintains it."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lynall","stahl"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.5.1.8"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A habit is a behaviour that has stopped asking why. At first you do something because of what it gets you. Repeat it enough and the situation itself pulls the behaviour out of you, regardless of the outcome. That shift, from goal to habit, is what makes both compulsions and addictions so hard to argue with.","teach":["Goal-directed behaviour is sensitive to the value of the outcome. Habitual behaviour is stimulus-driven and persists even when the outcome is devalued. Outcome devaluation is the experimental test.","Anatomically, the transition runs from ventral striatum and associative dorsomedial striatum, which support goal-directed action, to dorsolateral striatum, which supports habit."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","nice","noptp"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.5.1.9"] = {"free":true,"simple":"There is a difference between wanting something and enjoying it, and the brain keeps them in different places. In addiction the wanting grows enormous while the enjoying fades, which is why people describe chasing a feeling they no longer get. Nobody is choosing that.","teach":["The reward circuit: ventral tegmental area to nucleus accumbens, with amygdala, hippocampus, orbitofrontal and anterior cingulate cortex, and ventral pallidum as output.","Dopamine encodes reward prediction error, not pleasure. It fires to unexpected reward, falls silent when reward is fully predicted, and dips when expected reward is omitted. It transfers to the cue that predicts reward, which is why cues drive craving.","Wanting and liking are dissociable, Berridge and Robinson. Dopamine mediates incentive salience, the wanting. Hedonic pleasure, the liking, is mediated by opioid and endocannabinoid hedonic hotspots. In addiction, wanting sensitises while liking does not.","Anhedonia in depression maps onto reduced reward anticipation more than reduced consummatory pleasure, and is associated with blunted ventral striatal response to reward.","Psychosis: aberrant salience, Kapur. Dysregulated dopamine assigns significance to irrelevant stimuli, and the delusion is the explanation the person constructs for that experience. This unites the pharmacology with the phenomenology.","Emotional instability in borderline personality disorder involves altered reward and threat processing with reduced prefrontal regulation of amygdala responses.","Clinical translation: negative symptoms and depressive anhedonia are motivational deficits with a circuit basis, not laziness, and they respond poorly to D2 blockade because D2 blockade is part of the problem in mesocortical projections.","Behavioural addictions, gambling and gaming, engage the same circuit, which is why ICD-11 recognises gambling and gaming disorder as disorders due to addictive behaviours."],"cards":[["What does phasic dopamine firing actually encode?","Reward prediction error: the difference between reward received and reward expected. It transfers over learning from the reward itself to the cue that predicts it."],["Distinguish wanting from liking and say what happens in addiction.","Wanting is incentive salience, mediated by mesolimbic dopamine. Liking is hedonic pleasure, mediated by opioid and endocannabinoid hotspots. In addiction wanting sensitises while liking does not, producing intense craving without corresponding pleasure."],["State the aberrant salience hypothesis of psychosis.","Kapur: dysregulated dopamine release assigns motivational significance to stimuli that do not merit it. The delusion is a cognitive explanation the person constructs for this abnormal experience of significance."]],"note":["Kapur, Psychosis as a state of aberrant salience, American Journal of Psychiatry, 2003.","Dopamine agonists in Parkinson's disease cause impulse control disorders, gambling, hypersexuality and compulsive shopping, in a substantial minority. This is a direct human demonstration of the circuit.","Ventral striatal reward anticipation is blunted in depression and in schizophrenia with prominent negative symptoms.","Contingency management, which pays for verified abstinence, has the strongest evidence in stimulant dependence and works directly on this circuit.","Anhedonia is a transdiagnostic construct and is a Research Domain Criteria target."],"refs":["stahl","agrawal","semaddict"]};
TEACH["3.5.1.10"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Feeling something and managing what you feel are two different jobs done by two different parts of the brain, and they are connected by a cable. When the cable is weak, the feeling wins. That is the shape of most emotional dysregulation you will meet, whatever diagnosis is written above the bed.","teach":["Generation: the amygdala evaluates salience and threat, with input from a fast subcortical thalamic route and a slower cortical route. Insula supports interoception, the sense of the body's internal state.","Regulation: ventromedial and dorsolateral prefrontal cortex and anterior cingulate exert top-down control over the amygdala. The strength of this connectivity predicts regulatory capacity."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","gabbard","noptp"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.5.1.11"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Perception is a guess your brain makes and then checks. Most of the time the check passes and you never notice the guessing. Turn the sensory input down, as deafness or blindness does, and the guessing carries on unchecked. That is why a person who is going deaf may start hearing things, and it is not the same as going mad.","teach":["Visual pathway: retina, optic nerve, chiasm, optic tract, lateral geniculate nucleus, optic radiation, primary visual cortex. Then the dorsal where stream to parietal cortex and the ventral what stream to temporal cortex.","Auditory pathway: cochlea, cochlear nuclei, superior olive, lateral lemniscus, inferior colliculus, medial geniculate nucleus, primary auditory cortex in Heschl's gyrus."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","oyebode","lishman"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.5.1.12"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Attention is a doorman, and in ADHD the doorman is not asleep. He is being pulled in six directions at once and cannot decide who to let in. That is why the same child can be unable to sit through five minutes of maths and lose four hours to something absorbing. It is not a shortage of attention, it is a failure to regulate where it goes.","teach":["Attention networks, Posner: alerting, orienting, and executive control. ADHD affects the executive control network most consistently.","The circuit: prefrontal cortex, particularly dorsolateral and inferior frontal, with striatum, and the fronto-striato-cerebellar loops. Reduced volume and activity are reported in these regions.","Neurochemistry: dopamine and noradrenaline. Catecholamine signalling in prefrontal cortex follows an inverted U, so both too little and too much impair function. This is why stimulants improve attention at optimal doses and impair it at high ones.","Stimulants: methylphenidate blocks the dopamine and noradrenaline transporters. Amphetamines additionally reverse the transporters and release stored transmitter.","Non-stimulants: atomoxetine is a selective noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor; guanfacine is an alpha-2A adrenoceptor agonist strengthening prefrontal network connectivity.","Neurodevelopment: Shaw's work showed delayed rather than deviant cortical maturation, with peak cortical thickness reached around three years later than in controls, most marked in prefrontal regions.","Executive deficits are not universal in ADHD, and no cognitive test diagnoses it. Diagnosis is clinical, requiring symptoms before age 12, in two or more settings, with functional impairment.","The Continuous Performance Test measures sustained attention and is impaired in both ADHD and schizophrenia, which limits its diagnostic specificity."],"cards":[["Name Posner's three attention networks and say which is most affected in ADHD.","Alerting, orienting and executive control. The executive control network is most consistently affected."],["Explain the inverted U relationship for catecholamines in prefrontal cortex.","Prefrontal function is optimal at moderate dopamine and noradrenaline levels and impaired by both too little and too much, which is why stimulants improve attention at therapeutic doses and impair it at excessive ones."],["What did Shaw's neuroimaging work show in ADHD?","Delayed rather than deviant cortical maturation, with peak cortical thickness attained around three years later than in controls, most marked in prefrontal cortex."]],"note":["ADHD prevalence around 5 per cent in children and 2.5 per cent in adults; around two thirds have persisting symptoms into adulthood. Heritability is around 70 to 80 per cent.","Stimulant monitoring: height, weight, pulse and blood pressure. Growth suppression, appetite loss and insomnia are the common adverse effects.","Untreated ADHD is associated with increased risk of substance misuse; treatment does not increase and may reduce it.","NICE: parent training first in children under five and alongside medication thereafter; methylphenidate first-line medication in children, methylphenidate or lisdexamfetamine in adults.","Item 3.9.2 covers the integrated neurobiology of ADHD."],"refs":["agrawal","rutter","nice"]};
TEACH["3.5.1.13"] = {"free":false,"simple":"In dementia the problem is putting memories in. In post-traumatic stress disorder the problem is that one memory will not stay in. Both are memory disorders and they are almost opposite, and treating one as though it were the other is how people get hurt.","teach":["Systems: hippocampus and medial temporal lobe for encoding and consolidation of episodic memory; temporal neocortex for semantic memory; striatum and cerebellum for procedural memory; prefrontal cortex for working memory and retrieval strategy.","Consolidation transfers memory from hippocampal dependence to distributed neocortical storage over time, which is why remote memory survives hippocampal damage and recent memory does not. This is Ribot's law, the temporal gradient of retrograde amnesia."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lishman","nice"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.5.1.14"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Executive function is the part of you that says not yet. It plans, holds a goal steady while you get distracted, changes tack when the rules change, and stops you saying the thing. When the front of the brain underperforms, none of those work, and the person looks careless rather than ill.","teach":["Components: working memory, inhibition, set-shifting, planning, fluency, and monitoring. Miyake's model reduces these to updating, inhibition and shifting.","Anatomy: dorsolateral prefrontal cortex for working memory and set-shifting; inferior frontal gyrus and pre-supplementary motor area for response inhibition; anterior cingulate for conflict monitoring and error detection; orbitofrontal for value-based and reversal learning."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lynall","semgen"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.5.1.15"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There are two ways to know what somebody else is feeling. You can work it out, which is cold and deliberate, or you can feel a version of it yourself, which is automatic. Autism affects the first more than the second. Psychopathy affects the second more than the first, which is why a person can read you perfectly and not care at all.","teach":["Cognitive empathy, or theory of mind, is inferring another's mental states. Affective empathy is sharing another's emotional state. They are dissociable, anatomically and clinically.","Theory of mind network: temporoparietal junction, medial prefrontal cortex, superior temporal sulcus, and temporal poles."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","rutter","puriforen"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.5.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"When you stop doing anything in particular, your brain does not switch off. A specific network comes on: the one that daydreams, remembers, imagines the future and thinks about yourself. Another network watches for anything important enough to interrupt it. In several illnesses, the switching between the two goes wrong.","teach":["The default mode network comprises medial prefrontal cortex, posterior cingulate and precuneus, angular gyrus, and medial temporal lobe. It is active at rest and deactivates during externally focused tasks.","Its functions are self-referential thought, autobiographical memory, prospection, and mentalising."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lynall","agrawal"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.6.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The hypothalamus is the brain's control panel for the body's hormones. It sends short chemical orders down a private blood supply to the pituitary just below it, and the pituitary passes the message on to the thyroid, the adrenals, the gonads and the breast. Break any link in that chain and psychiatric symptoms are often the first thing anybody notices.","teach":["The hypothalamus releases hormones into the hypophyseal portal system, a private capillary network to the anterior pituitary. This is a vascular link, not a neural one.","The releasing factors: CRH drives ACTH; TRH drives TSH and prolactin; GnRH drives LH and FSH; GHRH drives growth hormone, opposed by somatostatin; dopamine tonically inhibits prolactin.","Prolactin is the exception in being under tonic inhibition rather than stimulation. Block dopamine and prolactin rises. This one fact explains antipsychotic hyperprolactinaemia entirely.","The posterior pituitary is neural, not vascular. Oxytocin and vasopressin are synthesised in the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei and transported down axons for release.","Feedback: long loop from the target gland hormone back to hypothalamus and pituitary; short loop from the pituitary hormone back to hypothalamus.","Clinical translation: hypothyroidism presents as depression with fatigue and cognitive slowing; hyperthyroidism as anxiety, and in older people as apathetic depression. Check thyroid function in every new mood presentation.","Cushing's syndrome produces depression in a majority and sometimes psychosis or mania, particularly with exogenous steroids. Addison's disease produces depression, apathy and fatigue.","Vasopressin and SIADH: hyponatraemia from SSRIs, carbamazepine or antipsychotics presents as confusion, drowsiness and seizures, and is commonest in older women in the first month of treatment."],"cards":[["Which anterior pituitary hormone is under tonic inhibition, and by what?","Prolactin, tonically inhibited by dopamine from the tuberoinfundibular pathway. Dopamine blockade therefore raises prolactin."],["How does the hypothalamus communicate with the anterior and posterior pituitary?","With the anterior pituitary through the hypophyseal portal blood system, a vascular link carrying releasing factors. With the posterior pituitary through direct neuronal projections from the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei."],["Which endocrine disorders most commonly present psychiatrically?","Hypothyroidism as depression with fatigue and cognitive slowing; hyperthyroidism as anxiety or, in older people, apathetic depression; Cushing's syndrome as depression, sometimes psychosis or mania; Addison's disease as depression and fatigue."]],"note":["Lithium causes hypothyroidism in up to 20 per cent, and hyperparathyroidism with hypercalcaemia. Check thyroid, calcium and renal function every six months.","Antipsychotics most likely to raise prolactin: amisulpride, risperidone, paliperidone and first-generation drugs. Least: aripiprazole, quetiapine, clozapine.","Sustained hyperprolactinaemia causes galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea, sexual dysfunction and reduced bone mineral density.","A prolactin above 2000 mIU/L, or above roughly 5000, warrants pituitary imaging to exclude a prolactinoma rather than assuming a drug cause.","Growth hormone response to clonidine is a research probe of noradrenergic function."],"refs":["agrawal","maudsley","maudphys"]};
TEACH["3.6.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Stress is a cascade with a brake on it. The hypothalamus shouts, the pituitary passes it on, the adrenal releases cortisol, and the cortisol goes back and tells the first two to stop. In long-term depression the brake works badly, which is what the old dexamethasone test was trying to demonstrate.","teach":["The HPA axis: CRH from the paraventricular nucleus, ACTH from the anterior pituitary, cortisol from the adrenal cortex, with negative feedback at pituitary, hypothalamus and hippocampus.","Cortisol has a diurnal rhythm, peaking shortly after waking, the cortisol awakening response, and reaching its trough around midnight.","Two receptor types: mineralocorticoid receptors, high affinity, occupied at basal levels and mediating tonic feedback; glucocorticoid receptors, lower affinity, occupied at stress levels and mediating the shutdown.","In melancholic depression there is hypercortisolaemia, blunted diurnal variation and impaired feedback. Around half of patients with melancholic depression fail to suppress on the dexamethasone suppression test. Sensitivity is too low for diagnostic use.","Glucocorticoid receptor resistance is the leading mechanism, with reduced sensitivity to feedback rather than absent feedback.","Effects of glucocorticoids: catabolic, immunosuppressive, hyperglycaemic. In the brain, prolonged elevation is associated with hippocampal dendritic atrophy and reduced neurogenesis, and impairs declarative memory.","Steroid psychiatry: exogenous corticosteroids commonly cause euphoria, insomnia and irritability, and at higher doses mania or psychosis. Depression is commoner with chronic use.","PTSD is the exception to the pattern: cortisol is typically low or normal with enhanced negative feedback, the opposite of melancholic depression.","Early life adversity produces long-lasting HPA changes, a leading mechanism linking childhood maltreatment to adult psychiatric disorder."],"cards":[["Describe the HPA axis and the site of negative feedback.","CRH from the hypothalamic paraventricular nucleus stimulates ACTH from the anterior pituitary, which stimulates cortisol from the adrenal cortex. Cortisol feeds back negatively at the pituitary, hypothalamus and hippocampus."],["What is dexamethasone non-suppression and how useful is it?","Failure of dexamethasone to suppress cortisol, found in around half of patients with melancholic depression. Sensitivity and specificity are too low for diagnostic use."],["How does the HPA axis differ in PTSD compared with melancholic depression?","In PTSD cortisol is typically low or normal with enhanced glucocorticoid negative feedback. In melancholic depression cortisol is elevated with impaired feedback."]],"note":["The cortisol awakening response is the rise of 50 to 75 per cent in the 30 to 45 minutes after waking.","Prolonged hypercortisolaemia is associated with hippocampal volume reduction, contributing to memory impairment in depression and Cushing's syndrome.","Steroid-induced psychiatric effects are dose-related; risk rises markedly above about 40 mg prednisolone daily.","Mifepristone and other glucocorticoid receptor antagonists have been trialled in psychotic depression with inconsistent results.","Allostatic load describes the cumulative physiological cost of chronic stress across systems."],"refs":["agrawal","noptp","semliaison"]};
TEACH["3.6.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Hormones do not simply respond to mood, they help produce it. That is why a thyroid blood test belongs in the first set of investigations for depression, and why missing it means treating an endocrine disease with an antidepressant for months.","teach":["Depression: hypercortisolaemia with impaired feedback in melancholic subtypes; blunted TSH response to TRH in around a quarter; reduced growth hormone response to clonidine; and reduced nocturnal melatonin.","Bipolar disorder: hypothyroidism is associated with rapid cycling and is commoner on lithium. Check thyroid function before and during lithium treatment."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudphys","noptp","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.6.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"When you have flu you do not just feel ill in your body. You withdraw, lose interest, sleep badly and cannot concentrate, which is a fair description of depression. That overlap is not a coincidence: the immune system talks directly to the brain, and it uses some of the same language.","teach":["Sickness behaviour, produced by inflammatory cytokines, comprises social withdrawal, anhedonia, fatigue, anorexia, reduced concentration and altered sleep. It is adaptive in acute illness and overlaps substantially with depression.","Cytokines reach the brain through circumventricular organs lacking a blood-brain barrier, by active transport, via vagal afferents, and by acting on cerebral endothelium."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lynall","oxneuropsy"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.7.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Heritability is the most misunderstood number in psychiatry. It does not say how much of your illness came from your genes. It says how much of the variation between people in a particular population, living in particular conditions, tracks with genetic variation. Change the conditions and the number changes.","teach":["Basic concepts: 23 pairs of chromosomes, 22 autosomal plus the sex pair. Mitosis produces identical daughter cells; meiosis produces gametes with recombination and independent assortment. Transcription makes mRNA, translation makes protein.","Family studies establish that a disorder aggregates in families but cannot separate genes from shared environment.","Twin studies compare monozygotic and dizygotic concordance. Heritability approximates twice the difference in correlation. The equal environments assumption is the standard criticism.","Adoption studies separate genetic from rearing environment and are the strongest natural design. The Danish adoption studies of schizophrenia are the classic.","Linkage analysis tracks co-segregation of a marker with disease within families, scored as a lod score, with 3 conventionally taken as significant. It works well for single-gene disorders and failed for common psychiatric illness.","Association studies compare allele frequencies between cases and controls. Genome-wide association studies do this across millions of variants, requiring p less than 5 x 10 to the minus 8 and very large samples.","Direct gene analysis sequences the gene itself. Gene tracking follows a linked marker when the gene is unknown.","Gene-environment interaction and correlation, covered in item 2.1, mean that heritability and environmental effects are not cleanly separable.","Key figures: schizophrenia heritability around 80 per cent, monozygotic concordance 40 to 50 per cent and dizygotic 10 to 15 per cent. The gap between 100 per cent shared genes and 50 per cent concordance is the space environment occupies."],"cards":[["Give monozygotic and dizygotic concordance for schizophrenia and the implication.","Around 40 to 50 per cent monozygotic and 10 to 15 per cent dizygotic. Since identical twins share all their genes but agree less than half the time, non-genetic factors must be substantial."],["What is a lod score and what value is conventionally significant?","The logarithm of the odds that a marker and a disease locus are linked rather than segregating independently. A lod score of 3, odds of 1000 to 1, is conventionally taken as significant linkage."],["State the equal environments assumption and why it matters.","Twin studies assume that monozygotic and dizygotic twin pairs share environments to a comparable extent. If monozygotic twins are treated more similarly, heritability is overestimated."]],"note":["Lifetime risk of schizophrenia: general population about 1 per cent; one affected parent about 13 per cent; sibling about 9 per cent; both parents about 40 per cent; monozygotic co-twin about 48 per cent.","Heritability estimates: bipolar disorder 60 to 85 per cent, autism above 80 per cent, ADHD 70 to 80 per cent, major depression 35 to 40 per cent, anxiety disorders 30 to 40 per cent.","Genome-wide significance is 5 x 10 to the minus 8, correcting for approximately one million independent tests.","Missing heritability describes the gap between twin-based heritability and variance explained by identified variants.","Genetic counselling in psychiatry uses empirical recurrence risks, not genotyping, for common disorders."],"refs":["agrawal","noptp","rutter"]};
TEACH["3.7.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Some psychiatric conditions come with a genetic address you can look up. A child with a particular deletion on chromosome 22 has about a one in four chance of developing schizophrenia, which is twenty-five times the population risk. Those conditions are rare, but recognising one changes everything you do next.","teach":["Chromosomal abnormalities: Down syndrome, trisomy 21, with virtually universal Alzheimer neuropathology by the fifth decade because APP is on chromosome 21. Klinefelter, 47,XXY. Turner, 45,X.","22q11.2 deletion, velocardiofacial or DiGeorge syndrome: cardiac anomalies, palatal abnormality, hypocalcaemia, learning difficulties, and a lifetime schizophrenia risk of roughly 25 per cent. It is the strongest known single genetic risk factor for schizophrenia."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","oxid","ssbp"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.7.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"To study a gene you first have to cut it out, copy it enough times to see, and then find it in a mixture. Every technique in this section is one of those three jobs, and they were invented in that order.","teach":["Restriction enzymes are bacterial endonucleases cutting DNA at specific palindromic recognition sequences, producing reproducible fragments.","Molecular cloning inserts a DNA fragment into a vector, plasmid, phage or artificial chromosome, propagated in a host cell to generate many copies."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","oxid"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["3.7.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Two people with the same diagnosis may have completely different genetics, and two people with the same genetic change may look completely different. That double mismatch is the central problem of psychiatric genetics and the reason the search for a gene for anything mostly failed.","teach":["Genetic heterogeneity: locus heterogeneity means mutations at different loci produce the same phenotype; allelic heterogeneity means different mutations at the same locus do so.","Phenotypic heterogeneity is the reverse: variable expressivity means the same genotype produces different severity or features; incomplete penetrance means some carriers show nothing at all."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lynall","noptp"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["3.7.5"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Identical twins have identical genes and do not always get the same illness. Something must be switching genes on and off differently in the two of them. Epigenetics is that switching, and the surprising part is that experience can operate the switches.","teach":["Epigenetics is heritable-through-cell-division change in gene expression without alteration of the DNA sequence.","Three mechanisms: DNA methylation, typically at CpG islands and generally silencing; histone modification, with acetylation generally opening chromatin and activating and deacetylation closing it; and non-coding RNAs, particularly microRNAs, regulating translation.","Meaney and Szyf's rat work is the foundational study: high maternal licking and grooming altered methylation of the glucocorticoid receptor promoter in offspring hippocampus, producing a lifelong difference in stress reactivity. Cross-fostering showed it was the care, not the genes.","Human parallel: altered glucocorticoid receptor promoter methylation has been reported in the brains of suicide victims with a history of childhood abuse.","Drugs modify epigenetics. Valproate is a histone deacetylase inhibitor. Antidepressants alter methylation of BDNF. Psychological therapy has been associated with methylation change, though this literature is small.","Transgenerational transmission: the Dutch Hunger Winter cohort shows altered methylation and metabolic outcomes decades after prenatal famine exposure. True transgenerational epigenetic inheritance across the germline in humans is not established.","Imprinting is a normal epigenetic phenomenon: parent-of-origin-specific expression, as in Prader-Willi and Angelman syndromes.","Read this literature carefully. Peripheral blood methylation is a poor proxy for brain, cell type composition confounds many studies, and the field has a replication problem."],"cards":[["Name the three principal epigenetic mechanisms.","DNA methylation, histone modification such as acetylation and deacetylation, and regulation by non-coding RNAs including microRNAs."],["Describe Meaney and Szyf's maternal care experiment and its significance.","High levels of maternal licking and grooming in rats reduced methylation of the glucocorticoid receptor promoter in offspring hippocampus, increasing receptor expression and reducing lifelong stress reactivity. Cross-fostering demonstrated that the effect was caused by maternal behaviour rather than by inherited genotype."],["Which mood stabiliser is a histone deacetylase inhibitor?","Sodium valproate."]],"note":["Methylation of CpG islands in promoter regions generally silences transcription; histone acetylation generally activates it.","The Dutch Hunger Winter cohort is the standard human example of prenatal exposure with lasting epigenetic and metabolic effects.","Peripheral blood methylation correlates poorly with brain methylation, which limits most human psychiatric epigenetic studies.","Monozygotic twin discordance for schizophrenia is a major motivation for epigenetic research.","Epigenetic clocks estimate biological age from methylation and show accelerated ageing in some psychiatric populations."],"refs":["agrawal","lynall","rutter"]};
TEACH["3.7.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"For the first time we can go into a living genome and edit a specific letter. The tool came from a bacterial defence system that cuts up invading viruses, and it works because you can hand it a short piece of RNA telling it exactly where to cut.","teach":["CRISPR, clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats, with CRISPR-associated Cas proteins, is a bacterial adaptive immune system repurposed for genome engineering.","Mechanism: a guide RNA directs the Cas9 nuclease to a complementary genomic sequence adjacent to a protospacer adjacent motif, where it produces a double-strand break."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lynall"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["3.8.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A brain is built in a strict order, and the order matters more than the speed. Cells are made, they crawl to where they belong, they wire up, and then most of the wiring is deliberately destroyed. Interrupt any of those steps at the wrong week and the consequences show up decades later.","teach":["Sequence: neural tube formation by week four, then proliferation, migration, differentiation, synaptogenesis, myelination, and pruning.","Neuronal migration is largely complete by the sixth month of gestation, guided by radial glia, building the cortex inside-out so that later-born neurones pass earlier ones to occupy more superficial layers."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lynall","rutter"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.8.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Some illnesses are not things that happen to a finished brain. They are things that happen while the brain is still being built, and then wait. The symptoms may not arrive for twenty years, but the trouble started before the person could walk.","teach":["The neurodevelopmental hypothesis of schizophrenia, Murray and Lewis, and Weinberger, 1987: an early disturbance of brain development interacts with normal maturational processes, particularly adolescent synaptic pruning, producing symptoms in early adulthood.","Supporting evidence: excess obstetric complications; minor physical anomalies; delayed motor and language milestones; premorbid cognitive and social difficulty documented in birth cohorts; absence of gliosis at post-mortem; and enlarged ventricles present at first episode before treatment."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","rutter","semgen"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.8.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Attachment is not sentiment, it is biology. A baby held by somebody who reads it accurately grows a stress system with a working brake. A baby left to manage alone grows one without. That difference is measurable in hormones decades later.","teach":["Attachment behaviour is regulated by a circuit including amygdala, hypothalamus, ventral striatum, anterior cingulate and orbitofrontal cortex, modulated by oxytocin, vasopressin and endogenous opioids.","Oxytocin, from the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei, is released during birth, breastfeeding and affiliative contact, and reduces amygdala reactivity to social threat."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","rutter","lynall"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["3.8.4.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Stress does not just feel bad, it rebuilds tissue. Sustained high cortisol shrinks the branches of neurones in the hippocampus and grows them in the amygdala. The part that remembers gets smaller and the part that fears gets bigger, which is a fair physical description of what chronic stress does to a person.","teach":["Glucocorticoids act on mineralocorticoid receptors at basal levels and glucocorticoid receptors at stress levels. Sustained elevation produces structural change.","The classic pattern: hippocampal dendritic atrophy and reduced neurogenesis; amygdala dendritic hypertrophy; and prefrontal dendritic retraction with impaired top-down regulation. Opposite directions in different structures."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","lynall","noptp"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.8.4.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Talking therapy changes the brain, and this is not a figure of speech. Scan somebody before and after a course of CBT for OCD and the overactive circuit has quietened, by roughly the same amount as it does with medication. Learning is a physical process, and psychotherapy is a form of learning.","teach":["Learning produces measurable structural change. London taxi drivers show increased posterior hippocampal grey matter proportional to years of experience, and juggling training produces measurable cortical change within weeks.","OCD: Baxter's studies showed caudate hypermetabolism normalising after successful treatment with either behaviour therapy or fluoxetine, to a comparable degree. This is the foundational demonstration that psychotherapy changes the brain."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","gabbard","lynall"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.8.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Intelligence is one of the most heritable things about a person and one of the most modifiable, which sounds contradictory until you remember that heritability depends on the environment. In a country where everybody gets good schooling, differences that remain are more genetic. Where schooling varies, they are less so.","teach":["IQ is defined with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15, and is normally distributed by construction. Two standard deviations below the mean, an IQ under 70, is the psychometric threshold for intellectual disability.","Intellectual disability requires three things: significantly impaired intellectual functioning, significantly impaired adaptive functioning, and onset during the developmental period. IQ alone is never sufficient."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxid","agrawal","rutter"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.8.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The same injury at two different ages does two different things. A blow to the front of the head in a child may leave school reports unchanged for years and then show up as an adult who cannot hold a job. Damage to a developing brain waits for the function it destroyed to be needed.","teach":["Kennard principle: earlier injury allows greater functional reorganisation. It is only partly true. Early diffuse injury can produce worse outcomes than the same injury later, because it disrupts the developing system rather than a completed one.","Growing into deficit: a frontal injury in early childhood may appear silent until the executive demands of adolescence and adulthood arrive and reveal it."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lishman","oxneuropsy","agrawal"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.9.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Autism is not a broken social module. It is a brain that processes detail superbly and context poorly, wired with too many local connections and too few long-distance ones. That single idea explains the strengths as well as the difficulties, which is why describing it only as a deficit gets it wrong.","teach":["Genetics: heritability above 80 per cent. Highly polygenic, with rare de novo copy number variants and single-gene syndromes such as fragile X, tuberous sclerosis and Rett syndrome accounting for a minority.","Connectivity: the leading account is local over-connectivity with long-range under-connectivity, giving strong detail processing and weak integration.","Early brain overgrowth in the first two to three years, with increased head circumference, followed by an atypical trajectory rather than continued excess.","Excitation-inhibition imbalance, with reduced GABAergic inhibition, is proposed and is consistent with the high rate of epilepsy.","Cognitive theories: theory of mind deficit, Baron-Cohen; weak central coherence, Frith, meaning a bias to detail over gestalt; and executive dysfunction. None alone accounts for the whole picture.","Cognitive empathy is impaired while affective empathy is relatively preserved. The double empathy problem notes the misunderstanding is mutual.","Environmental risk: advanced paternal age, prematurity, maternal infection and valproate exposure in pregnancy. There is no link with MMR vaccination; the 1998 paper was retracted and its author struck off.","Epidemiology: prevalence around 1 to 2 per cent, male to female ratio about 3 to 1 with substantial underdiagnosis in girls, who more often camouflage."],"cards":[["What is the leading connectivity account of autism?","Local over-connectivity with long-range under-connectivity, producing enhanced detail processing and impaired integration of information across regions."],["Name three cognitive theories of autism.","Theory of mind deficit (Baron-Cohen), weak central coherence (Frith), and executive dysfunction. Each explains part of the presentation and none explains all of it."],["Name the established environmental risk factors for autism.","Advanced paternal age, prematurity and low birth weight, maternal infection in pregnancy, and sodium valproate exposure in utero. MMR vaccination is not a risk factor."]],"note":["Heritability above 80 per cent; sibling recurrence risk around 10 to 20 per cent.","Single-gene and syndromic causes account for roughly 10 to 20 per cent: fragile X, tuberous sclerosis, Rett syndrome, 22q11.2 and 16p11.2.","Epilepsy affects around 20 to 30 per cent, with a bimodal onset in early childhood and adolescence.","No medication treats core autism. Risperidone and aripiprazole are licensed for associated irritability and aggression only.","Intranasal oxytocin has produced inconsistent trial results and is not an established treatment."],"refs":["rutter","agrawal","nice"]};
TEACH["3.9.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"ADHD is a problem of regulation rather than of attention itself, and the chemistry has a strange shape: too little prefrontal dopamine impairs function and so does too much. That inverted U is why the right dose helps and a bigger dose makes things worse.","teach":["Circuit: fronto-striatal and fronto-cerebellar loops, with reduced volume and activation in dorsolateral and inferior frontal cortex, caudate, and cerebellar vermis.","Neurochemistry: dopamine and noradrenaline. Prefrontal catecholamine signalling follows an inverted U, so both deficiency and excess impair function."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","agrawal","nice"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.9.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Addiction moves through three stages and each has its own chemistry. First the drug feels good. Then the brain adapts so that not having it feels bad. Finally the front of the brain, the part that would have said no, stops working properly. By the third stage the person is not chasing pleasure. They are avoiding a hole.","teach":["Koob and Volkow's three-stage cycle: binge and intoxication, driven by mesolimbic dopamine and the ventral striatum; withdrawal and negative affect, driven by recruitment of the extended amygdala with CRF and dynorphin; and preoccupation and anticipation, driven by prefrontal dysfunction and craving.","Common final pathway: increased dopamine in the nucleus accumbens, reached by different routes for different drugs.","Incentive sensitisation, Robinson and Berridge: wanting sensitises with repeated use while liking does not, which is why craving persists long after pleasure has gone.","Tolerance is both pharmacokinetic, through enzyme induction, and pharmacodynamic, through receptor downregulation. Withdrawal is the unmasking of those adaptations.","Habit formation: control shifts from ventral to dorsolateral striatum, from goal-directed to habitual, so the behaviour continues despite devalued outcome.","Relapse has three triggers with distinct substrates: drug cues acting through amygdala and prefrontal glutamate; stress acting through CRF in the extended amygdala; and drug priming, a small dose reinstating the whole pattern.","Alcohol pharmacology: GABA-A potentiation and NMDA inhibition, with compensatory NMDA upregulation explaining withdrawal seizures and delirium tremens.","Loss of tolerance after abstinence, following prison release or inpatient detoxification, is the commonest setting for fatal opioid overdose. Naloxone provision at discharge is the response."],"cards":[["Name Koob and Volkow's three stages of addiction with their neural substrates.","Binge and intoxication: mesolimbic dopamine and ventral striatum. Withdrawal and negative affect: extended amygdala, with CRF and dynorphin. Preoccupation and anticipation: prefrontal dysfunction and glutamatergic craving circuits."],["Distinguish wanting from liking in addiction.","Wanting is incentive salience mediated by mesolimbic dopamine and sensitises with repeated use. Liking is hedonic pleasure mediated by opioid and endocannabinoid systems and does not sensitise, so craving increases while enjoyment declines."],["Name the three principal triggers of relapse and their substrates.","Drug-associated cues, through amygdala and prefrontal glutamatergic projections; stress, through CRF in the extended amygdala; and drug priming, a small re-exposure reinstating drug-seeking."]],"note":["Delta FosB accumulates with repeated drug exposure and is proposed as a molecular switch for the persistence of addiction.","Naltrexone, an opioid antagonist, reduces alcohol reward; acamprosate modulates glutamate; disulfiram inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase.","Contingency management has the best evidence in stimulant dependence, where no substitution therapy is licensed.","Gambling and gaming disorder are classified in ICD-11 as disorders due to addictive behaviours, on the basis of shared circuitry.","Section 11 of this syllabus covers substance misuse clinically."],"refs":["semaddict","stahl","agrawal"]};
TEACH["3.9.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Fear is the alarm going off when there is a burglar. Anxiety is the alarm going off when there might be a burglar, one day, possibly. The same circuit produces both, and the difference is whether the threat is present or imagined.","teach":["Core circuit: amygdala detects threat; bed nucleus of the stria terminalis mediates sustained anxiety to uncertain threat; hypothalamus and periaqueductal grey generate the response; ventromedial prefrontal cortex and anterior cingulate regulate it.","The distinction that matters: amygdala mediates phasic fear to imminent specific threat; the bed nucleus of the stria terminalis mediates sustained anxiety to uncertain or distant threat. This maps onto phobia versus generalised anxiety."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","stahl","nice"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.9.5"] = {"free":true,"simple":"In PTSD the memory has not faded because it was never properly filed. It sits in raw sensory form with no time stamp, so when something triggers it the person does not remember the event, they re-live it in the present tense. That is why the treatment is not talking about it but putting it away properly.","teach":["Circuit: amygdala hyperreactivity, reduced ventromedial prefrontal regulation, and hippocampal dysfunction with reduced volume. The prefrontal brake fails while the alarm is over-sensitive.","Hippocampal volume reduction: twin studies suggest it may partly precede trauma and act as a vulnerability factor rather than being purely a consequence.","HPA axis: cortisol is typically low or normal with enhanced negative feedback and increased glucocorticoid receptor sensitivity. This is the opposite of melancholic depression, and it is examined.","Noradrenergic overactivity underlies hyperarousal, and is the rationale for prazosin in trauma nightmares.","Impaired extinction of conditioned fear, with reduced ventromedial prefrontal recruitment, is the best-replicated laboratory finding.","Ehlers and Clark's cognitive model: a poorly elaborated and inadequately contextualised trauma memory, retrieved involuntarily by cues and experienced with a sense of current threat, maintained by negative appraisals and by avoidance and rumination.","Reconsolidation: retrieval briefly returns a memory to a labile state, which is the rationale for propranolol reconsolidation-blockade research. Results are mixed.","ICD-11 complex PTSD adds disturbances in self-organisation to the three core clusters: affect dysregulation, negative self-concept, and difficulties in relationships.","Treatment: trauma-focused CBT and EMDR are recommended. Single-session psychological debriefing is not recommended and may be harmful."],"cards":[["How does the HPA axis in PTSD differ from that in melancholic depression?","In PTSD cortisol is typically low or normal with enhanced negative feedback and increased glucocorticoid receptor sensitivity. In melancholic depression cortisol is elevated with impaired feedback."],["What distinguishes complex PTSD from PTSD in ICD-11?","Complex PTSD includes the three PTSD clusters, re-experiencing, avoidance and sense of current threat, plus disturbances in self-organisation: affect dysregulation, negative self-concept, and difficulties in sustaining relationships."],["Summarise the Ehlers and Clark model of PTSD.","The trauma memory is poorly elaborated and inadequately integrated into autobiographical context, so it is retrieved involuntarily by cues and experienced with a sense of current threat. Negative appraisals of the trauma and its sequelae, and maintaining strategies such as avoidance, rumination and safety behaviours, prevent resolution."]],"note":["Prazosin, an alpha-1 antagonist, is used for trauma nightmares; trial evidence is mixed but it remains widely used.","Psychological debriefing after trauma is not recommended by NICE and may increase later symptoms.","Dissociation at the time of trauma, peritraumatic dissociation, predicts later PTSD.","PTSD is one of the few psychiatric diagnoses that requires a specified external event as a criterion.","Item 3.5.1.13 covers memory in PTSD in more detail."],"refs":["agrawal","nice","semgen"]};
TEACH["3.9.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"OCD is a brain stuck in a loop that says something is wrong, do something about it. The action brings relief for a moment, and the relief is precisely what teaches the loop to run again. That is why the treatment is not doing the action and waiting.","teach":["Circuit: the cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical loop, involving orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate, caudate and thalamus. Hyperactivity here is one of the most replicated functional imaging findings in psychiatry.","Baxter's studies: caudate hypermetabolism normalises after successful treatment with either behaviour therapy or fluoxetine, to a comparable degree."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["agrawal","nice","semgen"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.9.7"] = {"free":true,"simple":"For fifty years the story was that depression is a shortage of serotonin. That story is too simple to be true: the drugs raise serotonin within hours and people get better in weeks. What happens in those weeks is the actual answer, and it is about the brain rebuilding connections rather than topping up a chemical.","teach":["The monoamine hypothesis: depression as deficiency of serotonin, noradrenaline and dopamine. Its evidence was reserpine-induced depression, the action of MAOIs and tricyclics, and tryptophan depletion reversing SSRI response.","Its fatal problem is the time lag. Monoamine levels rise within hours; clinical improvement takes weeks. Something downstream must be doing the work.","The receptor and neuroplasticity account: 5-HT1A somatodendritic autoreceptor desensitisation over two to four weeks permits increased forebrain serotonin, and downstream CREB activation increases BDNF and hippocampal neurogenesis.","The neurotrophic hypothesis: stress lowers BDNF and neurogenesis; antidepressants, exercise and ECT raise them. Blocking neurogenesis in rodents abolishes some behavioural antidepressant effects.","HPA axis: hypercortisolaemia with impaired feedback in melancholic depression; dexamethasone non-suppression in around half of such patients.","Inflammation: elevated IL-6, TNF-alpha and CRP in a subgroup, roughly a quarter to a third. Interferon-alpha reliably induces depression, which is the strongest causal human evidence.","Circuits: reduced dorsolateral prefrontal activity, increased subgenual cingulate activity, amygdala hyperreactivity to negative stimuli, blunted ventral striatal reward response, and default mode network hyperconnectivity correlating with rumination.","Glutamate: ketamine produces antidepressant effect within hours through AMPA throughput and mTOR-dependent synaptogenesis, bypassing the monoamine route entirely. This is the strongest evidence that plasticity rather than monoamine level is the mechanism.","Sleep: reduced REM latency, increased REM density, reduced slow wave sleep, early morning wakening.","Genetics: heritability around 35 to 40 per cent, highly polygenic. The 5-HTTLPR by life events interaction has not replicated in large samples."],"cards":[["What is the principal objection to the simple monoamine hypothesis?","Monoamine levels rise within hours of starting an antidepressant while clinical improvement takes two to four weeks, so the therapeutic mechanism must lie in downstream adaptive changes rather than in the acute rise itself."],["Explain the delay in SSRI response.","Transporter blockade raises synaptic serotonin immediately, but somatodendritic 5-HT1A autoreceptors initially suppress raphe firing. Their desensitisation over two to four weeks permits increased forebrain serotonergic transmission and downstream neuroplastic change."],["Why is ketamine's rapid effect theoretically important?","It produces antidepressant effect within hours through glutamatergic mechanisms, AMPA throughput and mTOR-dependent synaptogenesis, without acting on monoamine transporters, indicating that synaptic plasticity rather than monoamine availability is the critical mechanism."]],"note":["Subgenual cingulate, Brodmann area 25, is the Mayberg deep brain stimulation target in treatment-resistant depression.","Esketamine was licensed in 2019 for treatment-resistant depression and must be given under supervision because of dissociation and blood pressure rise.","Elevated CRP predicts poorer SSRI response and, in some studies, better response to noradrenergic agents.","STAR*D: about a third remitted at step one and around two thirds cumulatively across four steps, with declining rates at each step.","ECT remains the most effective acute treatment for severe depression, particularly with psychotic features or food refusal."],"refs":["stahl","agrawal","nice"]};
TEACH["3.9.8"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Bipolar disorder is not depression with occasional high days. It is an illness of an unstable set point, in which the same brain swings past normal in both directions, and the drug that treats one pole can push the person into the other.","teach":["Genetics: heritability 60 to 85 per cent, among the highest in psychiatry. Highly polygenic with substantial genetic overlap with schizophrenia. CACNA1C and ANK3 are the best-known loci.","Circuits: reduced ventral prefrontal regulation of amygdala, with amygdala hyperreactivity to emotional stimuli. The regulatory failure is bidirectional, permitting swings in both directions."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["stahl","agrawal","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.9.9"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The dopamine hypothesis has been rewritten three times and the current version is the most useful. It is not that there is too much dopamine everywhere. It is that dopamine is released in the wrong place at the wrong time, so ordinary things arrive feeling enormously significant, and the delusion is the explanation the person builds for that experience.","teach":["Version I: too much dopamine, from the fact that all antipsychotics block D2 and that amphetamine causes psychosis.","Version II: regional specificity. Mesolimbic hyperdopaminergia produces positive symptoms; mesocortical hypodopaminergia produces negative and cognitive symptoms. This explains why D2 blockade can worsen negative symptoms.","Version III, Howes and Kapur: the final common pathway is presynaptic striatal dopamine dysregulation. Imaging shows increased dopamine synthesis and release capacity, present in the prodrome, and greatest in dorsal rather than ventral striatum.","Aberrant salience, Kapur: dysregulated dopamine assigns motivational significance to irrelevant stimuli. The delusion is a cognitive explanation for that abnormal experience, which is why antipsychotics dampen conviction rather than argue the belief away.","The glutamate hypothesis: NMDA antagonists such as ketamine and phencyclidine reproduce positive, negative and cognitive symptoms in healthy volunteers, which dopamine agonists do not. NMDA hypofunction on parvalbumin interneurones disinhibits downstream circuits.","GABA: reduced parvalbumin interneurone markers, reduced GAD67 expression, and impaired gamma oscillations.","Structural: enlarged ventricles and reduced grey matter present at first episode before treatment, progressive change over the early years, and reduced dendritic spine density with absence of gliosis.","Neurodevelopmental origin: obstetric complications, delayed milestones, premorbid cognitive difficulty, minor physical anomalies, and complement C4 in synaptic pruning.","Environmental: cannabis, urbanicity, migration and minority status, childhood adversity, older paternal age."],"cards":[["State version III of the dopamine hypothesis.","Howes and Kapur: the final common pathway is presynaptic striatal dopamine dysregulation, with increased dopamine synthesis and release capacity, present before onset in the prodrome and most marked in the dorsal striatum."],["State the aberrant salience hypothesis.","Kapur: dysregulated dopamine release assigns motivational significance to stimuli that do not warrant it. Delusions are the cognitive explanations the person constructs for this abnormal experience of significance, which is why antipsychotics reduce conviction rather than correct the belief directly."],["What evidence supports a glutamatergic contribution to schizophrenia?","NMDA receptor antagonists such as ketamine and phencyclidine reproduce positive, negative and cognitive symptoms in healthy volunteers and exacerbate them in patients, whereas dopamine agonists reproduce only positive symptoms."]],"note":["Antipsychotic efficacy requires roughly 60 to 70 per cent D2 occupancy; extrapyramidal effects appear above about 80 per cent.","Ventricular enlargement is present at first episode before treatment and is therefore not simply a drug effect.","Absence of gliosis argues for a neurodevelopmental rather than degenerative process.","Complement C4 drives the strongest GWAS signal and functions in synaptic pruning, linking genetics to the excessive pruning hypothesis.","Clozapine works in treatment resistance despite low D2 occupancy, which no current hypothesis fully explains."],"refs":["agrawal","stahl","semgen"]};
TEACH["3.9.10"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The symptoms people notice in schizophrenia are the voices and the beliefs. The symptom that decides whether somebody works, lives independently and has friends is the cognitive impairment, and it is the one nobody asks about and no drug treats.","teach":["Domains affected: processing speed, attention and vigilance, working memory, verbal and visual learning and memory, reasoning and problem solving, and social cognition. Processing speed shows the largest effect.","Effect size is around one standard deviation below controls, present across the illness."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semgen","agrawal","lynall"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.9.11"] = {"free":true,"simple":"There is a biological signature associated with acting on impulse in a way that harms you, and it has been found in different countries and different populations: low serotonin turnover. It does not predict who will die, and nothing does. But it tells us that impulsivity, not just sadness, is part of the mechanism.","teach":["The most replicated biological finding: low CSF 5-HIAA, the serotonin metabolite, associated with impulsive aggression, violent suicide attempts and completed suicide.","Postmortem findings: altered serotonin receptor binding in ventral prefrontal cortex, particularly increased 5-HT1A and altered 5-HT2A binding, and fewer serotonin transporter sites.","Ventral prefrontal cortex dysfunction impairs behavioural inhibition and decision-making, which is the neural substrate of the impulsive component.","HPA axis: dysregulation is reported, with both hyper- and hypocortisolaemia described in different populations.","Stress-diathesis model, Mann: suicidal behaviour requires a stressor, typically an acute psychiatric episode or life event, acting on a pre-existing diathesis comprising impulsive-aggressive traits, pessimism and altered serotonergic function.","Genetics: family, twin and adoption studies show heritability of suicidal behaviour independent of the heritability of the psychiatric disorder itself.","Cognitive factors: hopelessness predicts suicide better than depression severity; overgeneral autobiographical memory and impaired problem solving contribute; entrapment and defeat are central to the integrated motivational-volitional model.","Clinical translation: risk assessment tools have poor individual predictive value and must not determine who is treated or discharged. This is explicit NICE guidance. Means restriction is one of very few interventions with population-level evidence.","Lithium reduces suicide in mood disorders, and clozapine reduces suicidality in schizophrenia. These are the two pharmacological findings worth knowing."],"cards":[["What is the most replicated biological finding in suicidal behaviour?","Low cerebrospinal fluid 5-HIAA, the serotonin metabolite, associated with impulsive aggression, violent suicide attempts and completed suicide."],["State Mann's stress-diathesis model of suicidal behaviour.","Suicidal behaviour results from a stressor, typically an acute psychiatric episode or life event, acting on a pre-existing diathesis of impulsive-aggressive traits, pessimism and altered serotonergic function, which is partly independent of the psychiatric disorder itself."],["Which two drugs have specific evidence for reducing suicide?","Lithium in mood disorders, and clozapine for suicidality in schizophrenia."]],"note":["Hopelessness predicts eventual suicide better than depression severity; Beck Hopelessness Scale.","Risk assessment instruments have poor predictive value at the individual level; NICE is explicit that they must not be used to determine treatment or discharge.","The period immediately after discharge from psychiatric inpatient care carries markedly elevated risk.","Means restriction has population-level evidence, including analgesic pack size limits and barriers at known sites.","A safety plan made with the person, naming warning signs, coping steps, contacts and means restriction, is more useful than an assessment alone."],"refs":["semgen","nice","noptp"]};
TEACH["3.9.12"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Unexplained does not mean imaginary. The symptom is real, the distress is real, and the mechanism is real too: the brain is generating a prediction about the body that overrides what the body is actually reporting. Telling somebody there is nothing wrong is both untrue and the fastest way to lose them.","teach":["Terminology: ICD-11 uses bodily distress disorder, defined by excessive attention to distressing bodily symptoms and repeated contact with services, whether or not a medical condition is present. DSM-5 uses somatic symptom disorder. Both moved away from requiring symptoms to be medically unexplained.","Functional neurological disorder is diagnosed on positive signs, not by exclusion: Hoover's sign, tremor entrainment, and inconsistency between formal testing and observed function."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semliaison","icd11","lishman"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.9.13"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Delirium is acute brain failure. The attention system fails first, which is why the person cannot hold a thread, and why the diagnosis is made by asking somebody to count backwards rather than by asking how they feel. It is common, it is dangerous, and the quiet version is the one that gets missed.","teach":["Definition: an acute disturbance of attention and awareness, developing over hours to days, fluctuating, with additional cognitive disturbance, and caused by an underlying medical condition, substance or withdrawal.","The cardinal feature is inattention. Test it: serial sevens, months of the year backwards, digit span.","Neurochemistry: cholinergic deficiency and dopaminergic excess is the core hypothesis, supported by anticholinergic drugs precipitating delirium and by dopaminergic drugs doing the same. Serotonin, GABA, glutamate and melatonin are also implicated.","Neuroinflammation: systemic inflammation activates microglia and disrupts the blood-brain barrier, which is why sepsis and surgery are such potent precipitants.","EEG shows diffuse slowing with increased theta and delta. Delirium tremens is the exception, showing fast activity.","Subtypes: hyperactive, hypoactive and mixed. Hypoactive is commoner, is routinely missed, and carries a worse prognosis.","Distinguish from dementia: acute onset, fluctuating course, prominent inattention, clouded consciousness, altered sleep-wake cycle, and perceptual disturbance, in a person with an identifiable medical cause. Dementia is chronic and progressive with clear consciousness until late. Both can co-exist, and dementia is the strongest risk factor for delirium.","Delirium is not benign. It is associated with prolonged admission, functional decline, institutionalisation, subsequent dementia and increased mortality.","Management: identify and treat the cause. Environmental measures, orientation, sensory aids, mobilisation, hydration, sleep hygiene. Antipsychotics only for severe distress or risk, at low dose, briefly, and avoided in Lewy body disease and Parkinson's disease."],"cards":[["What is the cardinal diagnostic feature of delirium and how is it tested?","Impaired attention. Test with serial sevens, reciting months of the year backwards, or digit span."],["State the core neurochemical hypothesis of delirium.","Cholinergic deficiency with dopaminergic excess, supported by anticholinergic drugs precipitating delirium and by improvement when anticholinergic burden is reduced."],["Which subtype of delirium is commonest and why does it matter?","Hypoactive delirium. It is frequently missed because the patient is quiet and compliant, and it carries a worse prognosis than the hyperactive form."]],"note":["The 4AT and the Confusion Assessment Method are the standard screening instruments; NICE recommends routine screening in at-risk inpatients.","Dementia is the single strongest risk factor for delirium; others are age, sensory impairment, polypharmacy, surgery, infection and dehydration.","Multicomponent non-pharmacological prevention reduces incidence by around a third.","Delirium is independently associated with subsequent dementia, and the relationship may be causal.","In Lewy body disease and Parkinson's disease dementia, antipsychotics can cause severe deterioration and death."],"refs":["oxoldage","nice","semliaison"]};
TEACH["3.10.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Twenty-five years and tens of billions of pounds went into removing amyloid from the brains of people with Alzheimer's disease. It worked: the amyloid came out. The patients mostly did not get better. That gap between clearing the protein and helping the person is the central controversy of the whole field.","teach":["The amyloid cascade hypothesis: amyloid-beta accumulation is the initiating event, leading to tau pathology, inflammation, synaptic loss and dementia. Genetics support it, since APP, PSEN1 and PSEN2 mutations and Down syndrome all increase amyloid production and cause disease.","The problems: amyloid burden correlates poorly with cognitive severity; a substantial minority of cognitively normal older people are amyloid positive; and repeated amyloid-lowering trials failed to produce clinically meaningful benefit."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","agrawal","nice"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.10.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Auguste Deter was admitted in Frankfurt in November 1901. Asked to write her name, she said, I have lost myself. Alzheimer presented her case in 1906 and the room asked no questions. What he had described turned out not to be a rare disease of the middle-aged but the commonest cause of dependency in old age.","teach":["Pathology: extracellular beta-amyloid plaques and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles of hyperphosphorylated tau, with synaptic and neuronal loss and cortical atrophy.","Distribution: earliest changes in entorhinal cortex and hippocampus, spreading to limbic then neocortical regions. This is Braak staging, and it predicts the clinical sequence.","Macroscopic: generalised cortical atrophy, widened sulci, narrowed gyri, ventricular enlargement, with medial temporal atrophy prominent on coronal MRI.","Neurochemistry: cholinergic deficit from loss of nucleus basalis of Meynert neurones, which is the rationale for cholinesterase inhibitors. Glutamatergic excitotoxicity is the rationale for memantine.","Genetics: early-onset autosomal dominant forms involve APP on chromosome 21, PSEN1 on 14 and PSEN2 on 1, accounting for under 1 per cent of cases. Late-onset is polygenic, with APOE e4 raising risk in a dose-dependent way and e2 protective.","Down syndrome: virtually universal Alzheimer neuropathology by the fifth decade through APP gene dosage on chromosome 21.","Clinical course: insidious amnestic onset with impaired encoding, so cueing does not help; then dysphasia, dyspraxia and agnosia; then behavioural and psychological symptoms; then physical dependency.","Investigation: MRI showing medial temporal atrophy; FDG-PET showing temporoparietal hypometabolism; CSF showing low amyloid-beta 42 with raised total and phosphorylated tau.","Treatment: cholinesterase inhibitors for mild to moderate disease, memantine for moderate to severe or where cholinesterase inhibitors are not tolerated. Neither modifies the underlying disease."],"cards":[["Name the pathological hallmarks of Alzheimer's disease and where they appear first.","Extracellular beta-amyloid plaques and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles of hyperphosphorylated tau, appearing first in the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus and spreading to limbic then neocortical regions."],["How does APOE genotype affect risk?","APOE e4 increases risk of late-onset Alzheimer's disease in a dose-dependent manner, with homozygotes at greatest risk and earlier onset. APOE e2 is protective. Genotyping is not used diagnostically or predictively in practice."],["Which genes cause early-onset autosomal dominant Alzheimer's disease?","APP on chromosome 21, presenilin 1 on chromosome 14, and presenilin 2 on chromosome 1. Together these account for under 1 per cent of all cases."]],"note":["Auguste Deter was admitted 25 November 1901; Alzheimer presented the case on 3 November 1906; she died on 8 April 1906. The frequently cited date of 1897 is wrong.","Prevalence roughly doubles every five years after 65, reaching around 20 per cent over 85.","CSF biomarker profile: reduced amyloid-beta 42, raised total tau and raised phosphorylated tau.","Cholinesterase inhibitor adverse effects: nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, bradycardia, syncope and vivid dreams. Caution with cardiac conduction disease.","Antipsychotics in dementia raise stroke risk roughly threefold and increase mortality; use only for severe distress or risk, at the lowest dose, time-limited."],"refs":["oxoldage","nice","lishman"]};
TEACH["3.10.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Vascular dementia is the one you can prevent. Every stroke, every year of untreated blood pressure and every unmanaged diabetes takes a small piece, and unlike the protein diseases, the causes are all things medicine already knows how to treat.","teach":["Subtypes: multi-infarct dementia from multiple cortical infarcts; subcortical small vessel disease with lacunes and white matter change, which is the commonest; strategic infarct dementia from a single lesion in a critical site such as thalamus or angular gyrus; and mixed dementia with concurrent Alzheimer pathology.","Classical presentation: stepwise deterioration with abrupt onset, patchy deficits, preserved insight and personality until late, focal neurological signs, and emotional lability. The subcortical form is often gradual rather than stepwise, which is why the classical description misleads."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","nice","lishman"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.10.4"] = {"free":true,"simple":"A man in his fifties stops caring. He eats sweets by the packet, says something appalling at a funeral, and his memory is perfect. He is not depressed and he is not being difficult. Frontotemporal dementia takes the personality first and leaves the memory, which is exactly backwards from what everybody expects, and it is why these patients are so often sent to general psychiatry first.","teach":["Three clinical syndromes: behavioural variant, semantic dementia, and progressive non-fluent aphasia.","Behavioural variant: early personality change, disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy, perseverative and stereotyped behaviour, hyperorality and dietary change, with relatively preserved memory and visuospatial function.","Semantic dementia: fluent speech empty of content, loss of word meaning, impaired naming and single-word comprehension, with associative agnosia. Left anterior temporal atrophy.","Progressive non-fluent aphasia: effortful agrammatic speech with apraxia of speech, comprehension of single words preserved. Left perisylvian atrophy.","Pathology: TDP-43 inclusions in around half; tau in most of the remainder, including Pick bodies in classical Pick's disease; a small proportion FUS. The clinical syndrome does not reliably predict the protein.","Genetics: a substantial minority, up to a third, have a family history. C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion is the commonest, and is also the commonest genetic cause of motor neurone disease. MAPT and progranulin are the others.","The overlap with motor neurone disease is important: around 15 per cent of frontotemporal dementia patients develop motor neurone disease, and the combination carries a much shorter survival.","Onset is typically before 65, making it one of the commonest causes of young-onset dementia alongside Alzheimer's disease.","Investigation: MRI showing frontal and anterior temporal atrophy, often asymmetrical; FDG-PET showing frontal and anterior temporal hypometabolism. MMSE may be entirely normal.","Treatment: no disease-modifying or symptomatic cognitive treatment. Cholinesterase inhibitors are not indicated and may worsen behaviour. SSRIs may help behavioural symptoms. Management is largely non-pharmacological and carer-focused."],"cards":[["Name the three clinical syndromes of frontotemporal lobar degeneration.","Behavioural variant frontotemporal dementia, semantic dementia, and progressive non-fluent aphasia."],["Which proteins accumulate in frontotemporal lobar degeneration?","TDP-43 in around half of cases, tau in most of the remainder including Pick bodies, and FUS in a small proportion. The clinical syndrome does not reliably predict the underlying protein."],["Which genetic mutation is commonest in familial frontotemporal dementia and what else does it cause?","C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion, which is also the commonest genetic cause of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis. MAPT and progranulin mutations account for most of the remainder."]],"note":["Onset is typically before 65; it is a leading cause of young-onset dementia.","Around 15 per cent develop motor neurone disease, which markedly shortens survival.","The MMSE is frequently normal in behavioural variant frontotemporal dementia; use frontal and social cognition testing.","Cholinesterase inhibitors are not indicated and may worsen behavioural symptoms.","Behavioural variant FTD is regularly misdiagnosed as depression, mania, personality disorder or midlife crisis before the diagnosis is made."],"refs":["oxoldage","lishman","oxneuropsy"]};
TEACH["3.10.5"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Robin Williams was told he had early Parkinson's disease. After he died the autopsy found something else: Lewy bodies through the whole cortex, among the worst the neuropathologist had seen. He had hallucinations, delusions, insomnia and a collapse in his ability to remember lines. The diagnosis was missed, and it is missed constantly.","teach":["Alpha-synuclein is the protein. Lewy bodies are intracytoplasmic inclusions, brainstem in Parkinson's disease and cortical as well in dementia with Lewy bodies.","Core clinical features of dementia with Lewy bodies: fluctuating cognition with pronounced variation in attention and alertness; recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations; REM sleep behaviour disorder, which may precede everything by years; and one or more spontaneous parkinsonian features.","Supportive features: severe neuroleptic sensitivity, postural instability, repeated falls, syncope, autonomic dysfunction, hypersomnia, hyposmia, delusions and apathy.","Indicative biomarkers: reduced dopamine transporter uptake on DaTSCAN, abnormal MIBG cardiac scintigraphy, and confirmation of REM sleep without atonia on polysomnography.","The one-year rule separates dementia with Lewy bodies from Parkinson's disease dementia: dementia beginning before, with, or within a year of parkinsonism is DLB; more than a year after motor onset is Parkinson's disease dementia. The pathology is the same.","Cognitive profile is attentional, executive and visuospatial rather than primarily amnestic, which is another reason it is mistaken for delirium or depression.","Severe neuroleptic sensitivity occurs in a substantial proportion and can cause irreversible deterioration and death. Antipsychotics must be avoided; if unavoidable, quetiapine or clozapine at very low dose.","Cholinesterase inhibitors work better here than in Alzheimer's disease, particularly for hallucinations and fluctuation. Rivastigmine is licensed for Parkinson's disease dementia.","REM sleep behaviour disorder is the strongest prodromal marker of the synucleinopathies, and most people with it eventually develop one."],"cards":[["Name the four core clinical features of dementia with Lewy bodies.","Fluctuating cognition with pronounced variation in attention and alertness; recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations; REM sleep behaviour disorder; and one or more spontaneous cardinal features of parkinsonism."],["State the one-year rule.","If dementia begins before, concurrently with, or within one year of the onset of parkinsonism, the diagnosis is dementia with Lewy bodies. If it begins more than a year after established Parkinson's disease, it is Parkinson's disease dementia. The underlying pathology is the same."],["Why must antipsychotics be avoided in Lewy body disease?","Severe neuroleptic sensitivity occurs in a substantial proportion, producing marked worsening of parkinsonism, sedation, confusion, autonomic instability and irreversible deterioration, with an associated increase in mortality."]],"note":["Dementia with Lewy bodies is the second or third commonest degenerative dementia and is substantially underdiagnosed.","Multiple system atrophy and pure autonomic failure are the other synucleinopathies; progressive supranuclear palsy and corticobasal degeneration are tauopathies.","Robin Williams was diagnosed with early Parkinson's disease in May 2014 and died in August 2014; autopsy showed diffuse Lewy body disease. His widow published the account in Neurology in 2016.","Cholinesterase inhibitors are more effective for hallucinations and fluctuation in Lewy body disease than in Alzheimer's disease.","REM sleep behaviour disorder confers a high long-term risk of conversion to a synucleinopathy and should be asked about directly."],"refs":["oxoldage","lishman","nice"]};
TEACH["3.10.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A prion is a protein that has folded the wrong way and can persuade its neighbours to fold the same way. It is an infection with no genetic material at all, which is why the idea took so long to be accepted, and why the usual sterilisation does not destroy it.","teach":["The prion protein PrP-C is a normal cellular protein. The disease form PrP-Sc is a misfolded isoform, rich in beta-sheet, which templates the misfolding of normal protein. Prusiner received the 1997 Nobel Prize for the concept.","Pathology: spongiform change, neuronal loss and astrocytosis, with no inflammatory infiltrate, which is unusual for an infection."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lishman","oxneuropsy","agrawal"],"n":{"teach":10,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["3.10.7"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Before effective treatment, HIV commonly reached the brain and produced a dementia in people in their thirties. Modern therapy changed that almost completely. What is left is milder, commoner, and easy to attribute to depression or to the drugs instead.","teach":["HIV enters the CNS early, within days to weeks of infection, carried across the blood-brain barrier in infected monocytes, the Trojan horse mechanism. It infects microglia and macrophages, not neurones directly.","Damage is indirect, through inflammatory cytokines, viral proteins including gp120 and Tat, and excitotoxicity."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lishman","oxneuropsy","semliaison"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.1.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Almost every psychiatric drug we have was found by accident, by somebody paying attention to the wrong result. A drug for surgical shock quietened psychotic wards. A failed antipsychotic lifted depression. A tuberculosis drug made patients cheerful. Not one of them was designed from a theory of the illness, and that is why the theories came afterwards.","teach":["1949: John Cade gave lithium to a man continuously manic for five years, after testing it on guinea pigs. He published ten cases in the Medical Journal of Australia and the world ignored it for twenty-one years.","1952: Delay and Deniker gave chlorpromazine, developed as an antihistamine and surgical potentiator, to psychiatric patients in Paris. The wards changed within months and the asylums began to empty.","1957: Roland Kuhn tested imipramine as an antipsychotic. It did not work for psychosis; it lifted depression instead. The tricyclics followed.","1950s: iproniazid, an antitubercular drug, was noticed to elevate mood, giving the monoamine oxidase inhibitors.","1960: chlordiazepoxide, then diazepam in 1963, gave a sedative that did not kill in overdose. That safety was the problem: everybody was given them, for years.","1971: clozapine was withdrawn after agranulocytosis deaths in Finland. Kane's 1988 trial showed it worked where everything else had failed, and it returned with mandatory blood monitoring.","1987: fluoxetine was licensed. The SSRIs were not more effective than tricyclics; they were safer in overdose and better tolerated, and that changed prescribing entirely.","2019: esketamine was licensed, the first antidepressant in decades acting within hours rather than weeks, and the first working outside the monoamine system.","The pattern to take from this: mechanism was inferred from drug action, not the reverse. The monoamine hypothesis exists because the drugs worked, which is a weak basis for a theory of illness."],"cards":[["Who introduced lithium and chlorpromazine to psychiatry, and when?","John Cade gave lithium in 1948 and published in 1949. Delay and Deniker gave chlorpromazine to psychiatric patients in Paris in 1952."],["How was the antidepressant effect of imipramine discovered?","Roland Kuhn tested it in 1957 as a possible antipsychotic. It failed for psychosis but elevated the mood of depressed patients, and he recognised the significance of the unexpected result."],["Why was clozapine withdrawn and why did it return?","It was withdrawn in 1975 after agranulocytosis deaths in Finland. Kane's 1988 trial demonstrated efficacy in treatment-resistant schizophrenia where other drugs had failed, and it returned with mandatory haematological monitoring."]],"note":["Cade published ten cases in the Medical Journal of Australia, 1949. Lithium was not licensed in the USA until 1970.","Chlorpromazine was synthesised by Charpentier at Rhone-Poulenc; Laborit noticed its effect on surgical patients before Delay and Deniker used it psychiatrically.","The SSRIs were a tolerability and safety advance, not an efficacy advance over tricyclics.","Wagner-Jauregg's malaria therapy for general paralysis of the insane, 1917, won the only Nobel Prize awarded for a psychiatric treatment, in 1927.","The serendipity of psychopharmacology is itself an examinable theme: mechanisms were reverse-engineered from clinical observation."],"refs":["stahl","maudsley","noptp"]};
TEACH["4.1.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Drug classes are named after what people first used them for, not what they do. Antipsychotics treat mania and depression, antidepressants treat anxiety and pain, and anticonvulsants stabilise mood. The names are historical accidents, and taking them literally will mislead you.","teach":["Antipsychotics: first-generation, typical, subdivided by potency into high potency such as haloperidol and low potency such as chlorpromazine. Second-generation, atypical, defined loosely by lower extrapyramidal burden at therapeutic doses. Third-generation refers to the dopamine partial agonists: aripiprazole, cariprazine, brexpiprazole.","The typical and atypical distinction is unsatisfactory. It is a spectrum of receptor affinity, not two categories, and CATIE and CUtLASS showed no clear superiority of second-generation drugs other than clozapine."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","stahl","bnf"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.1.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The placebo is not nothing. It is what the ritual of treatment does on its own: being taken seriously, being examined, being given something by somebody who believes it will help. In depression and pain that effect is large enough to change the design of every trial you will ever read.","teach":["The placebo response is the improvement seen with an inert intervention. It is not the same as spontaneous remission, regression to the mean or natural fluctuation, though trials confound all four.","Mechanisms: expectancy, classical conditioning from prior treatment experience, and the therapeutic relationship itself. Placebo analgesia is opioid-mediated and is reversed by naloxone, which demonstrates a physiological mechanism.","Factors that increase it: injection rather than tablet, larger tablets, branded appearance, more frequent dosing, an enthusiastic and confident prescriber, and a longer consultation.","The placebo response is largest in depression, anxiety and pain, and has risen over decades in antidepressant trials, which is one reason newer drugs struggle to separate from placebo.","Nocebo: adverse effects produced by expectation. It accounts for a substantial proportion of reported side effects, and warning a patient about a specific effect makes it more likely to be reported.","In trials, the placebo arm controls for all of this. Open-label observation cannot distinguish drug effect from placebo, natural history and regression to the mean.","Ethics: deceptive placebo use in clinical practice is not acceptable. Open-label placebo, given with full explanation, has produced positive results in some trials, which is theoretically interesting.","The practical lesson is that how you prescribe is part of the prescription. Confidence, explanation and continuity all increase the drug's measured effect."],"cards":[["Name four factors that increase the placebo response.","Injection rather than oral administration, branded rather than generic appearance, more frequent dosing, and an enthusiastic confident prescriber. Longer consultations and larger tablets also increase it."],["What is the nocebo effect and why does it matter clinically?","Adverse effects produced by negative expectation rather than by pharmacology. It accounts for a substantial share of reported side effects, and the manner in which risks are communicated directly affects how many are experienced."],["What evidence indicates placebo analgesia has a physiological mechanism?","It is reversed by naloxone, indicating mediation by endogenous opioids, and is accompanied by measurable changes in brain activity in pain-processing regions."]],"note":["Placebo response in antidepressant trials has increased over successive decades, contributing to the difficulty of demonstrating drug-placebo separation.","Regression to the mean: patients enter trials when at their worst, so improvement is expected regardless of treatment.","Active placebos, which reproduce side effects, reduce the apparent effect size of tricyclics, suggesting unblinding contributes to measured efficacy.","Placebo response is smallest in mania, psychosis and OCD, and largest in depression, anxiety and pain.","Deceptive placebo prescribing breaches informed consent and is not acceptable practice."],"refs":["maudsley","lawrie","noptp"]};
TEACH["4.1.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Roughly half of people on long-term medication for anything do not take it as prescribed, and psychiatry is no exception. The commonest reason is not forgetfulness or lack of insight. It is that the person made a reasonable decision, based on side effects and on what they believe the drug is doing, and did not tell you.","teach":["Terminology: compliance implies obedience; adherence implies agreement; concordance describes a negotiated shared decision. The words carry the model of the relationship.","Rates: around 50 per cent of patients on long-term medication for chronic conditions do not take it as prescribed. In schizophrenia estimates of non-adherence range widely, commonly cited around 40 to 50 per cent."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","nice","semgen"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.1.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"You cannot tell whether a drug is working by asking how the person seems. Measure the same thing, the same way, at intervals, and both of you can see the direction of travel. Without that you will keep drugs going for years on the assumption that they must be doing something.","teach":["Efficacy is effect under ideal trial conditions; effectiveness is effect in routine practice; efficiency is effectiveness relative to cost.","Measure at baseline, or you have nothing to compare with. This is the single commonest failure."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","nice","lawrie"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.1.6.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Before a drug reaches a patient it goes through two early stages that most people never hear about. First it is given to a small number of healthy volunteers to find out what the body does to it and what dose is tolerable. Then it is given to a small number of patients to see whether it does anything at all.","teach":["Preclinical work comes first: in vitro pharmacology, animal toxicology, and animal models. This precedes any human exposure and is required by the Nuremberg Code and the Declaration of Helsinki.","Phase I: first in human. Typically 20 to 100 healthy volunteers, though patients are used where the drug is too toxic for healthy subjects, as in oncology. Objectives are safety, tolerability, pharmacokinetics, and determination of the maximum tolerated dose. Usually open-label with dose escalation."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lawrie","freeman","noptp"],"n":{"teach":7,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.1.6.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The randomised controlled trial exists to answer one question: was it the drug, or would this have happened anyway? Everything in its design, the randomisation, the blinding, the placebo, the analysis, is there to close off a way of being fooled.","teach":["Randomisation balances known and unknown confounders between groups. It is the only method that handles the confounders you did not think of.","Allocation concealment prevents the person recruiting from knowing the next allocation, and prevents selection bias at entry. It is distinct from blinding, which prevents performance and detection bias afterwards.","Blinding: single, double or triple. In psychiatry, side effects frequently unblind participants and assessors, which is why active placebos matter and why blinding should be formally assessed.","Endpoints: the primary outcome must be specified in advance and drives the sample size calculation. Multiple secondary outcomes inflate type I error, and reporting a secondary outcome as though it were primary is outcome switching.","Superiority trials test whether the new treatment is better. Non-inferiority trials test whether it is not worse by more than a prespecified margin, and are used when a placebo arm would be unethical. The margin must be justified in advance.","The analysis differs by design. Intention-to-treat preserves randomisation, is conservative for superiority, and is the primary analysis. Per-protocol exaggerates efficacy and is preferred for non-inferiority, where a conservative analysis would favour the wrong conclusion.","Missing data: last observation carried forward biases in the direction of the last measurement and is now discouraged. Mixed models and multiple imputation are preferred, and the amount and pattern of missingness must be reported.","Psychiatry-specific problems: high placebo response, high dropout, short trial durations relative to illness course, symptom scales as surrogate outcomes, exclusion of comorbidity and suicidality producing unrepresentative samples, and industry sponsorship associated with more favourable results.","CONSORT governs reporting. Trial registration before recruitment exists to expose selective outcome reporting."],"cards":[["Distinguish allocation concealment from blinding.","Allocation concealment hides the upcoming treatment assignment from the person enrolling participants, preventing selection bias at entry. Blinding hides the assigned treatment from participants, clinicians or assessors after randomisation, preventing performance and detection bias."],["Why is intention-to-treat conservative in superiority trials but not in non-inferiority trials?","Intention-to-treat includes non-adherent participants, diluting the treatment effect and making superiority harder to demonstrate. In a non-inferiority trial that dilution pushes the groups together and makes non-inferiority easier to claim falsely, so per-protocol analysis is also required."],["Name three problems specific to randomised trials in psychiatry.","High and rising placebo response; high dropout rates; short trial duration relative to the course of illness; use of symptom rating scales as surrogate outcomes; and exclusion of comorbidity and suicidality producing unrepresentative samples."]],"note":["CONSORT for trials, PRISMA for systematic reviews, STROBE for observational studies.","Last observation carried forward is discouraged; mixed models for repeated measures and multiple imputation are preferred.","Industry-sponsored trials are more likely to report results favourable to the sponsor, an effect consistent across therapeutic areas.","Cluster randomised trials must account for clustering using the intracluster correlation coefficient.","Section 14 of this syllabus covers critical review in full and carries a third of Paper B."],"refs":["lawrie","peacock","freeman"]};
TEACH["4.1.7"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A licence tells you what a company was allowed to advertise a drug for, based on the trials they chose to run. It does not tell you what the drug does. Half of psychiatric prescribing for children, and a good deal for adults, is outside the licence, and that is lawful, common and sometimes the only reasonable option.","teach":["In the UK, medicines are licensed by the MHRA. A marketing authorisation specifies indication, population, dose and route, based on the evidence the company submitted.","Off-label use is prescribing outside the terms of the licence: different indication, age group, dose or route. Unlicensed use is prescribing a product with no UK marketing authorisation at all."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["bnf","maudsley","nice"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.1.8"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Rational prescribing is not a formula, it is a habit of asking four questions before you write anything. What am I treating, how will I know if it worked, what is the worst thing this could do to this particular person, and when will I stop.","teach":["Define the target. Prescribing for a diagnosis is weaker than prescribing for a defined problem with a measurable outcome. Write down what should change and by when.","Choose on the basis of evidence for that indication, the individual's physical health, previous response and previous adverse effects, interactions, and the person's own preference. Previous response is one of the strongest predictors and is routinely not asked about.","Start low and go slow in the elderly, in hepatic and renal impairment, and in intellectual disability, where sensitivity is greater and reporting of adverse effects is harder.","One change at a time. Changing two things simultaneously means you learn nothing from either.","Give an adequate trial at an adequate dose before declaring failure: four to six weeks for antidepressants, six weeks for antipsychotics, twelve weeks at higher doses for OCD.","Risk-benefit appraisal is individual, not generic. The same drug carries different risk in a 24-year-old man, a pregnant woman and an 84-year-old with atrial fibrillation and renal impairment.","Polypharmacy: each additional drug adds interaction risk and adherence burden. High-dose antipsychotic treatment and antipsychotic polypharmacy have no evidence base for routine use and increase adverse effects.","Have a stopping plan from the start. Deprescribing is an active clinical task, not an absence of one, and drugs continued indefinitely by default are a major source of harm.","Document the reasoning. A prescription with no recorded rationale is one that nobody will dare to stop."],"cards":[["What is the risk-benefit appraisal in rational prescribing?","An individualised judgement weighing the expected benefit for this person's condition and severity against the specific risks their age, physical health, comorbidities, other medication, reproductive status and preferences confer, rather than a generic class-level assessment."],["Why should only one medication change be made at a time?","Because simultaneous changes make it impossible to attribute either benefit or adverse effect to a specific intervention, so nothing is learned and future decisions are made on unreliable information."],["What is the evidence base for high-dose antipsychotic treatment?","There is none for routine use. Doses above the licensed maximum do not improve response and increase adverse effects, including QTc prolongation and extrapyramidal symptoms. Any such prescribing requires documented rationale, consent where possible, and enhanced monitoring."]],"note":["Previous response and previous adverse effects are among the strongest predictors of future response and are frequently not elicited.","Antipsychotic polypharmacy is common in practice, lacks an evidence base outside clozapine augmentation, and increases metabolic and cardiac risk.","STOMP is the national programme to stop over-medication of people with a learning disability, autism or both.","Every long-term prescription should have a documented review date.","Anticholinergic burden should be calculated in older adults; it increases falls, confusion and mortality."],"refs":["maudsley","bnf","nice"]};
TEACH["4.2.1.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Four verbs, in order: absorb, distribute, metabolise, excrete. Almost every kinetic question is asking which of those four something has interfered with. Get the order in your head and most of them answer themselves.","teach":["Absorption: movement from the site of administration into the systemic circulation. Most oral drugs are absorbed in the small intestine by passive diffusion of the unionised lipid-soluble form.","First-pass metabolism: absorbed drug passes through the gut wall and portal circulation to the liver before reaching the systemic circulation. Extensive first-pass metabolism produces low oral bioavailability.","Distribution: from blood into tissues, determined by lipid solubility, protein binding, tissue perfusion and ability to cross membranes including the blood-brain barrier.","Metabolism: usually hepatic. Phase I reactions, oxidation, reduction and hydrolysis, largely by cytochrome P450, introduce or expose a reactive group. Phase II reactions conjugate, usually by glucuronidation, producing a water-soluble compound for excretion.","Metabolites are not always inactive. Fluoxetine gives norfluoxetine, risperidone gives paliperidone, amitriptyline gives nortriptyline, diazepam gives desmethyldiazepam, oxazepam and temazepam. Codeine is a prodrug requiring CYP2D6 conversion to morphine.","Excretion: predominantly renal for water-soluble compounds and their metabolites, with biliary and faecal routes for others. Lithium is the psychiatric exception: not metabolised at all and excreted unchanged by the kidney.","First-order kinetics: a constant proportion is eliminated per unit time, so half-life is constant. This applies to almost all drugs at therapeutic doses.","Zero-order kinetics: a constant amount is eliminated per unit time because the enzyme is saturated. Small dose increases then produce disproportionate rises in concentration. Alcohol, phenytoin, and aspirin at high dose."],"cards":[["Distinguish phase I from phase II metabolism.","Phase I reactions, oxidation, reduction and hydrolysis, largely mediated by cytochrome P450, introduce or unmask a functional group and may produce active metabolites. Phase II reactions conjugate the compound, usually by glucuronidation, producing an inactive water-soluble product for excretion."],["Which psychotropic is not metabolised at all, and what follows?","Lithium. It is not protein-bound and not metabolised, and is excreted unchanged by the kidney, where it is handled like sodium. Sodium depletion, dehydration, thiazides, NSAIDs and ACE inhibitors therefore raise the level."],["Define zero-order kinetics and name relevant drugs.","A constant amount rather than a constant proportion is eliminated per unit time, because the metabolising enzyme is saturated. Small dose increases produce disproportionately large concentration rises. Alcohol, phenytoin, and aspirin at high dose."]],"note":["Active metabolites to know: fluoxetine to norfluoxetine, half-life 4 to 16 days; risperidone to paliperidone; amitriptyline to nortriptyline; diazepam to desmethyldiazepam, oxazepam and temazepam.","Lorazepam, oxazepam and temazepam undergo glucuronidation only, with no active metabolites, which is why they are preferred in hepatic impairment.","Codeine is a prodrug requiring CYP2D6; poor metabolisers get no analgesia and ultra-rapid metabolisers risk toxicity.","Gut wall CYP3A4 contributes substantially to first-pass metabolism, which is why grapefruit juice raises levels of some drugs.","Enterohepatic recirculation prolongs the effect of some drugs and is disrupted by antibiotics."],"refs":["stahl","maudsley","bnf"]};
TEACH["4.2.1.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"How a drug gets in decides how fast it works and how much of it survives the journey. Swallow it and the liver takes a cut before it reaches the brain. Inject it and there is no cut at all, which is why the intramuscular dose of many drugs is smaller than the tablet.","teach":["Intravenous: 100 per cent bioavailability by definition, immediate onset, no absorption phase and no first-pass metabolism. It is the reference against which other routes are measured.","Oral: convenient, and subject to variable absorption, food effects, gut motility and first-pass metabolism. Onset over 30 minutes to hours."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","bnf","stahl"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.2.1.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Most drugs travel through the blood stuck to proteins, and only the unstuck fraction does anything. That matters when the protein runs low, as in liver disease, pregnancy or malnutrition, because the same total level now contains more free drug.","teach":["Acidic drugs bind mainly to albumin. Basic drugs bind mainly to alpha-1 acid glycoprotein. Only the free, unbound fraction is pharmacologically active and available for metabolism and excretion.","Highly protein-bound psychotropics include most antipsychotics, tricyclics, SSRIs, valproate, diazepam and phenytoin. Lithium is the notable exception with essentially no protein binding."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","bnf","maudphys"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.2.1.4"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Four numbers describe what a drug does over time. How high it goes, how quickly it gets there, how long it takes to halve, and the total exposure. Half-life is the one that decides your dosing interval, how long to steady state, and how bad the discontinuation will be.","teach":["Half-life, t½, is the time for the plasma concentration to fall by half. With first-order kinetics it is constant regardless of concentration.","Steady state is reached after approximately four to five half-lives of regular dosing. The same applies on stopping: it takes four to five half-lives to clear.","This one rule governs practice. Fluoxetine, with norfluoxetine's half-life of 4 to 16 days, takes weeks to reach steady state and weeks to clear, requiring a long washout before an MAOI. Paroxetine and venlafaxine, with short half-lives and no long-acting metabolite, produce the worst discontinuation symptoms.","Tmax is the time to peak plasma concentration and reflects absorption rate. Cmax is the peak concentration and relates to peak-related adverse effects such as sedation and postural hypotension.","Modified-release formulations lower Cmax and lengthen Tmax, reducing peak-related side effects, which is the rationale for slow-release preparations of venlafaxine, quetiapine and lithium.","Volume of distribution is the theoretical volume needed to contain the total amount of drug at the measured plasma concentration. A high value indicates extensive tissue distribution rather than a real anatomical space; lipophilic drugs have high values.","Area under the curve represents total systemic exposure over time, and is the measure used for bioequivalence and for many drug interaction studies.","Clearance is the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time and, with the dosing rate, determines steady-state concentration.","A drug with a high volume of distribution is poorly removed by dialysis, because most of it is not in the plasma."],"cards":[["How many half-lives are needed to reach steady state, and why does it matter both ways?","Approximately four to five. The same applies on stopping, so a drug with a long half-life takes weeks to reach steady state and weeks to clear, which determines washout periods before switching."],["Which SSRIs cause the worst discontinuation symptoms and why?","Paroxetine and venlafaxine, because they have short half-lives with no long-acting active metabolite, so plasma levels fall rapidly on omission. Fluoxetine causes the least because of norfluoxetine's half-life of 4 to 16 days."],["What does a large volume of distribution indicate, and what is the practical consequence?","Extensive distribution into tissues rather than confinement to plasma, characteristic of lipophilic drugs. Such drugs are poorly removed by haemodialysis because little of the total body burden is in the plasma compartment."]],"note":["Fluoxetine requires a washout of at least five weeks before starting an irreversible MAOI; other SSRIs require around two weeks.","Lithium has a low volume of distribution and no protein binding, which is why haemodialysis is effective in severe toxicity.","Cmax relates to peak-related adverse effects; modified-release preparations reduce them by lowering the peak.","Bioequivalence is assessed on AUC and Cmax, with the 90 per cent confidence interval required to fall within 80 to 125 per cent of the reference.","Loading doses are calculated from volume of distribution; maintenance doses from clearance."],"refs":["stahl","maudsley","bnf"]};
TEACH["4.2.1.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"For nearly every drug, doubling the dose roughly doubles the level. For a few, doubling the dose can quadruple it or worse, because the enzyme that clears it is already working flat out. Those are the drugs that catch people out.","teach":["First-order (linear) kinetics: a constant fraction of drug is eliminated per unit time. Half-life is constant, and concentration is proportional to dose. This describes almost all drugs at therapeutic concentrations.","Zero-order (saturation) kinetics: a constant amount is eliminated per unit time because the metabolising enzyme is saturated. Half-life is not constant, and small dose increases produce disproportionate rises in concentration."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["bnf","maudsley","stahl"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.2.1.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Most drugs are broken down by a family of liver enzymes called cytochrome P450. Some drugs jam those enzymes, and levels of everything else shoot up within a day. Others make the liver build more of them, and levels quietly fall over a fortnight. Fast up, slow down: that asymmetry is the whole of the clinical risk.","teach":["The clinically important isoenzymes in psychiatry are CYP1A2, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, CYP2D6 and CYP3A4. CYP3A4 metabolises the largest number of drugs; CYP2D6 is the most polymorphic.","Inhibition is competitive and rapid, occurring within hours to a day or two. Levels of the substrate rise. This is where acute toxicity comes from."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","bnf","stahl"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.2.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The same dose does different things at different points in a life. A pregnant woman clears some drugs twice as fast. An eighty-year-old clears them half as fast and holds fat-soluble ones for far longer. The prescription that was right last year may be wrong now for reasons that have nothing to do with the illness.","teach":["Neonates and infants: immature hepatic enzymes and reduced renal clearance, higher total body water, and an immature blood-brain barrier. Prolonged half-lives and greater CNS penetration.","Children: proportionally larger liver relative to body weight and higher metabolic capacity, so weight-adjusted clearance is often higher than in adults and mg per kg doses may exceed adult equivalents."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","maudphys","bnf"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.2.3.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"To act on the brain a drug has to be greasy enough to cross a fatty barrier. That greasiness is also why it hangs around in body fat, why it sedates, and why an obese patient and a thin one do not behave the same way on the same dose.","teach":["Lipophilic drugs cross membranes readily, including the blood-brain barrier, by passive diffusion. Hydrophilic drugs do not, and require a transporter or must act peripherally.","Most psychotropics are lipophilic by necessity: they have to reach the brain. Lithium is the exception, a small hydrophilic ion that enters via sodium channels and transporters."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["stahl","bnf","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.2.3.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The brain has a wall around it. Capillaries elsewhere in the body leak; in the brain they are sealed shut and guarded by pumps that throw foreign molecules back out. That wall is the reason most drugs do nothing psychiatric, and the reason some drugs suddenly do when a person is very ill.","teach":["Structure: continuous capillary endothelium with tight junctions, no fenestrations, minimal pinocytosis, surrounded by pericytes and astrocyte end-feet, with a basement membrane. Astrocytes induce and maintain the barrier.","Crossing it: small lipophilic unionised molecules cross by passive diffusion. Larger or hydrophilic molecules require carrier-mediated transport, as for glucose via GLUT1 and amino acids via LAT1. L-DOPA crosses on the amino acid transporter, which is why it is given rather than dopamine.","Efflux transporters actively remove substrates back into the blood. P-glycoprotein is the most important, and many psychotropics are substrates. Genetic and drug-induced variation in P-glycoprotein activity alters brain exposure independently of plasma level.","Circumventricular organs lack a barrier by design: the area postrema, allowing the chemoreceptor trigger zone to detect toxins and induce vomiting; the median eminence; and the organum vasculosum for osmoreception.","The barrier is not fixed. It is disrupted by inflammation, infection, seizures, hypoxia, trauma and hypertension, which is why drug effects and delirium can appear in acutely unwell patients at doses previously tolerated.","It is immature in the neonate, which increases CNS exposure, and becomes more permeable with age and in dementia.","Clinical translation: domperidone acts peripherally because it is a poor CNS penetrant; second-generation antihistamines are non-sedating because they are P-glycoprotein substrates excluded from the brain; and loperamide is a peripherally restricted opioid for the same reason, until the efflux pump is overwhelmed in overdose.","Drug delivery across the barrier remains the central obstacle to CNS therapeutics, and is why monoclonal antibodies achieve only around 0.1 per cent brain penetration."],"cards":[["Describe the structure of the blood-brain barrier.","Continuous non-fenestrated capillary endothelium joined by tight junctions with minimal pinocytosis, surrounded by pericytes, a basement membrane and astrocyte end-feet, which induce and maintain barrier properties."],["What is P-glycoprotein and why does it matter?","An ATP-dependent efflux transporter in the blood-brain barrier endothelium that actively pumps substrates back into the blood. Many psychotropics are substrates, so variation in its activity alters brain drug concentration independently of plasma level."],["Name three circumventricular organs and their functions.","The area postrema, containing the chemoreceptor trigger zone for emesis; the median eminence, for release of hypothalamic hormones into the portal system; and the organum vasculosum of the lamina terminalis, for osmoreception."]],"note":["L-DOPA is given rather than dopamine because it crosses on the large neutral amino acid transporter; peripheral decarboxylase inhibitors prevent its conversion before it reaches the brain.","Second-generation antihistamines are non-sedating largely because they are P-glycoprotein substrates.","Loperamide is a peripherally restricted opioid; in massive overdose the efflux system is overwhelmed and central and cardiac toxicity occur.","Barrier disruption in inflammation, sepsis and seizure increases CNS drug exposure and contributes to delirium.","Monoclonal antibodies achieve roughly 0.1 per cent brain penetration, which is the central obstacle for anti-amyloid therapies."],"refs":["agrawal","stahl","bnf"]};
TEACH["4.2.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Two people given the same dose can end up with wildly different levels, because they inherited different versions of the enzyme that clears it. Testing for that is now possible and cheap. Whether it improves outcomes is a different question, and the honest answer is that the evidence is thin.","teach":["Pharmacogenetics studies the effect of single gene variants on drug response. Pharmacogenomics is the broader study of the whole genome's contribution.","The best-established variation is in CYP2D6, which is highly polymorphic with over a hundred alleles including gene deletions and duplications. Phenotypes are poor, intermediate, extensive (normal) and ultra-rapid metaboliser."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","bnf","stahl"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.2.5"] = {"free":true,"simple":"For most psychiatric drugs a blood level tells you almost nothing useful, because the level does not correlate with whether the person is getting better. For three of them it tells you a great deal, and for one of those it prevents deaths.","teach":["Therapeutic drug monitoring is indicated when the drug has a narrow therapeutic index, non-linear kinetics, wide interindividual variation in clearance, an established concentration-effect relationship, or where toxicity is hard to distinguish from the illness.","Lithium is the clearest case. Sample twelve hours post-dose. Therapeutic range 0.4 to 1.0 mmol/L, commonly 0.6 to 0.8 for maintenance and higher in acute mania. Toxicity above 1.5 and severe above 2.0.","Lithium monitoring schedule: level after five to seven days and after every dose change, then every three months in the first year and at least six-monthly thereafter, with U&Es, eGFR, calcium and thyroid function at least every six months.","Clozapine: a plasma level above about 350 micrograms per litre is associated with better response. Levels are used to assess adherence, to investigate non-response before declaring treatment failure, to investigate suspected toxicity, and after any change in smoking status. Seizure risk rises above about 600.","Anticonvulsants used as mood stabilisers: valproate levels correlate poorly with mood response and are used chiefly for adherence and toxicity. Carbamazepine levels are more useful, particularly given autoinduction.","Antidepressants and antipsychotics generally: routine level monitoring is not indicated because the concentration-response relationship is weak. Exceptions are suspected toxicity, non-response despite adequate dosing, suspected non-adherence, and specific drugs such as nortriptyline, which has a therapeutic window with reduced efficacy above it.","The limitations that matter: plasma level is not brain level, P-glycoprotein and receptor occupancy vary between individuals, timing of the sample is frequently wrong, and a level taken before steady state is uninterpretable.","A level is a clinical tool, not a target. Treating the number rather than the patient is the standard error."],"cards":[["Give the therapeutic and toxic lithium levels and the correct sampling time.","Therapeutic 0.4 to 1.0 mmol/L, commonly 0.6 to 0.8 for maintenance. Toxicity above 1.5 and severe toxicity above 2.0. The sample is taken twelve hours after the last dose."],["When should a clozapine plasma level be measured?","When response is inadequate despite an apparently adequate dose, when non-adherence is suspected, when toxicity is suspected, and after any change in smoking status. A level above about 350 micrograms per litre is associated with better response."],["Why is routine therapeutic drug monitoring unhelpful for most antidepressants and antipsychotics?","Because the relationship between plasma concentration and clinical response is weak for most of these drugs, so the level does not guide dosing. Plasma concentration also does not reliably reflect brain concentration or receptor occupancy."]],"note":["A lithium level taken before steady state, less than five days after a dose change, cannot be interpreted.","Nortriptyline has a therapeutic window, with reduced efficacy above as well as below the range.","Clozapine seizure risk rises above about 600 micrograms per litre; prophylactic valproate is sometimes added at high levels.","Every patient on lithium should hold a lithium alert card, a record book and information on toxicity.","NICE requires that lithium is not started unless there are arrangements for reliable monitoring."],"refs":["maudsley","nice","bnf"]};
TEACH["4.3.1.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Learn seven receptors and you can predict the side effects of almost any psychiatric drug without looking them up. Block histamine and the person is sedated and gains weight. Block muscarinic and the mouth dries. Block alpha-1 and they faint standing up. The side effect list is just the receptor list read out loud.","teach":["Dopamine D1 to D5. D1-like (D1, D5) are Gs-coupled; D2-like (D2, D3, D4) are Gi-coupled. D2 blockade gives antipsychotic effect, extrapyramidal side effects and hyperprolactinaemia.","Serotonin, fourteen subtypes. 5-HT1A: autoreceptor desensitisation underlies antidepressant delay; partial agonism gives anxiolysis. 5-HT2A: blockade contributes to atypicality and improves sleep continuity; agonism is how psychedelics work. 5-HT2C: blockade increases appetite and weight. 5-HT3: the only ionotropic subtype, blockade is antiemetic.","Noradrenaline. Alpha-1 blockade causes postural hypotension, dizziness, nasal congestion and priapism. Alpha-2 is an inhibitory autoreceptor; blockade by mirtazapine increases noradrenaline and serotonin release. Beta blockade is used for akathisia and somatic anxiety.","Histamine H1 blockade causes sedation, weight gain and increased appetite. This single receptor explains most of the sedation across psychiatry.","Muscarinic M1 to M5 blockade causes dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, urinary retention, tachycardia, and centrally confusion and memory impairment. Anticholinergic burden is a leading cause of falls and delirium in older people.","Glutamate: AMPA, NMDA, kainate ionotropic; mGluR metabotropic. NMDA antagonism by ketamine and memantine.","GABA-A ionotropic chloride channel, the site of benzodiazepines, barbiturates, Z-drugs and alcohol. GABA-B metabotropic, the site of baclofen.","Transporters are targets too, not receptors: SERT for SSRIs, NET for reboxetine and part of SNRI action, DAT for methylphenidate and cocaine, VMAT2 for tetrabenazine.","The clinical method: take any drug, list its receptor affinities, and the adverse effect profile follows. That is how to answer these questions rather than memorising lists."],"cards":[["Which receptor blockade produces sedation and weight gain?","Histamine H1 blockade produces sedation and, with 5-HT2C blockade, increased appetite and weight gain."],["What are the consequences of alpha-1 adrenoceptor blockade?","Postural hypotension, dizziness, reflex tachycardia, nasal congestion, and priapism, which is the mechanism behind trazodone-associated priapism."],["List the peripheral and central effects of muscarinic blockade.","Peripheral: dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, urinary retention and tachycardia. Central: confusion, impaired memory and, at higher burden, delirium."]],"note":["Antipsychotic efficacy requires roughly 60 to 70 per cent D2 occupancy; extrapyramidal effects above about 80 per cent; hyperprolactinaemia from around 72 per cent.","Anticholinergic burden scales exist and should be used in older adults; higher burden predicts falls, cognitive decline and mortality.","5-HT2A blockade with D2 blockade is the classical explanation of atypicality, though clozapine's superiority is not explained by it.","Priapism from trazodone is a urological emergency requiring immediate referral.","Beta-blockade with propranolol is the best-evidenced pharmacological treatment for akathisia."],"refs":["stahl","maudsley","bnf"]};
TEACH["4.3.1.2.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Most receptors in the brain do not open a hole. They ring a bell inside the cell, and the bell is a G protein. Slower than a hole, but much longer lasting, and able to change what genes the cell reads. That is how a tablet taken today alters a brain over the next month.","teach":["G-protein coupled receptors are seven-transmembrane proteins, the largest receptor superfamily and the target of a large proportion of all drugs.","Mechanism: ligand binding produces a conformational change, GDP is exchanged for GTP on the G alpha subunit, and the heterotrimer dissociates. Both G alpha and G beta-gamma act on effectors. Intrinsic GTPase activity terminates the signal."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["stahl","agrawal"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.1.2.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The other kind of receptor is a hole in the wall with a lock on it. The transmitter is the key, the hole opens, ions rush through, and the whole thing is over in milliseconds. Fast, simple, and the target of every sedative you will ever prescribe.","teach":["Ionotropic receptors are ligand-gated ion channels. Binding opens the pore directly, with millisecond onset and offset. No second messengers are involved.","GABA-A: a pentamer, usually two alpha, two beta and one gamma subunit, forming a chloride channel. Opening hyperpolarises the cell. Benzodiazepines bind at the alpha-gamma interface as positive allosteric modulators and increase the frequency of channel opening. Barbiturates increase the duration of opening and at high dose can open the channel without GABA, which is why they kill in overdose."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["stahl","agrawal","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.1.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Affinity is how tightly a drug holds on. Efficacy is what it does once it is holding on. A drug can hold on beautifully and do absolutely nothing, and that drug is an antagonist. Keeping those two ideas separate solves most pharmacodynamics questions.","teach":["Affinity is the tendency of a drug to bind its receptor, expressed as the dissociation constant Kd. A low Kd means high affinity. Ki is the inhibition constant from competition binding.","Efficacy, or intrinsic activity, is the ability to produce a response once bound. Full agonist 1, partial agonist between 0 and 1, antagonist 0, inverse agonist below 0."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["stahl","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.1.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"You do not need to memorise side effect lists if you know each drug's receptor profile. Olanzapine hits histamine hard, so it sedates and adds weight. Haloperidol hits D2 hard and little else, so it causes movement problems and few metabolic ones. The profile predicts the patient's experience.","teach":["Haloperidol: high-affinity D2 antagonist with little else. High extrapyramidal burden, hyperprolactinaemia, QTc prolongation especially intravenously, low metabolic burden, little sedation.","Chlorpromazine: D2 plus substantial H1, alpha-1 and muscarinic blockade. Sedation, postural hypotension, anticholinergic effects, photosensitivity, and lower extrapyramidal burden than haloperidol at equivalent antipsychotic effect."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","stahl","bnf"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.1.5.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Intrinsic activity is a number between zero and one describing what a drug does after it has bound. One means it does everything the natural transmitter would. Zero means it does nothing at all. The interesting drugs sit in between.","teach":["Intrinsic activity, or intrinsic efficacy, is the ability of a bound ligand to activate the receptor and produce a response.","Scale: full agonist 1, partial agonist between 0 and 1, neutral antagonist 0, inverse agonist negative."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["stahl","maudsley","semaddict"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.1.5.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Four words that get muddled constantly. An agonist switches the receptor on. An antagonist blocks it and does nothing itself. A partial agonist switches it partly on. An inverse agonist switches off activity that was happening even without a transmitter.","teach":["Agonism: binding produces the same response as the endogenous transmitter. Full agonists produce the maximal response.","Antagonism: binding produces no response and prevents agonist binding. A neutral antagonist has affinity and zero efficacy.","Competitive antagonism is surmountable. It shifts the dose-response curve to the right with the same maximum, and can be overcome by increasing agonist concentration. Naloxone at the mu receptor.","Non-competitive or irreversible antagonism is insurmountable. It lowers the maximum response and shifts the curve down. Phenoxybenzamine at alpha receptors; irreversible MAOIs, where recovery requires new enzyme synthesis, which is why a two-week washout is needed.","Partial agonism: submaximal maximal response, acting as agonist in low tone and antagonist in high tone.","Inverse agonism: reduces constitutive receptor activity below baseline, producing an effect opposite to that of an agonist. Many drugs classified as antagonists are in fact inverse agonists. Rimonabant, a CB1 inverse agonist, was withdrawn because of depression and suicidality.","Allosteric modulation acts at a site distinct from the orthosteric site. Positive allosteric modulators such as benzodiazepines enhance the effect of the endogenous transmitter without activating the receptor alone, which is the reason for their overdose safety.","Physiological antagonism is opposition through a different receptor system, as adrenaline opposes histamine in anaphylaxis. Chemical antagonism is direct inactivation, as chelation.","Naming caution: naloxone at mu is competitive and surmountable, so in overdose with a very high opioid load repeated doses or an infusion are needed."],"cards":[["Distinguish competitive from non-competitive antagonism on a dose-response curve.","Competitive antagonism shifts the curve to the right with an unchanged maximum and is surmountable by increasing agonist concentration. Non-competitive or irreversible antagonism lowers the maximum response and cannot be overcome by more agonist."],["What is an inverse agonist and give a clinical example.","A ligand that reduces constitutive receptor activity below its baseline, producing an effect opposite to an agonist. Rimonabant, a CB1 inverse agonist licensed for obesity, was withdrawn because of depression, anxiety and suicidality."],["Why are benzodiazepines safer than barbiturates in overdose, in terms of allosteric action?","Benzodiazepines are positive allosteric modulators that enhance the effect of GABA and cannot open the channel without it, so their effect has a ceiling. Barbiturates can directly open the channel at high concentrations, producing unbounded CNS depression."]],"note":["Irreversible MAOIs require a two-week washout because recovery depends on synthesis of new enzyme; moclobemide is reversible and needs only about 24 hours.","Naloxone has a shorter half-life than most opioids, so repeated doses or an infusion may be required.","Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines but may precipitate seizures in mixed overdose or benzodiazepine dependence.","Physiological antagonism, such as adrenaline in anaphylaxis, works through a different receptor system rather than at the same receptor.","Many drugs historically described as antagonists are inverse agonists at constitutively active receptors."],"refs":["stahl","bnf","maudsley"]};
TEACH["4.3.1.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"When a receptor is switched on, the message does not stop at the wall. It runs inside, gets amplified, and can end up changing which genes the cell reads. That is why a drug taken today can still be changing the brain a month later.","teach":["Second messengers: cAMP from adenylyl cyclase, activating protein kinase A; IP3 releasing intracellular calcium; diacylglycerol activating protein kinase C; and calcium acting through calmodulin and CaMKII.","Protein kinases phosphorylate target proteins, altering ion channel behaviour, receptor sensitivity, cytoskeletal structure and transcription factor activity."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["stahl","agrawal","lynall"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.2.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Every antipsychotic ever licensed does one thing: it turns down dopamine at the D2 receptor. Everything else about them, the sedation, the weight, the movement problems, is what else they happen to bump into on the way.","teach":["The shared mechanism is D2 receptor antagonism, or partial agonism for aripiprazole, cariprazine and brexpiprazole. No licensed antipsychotic lacks D2 activity.","Occupancy thresholds: roughly 60 to 70 per cent for antipsychotic effect, above about 80 per cent for extrapyramidal effects, around 72 per cent for hyperprolactinaemia.","Onset is not immediate. Receptor blockade is immediate but clinical response develops over days to weeks, with depolarisation block of dopamine neurones and downstream adaptation proposed as the explanation.","5-HT2A antagonism combined with D2 antagonism is the classical explanation of atypicality: 5-HT2A blockade increases dopamine release in nigrostriatal and mesocortical pathways, reducing extrapyramidal effects and possibly helping negative symptoms.","Fast dissociation, Kapur and Seeman: clozapine and quetiapine leave the D2 receptor rapidly, giving transient rather than sustained blockade and low extrapyramidal burden.","Partial agonism stabilises rather than blocks, reducing signalling where dopamine is high and maintaining it where dopamine is low.","Clozapine remains unexplained. It has low D2 occupancy, high D4, 5-HT2A, muscarinic, histaminergic and alpha-adrenergic activity, and is uniquely effective in treatment resistance. No current model accounts for this.","Glutamatergic and muscarinic mechanisms are the current frontier. Xanomeline-trospium, a muscarinic M1/M4 agonist paired with a peripherally restricted antagonist, is the first non-D2 antipsychotic mechanism to reach licensing in the United States.","The mechanistic point for the exam: efficacy against positive symptoms tracks D2 occupancy; efficacy against negative and cognitive symptoms does not, and that is the unsolved problem."],"cards":[["What is the shared mechanism of all licensed antipsychotics?","D2 receptor antagonism, or D2 partial agonism in the case of aripiprazole, cariprazine and brexpiprazole."],["State the classical explanation of atypicality.","Combined 5-HT2A antagonism with D2 antagonism. 5-HT2A blockade increases dopamine release in nigrostriatal and mesocortical pathways, reducing extrapyramidal effects at antipsychotic doses."],["Why is clozapine's efficacy mechanistically unexplained?","It achieves relatively low D2 occupancy yet is uniquely effective in treatment-resistant schizophrenia. Its high affinity for D4, 5-HT2A, muscarinic, histaminergic and alpha-adrenergic receptors has not been shown to account for the difference."]],"note":["There is no evidence that exceeding licensed doses improves response; adverse effects rise without benefit.","Depolarisation block of dopamine neurones over two to three weeks is one proposed explanation for delayed onset.","Amisulpride is a selective D2 and D3 antagonist with preferential limbic action and, at low dose, presynaptic autoreceptor blockade used for negative symptoms.","CATIE and CUtLASS found no clear superiority of second-generation drugs other than clozapine.","Cariprazine has relative D3 preference and is licensed for predominant negative symptoms in some jurisdictions."],"refs":["stahl","maudsley","semgen"]};
TEACH["4.3.2.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Antidepressants raise serotonin or noradrenaline within hours, and people get better in weeks. That gap is the most important fact about them. Whatever they do that helps, it is not the immediate chemical change; it is what the brain does in response over the following month.","teach":["SSRIs block the serotonin transporter, raising synaptic serotonin immediately. Somatodendritic 5-HT1A autoreceptors initially suppress raphe firing; their desensitisation over two to four weeks permits increased forebrain transmission. This is the standard explanation of the delay.","SNRIs block serotonin and noradrenaline transporters. Venlafaxine is predominantly serotonergic at low dose and adds noradrenergic action above about 150 mg. Duloxetine is dual across its range.","Tricyclics block both transporters plus H1, muscarinic and alpha-1 receptors, and sodium channels, which is why they sedate, cause anticholinergic effects and are cardiotoxic in overdose.","MAOIs inhibit monoamine oxidase, increasing stored and released monoamines. Irreversible inhibitors require a two-week washout and dietary tyramine restriction; moclobemide is reversible and selective for MAO-A.","Mirtazapine blocks alpha-2 auto- and heteroreceptors, increasing noradrenaline and serotonin release, with 5-HT2 and 5-HT3 blockade directing serotonin towards 5-HT1A. Strong H1 blockade gives sedation and appetite increase.","Bupropion inhibits noradrenaline and dopamine reuptake, with no serotonergic action, hence minimal sexual dysfunction and no weight gain, but lowered seizure threshold.","Agomelatine is an MT1 and MT2 agonist and 5-HT2C antagonist, resynchronising circadian rhythm; it requires liver function monitoring.","Vortioxetine combines transporter inhibition with multiple direct receptor actions.","Ketamine and esketamine act at NMDA receptors, producing effect within hours through AMPA throughput and mTOR-dependent synaptogenesis, which is the strongest evidence that plasticity rather than monoamine level is the mechanism.","Downstream, all of these converge on CREB, BDNF and increased synaptic plasticity, which is the neurotrophic account."],"cards":[["Explain the delay in SSRI response.","Transporter blockade raises synaptic serotonin at once, but somatodendritic 5-HT1A autoreceptors initially inhibit raphe firing. Their desensitisation over two to four weeks allows increased forebrain serotonergic transmission and downstream neuroplastic change."],["Describe mirtazapine's mechanism.","Antagonism of alpha-2 auto- and heteroreceptors increases noradrenaline and serotonin release. 5-HT2 and 5-HT3 blockade directs serotonergic transmission towards 5-HT1A and reduces nausea and sexual dysfunction. Strong H1 blockade produces sedation and appetite increase."],["Why is ketamine's mechanism theoretically significant?","It produces antidepressant effect within hours without acting on monoamine transporters, through glutamatergic AMPA throughput and mTOR-dependent synaptogenesis, indicating that synaptic plasticity rather than monoamine availability is the therapeutic mechanism."]],"note":["Venlafaxine becomes meaningfully noradrenergic above about 150 mg daily.","Bupropion is contraindicated in eating disorders and in seizure disorder because it lowers seizure threshold.","Agomelatine requires liver function tests at baseline and periodically because of hepatotoxicity.","Cipriani's 2018 network meta-analysis found all 21 antidepressants studied more effective than placebo, with modest differences between them.","Antidepressants can precipitate a switch to mania in bipolar disorder and should not be used unopposed."],"refs":["stahl","maudsley","nice"]};
TEACH["4.3.2.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Mood stabilisers are the odd group, because they do not share a receptor. Lithium is a salt, valproate is an anticonvulsant, lamotrigine is another, and several antipsychotics qualify. What they share is not a target but an effect further inside the cell.","teach":["Lithium acts intracellularly, not at a receptor. Two main hypotheses: inhibition of inositol monophosphatase producing inositol depletion and dampening of phosphatidylinositol signalling; and inhibition of glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta, affecting circadian, apoptotic and plasticity pathways.","Lithium also increases BDNF and grey matter volume in imaging studies, and is neuroprotective in animal models."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","stahl","nice"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.2.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Sedatives all work on the same brake pedal in the brain, the GABA-A receptor. Benzodiazepines press it harder when the natural transmitter is already there. Barbiturates can press it themselves, with nobody's permission, which is exactly why they kill people and benzodiazepines mostly do not.","teach":["Benzodiazepines bind at the alpha-gamma interface of the GABA-A receptor as positive allosteric modulators, increasing the frequency of chloride channel opening. They require GABA to be present, which gives their effect a ceiling.","Barbiturates increase the duration of channel opening and at higher concentrations open the channel directly without GABA. There is no ceiling, hence respiratory depression and death in overdose."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","bnf","nice"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.2.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The dementia drugs do not stop the disease. They borrow function from the neurones that are still alive, and the loan comes due. That is not a reason to withhold them, but it is the honest way to describe them to a family who are hoping for more.","teach":["Cholinesterase inhibitors: donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine. They inhibit acetylcholinesterase, increasing synaptic acetylcholine, on the basis of the cholinergic deficit from nucleus basalis of Meynert degeneration.","Donepezil is a reversible selective acetylcholinesterase inhibitor with a long half-life allowing once-daily dosing. Rivastigmine inhibits both acetylcholinesterase and butyrylcholinesterase and is available as a patch, which reduces gastrointestinal effects. Galantamine additionally modulates nicotinic receptors allosterically."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","oxoldage","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.2.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Opioids work because the brain already has its own opioid system for damping pain and producing relief. The drugs simply do it harder and for longer. Everything else about them, the constipation, the euphoria, the deaths, follows from the same receptor.","teach":["Three receptor types, all G-protein coupled and Gi-linked: mu, delta and kappa. Endogenous ligands are endorphins, enkephalins and dynorphins.","Mu receptor activation produces analgesia, euphoria, respiratory depression, miosis, constipation, cough suppression, and physical dependence. Almost all clinically relevant opioid effect is mu-mediated."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","stahl","bnf"],"n":{"teach":10,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.2.7"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Stimulants all raise dopamine and noradrenaline, but they do it in two different ways, and the difference decides how dangerous they are. One blocks the door so the transmitter cannot get back in. The other kicks the door open and empties the room.","teach":["Methylphenidate blocks the dopamine and noradrenaline transporters, preventing reuptake. Its effect depends on ongoing release, which limits the maximum achievable increase.","Amphetamines block the transporters and additionally reverse their direction and displace transmitter from vesicles via VMAT2, producing much larger and release-independent increases. This is why amphetamines have higher abuse potential."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","nice","semaddict"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.2.8"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Cannabis works because the brain makes its own version already, and that system runs backwards: the receiving cell releases a chemical that travels back to the sending cell and tells it to slow down. Flooding that system with THC is why the effects are so diffuse.","teach":["The endocannabinoid system: endogenous ligands anandamide and 2-arachidonoylglycerol, acting at CB1 and CB2 receptors, both Gi-coupled.","CB1 is the most abundant G-protein coupled receptor in the brain, located presynaptically. Endocannabinoids are synthesised on demand by the postsynaptic cell and travel backwards across the synapse, which is retrograde signalling, inhibiting further transmitter release."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","stahl","agrawal"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.2.9"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The classical psychedelics all do one thing: they switch on a particular serotonin receptor in the cortex. That single action loosens the brain's habit of predicting what it is about to see, which is why the world looks unfamiliar and why people describe the self dissolving.","teach":["Classical psychedelics, LSD, psilocybin, mescaline and DMT, are 5-HT2A receptor agonists. 5-HT2A agonism is necessary for the subjective effect: pretreatment with a 5-HT2A antagonist such as ketanserin abolishes it.","5-HT2A receptors are densely expressed on layer V cortical pyramidal neurones, and their activation increases glutamatergic activity and cortical excitability."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","stahl","agrawal"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.2.10"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Novel psychoactive substances were designed to sit just outside the law, which meant they were designed by chemists who had never tested them on anybody. That is why they are more dangerous than the drugs they imitate, not less.","teach":["Definition: substances designed to reproduce the effects of controlled drugs while falling outside existing legislation. The Psychoactive Substances Act 2016 in the UK reversed the approach by prohibiting any psychoactive substance not specifically exempted.","Synthetic cannabinoid receptor agonists, including Spice and Black Mamba, are full CB1 agonists unlike THC's partial agonism. They cause severe agitation, psychosis, seizures, hyperthermia, acute kidney injury and death, and are prevalent in prison and homeless populations."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","bnf","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.3.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The dopamine hypothesis has been rewritten three times, and each version was forced by evidence that the previous one could not explain. The current version is not that there is too much dopamine, but that it is released at the wrong moments, so ordinary things arrive feeling important.","teach":["Version I, too much dopamine: based on all antipsychotics blocking D2, on the correlation between clinical potency and D2 affinity, and on amphetamine producing psychosis.","Problems with version I: post-mortem D2 elevation was confounded by treatment, and negative and cognitive symptoms did not improve and sometimes worsened with D2 blockade.","Version II, regional specificity: mesolimbic hyperdopaminergia produces positive symptoms; mesocortical hypodopaminergia produces negative and cognitive symptoms. This explained the asymmetry of treatment response.","Problems with version II: it did not explain the origin of the dysregulation, nor the failure of D2 blockade in a third of patients, nor clozapine's superiority at low D2 occupancy.","Version III, Howes and Kapur: the final common pathway is presynaptic striatal dopamine dysregulation, with increased synthesis and release capacity demonstrated on PET, present in the prodrome and in those at ultra-high risk, and greatest in the dorsal rather than the ventral striatum.","Multiple upstream routes, genetic, obstetric, social and drug-related, converge on that final pathway, which is why so many different risk factors produce one syndrome.","Aberrant salience is the phenomenological bridge: dysregulated dopamine assigns significance to stimuli that do not merit it, and the delusion is the person's explanation for that experience. This is why antipsychotics reduce conviction rather than correct the belief.","The unresolved problems: treatment resistance may involve normal striatal dopamine, suggesting a mechanistically distinct group; and negative and cognitive symptoms remain untreated by any dopaminergic strategy."],"cards":[["State version III of the dopamine hypothesis and its key evidence.","Howes and Kapur: the final common pathway is presynaptic striatal dopamine dysregulation with increased dopamine synthesis and release capacity, demonstrated by PET, present in the prodromal and ultra-high-risk states, and most marked in the dorsal striatum."],["Why did version I of the dopamine hypothesis fail?","Post-mortem receptor findings were confounded by antipsychotic treatment, and it could not explain why negative and cognitive symptoms fail to improve or worsen with D2 blockade, nor why a substantial minority of patients do not respond."],["How does aberrant salience link pharmacology to phenomenology?","Dysregulated dopamine release assigns motivational significance to irrelevant stimuli, producing an abnormal experience of significance. The delusion is the explanatory framework the person constructs for it, which is why antipsychotics dampen conviction rather than argue the belief away."]],"note":["PET studies show elevated dopamine synthesis capacity in the prodrome, before frank psychosis.","Treatment-resistant patients may show normal striatal dopamine synthesis capacity, suggesting a distinct mechanism.","Amphetamine challenge produces greater striatal dopamine release in patients than controls.","The hypothesis explains positive symptoms well and negative and cognitive symptoms poorly.","Kapur, Psychosis as a state of aberrant salience, American Journal of Psychiatry, 2003."],"refs":["stahl","semgen","agrawal"]};
TEACH["4.3.3.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The biogenic amine hypothesis says depression is a shortage of serotonin, noradrenaline and dopamine. It was built backwards from the fact that the drugs worked, and it has one problem it has never solved: the chemicals go up in hours and people get better in weeks.","teach":["Origin: reserpine, which depletes monoamines by blocking VMAT2, was associated with depression; iproniazid and imipramine, which raise monoamines, relieved it. Schildkraut formalised the catecholamine hypothesis in 1965.","The core claim: depression results from a functional deficiency of serotonin, noradrenaline or dopamine at central synapses."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["stahl","noptp","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.3.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Give a healthy young person a drug that blocks acetylcholine and they will fail memory tests like an old person. Take it away and they recover. That experiment is why we thought boosting acetylcholine would treat Alzheimer's disease, and why the treatments we have do help a little.","teach":["The cholinergic hypothesis of cognition: central cholinergic transmission is required for attention and for encoding new memories, and its loss produces the cognitive deficit of Alzheimer's disease.","Anatomy: nucleus basalis of Meynert projecting to neocortex, and medial septal nucleus and diagonal band projecting to hippocampus."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","stahl","nice"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.3.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"An older idea about mood: it is not the level of any one chemical that matters but the balance between two of them. Too much acetylcholine relative to noradrenaline gives depression; the reverse gives mania. It is largely superseded, but it explains several things the simpler hypothesis cannot.","teach":["Janowsky's cholinergic-adrenergic balance hypothesis, 1972: depression reflects relative cholinergic predominance and mania relative adrenergic predominance.","Supporting evidence: cholinergic agonists such as physostigmine can induce depressive symptoms and can transiently reverse mania. Anticholinergic drugs can produce elevated mood and are misused for that effect."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["stahl","noptp","agrawal"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.3.5"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Give a healthy volunteer ketamine and they will produce every kind of symptom of schizophrenia: the voices, the odd beliefs, the flatness and the thinking problems. Give them amphetamine and they will only produce the voices and the beliefs. That difference is the strongest single argument for the glutamate hypothesis.","teach":["The core claim: NMDA receptor hypofunction underlies schizophrenia, particularly the negative and cognitive symptoms that dopaminergic models fail to explain.","The founding evidence: phencyclidine and ketamine, non-competitive NMDA antagonists, produce positive, negative and cognitive symptoms in healthy volunteers and exacerbate symptoms in patients. Dopamine agonists produce only positive symptoms.","The mechanism: NMDA receptors on fast-spiking parvalbumin GABA interneurones are particularly sensitive. Their hypofunction disinhibits downstream pyramidal neurones, producing excessive and disorganised cortical glutamate release and impaired gamma synchrony.","This links to dopamine rather than competing with it. Cortical disinhibition disrupts descending regulation of subcortical dopamine neurones, producing the striatal dopamine dysregulation of version III. Glutamate is upstream; dopamine is the final common pathway.","Supporting evidence: reduced parvalbumin interneurone markers and reduced GAD67 expression post-mortem; genetic association with GRIN2A and other glutamatergic genes; and anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis producing a florid psychotic presentation.","Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis is the clinical proof of principle. Antibodies against the NMDA receptor produce psychosis, then seizures, movement disorder and autonomic instability, and it is treatable.","Therapeutic attempts have largely failed. Glycine, D-serine and D-cycloserine augmentation produced inconsistent results, and the mGluR2/3 agonist pomaglumetad failed in phase III.","The amyloid of psychiatry problem applies here: a strong mechanistic hypothesis that has not yet produced a treatment.","The exception worth knowing: ketamine's antidepressant action shows that manipulating this system therapeutically is possible, just not yet in schizophrenia."],"cards":[["What is the strongest evidence for the glutamate hypothesis of schizophrenia?","NMDA receptor antagonists such as ketamine and phencyclidine reproduce positive, negative and cognitive symptoms in healthy volunteers and exacerbate symptoms in patients, whereas dopamine agonists reproduce only positive symptoms."],["How does NMDA hypofunction produce cortical disinhibition?","NMDA receptors on fast-spiking parvalbumin GABA interneurones are especially sensitive to blockade. Their hypofunction reduces inhibition of pyramidal neurones, producing excessive disorganised glutamate release and impaired gamma oscillatory synchrony."],["How do the glutamate and dopamine hypotheses relate?","They are complementary rather than competing. Cortical NMDA hypofunction and disinhibition disrupt descending regulation of subcortical dopamine neurones, producing the presynaptic striatal dopamine dysregulation that constitutes the final common pathway."]],"note":["Reduced GAD67 and parvalbumin interneurone markers are among the most replicated post-mortem findings in schizophrenia.","Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis is treatable and must be excluded in atypical first-episode psychosis, particularly in young women with seizures or movement disorder.","Glutamatergic treatments for schizophrenia have consistently failed in phase III despite strong preclinical rationale.","GRIN2A is among the glutamatergic genes reaching genome-wide significance.","Ketamine's antidepressant effect demonstrates that the glutamate system is therapeutically tractable in other conditions."],"refs":["stahl","agrawal","semgen"]};
TEACH["4.3.3.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"For thirty years the plan was simple: amyloid causes Alzheimer's disease, so remove the amyloid. The amyloid came out and the patients did not get much better. That is the most expensive negative result in the history of medicine, and the field is still arguing about what it means.","teach":["The hypothesis, Hardy and Higgins 1992: accumulation of amyloid-beta, particularly Abeta42, is the initiating event, leading to tau hyperphosphorylation, neurofibrillary tangles, inflammation, synaptic loss and dementia.","Amyloid precursor protein is cleaved either by alpha-secretase, the non-amyloidogenic route, or by beta- and gamma-secretase, producing amyloid-beta. Abeta42 aggregates preferentially."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","nice","agrawal"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.4.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Most side effects are not mysterious. They are the drug doing exactly what it does, in a part of the body where you did not want it. Block histamine and the person is sleepy. Block alpha-1 and they faint standing up. Learn the receptors and you can predict the complaint before the patient makes it.","teach":["Postural hypotension: alpha-1 blockade. Worst with chlorpromazine, clozapine, quetiapine, risperidone and tricyclics. Highest risk in the elderly and on initiation or dose increase. Causes falls and fractures. Manage by slow titration, dose reduction, adequate hydration and reviewing antihypertensives.","Sedation: H1 blockade, with alpha-1 and muscarinic contributions. Greatest with clozapine, olanzapine, quetiapine, chlorpromazine and mirtazapine. Often useful early and unwanted later; consider evening dosing before switching.","Sexual dysfunction: multifactorial. Serotonergic 5-HT2 stimulation with SSRIs; hyperprolactinaemia with D2 blockade; alpha-1 blockade impairing ejaculation; and anticholinergic effects. Affects 30 to 70 per cent on SSRIs. It is the commonest unreported reason for stopping medication, so ask directly.","Weight gain and metabolic dysfunction: H1 and 5-HT2C antagonism, with muscarinic M3 blockade impairing insulin secretion. Greatest with clozapine and olanzapine; least with aripiprazole, lurasidone, ziprasidone and amisulpride. Most weight is gained in the first months, which is when to intervene.","Metabolic monitoring is mandatory: weight and waist, blood pressure, fasting glucose or HbA1c, and lipids at baseline, 12 weeks, one year, then annually. Metformin has the best evidence for mitigation, and switching to a lower-risk drug is effective.","QTc prolongation: blockade of the hERG potassium channel delaying repolarisation, risking torsades de pointes. Highest with intravenous haloperidol, pimozide and sertindole; citalopram and escitalopram are dose-capped, more strictly in the elderly. Lowest with aripiprazole and lurasidone.","QTc above 500 ms, or an increase of more than 60 ms from baseline, requires action. Correct potassium and magnesium, review all QT-prolonging drugs including antiemetics and antibiotics, and seek cardiology advice.","Anticholinergic effects: dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, urinary retention, tachycardia, and centrally confusion and memory impairment. Constipation on clozapine can progress to ileus and death and must be asked about at every review.","The clinical habit worth forming: match every complaint to a receptor before assuming it is psychological."],"cards":[["Which receptor actions produce weight gain, and which drugs are worst and best?","Histamine H1 and 5-HT2C antagonism, with muscarinic M3 blockade contributing to metabolic dysfunction. Worst: clozapine and olanzapine. Best: aripiprazole, lurasidone, ziprasidone and amisulpride."],["What QTc findings require action?","A QTc above 500 ms, or an increase of more than 60 ms from baseline. Correct potassium and magnesium, review all QT-prolonging drugs, reduce or switch the offending drug, and seek cardiology advice."],["Why must constipation be asked about at every clozapine review?","Clozapine causes severe gastrointestinal hypomotility which can progress to ileus, bowel obstruction, perforation and death. It causes more clozapine-related deaths than agranulocytosis and is frequently not volunteered by patients."]],"note":["Most antipsychotic weight gain occurs in the first three to six months, which is the window for intervention.","Metformin has the best evidence for mitigating antipsychotic-induced weight gain; GLP-1 agonists are under study.","Sexual dysfunction is the commonest unreported cause of covert discontinuation; ask directly and at baseline.","Hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia markedly increase torsades risk and are common in eating disorders and alcohol misuse.","Anticholinergic burden should be calculated in older adults and reduced wherever possible."],"refs":["maudsley","maudphys","bnf"]};
TEACH["4.3.4.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Two emergencies that look alike and are managed in opposite ways. One builds over days with the muscles locked rigid, after a drug that blocks dopamine. The other arrives in hours with the reflexes jumping and the feet clonic, after a drug that raises serotonin. Feel the ankles and you will usually know which.","teach":["Extrapyramidal syndromes and their time course: acute dystonia in hours to days; akathisia in days to weeks; parkinsonism in weeks to months; tardive dyskinesia after months to years.","Acute dystonia: sustained muscle contraction, torticollis, oculogyric crisis, trismus, and laryngeal dystonia which can obstruct the airway. Young men on high-potency drugs are at highest risk. Treat with procyclidine 5 to 10 mg intramuscularly or intravenously; effect within minutes.","Akathisia: subjective inner restlessness with inability to keep still, worst in the legs. It is associated with suicidal ideation and is routinely mistaken for agitation, leading to a dose increase that makes it worse. Reduce the dose or switch; propranolol has the best evidence; anticholinergics do not help.","Tardive dyskinesia: choreoathetoid movements, classically orofacial. Risk rises with age, female sex, duration, first-generation drugs, diabetes and previous acute extrapyramidal effects. Anticholinergics worsen it. Reduce or switch, ideally to clozapine; tetrabenazine and VMAT2 inhibitors are options.","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome: hyperthermia, lead-pipe rigidity, autonomic instability, altered consciousness, and very high creatine kinase, with leucocytosis and raised transaminases. Onset over 24 to 72 hours. Follows dopamine blockade or abrupt dopamine withdrawal, including levodopa cessation.","NMS management: stop the antipsychotic, transfer to medical care, cool, rehydrate aggressively, monitor for rhabdomyolysis, acute kidney injury and DIC. Consider dantrolene, bromocriptine or lorazepam. Mortality is around 10 per cent. Rechallenge after at least two weeks with a structurally different lower-potency drug at low dose.","Serotonin syndrome: onset within hours of a serotonergic drug or dose increase. The triad is neuromuscular excitation, autonomic instability and altered mental state. Hyperreflexia and clonus, particularly ocular and lower limb, with tremor, diarrhoea and agitation. The Hunter criteria are the diagnostic standard.","The distinction in one line: NMS is rigid, hyporeflexic and slow; serotonin syndrome is clonic, hyperreflexic and fast. Serotonin syndrome has diarrhoea; NMS does not.","Serotonin syndrome management: stop the serotonergic drug, supportive care, benzodiazepines for agitation and rigidity, active cooling, and cyproheptadine in severe cases."],"cards":[["Distinguish neuroleptic malignant syndrome from serotonin syndrome.","NMS: onset over 24 to 72 hours, lead-pipe rigidity, hyporeflexia, bradykinesia, very high creatine kinase, following dopamine blockade or dopamine withdrawal. Serotonin syndrome: onset within hours, hyperreflexia and clonus especially ocular and lower limb, tremor, diarrhoea and agitation, following a serotonergic drug or dose increase."],["How is acute dystonia treated and who is at highest risk?","Procyclidine 5 to 10 mg intramuscularly or intravenously, effective within minutes. Highest risk in young men, on high-potency antipsychotics, with rapid dose escalation, in the first hours to days of treatment."],["How is akathisia managed and what does not work?","Reduce the dose or switch antipsychotic. Propranolol has the best evidence; low-dose clonazepam is an alternative. Anticholinergics are ineffective. Recognising it matters because it is regularly mistaken for agitation and treated with a dose increase."]],"note":["NMS mortality is around 10 per cent, mainly from rhabdomyolysis, renal failure, respiratory failure and DIC.","NMS can follow abrupt withdrawal of levodopa or of dopamine agonists, not only antipsychotic administration.","The Hunter criteria are more sensitive and specific than the Sternbach criteria for serotonin syndrome.","Akathisia is associated with suicidal ideation and with non-adherence.","Rating scales: AIMS, Barnes Akathisia Rating Scale, Simpson-Angus."],"refs":["maudsley","bnf","semgen"]};
TEACH["4.3.4.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Some adverse effects are not dose-related and not predictable from the pharmacology. They happen to a small number of people for reasons largely to do with their immune system, and the only defence is knowing which drug does which, and acting the moment a patient reports the right symptom.","teach":["Idiosyncratic reactions are not dose-dependent, are not predictable from the drug's receptor profile, and often have an immunological or genetic basis.","Clozapine and neutropenia: agranulocytosis occurs in around 0.8 per cent, most in the first 18 weeks. Mandatory monitoring is FBC weekly for 18 weeks, fortnightly to 52 weeks, then monthly. Any fever or sore throat requires an urgent FBC. Benign ethnic neutropenia is important to recognise so that patients of African ancestry are not wrongly excluded."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","bnf","nice"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.5"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Every prescribing decision in pregnancy is a comparison, never a choice between a risk and no risk. Untreated severe mental illness harms both mother and baby, and stopping a drug is itself a decision with consequences. The one absolute is valproate.","teach":["Sodium valproate is the clearest teratogen in psychiatry: roughly 10 per cent risk of major congenital malformation including neural tube defects, and around 30 to 40 per cent risk of neurodevelopmental disorder in exposed children. It must not be used in women and girls of childbearing potential unless the conditions of the Pregnancy Prevention Programme are met.","Lithium: increased risk of cardiac malformation including Ebstein's anomaly. The relative risk is substantially raised but the absolute risk remains low, of the order of one in a thousand or lower for Ebstein's. It requires specialist counselling, level monitoring across pregnancy, and dose review immediately postpartum because clearance falls abruptly at delivery.","Carbamazepine: neural tube defects and craniofacial abnormalities; folate supplementation is advised. Lamotrigine has the most reassuring profile of the anticonvulsants in pregnancy but requires dose adjustment because clearance rises markedly and falls rapidly postpartum.","Antidepressants: SSRIs are not major teratogens. Paroxetine has been associated with a small increase in cardiac defects, though the evidence is inconsistent. Third-trimester exposure is associated with neonatal adaptation syndrome, with jitteriness, respiratory distress and feeding difficulty, usually resolving within two weeks, and with a small absolute increase in persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn.","Antipsychotics: no consistent evidence of major teratogenicity. Olanzapine and quetiapine increase gestational diabetes risk, so glucose tolerance testing is needed. Late exposure can cause extrapyramidal and withdrawal signs in the neonate.","Benzodiazepines: floppy infant syndrome and neonatal withdrawal with third-trimester or intrapartum exposure. The older association with cleft palate is not well supported.","Timing: organogenesis occurs in the first trimester, weeks three to eight, which is the window for structural malformation. Later exposure affects growth, neurodevelopment and neonatal adaptation.","Breastfeeding: sertraline and paroxetine have low milk transfer. Fluoxetine accumulates because of its long half-life. Lithium is generally avoided. Clozapine is contraindicated because of the risk of neonatal agranulocytosis.","The counselling standard: give absolute risks, not relative ones, discuss the risk of untreated illness explicitly, involve perinatal mental health services early, and document the discussion."],"cards":[["What are the risks of sodium valproate in pregnancy and what is the regulatory position?","Around 10 per cent risk of major congenital malformation including neural tube defects, and 30 to 40 per cent risk of neurodevelopmental disorder in exposed children. It must not be used in women and girls of childbearing potential unless the conditions of the Pregnancy Prevention Programme are met."],["What is neonatal adaptation syndrome?","A self-limiting syndrome after third-trimester SSRI exposure comprising jitteriness, irritability, respiratory distress, temperature instability and feeding difficulty, usually resolving within two weeks."],["Why must lithium levels be monitored through pregnancy and reviewed at delivery?","Renal clearance rises substantially in pregnancy, lowering levels, and falls abruptly at delivery with the loss of the expanded plasma volume, creating a risk of toxicity in the immediate postpartum period."]],"note":["Organogenesis occurs in weeks three to eight of gestation, the window for structural malformation.","The absolute risk of Ebstein's anomaly with lithium remains low despite a markedly raised relative risk; counsel with absolute figures.","Lamotrigine clearance rises markedly in pregnancy and falls rapidly postpartum; plan adjustment in both directions.","Clozapine is contraindicated in breastfeeding because of neonatal agranulocytosis risk.","The risk of untreated illness, including relapse, self-neglect, suicide and effects on the infant, must be part of every discussion."],"refs":["maudsley","nice","bnf"]};
TEACH["4.3.6.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Anticholinergics are prescribed to fix the movement problems that antipsychotics cause. They work well for two of those problems, do nothing for a third, and make a fourth worse. They also quietly impair memory and are traded on wards for their euphoriant effect.","teach":["Procyclidine, orphenadrine, trihexyphenidyl and benztropine are muscarinic antagonists used for drug-induced parkinsonism and acute dystonia.","They work for acute dystonia, dramatically and within minutes when given parenterally, and for drug-induced parkinsonism. They do not work for akathisia and they worsen tardive dyskinesia."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","bnf","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.6.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Psychiatrists prescribe anticonvulsants for reasons that have nothing to do with seizures, and they also inherit patients already on them. Both directions require knowing that these drugs interact with almost everything and that some of them cause psychiatric symptoms of their own.","teach":["Non-mood-stabilising anticonvulsants encountered in psychiatry include levetiracetam, topiramate, gabapentin, pregabalin, phenytoin and phenobarbital.","Levetiracetam binds synaptic vesicle protein 2A. It causes irritability, aggression, depression and, uncommonly, psychosis in a substantial minority, and is a common cause of behavioural disturbance in patients with epilepsy. Pyridoxine is sometimes used to mitigate this."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","bnf","lishman"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.6.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Pain and psychiatric illness feed each other, and the drugs cross over in both directions. Antidepressants relieve pain at doses too low to treat depression, and opioids prescribed for pain produce depression, low testosterone and dependence over the long term.","teach":["Non-opioid analgesics: paracetamol, and NSAIDs which increase gastrointestinal bleeding risk substantially when combined with SSRIs, since SSRIs deplete platelet serotonin. Consider gastroprotection when both are prescribed.","Antidepressants as analgesics: amitriptyline and duloxetine act on descending inhibitory pathways from periaqueductal grey through rostral ventromedial medulla to the dorsal horn, using serotonin and noradrenaline. Analgesia occurs at doses below the antidepressant range, which is why 10 to 75 mg amitriptyline is used in neuropathic pain."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["bnf","maudphys","nice"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.6.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Beta-blockers do not treat anxiety. They treat the body's version of it: the pounding heart, the shaking hands, the sweating. For a musician before a performance that is exactly what is needed. For a person with generalised anxiety it treats the symptom and leaves the disorder.","teach":["Beta-adrenoceptor antagonists reduce peripheral sympathetic manifestations: tachycardia, tremor, palpitations and sweating. They do not treat the cognitive component of anxiety.","Propranolol is non-selective and lipophilic, crossing the blood-brain barrier. Atenolol is cardioselective and hydrophilic, with less CNS penetration."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","bnf","nice"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.6.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Dopamine agonists are the mirror image of antipsychotics. Where one blocks dopamine and causes stiffness and flatness, the other floods it and causes hallucinations and gambling. Seeing them as opposite ends of one dial makes both sets of side effects predictable.","teach":["Drugs: levodopa with a peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor, and direct agonists ropinirole, pramipexole, rotigotine and, historically, cabergoline and bromocriptine.","Uses: Parkinson's disease, restless legs syndrome, hyperprolactinaemia including antipsychotic-induced, and NMS."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["bnf","oxoldage","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.6.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Adrenergic agonists are prescribed in psychiatry for two opposite reasons: to calm a child with ADHD, and to steady a person coming off opioids. Both work by turning down the same over-firing noradrenergic nucleus in the brainstem.","teach":["Alpha-2 agonists clonidine and guanfacine act on presynaptic autoreceptors, reducing noradrenaline release, and postsynaptically in prefrontal cortex, where guanfacine's alpha-2A selectivity strengthens network connectivity.","Guanfacine is licensed for ADHD in children and adolescents where stimulants are unsuitable or ineffective. It is non-stimulant, has no abuse potential, and causes sedation, hypotension and bradycardia."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["bnf","maudsley","semaddict"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.3.7"] = {"free":true,"simple":"ECT is the most effective treatment we have for severe depression and nobody can say exactly why it works. What is clear is that it is not simply the seizure. It changes the receptors, raises the growth factors and, uniquely among our treatments, appears to grow new cells in the hippocampus.","teach":["Monoamines: ECT increases the sensitivity of postsynaptic 5-HT1A receptors and downregulates beta-adrenoceptors, changes shared with antidepressants but produced faster.","Dopamine: ECT enhances dopaminergic transmission, which is why it is effective in Parkinson's disease with depression and in catatonia and neuroleptic malignant syndrome.","GABA: ECT increases GABAergic transmission, and the rising seizure threshold across a course is attributed to this. This is also the leading account of its anticonvulsant and therapeutic action.","The anticonvulsant hypothesis: the therapeutic effect correlates with the rise in seizure threshold and the shortening of seizures across a course, suggesting that the brain's inhibitory response to the seizure, not the seizure itself, is therapeutic.","Neurotrophic: ECT produces the largest increase in BDNF of any antidepressant treatment and increases hippocampal neurogenesis in animals. Hippocampal and amygdala volume increase measurably in humans over a course.","Neuroendocrine: ECT normalises the dysregulated HPA axis, with restoration of dexamethasone suppression accompanying clinical response.","Network effects: reduced functional connectivity in the subgenual cingulate and altered default mode network connectivity following a course.","Clinical facts that follow: bilateral placement is more effective and more cognitively costly than right unilateral; efficacy relates to the electrical dose relative to seizure threshold, not merely to producing a seizure; and the anaesthetic agent matters, since propofol raises seizure threshold.","Adverse effects: retrograde and anterograde memory disturbance, with autobiographical memory most affected. Most recovers over weeks to months, and a minority report persistent gaps."],"cards":[["State the anticonvulsant hypothesis of ECT.","The therapeutic effect correlates with the progressive rise in seizure threshold and shortening of seizure duration across a course, suggesting that the brain's endogenous inhibitory, largely GABAergic, response to repeated seizures is therapeutic rather than the seizure itself."],["What neurotrophic changes does ECT produce?","The largest increase in BDNF of any antidepressant treatment, increased hippocampal neurogenesis in animal models, and measurable increases in hippocampal and amygdala volume in humans over a course of treatment."],["Why is ECT effective in Parkinson's disease and catatonia?","It enhances dopaminergic transmission, which improves motor symptoms in Parkinson's disease and is thought to underlie its efficacy in catatonia and in neuroleptic malignant syndrome."]],"note":["Bitemporal placement is more effective and more cognitively costly than right unilateral; high-dose right unilateral narrows the gap.","Propofol raises seizure threshold; the choice of anaesthetic affects seizure adequacy.","ECT remains the most effective acute treatment for severe depression, particularly with psychotic features, stupor or refusal of food and fluid.","Autobiographical memory is the domain most affected; most recovers over weeks to months.","The ECT Handbook, Ferrier and Waite, is the College-listed reference. Item 6.3.1 and 6.3.2 cover the clinical aspects."],"refs":["ecthandbk","stahl","nice"]};
TEACH["4.4.1.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Before you write anything, answer one question: what should be different in six weeks, and how will we both know. If you cannot answer it, you are not treating anything, you are hoping. And a drug started without a target is a drug nobody will ever dare stop.","teach":["Define the target symptom or syndrome, not just the diagnosis. Sleep, appetite, concentration, agitation and suicidal thinking respond at different rates and are worth naming separately.","Set a measurable outcome and a time frame. Record a baseline score. Without a baseline you cannot demonstrate improvement or its absence, and this is the commonest failure in routine practice.","Distinguish the phases of treatment, because the goal changes with each: acute treatment aims at response and then remission; continuation prevents relapse of the same episode; maintenance prevents recurrence of a new episode.","Response is conventionally a 50 per cent reduction in symptom score; remission is a fall below a defined threshold with minimal residual symptoms. Residual symptoms predict relapse and should not be accepted as an endpoint.","Agree the goal with the patient, and include their goal. A patient's target is often function, sleep or being able to work, not a score.","Include a tolerability goal. A treatment that removes symptoms and produces intolerable sexual dysfunction or weight gain has not succeeded.","Set a review date and a stopping plan at the outset. Deprescribing is an active clinical task, and drugs continued by default cause substantial harm.","Document the rationale. A prescription with no recorded reason will be continued indefinitely by clinicians who do not know why it was started.","Where the goal is symptom control rather than cure, say so plainly. In dementia, in chronic psychosis and in personality disorder, honesty about what medication can and cannot do is part of the treatment."],"cards":[["Name the three phases of treatment and the goal of each.","Acute treatment, aiming at response then remission; continuation treatment, preventing relapse of the current episode; and maintenance treatment, preventing recurrence of a new episode."],["Why is recording a baseline essential?","Without a baseline measurement, improvement or its absence cannot be demonstrated, response cannot be judged, and decisions about continuing, switching or stopping are made on impression rather than evidence."],["Why do residual symptoms matter after treatment?","They predict relapse and poorer functional outcome, so remission with minimal residual symptoms rather than mere response should be the target, and persistent residual symptoms warrant further optimisation."]],"note":["Every long-term prescription should carry a documented indication and a review date.","Functional recovery lags symptomatic recovery and should be measured separately.","Shared decision making is explicit in NICE guidance and improves adherence.","Patient-defined goals frequently differ from clinician-defined goals and should be elicited.","A tolerability goal is part of the treatment goal, not a separate consideration."],"refs":["nice","maudsley","semgen"]};
TEACH["4.4.1.2.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The same diagnosis does not mean the same prescription. Mild depression and severe depression with psychosis are the same word and different illnesses, and the difference decides not just the dose but whether a drug is the right treatment at all.","teach":["Severity determines whether medication is indicated at all. In mild depression, antidepressants have a poor risk-benefit balance and NICE recommends psychological or low-intensity interventions first. Drug-placebo difference increases with baseline severity.","Depression: for moderate to severe illness an SSRI is first line, with psychological therapy. Psychotic depression requires an antidepressant plus an antipsychotic, or ECT; an antidepressant alone is inadequate."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","semgen"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.4.1.2.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The same drug is used differently at three points in an illness: getting somebody well, keeping them well through the current episode, and stopping the next one. Most relapses happen because treatment stopped at the end of the first phase.","teach":["Acute phase: aims at response then remission. Antidepressants are judged at four to six weeks at a therapeutic dose; antipsychotics at six weeks; OCD at twelve weeks.","Continuation phase: the same dose that achieved remission is continued to prevent relapse of the same episode. In depression, at least six months after remission for a first episode."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","semgen"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.4.1.2.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The physical health of a psychiatric patient decides which drug is safe far more often than the psychiatric diagnosis decides which drug is effective. A patient with a long QT, chronic kidney disease and diabetes has already narrowed your options before you have thought about symptoms.","teach":["Cardiac: avoid or use with caution QT-prolonging drugs where there is existing QT prolongation, bradycardia, heart failure or electrolyte disturbance. Aripiprazole and lurasidone have the lowest QT burden. Citalopram and escitalopram are dose-capped. Tricyclics are contraindicated after recent myocardial infarction and are cardiotoxic in overdose; sertraline is the usual choice post-infarction.","Renal: lithium, amisulpride, sulpiride and paliperidone are substantially renally cleared and require dose reduction or avoidance. Lithium is relatively contraindicated in significant renal impairment. Gabapentin and pregabalin also accumulate."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudphys","maudsley","bnf"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.4.1.2.4"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Start low and go slow is not a slogan, it is arithmetic. An eighty-year-old clears drugs at half the rate, holds fat-soluble ones far longer, and has a brain that is more sensitive to every one of them. The same tablet is not the same dose.","teach":["Children and adolescents: most psychotropic prescribing is off-label because trials are scarce. Weight-adjusted clearance is often higher than in adults, so mg per kg doses may exceed adult equivalents. Fluoxetine is the only antidepressant with a favourable balance in this age group and is used with psychological therapy and close early monitoring for suicidal ideation.","Adolescents require particular attention to activation and akathisia in the first weeks, when suicidal ideation risk is highest.","Adults: standard dosing, with attention to reproductive status in anyone of childbearing potential. Valproate must not be used in women and girls of childbearing potential outside the Pregnancy Prevention Programme.","Older adults: reduced hepatic mass and blood flow, reduced renal clearance, reduced albumin, reduced total body water and increased body fat. Lipophilic drugs such as diazepam accumulate markedly; water-soluble drugs reach higher peak concentrations.","Older adults are more sensitive to anticholinergic, sedative, orthostatic and extrapyramidal effects at any given concentration, independent of pharmacokinetics.","Specific risks in old age: hyponatraemia from SSRI-induced SIADH, commonest in the first month; falls from sedation and postural hypotension; and increased stroke and mortality risk from antipsychotics in dementia.","Start at a half to a third of the adult dose in older people, titrate slowly, and review often. But do not undertreat: therapeutic nihilism in old age psychiatry is as harmful as overprescribing, and depression in older people responds well to adequate treatment.","Anticholinergic burden should be actively calculated and reduced in older adults, as it predicts falls, delirium, cognitive decline and mortality.","Intellectual disability: greater sensitivity, greater difficulty reporting adverse effects, and a high rate of inappropriate long-term prescribing. STOMP is the national programme addressing this."],"cards":[["Which antidepressant is first line in children and adolescents, and why?","Fluoxetine, the only antidepressant with a favourable risk-benefit balance in this age group, used alongside psychological therapy with close monitoring for suicidal ideation in the early weeks."],["Why do older people require lower doses, beyond reduced clearance?","Increased body fat and reduced total body water alter distribution, and there is increased pharmacodynamic sensitivity at the receptor level, so older people experience greater anticholinergic, sedative, orthostatic and extrapyramidal effects at any given plasma concentration."],["Name three specific prescribing risks in older adults.","Hyponatraemia from SSRI-induced SIADH, commonest in the first month; falls from sedation and postural hypotension; and increased stroke and mortality risk from antipsychotics prescribed in dementia."]],"note":["Therapeutic nihilism in older adults is as harmful as overprescribing; depression in old age responds well to adequate treatment including ECT.","STOMP addresses over-medication of people with a learning disability, autism or both.","Anticholinergic burden scales should be used routinely in older adults.","Valproate must not be used in women and girls of childbearing potential outside the Pregnancy Prevention Programme.","Most psychotropic prescribing in children is off-label; document the rationale and inform the family."],"refs":["maudsley","nice","oxoldage"]};
TEACH["4.4.1.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There is no such thing as a safe drug, only a drug whose risks are worth taking for this person. The comparison is never with no risk. It is with the risk of leaving the illness untreated, and that risk is usually the larger one.","teach":["The comparison must be explicit: the risk of the drug against the risk of the untreated condition, not against zero. Untreated severe depression carries a substantial mortality; untreated psychosis carries deterioration, disengagement and risk.","Individualise. The same drug carries different risk in a 24-year-old man, a woman planning pregnancy, and an 84-year-old with atrial fibrillation and renal impairment."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","nice","noptp"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.4.2.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Two of the commonest mistakes in psychiatry are giving too little for too short a time and then calling the drug a failure, and giving more than the maximum in the belief that more must work better. Both come from not knowing the numbers.","teach":["Minimum effective dose: the lowest dose that produces the therapeutic effect. For many antipsychotics and SSRIs the dose-response curve is flat above this, so increasing the dose adds side effects without adding benefit.","Antipsychotics: haloperidol 4 to 6 mg, olanzapine 10 mg, risperidone 4 mg and aripiprazole 10 mg daily are around the effective dose for most patients with first-episode psychosis. Exceeding licensed maxima has no evidence base.","Antidepressants: SSRIs have a relatively flat dose-response curve for depression above the starting dose, so increasing beyond the standard dose helps a minority. In OCD and in some anxiety disorders higher doses genuinely are needed.","Time to response: some improvement is usually visible by two weeks and predicts eventual response. Judge antidepressants at four to six weeks at a therapeutic dose, antipsychotics at six weeks, and OCD at twelve weeks.","Early improvement at two weeks is one of the better predictors of eventual response, so absence of any change by then is a reason to review dose and adherence rather than to wait passively.","Lithium: therapeutic 0.4 to 1.0 mmol/L, commonly 0.6 to 0.8 for maintenance, higher in acute mania, measured twelve hours post-dose.","Clozapine: plasma levels above about 350 micrograms per litre are associated with better response; seizure risk rises above about 600.","Before declaring a treatment failure, check three things: was the dose adequate, was the duration adequate, and was the drug actually taken. Failure of any of these is far commoner than true non-response.","Titrate to effect and tolerability, not to the maximum in the formulary."],"cards":[["What must be verified before declaring a drug ineffective?","That the dose was adequate, that the duration was adequate, and that the drug was actually taken. Non-adherence and inadequate trials are far commoner than true non-response."],["How long should an antidepressant, an antipsychotic and an OCD trial run before judging response?","Antidepressants four to six weeks at a therapeutic dose, antipsychotics six weeks, and OCD at least twelve weeks at a higher dose."],["What is the evidence for exceeding licensed antipsychotic doses?","There is none for routine use. The dose-response curve is flat above the effective range, so higher doses add adverse effects including QT prolongation and extrapyramidal symptoms without improving efficacy."]],"note":["Early improvement at two weeks predicts eventual response and should prompt review if absent.","SSRIs have a relatively flat dose-response curve in depression but a steeper one in OCD.","Lithium level is taken twelve hours post-dose; a level before steady state cannot be interpreted.","Clozapine levels guide dosing where response is inadequate or toxicity is suspected.","High-dose antipsychotic treatment requires documented rationale, consent where possible, and enhanced monitoring including ECG."],"refs":["maudsley","nice","bnf"]};
TEACH["4.4.2.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"When somebody does not get better, the temptation is to add another drug. The better move almost every time is to go back and check whether the first one was ever given a fair chance, because inadequate treatment looks exactly like resistance.","teach":["Pseudoresistance is the first consideration: inadequate dose, inadequate duration, non-adherence, wrong diagnosis, untreated comorbidity such as substance misuse or a physical condition, and unaddressed psychosocial maintaining factors.","Treatment-resistant depression: conventionally failure to respond to two antidepressants of adequate dose and duration. Options are switching within or between classes, augmentation, combination, ECT, and specialist interventions.","Augmentation in depression: lithium has the longest evidence; an atypical antipsychotic, quetiapine or aripiprazole, is licensed for augmentation; liothyronine is an option. Combination of an SSRI with mirtazapine is used.","ECT remains the most effective acute treatment for severe depression, and is particularly indicated with psychotic features, stupor, food refusal or high suicide risk.","Newer options: ketamine and esketamine, licensed under supervision, and repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation, approved by NICE for treatment-resistant depression.","Treatment-resistant schizophrenia: failure to respond to two antipsychotics, at least one a second-generation drug, each at a therapeutic dose for at least six weeks, with adherence confirmed. Clozapine is then indicated.","Clozapine should not be delayed. Delay after criteria are met is common and is associated with poorer response, and around a third of patients who would benefit never receive it.","Clozapine augmentation, where clozapine alone is inadequate at an adequate plasma level, has a weak evidence base. Amisulpride and ECT have the least poor evidence.","STAR*D taught the shape of the problem: about a third remitted at step one and around two thirds cumulatively across four steps, with declining remission and rising relapse at each successive step."],"cards":[["What is pseudoresistance and what does it comprise?","Apparent treatment resistance caused by inadequate dose, inadequate duration, non-adherence, incorrect diagnosis, untreated comorbidity including substance misuse and physical illness, or unaddressed psychosocial maintaining factors."],["Define treatment-resistant schizophrenia and state the consequence.","Failure to respond to adequate trials of two antipsychotics, at least one second-generation, each for at least six weeks at a therapeutic dose with adherence confirmed. Clozapine is then indicated and should be offered without delay."],["What augmentation strategies have evidence in treatment-resistant depression?","Lithium augmentation, atypical antipsychotic augmentation with quetiapine or aripiprazole, liothyronine, combination with mirtazapine, ECT, and specialist options including ketamine or esketamine and rTMS."]],"note":["STAR*D: approximately a third remitted at step one, around two thirds cumulatively over four steps, with declining remission at each step.","Delay in initiating clozapine after criteria are met is associated with poorer response.","Around a third of patients who meet criteria for clozapine never receive it.","Clozapine augmentation has a weak evidence base; check the plasma level and adherence first.","Reassess the diagnosis in any apparently resistant case; bipolar depression treated as unipolar is a common cause."],"refs":["maudsley","nice","semgen"]};
TEACH["4.4.2.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Swapping one drug for another sounds simple and is the point at which many patients get worse. Stop the first too fast and you get discontinuation symptoms or relapse. Overlap carelessly and you get serotonin syndrome. The method matters as much as the choice.","teach":["Four strategies: direct switch, stopping one and starting the other next day; cross-taper, reducing one while introducing the other; taper and immediate switch; and taper with a washout before starting.","Cross-tapering is usually preferred because it avoids both discontinuation symptoms and a period without cover. It requires care where the two drugs have overlapping toxicity."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","bnf","nice"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.4.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Monitoring is where psychiatry most often fails its patients, and it is not because anybody forgets that clozapine needs bloods. It is because nobody weighed the patient at the start, so nobody can tell that they have gained twenty kilograms.","teach":["Antipsychotics: weight and waist circumference, blood pressure, pulse, fasting glucose or HbA1c, lipid profile, prolactin where indicated, and ECG where the drug or the patient warrants it. Timing is baseline, 12 weeks, one year, then annually. Weight should be checked more often in the first months, when most gain occurs.","Clozapine: FBC weekly for 18 weeks, fortnightly to 52 weeks, then monthly, through the registered monitoring service. Plus troponin, CRP and ECG at baseline and in the first eight weeks for myocarditis, plasma levels where indicated, and enquiry about constipation at every review.","Lithium: level after five to seven days and after every dose change, then every three months for the first year and at least six-monthly thereafter. U&Es, eGFR, calcium and thyroid function at baseline and at least every six months. Weight and ECG where indicated.","Valproate: FBC and liver function at baseline and after six months; weight; and, in anyone of childbearing potential, annual review under the Pregnancy Prevention Programme.","Lamotrigine: no routine bloods, but explicit warning about rash and immediate cessation if one develops.","Antidepressants: sodium at baseline and after two weeks in older people or those on diuretics; review at one to two weeks in under-25s and anyone at increased suicide risk; blood pressure with venlafaxine; liver function with agomelatine.","Cholinesterase inhibitors: pulse and cardiac history at baseline, with ECG where conduction disease is suspected.","Extrapyramidal effects: ask and examine at every review, using AIMS, the Barnes Akathisia Rating Scale and Simpson-Angus. Akathisia in particular will not be volunteered.","The principle: a monitoring schedule that is not written down and not owned by a named clinician will not happen. Physical health monitoring in severe mental illness is repeatedly shown to be poor, and the life expectancy gap is the consequence."],"cards":[["Give the full clozapine monitoring requirements.","FBC weekly for 18 weeks, fortnightly to 52 weeks, then monthly through the registered service. Baseline and first eight weeks monitoring for myocarditis with troponin, CRP and ECG. Plasma levels where response or toxicity is in question, and enquiry about constipation at every review."],["Give the lithium monitoring schedule.","Level after five to seven days and after every dose change, then three-monthly for the first year and at least six-monthly thereafter. U&Es, eGFR, calcium and thyroid function at baseline and at least every six months."],["What antipsychotic monitoring is required and when?","Weight and waist, blood pressure, pulse, fasting glucose or HbA1c, lipids, prolactin where indicated, and ECG where warranted, at baseline, 12 weeks, one year and then annually, with more frequent weight monitoring in the first months."]],"note":["Most antipsychotic weight gain occurs in the first three to six months, so early frequent weighing is what matters.","Physical health monitoring in severe mental illness is consistently poor in audit, and the 15 to 20 year life expectancy gap is the consequence.","Lamotrigine requires no routine bloods but requires explicit rash counselling.","Venlafaxine requires blood pressure monitoring at higher doses.","A monitoring plan needs a named responsible clinician, or it does not happen."],"refs":["maudsley","nice","maudphys"]};
TEACH["4.4.4.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Alcohol withdrawal can kill and opioid withdrawal cannot. That single asymmetry decides how you manage the ward: the shaking alcoholic needs urgent treatment, and the sweating opioid user needs comfort and a plan.","teach":["Alcohol withdrawal: tremor, sweating, anxiety and nausea at 6 to 12 hours; seizures typically 12 to 48 hours; delirium tremens at 48 to 72 hours, occasionally later. Delirium tremens carries a mortality of a few per cent even when treated.","Mechanism: chronic alcohol potentiates GABA-A and inhibits NMDA, producing compensatory GABA-A downregulation and NMDA upregulation. On withdrawal, unopposed glutamatergic excitation lowers the seizure threshold.","Management: a reducing regimen of chlordiazepoxide, either fixed-dose or symptom-triggered using CIWA-Ar. Lorazepam is preferred in significant hepatic impairment because it undergoes glucuronidation only.","Parenteral thiamine must be given before any carbohydrate load, to prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy. Do not wait for the full triad of confusion, ophthalmoplegia and ataxia, which is present in only a minority.","Alcoholic hallucinosis occurs in clear consciousness, usually auditory, and is distinct from delirium tremens.","Opioid withdrawal: yawning, lacrimation, rhinorrhoea, sweating, dilated pupils, piloerection, myalgia, abdominal cramps, diarrhoea, vomiting, insomnia and craving. Highly unpleasant, rarely dangerous in the physically well, but dangerous in pregnancy where it risks miscarriage and premature labour.","Opioid management: substitution with methadone or buprenorphine, or symptomatic treatment with lofexidine, antiemetics, antidiarrhoeals and analgesics.","Benzodiazepine and stimulant withdrawal: benzodiazepine withdrawal can cause seizures and requires a slow taper. Stimulant withdrawal produces hypersomnia, hyperphagia, dysphoria and craving, and is not physically dangerous but carries suicide risk.","Loss of tolerance after abstinence is the most dangerous moment. Fatal opioid overdose most often occurs after prison release or inpatient detoxification, which is why take-home naloxone belongs in the discharge plan."],"cards":[["Give the time course of alcohol withdrawal.","Tremor, sweating and anxiety at 6 to 12 hours; seizures typically at 12 to 48 hours; delirium tremens at 48 to 72 hours, occasionally later."],["What must be given before any carbohydrate in alcohol withdrawal, and why?","Parenteral thiamine, to prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy. Glucose administration in a thiamine-deficient patient can precipitate it. Treat on suspicion, since the full triad is present in only a minority."],["When is opioid withdrawal genuinely dangerous?","In pregnancy, where it risks miscarriage and premature labour, and in those with significant physical comorbidity. In the physically well it is severely unpleasant but not usually life-threatening, unlike alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal."]],"note":["CIWA-Ar guides symptom-triggered alcohol withdrawal regimens and reduces total benzodiazepine exposure.","Lorazepam, oxazepam and temazepam are preferred in hepatic impairment.","Delirium tremens shows fast EEG activity, unlike other deliria which show slowing.","Take-home naloxone should be provided at discharge from detoxification and on prison release.","Benzodiazepine withdrawal can cause seizures and requires a slow taper, typically over months."],"refs":["semaddict","nice","maudsley"]};
TEACH["4.4.4.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Coming off a prescribed psychiatric drug can be genuinely hard, and for years the profession said it was not. Discontinuation symptoms are real, they can be severe and prolonged, and telling a patient the illness has returned when it is actually withdrawal does real harm.","teach":["Antidepressant discontinuation symptoms: flu-like symptoms, insomnia, vivid dreams, nausea, imbalance, sensory disturbance including electric-shock sensations often described as brain zaps, and hyperarousal with anxiety and irritability.","Onset is within days of reduction or omission, and symptoms resolve rapidly on reinstatement. That timing distinguishes them from relapse, which develops over weeks and does not resolve immediately on restarting.","Paroxetine and venlafaxine are worst because of short half-lives with no long-acting metabolite. Fluoxetine is least affected because norfluoxetine has a half-life of four to sixteen days.","The evidence base changed. Guidance previously described discontinuation as mild and self-limiting over one to two weeks. Current NICE guidance recognises that symptoms can be severe and prolonged in a substantial minority, and recommends slow tapering, often over months, with hyperbolic dose reduction, taking proportionally smaller steps at lower doses because receptor occupancy falls steeply at the bottom of the dose range.","Benzodiazepine withdrawal: anxiety, insomnia, tremor, sweating, perceptual hypersensitivity, depersonalisation and seizures. Onset within one to two days for short-acting drugs and up to a week for long-acting ones. Switch to an equivalent dose of diazepam and reduce by around an eighth every two to four weeks, guided by the patient.","Antipsychotic discontinuation: cholinergic rebound with agitation, nausea, sweating and insomnia; withdrawal dyskinesias; and supersensitivity psychosis. Abrupt discontinuation carries a high relapse risk.","Lithium: abrupt discontinuation causes rebound mania. Withdraw over at least four weeks where stopping is agreed.","The clinical duty: warn before starting, not only when stopping. Patients who are told to expect discontinuation symptoms cope far better and are less likely to conclude that they cannot manage without the drug.","Deprescribing is an active clinical task requiring time, a plan and follow-up, and should not be attempted in a crisis or at a single appointment."],"cards":[["How are antidepressant discontinuation symptoms distinguished from relapse?","Discontinuation symptoms begin within days of dose reduction or omission and resolve rapidly on reinstatement. Relapse develops over weeks, involves the original depressive symptoms, and does not resolve immediately on restarting."],["What is hyperbolic tapering and why is it recommended?","Reducing by proportionally smaller absolute amounts as the dose falls, because receptor occupancy declines steeply at the bottom of the dose range. Linear reductions therefore produce disproportionately large pharmacological changes at low doses."],["How should benzodiazepine withdrawal be managed?","Switch to an equivalent dose of diazepam and reduce by around one eighth of the dose every two to four weeks, guided by the patient's symptoms, over months rather than weeks."]],"note":["Paroxetine and venlafaxine cause the most severe discontinuation symptoms; fluoxetine the least.","NICE guidance now recognises that discontinuation symptoms can be severe and prolonged in a substantial minority.","Supersensitivity psychosis after abrupt antipsychotic withdrawal reflects D2 receptor upregulation.","Abrupt lithium discontinuation causes rebound mania; taper over at least four weeks.","Warn patients about discontinuation before starting, not when stopping."],"refs":["nice","maudsley","bnf"]};
TEACH["4.4.5.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Almost nothing we prescribe for children has been properly tested in children. That is not a reason to withhold treatment, but it is a reason to prescribe slowly, monitor closely and say honestly what is and is not known.","teach":["Most psychotropic prescribing in children and adolescents is off-label. Say so, record the reasoning, and involve the family in the decision.","Depression: fluoxetine is first line and the only antidepressant with a favourable risk-benefit balance in this age group. It is used alongside psychological therapy, not instead of it. Sertraline and citalopram are second line."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["rutter","nice","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.4.5.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Start low, go slow, review often, and do not confuse caution with abandonment. Older people are more sensitive to every psychiatric drug, and they are also the group most likely to be left with untreated depression because somebody decided they were too frail to treat.","teach":["Pharmacokinetic changes: reduced hepatic mass and blood flow, reduced renal clearance, reduced albumin, reduced total body water and increased body fat. Lipophilic drugs accumulate; hydrophilic drugs reach higher peaks.","Pharmacodynamic changes: greater sensitivity at any given concentration to anticholinergic, sedative, orthostatic and extrapyramidal effects.","Start at a half to a third of the adult dose and titrate slowly, but titrate. Underdosing to an ineffective level is not caution, it is exposure to risk without benefit.","Antidepressants: sertraline, citalopram at reduced maximum dose, and mirtazapine are commonly used. Check sodium at baseline and after two weeks; SSRI-induced hyponatraemia is commonest in older women in the first month.","Antipsychotics in dementia: increase stroke risk roughly threefold and increase mortality. Use only for severe distress or risk, at the lowest dose, time-limited, with documented review. Avoid entirely in Lewy body disease and Parkinson's disease dementia where possible.","Benzodiazepines: strongly associated with falls, fractures and confusion. Prefer short-acting agents if unavoidable, and for the shortest possible time.","Anticholinergic burden should be calculated across the whole prescription and reduced. It predicts falls, delirium, cognitive decline and mortality, and directly opposes cholinesterase inhibitor treatment.","Lithium in older people: reduced renal clearance means lower doses and more frequent monitoring, with a lower target level, and greater vulnerability to dehydration and to interacting drugs.","ECT is effective and well tolerated in severe late-life depression, particularly with psychosis, stupor or food refusal, and should be considered earlier rather than as a last resort."],"cards":[["What are the risks of antipsychotics in dementia?","An approximately threefold increase in stroke risk and an increase in all-cause mortality. They should be used only for severe distress or risk, at the lowest effective dose, time-limited and reviewed, and avoided in Lewy body disease where possible."],["What monitoring is specifically required when starting an SSRI in an older person?","Serum sodium at baseline and after approximately two weeks, because of the risk of hyponatraemia from SIADH, which is commonest in older women, in those on diuretics, and in the first month of treatment."],["Why is anticholinergic burden particularly important in older adults?","It predicts falls, delirium, cognitive decline and mortality, accumulates across all prescribed drugs including those prescribed by other specialties, and directly opposes cholinesterase inhibitor treatment in dementia."]],"note":["Therapeutic nihilism is a real harm; late-life depression responds well to adequate treatment including ECT.","Lipophilic benzodiazepines such as diazepam accumulate markedly in older people.","Falls risk should be assessed before and after any sedative or antihypertensive-acting psychotropic is started.","Deprescribing reviews are a core part of old age psychiatry practice.","Antipsychotic prescribing in care homes remains a recognised quality problem."],"refs":["oxoldage","nice","maudsley"]};
TEACH["4.4.5.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"In severe eating disorders the danger is not the drug, it is the body it is going into. A starved heart has a long QT, low potassium and no reserve, and the drug that would be routine anywhere else can be the thing that stops it.","teach":["Physiological context: bradycardia, hypotension, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hypophosphataemia, prolonged QTc, and hepatic impairment from starvation. All increase drug risk.","No drug treats anorexia nervosa itself. Weight restoration and psychological therapy are the treatment. Medication addresses comorbidity, and this must be said clearly to families hoping otherwise."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudphys","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.4.5.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"When the liver or the kidney is failing, the drug does not leave. When the heart is failing, the drug that prolongs the QT has much less margin. In each case the answer is the same: pick the drug that does not depend on the failing organ.","teach":["Hepatic impairment: reduced first-pass metabolism raises bioavailability; reduced albumin raises free drug; reduced clearance prolongs half-life; and encephalopathy risk means sedatives must be used with great care.","In hepatic impairment prefer drugs cleared by glucuronidation or renally: lorazepam, oxazepam and temazepam among benzodiazepines. Avoid or use very cautiously valproate, agomelatine, duloxetine and chlorpromazine. Start at low doses and monitor liver function."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudphys","maudsley","semliaison"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.4.5.5"] = {"free":true,"simple":"There is no risk-free option in pregnancy. Treating carries risk to the baby and not treating carries risk to both. The only wrong answer is to stop a drug abruptly the moment a pregnancy test is positive, which is the commonest thing that happens.","teach":["The framing: every decision is a comparison between the risk of the drug and the risk of untreated illness. Relapse in pregnancy and the postpartum period carries risk of self-neglect, suicide, and harm to the developing child through maternal illness.","Abrupt discontinuation on discovering pregnancy is common, is usually the wrong decision, and frequently precipitates relapse at the point of greatest vulnerability. Organogenesis is largely complete by week eight, so stopping on a positive test rarely prevents malformation.","Valproate is the one near-absolute: roughly 10 per cent major malformation and 30 to 40 per cent neurodevelopmental disorder in exposed children. It must not be used in women and girls of childbearing potential outside the Pregnancy Prevention Programme.","Lithium: cardiac malformation including Ebstein's anomaly, with markedly raised relative but low absolute risk. If continued, monitor levels through pregnancy as clearance rises, obtain fetal echocardiography, and reduce or withhold around delivery with prompt review because clearance falls abruptly.","Antidepressants: SSRIs are not major teratogens. Sertraline is commonly preferred. Third-trimester exposure gives neonatal adaptation syndrome and a small absolute increase in persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn.","Antipsychotics: no consistent evidence of major teratogenicity. Olanzapine and quetiapine increase gestational diabetes risk, requiring glucose tolerance testing.","Postpartum: this is the highest-risk period. Postpartum psychosis affects one to two per thousand deliveries, usually within two weeks, with risk raised more than twentyfold in bipolar disorder and around 50 per cent after a previous episode. Prophylaxis should be planned before delivery.","Breastfeeding: sertraline and paroxetine have low milk transfer. Fluoxetine accumulates in the infant. Lithium is generally avoided. Clozapine is contraindicated because of neonatal agranulocytosis risk.","Practical requirement: involve perinatal mental health services early, ideally before conception. Preconception counselling is where these decisions should be made, not in the first antenatal appointment."],"cards":[["Why is stopping a psychotropic on discovering pregnancy usually wrong?","Organogenesis is largely complete by week eight, so stopping on a positive test rarely prevents malformation, while abrupt discontinuation risks relapse at a period of high vulnerability with consequences for both mother and child."],["What are the risks of lithium in pregnancy and how is it managed if continued?","Increased risk of cardiac malformation including Ebstein's anomaly, with high relative but low absolute risk. Management includes specialist counselling, level monitoring through pregnancy as clearance rises, fetal echocardiography, and dose review around delivery when clearance falls abruptly."],["What is the risk of postpartum psychosis and in whom?","One to two per thousand deliveries in the general population, raised more than twentyfold in women with bipolar disorder, and around 50 per cent after a previous episode of postpartum psychosis. Prophylaxis should be planned before delivery."]],"note":["Valproate must not be used in women and girls of childbearing potential outside the Pregnancy Prevention Programme.","Suicide is a leading cause of maternal death in the UK; MBRRACE-UK is the source.","Mother and baby units exist so that admission does not separate mother and infant.","Lamotrigine clearance rises markedly in pregnancy and falls rapidly postpartum, requiring planned adjustment both ways.","Preconception counselling is where these decisions should be made; NICE CG192 covers antenatal and postnatal mental health."],"refs":["nice","maudsley","semgen"]};
TEACH["4.4.6"] = {"free":true,"simple":"You cannot detoxify somebody into recovery, but you can keep them alive long enough to have the chance. That is the honest case for substitution treatment, and the evidence for it is among the strongest in psychiatry.","teach":["Alcohol relapse prevention. Acamprosate modulates glutamatergic transmission and reduces craving; start soon after detoxification. Naltrexone, an opioid antagonist, reduces the reward of drinking and is useful for reducing heavy drinking as well as maintaining abstinence. Disulfiram inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase, causing an aversive reaction with flushing, nausea, tachycardia and hypotension; it requires supervision and motivation. Nalmefene is licensed for reduction of heavy drinking.","Alcohol withdrawal: chlordiazepoxide reducing regimen, or lorazepam in significant hepatic impairment, with parenteral thiamine before carbohydrate.","Opioid substitution. Methadone is a full mu agonist with good retention in treatment, a long variable half-life, greater overdose risk, and QTc prolongation. Buprenorphine is a high-affinity partial mu agonist with a ceiling on respiratory depression, safer in overdose, less sedating, but able to precipitate withdrawal if started too soon.","Opioid substitution treatment reduces mortality, illicit use, injecting risk behaviour, blood-borne virus transmission and criminal activity. Retention in treatment is the outcome that matters most.","Naltrexone is used for opioid relapse prevention after detoxification in motivated patients, and requires full detoxification first or it precipitates withdrawal.","Naloxone reverses opioid overdose. Its half-life is shorter than most opioids, so repeated doses or an infusion may be needed, and more is required for synthetic opioids such as nitazenes. Take-home naloxone should be offered widely.","Smoking cessation: varenicline, a nicotinic partial agonist, has the best efficacy; bupropion and nicotine replacement are alternatives. Stopping smoking raises clozapine and olanzapine levels, which must be anticipated.","Stimulant dependence: no licensed substitution therapy exists. Contingency management, which provides incentives for verified abstinence, has the best evidence.","Harm reduction is treatment: needle exchange, blood-borne virus screening and hepatitis B vaccination, take-home naloxone, and supervised consumption where available."],"cards":[["Name the drugs used in alcohol relapse prevention and their mechanisms.","Acamprosate, a glutamatergic modulator reducing craving. Naltrexone, an opioid antagonist reducing the reward of drinking. Disulfiram, an aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibitor producing an aversive reaction. Nalmefene, licensed for reduction of heavy drinking."],["What outcomes does opioid substitution treatment improve?","Mortality, retention in treatment, illicit opioid use, injecting risk behaviour, blood-borne virus transmission and criminal activity. Retention in treatment is the outcome most closely linked to reduced mortality."],["What treatment has the best evidence in stimulant dependence?","Contingency management, providing incentives for verified abstinence. There is no licensed substitution therapy for stimulant dependence."]],"note":["Naltrexone requires full opioid detoxification before starting or it precipitates withdrawal.","Varenicline has the best efficacy for smoking cessation; earlier neuropsychiatric concerns were not substantiated by the EAGLES trial.","Stopping smoking raises clozapine and olanzapine levels through loss of CYP1A2 induction.","Disulfiram requires supervision, motivation and clear warning about the reaction, including with alcohol in foods and toiletries.","Loss of tolerance after abstinence is the commonest setting for fatal opioid overdose; provide take-home naloxone."],"refs":["semaddict","nice","maudsley"]};
TEACH["4.4.7"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Rapid tranquillisation is the last item on the list and the first thing people reach for. Before any of it: check the glucose, the observations and whether this is delirium, withdrawal, pain or a head injury. An agitated person who is sedated is an agitated person whose diagnosis has just been hidden.","teach":["Exclude a medical cause first: hypoglycaemia, hypoxia, sepsis, head injury, intoxication or withdrawal, pain, urinary retention, constipation, and delirium from any cause. Check a capillary glucose and basic observations.","The sequence: de-escalation, then oral medication, then parenteral as a last resort. De-escalation means space, one person talking, reduced stimulation, an offer of something concrete such as food or a phone call, and an offer of oral medication.","Oral options: lorazepam, or promethazine, or an oral antipsychotic such as olanzapine, haloperidol or risperidone, depending on local policy and prior response.","Parenteral: lorazepam intramuscularly, or an intramuscular antipsychotic. Intramuscular olanzapine must not be given within one hour of parenteral benzodiazepine because of the risk of severe cardiorespiratory depression.","Haloperidol requires an ECG before or as soon as practicable after parenteral use, because of QT prolongation. Intravenous haloperidol carries the highest torsades risk and is avoided.","Monitoring after parenteral administration: pulse, blood pressure, respiratory rate, temperature, oxygen saturation and level of consciousness, at intervals set by local policy, and more frequently if the patient is asleep. Flumazenil must be available where a benzodiazepine has been given.","Restraint is a medical event with medical consequences: positional asphyxia, rhabdomyolysis and cardiac arrest. Prone restraint is particularly dangerous. Someone must be watching the airway throughout, and the David Bennett and Seni Lewis inquiries followed restraint deaths.","Legal basis: treatment without consent requires the Mental Health Act, or common law and the Mental Capacity Act in an emergency where capacity is absent. Record which applies.","Debrief afterwards, with the team and with the patient. This is part of the intervention. Ask about akathisia, which is regularly the cause of the agitation being treated."],"cards":[["What must be excluded before treating agitation as psychiatric?","Hypoglycaemia, hypoxia, sepsis, head injury, intoxication or withdrawal, pain, urinary retention, constipation and delirium from any cause. Check capillary glucose and basic observations."],["What monitoring is required after parenteral rapid tranquillisation?","Pulse, blood pressure, respiratory rate, temperature, oxygen saturation and level of consciousness at intervals defined by local policy, more frequently if the patient is asleep or has received intramuscular medication, with flumazenil available where a benzodiazepine has been given."],["What is the interaction warning for intramuscular olanzapine?","It must not be given within one hour of parenteral benzodiazepine because of the risk of severe cardiorespiratory depression, hypotension and death."]],"note":["Akathisia is regularly mistaken for agitation and treated with a dose increase, which worsens it. Ask about inner restlessness.","Prone restraint carries a risk of positional asphyxia; the David Bennett and Olaseni Lewis inquiries followed restraint deaths.","The Mental Health Units (Use of Force) Act 2018 requires recording and annual publication of every use of force with patient characteristics.","NICE NG10 covers violence and aggression and short-term management.","Local rapid tranquillisation policy must be known before being on call, not looked up during an incident."],"refs":["nice","maudsley","semgen"]};
TEACH["4.4.8"] = {"free":false,"simple":"When a drug does something unexpected and harmful, somebody has to write it down somewhere that other people will see. That system is the Yellow Card, it depends entirely on clinicians bothering, and most of us do not.","teach":["The Yellow Card scheme, run by the MHRA, is the UK system for reporting suspected adverse drug reactions. Reports can be made by health professionals, patients and carers.","What to report: all suspected reactions for black triangle medicines, which are under additional monitoring; all serious suspected reactions for established medicines; and all suspected reactions in children, in the elderly and in pregnancy."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["bnf","maudsley","nice"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.4.9"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Nobody thinks to ask, and then somebody has a crash. Sedating medication, the illness itself and alcohol all impair driving, and there are legal duties on both the patient and on you that most doctors could not state if asked.","teach":["The legal position in the UK: the driver has the duty to inform the DVLA of a relevant condition. The doctor's duty is to advise the patient of that obligation, to record the advice, and to advise them not to drive where appropriate.","If a patient continues to drive when unfit, and cannot or will not inform the DVLA, GMC guidance permits disclosure to the DVLA medical adviser in the public interest, after making every reasonable effort to persuade the patient and telling them of your intention to disclose.","DVLA standards vary by condition and are set out in the Assessing Fitness to Drive guidance, which is updated regularly and should be consulted rather than remembered.","Broad principles: acute psychosis, mania and severe depression with impaired concentration or psychotic features require cessation of driving until stable and well; the person must be stable for a specified period and comply with treatment before relicensing.","Dementia: mild cognitive impairment does not automatically preclude driving, and the assessment concerns function and safety rather than diagnosis alone. On-road assessment is often needed.","Alcohol and drug dependence: persistent misuse or dependence requires licence revocation, with a defined period of controlled use or abstinence before relicensing, and different periods for cars and for vocational licences.","Medication: sedating drugs impair driving, particularly in the first days and after dose increase. It is an offence to drive while impaired by prescribed medication, and there are specified blood limits for certain drugs including benzodiazepines and methadone, with a statutory medical defence where they are taken as prescribed.","Advise patients not to drive when starting or increasing a sedating drug, and to avoid alcohol.","Other complex behaviours: operating machinery, working at height, caring responsibilities, and shift work all carry the same considerations and are almost never discussed. Document what you advised."],"cards":[["Whose duty is it to inform the DVLA, and what is the doctor's role?","The driver has the legal duty to inform the DVLA. The doctor's duty is to advise the patient of that obligation, advise them not to drive where appropriate, and record the advice given."],["When may a doctor disclose to the DVLA without consent?","Where the patient continues to drive when unfit and cannot or will not inform the DVLA, disclosure to the DVLA medical adviser is permitted in the public interest, after every reasonable effort to persuade the patient and after informing them of the intention to disclose."],["What is the legal position on driving while taking prescribed sedating medication?","It is an offence to drive while impaired by any drug, including prescribed medication. Specified blood limits exist for certain drugs including benzodiazepines and methadone, with a statutory medical defence where the drug is taken as prescribed and the driver is not impaired."]],"note":["DVLA Assessing Fitness to Drive is updated regularly and should be consulted rather than recalled from memory.","Vocational licence standards are considerably stricter than for cars and motorcycles.","Advise against driving when starting or increasing any sedating medication.","Record the advice given; failure to advise is the commonest medicolegal criticism in this area.","The same considerations apply to machinery, working at height and caring responsibilities, and are rarely discussed."],"refs":["bnf","maudsley","noptp"]};
TEACH["4.4.10.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Most dangerous drug interactions in psychiatry are one drug jamming the enzyme that clears another. Levels rise within a day and the patient becomes toxic while everybody is still congratulating themselves on treating the chest infection.","teach":["Kinetic interactions occur at absorption, distribution, metabolism or excretion. Metabolism is where most clinically important ones happen.","Enzyme inhibition is rapid, within hours to a day or two, and raises substrate levels. Enzyme induction takes one to three weeks to develop and a similar time to reverse, and lowers them.","The inhibitors: fluoxetine and paroxetine at CYP2D6; fluvoxamine at CYP1A2 and CYP2C19; and among non-psychotropics, macrolides, azole antifungals, ritonavir and grapefruit juice at CYP3A4.","The inducers: carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, rifampicin and St John's wort. Smoking induces CYP1A2 through tar, not nicotine.","The clozapine examples worth memorising. Fluvoxamine can raise clozapine levels several-fold. Ciprofloxacin and erythromycin raise them. Stopping smoking raises them, sometimes to toxicity within days. Carbamazepine lowers them and must not be combined because of additive bone marrow suppression.","Lithium is not metabolised, so its interactions are renal. Thiazides, NSAIDs and ACE inhibitors reduce renal clearance and raise lithium levels. Sodium depletion from dehydration, vomiting, diarrhoea or a low-salt diet does the same. This is the commonest route to lithium toxicity.","Lamotrigine is glucuronidated, not a CYP substrate. Valproate inhibits its glucuronidation, so the dose is halved; carbamazepine induces it, so the dose is roughly doubled.","Absorption interactions: antacids, iron and calcium reduce absorption of some drugs; anticholinergics slow gastric emptying.","The habit: before adding any drug to a patient on clozapine, lithium, an anticonvulsant or a tricyclic, check the interaction. Before stopping one, check it again, because stopping an inhibitor lowers levels and stopping an inducer raises them."],"cards":[["What raises clozapine levels, and by which mechanisms?","Fluvoxamine and ciprofloxacin through CYP1A2 inhibition, erythromycin through CYP3A4, and smoking cessation through loss of CYP1A2 induction by tobacco tar. Caffeine also contributes."],["Which drugs raise lithium levels and why?","Thiazide diuretics, NSAIDs and ACE inhibitors, all of which reduce renal lithium clearance. Sodium depletion from dehydration, vomiting, diarrhoea or a low-salt diet has the same effect, because lithium is handled like sodium."],["How must lamotrigine dosing change with valproate and with carbamazepine?","Valproate inhibits lamotrigine glucuronidation, so the lamotrigine dose is approximately halved. Carbamazepine induces it, so the dose is approximately doubled."]],"note":["Stopping an enzyme inhibitor lowers substrate levels; stopping an inducer raises them. Both directions matter.","Carbamazepine should not be combined with clozapine because of additive bone marrow suppression.","St John's wort is a potent CYP3A4 inducer and reduces the efficacy of oral contraceptives, anticoagulants, ciclosporin and antiretrovirals.","Smoke-free ward policies have precipitated clozapine toxicity; anticipate and monitor levels.","Lithium toxicity most commonly follows dehydration or the addition of a diuretic, NSAID or ACE inhibitor."],"refs":["maudsley","bnf","stahl"]};
TEACH["4.4.10.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The other kind of interaction needs no enzyme at all. Two drugs that both raise serotonin, or both prolong the QT, or both sedate, simply add up. Nothing changes in the liver; the effects meet in the patient.","teach":["Pharmacodynamic interactions occur when drugs act on the same system, additively, synergistically or antagonistically, without any change in plasma concentration.","Serotonergic: combining SSRIs or SNRIs with MAOIs, tramadol, linezolid, triptans, St John's wort, lithium or other serotonergic agents risks serotonin syndrome. The MAOI combination is the most dangerous and requires the washouts described earlier."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","bnf","maudphys"],"n":{"teach":10,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["4.4.11"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Before you diagnose depression in a medical patient, look at the drug chart. Steroids, interferon, some heart drugs and several antibiotics all produce psychiatric symptoms, and stopping the drug is a better treatment than adding another one.","teach":["Corticosteroids: euphoria, insomnia and irritability commonly; mania or psychosis at higher doses; depression with chronic use. Risk rises markedly above about 40 mg prednisolone daily. Steroid psychosis usually resolves on dose reduction.","Interferon-alpha: causes depression in a substantial minority and is the strongest human causal evidence linking inflammation to depression."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudphys","bnf","semliaison"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["5.1.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"In the 1960s the same patients were called schizophrenic far more often in New York than in London. Not different patients. The same ones, on film, watched by both sets of psychiatrists. That study is why we have operational criteria, and why classification is not a filing exercise but the thing that makes psychiatry a science at all.","teach":["Purpose of classification: communication between clinicians, prediction of course and treatment response, and research that can be replicated. It is a tool, not a description of nature.","Kraepelin, 1899: split the psychoses by longitudinal course rather than cross-sectional symptoms, distinguishing dementia praecox from manic-depressive insanity. Every classification since argues with him and none escapes him.","Bleuler, 1908 and 1911: renamed dementia praecox schizophrenia because it neither always dements nor always begins early. His four As are associations, affect, ambivalence and autism.","Schneider's first-rank symptoms: thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting; third-person auditory hallucinations; running commentary; delusional perception; passivity of affect, impulse and volition; and somatic passivity. Historically pathognomonic, now known to occur in mania and organic states, and removed from the definition in both ICD-11 and DSM-5.","The US-UK Diagnostic Project showed enormous transatlantic disagreement, and drove the operational criteria movement.","DSM-III, 1980: operational criteria, a multiaxial system, and the admission of PTSD. It transformed reliability at the cost of an unresolved argument about validity.","Reliability is agreement between observers; validity is whether the category corresponds to something real. A diagnosis can be perfectly reliable and still not carve nature at a joint.","Categorical classification assigns to a discrete class; dimensional places on a continuum. ICD-11 personality disorder is dimensional; ICD-10 was categorical. Most psychiatric phenomena are dimensional and are forced into categories for practical reasons.","Comorbidity is the standing embarrassment: if categories were valid, they would not co-occur as often as they do."],"cards":[["What did Kraepelin contribute, and on what basis?","He distinguished dementia praecox from manic-depressive insanity in 1899 on the basis of longitudinal course and outcome rather than cross-sectional symptoms, establishing course as a validating principle."],["Name Schneider's first-rank symptoms and state their current status.","Thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting; third-person auditory hallucinations; running commentary; delusional perception; passivity of affect, impulse and volition; and somatic passivity. They occur in mania and organic states, lack diagnostic specificity, and have been removed from the definition of schizophrenia in ICD-11 and DSM-5."],["Distinguish reliability from validity in diagnosis.","Reliability is the extent to which different observers reach the same diagnosis. Validity is whether the diagnostic category corresponds to a genuine entity, predicting course, treatment response and aetiology. A category can be highly reliable and still lack validity."]],"note":["The US-UK Diagnostic Project demonstrated that the same patients were diagnosed with schizophrenia far more often in New York than in London.","Robins and Guze proposed five validators: clinical description, laboratory study, delimitation from other disorders, follow-up study and family study.","DSM-III, 1980, introduced operational criteria and is the single most consequential event in modern psychiatric classification.","Cohen's kappa measures agreement beyond chance; above 0.8 is very good and 0.6 to 0.8 good.","Research Domain Criteria, RDoC, is a research framework of dimensional constructs across diagnoses, not a clinical classification."],"refs":["tyrer11","icd11","dsm5"]};
TEACH["5.1.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There is a difference between what is wrong with an organ, what a person cannot do because of it, and what their life is like as a result. Confusing the three is why services measure the wrong thing and why two people with identical illnesses can have entirely different lives.","teach":["The older WHO framework, ICIDH 1980: impairment, a loss or abnormality of structure or function; disability, restriction in performing an activity; handicap, the social disadvantage resulting from either.","The criticism: it was linear and located the problem entirely in the person, treating disadvantage as an inevitable consequence of impairment rather than of how society is arranged."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["icd11","morgan","oxid"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["5.2.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The history is the investigation. There is no blood test and no scan that makes a psychiatric diagnosis, so the interview is the instrument, and how carefully you use it decides what you find. Take it in a fixed order so that nothing is lost in a difficult conversation.","teach":["Structure of the history: presenting complaint and its history; past psychiatric history; past medical history; drug history including over-the-counter, illicit and herbal; family history; personal and developmental history; social circumstances; forensic history; and premorbid personality.","Premorbid personality is what the person was like when well, obtained from them and from an informant, and it is the baseline against which everything else is judged. It is routinely omitted and its absence makes change impossible to demonstrate.","Collateral history is not optional where insight is impaired, in cognitive presentations, in mania, and in children. Record what you could not obtain and why.","The mental state examination is what you observe now, not what the patient reports about the past. That distinction is the commonest error in written examinations.","MSE headings: appearance and behaviour; speech; mood and affect; thought, comprising form, content and possession; perception; cognition; and insight. Risk is recorded alongside.","Mood is the sustained subjectively reported emotional state; affect is the observed moment-to-moment emotional expression, described by range, reactivity, congruity and stability.","Thought form is how thinking is organised; content is what is thought; possession is whether the person experiences their thoughts as their own.","Insight is not present or absent. It has three components: awareness that something is wrong, attribution of that to illness, and willingness to accept treatment. They dissociate, and describing which components are present is more useful than a single word.","Formulation, not just diagnosis: predisposing, precipitating, perpetuating and protective factors, across biological, psychological and social domains. The diagnosis names the disorder; the formulation explains why this person has this problem now."],"cards":[["Name the headings of the mental state examination.","Appearance and behaviour; speech; mood and affect; thought, comprising form, content and possession; perception; cognition; and insight, with risk recorded alongside."],["Distinguish mood from affect.","Mood is the sustained, subjectively reported emotional state. Affect is the observed, moment-to-moment emotional expression, described in terms of range, reactivity, congruity and stability."],["What are the three components of insight?","Awareness that something is wrong; attribution of that abnormality to mental illness; and acceptance of the need for treatment. They can be present or absent independently."]],"note":["The mental state examination describes what is observed at interview; historical symptoms belong in the history.","Premorbid personality provides the baseline and is routinely omitted.","Formulation uses the four Ps across biological, psychological and social domains.","Record what collateral information could not be obtained and why.","Document what was asked and the answer given, not only your conclusion; the note is read later by people who were not there."],"refs":["oyebode","oxhandbk","fish"]};
TEACH["5.2.2.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The most frightening symptom in psychiatry is not hearing voices. It is the loss of the boundary between you and everything else: the sense that your thoughts are being put in, taken out, or broadcast so that strangers know them. That is a disorder of the self, and it is the core of schizophrenia.","teach":["The self has several aspects that can be disturbed independently: awareness of activity, unity, continuity over time, and the boundary between self and not-self. Jaspers set these out.","Thought alienation: thought insertion, the experience that thoughts are placed in one's mind by an external agency; thought withdrawal, that they are removed; thought broadcasting, that they are accessible to others.","Passivity phenomena, also called made experiences: made actions, made feelings, made impulses, and somatic passivity, in which bodily sensations are experienced as imposed from outside.","The critical feature is the loss of the sense of agency and ownership, not the content. The patient does not think their thoughts are being read; they experience the thoughts as not theirs.","These are Schneiderian first-rank symptoms. They are not pathognomonic, occurring in mania and organic states, and have been removed from the diagnostic definition in ICD-11 and DSM-5, but remain highly characteristic.","Depersonalisation: a distressing sense of unreality or detachment from oneself, as though observing oneself from outside, with insight retained. Derealisation applies the same quality to the surroundings.","Depersonalisation occurs in health under fatigue and stress, in anxiety and panic, in temporal lobe epilepsy, in dissociative disorders and with drugs, particularly cannabis and ketamine.","Autoscopy is seeing one's own body from outside. A Doppelganger experience is the sense of a double.","Dissociative identity disorder involves disruption of identity with distinct personality states. It is genuinely contested, culturally patterned, and should be described carefully and without either dismissal or enthusiasm."],"cards":[["Define thought insertion and state what makes it a disorder of the self.","The experience that thoughts in one's own mind are not one's own but have been placed there by an external agency. It is a disorder of the self because what is lost is the sense of ownership and agency for the thought, not merely the belief about its origin."],["Define depersonalisation and derealisation.","Depersonalisation is a distressing sense of unreality, detachment or being an observer of oneself, with insight retained. Derealisation is the same quality of unreality applied to the surroundings, which may seem flat, artificial or lifeless."],["Name the passivity phenomena.","Made actions, made feelings, made impulses, and somatic passivity, in which movements, emotions, impulses or bodily sensations are experienced as imposed by an external agency."]],"note":["Depersonalisation occurs in health, in anxiety and panic, in temporal lobe epilepsy, in dissociative disorders and with cannabis and ketamine.","Insight is characteristically preserved in depersonalisation, which distinguishes it from psychotic experience.","The first-rank symptoms are highly characteristic but not pathognomonic and are no longer part of the diagnostic definition.","Sims' Symptoms in the Mind and Fish's Clinical Psychopathology are the College-listed texts for this material.","Describe the phenomenon in the patient's own words before naming it; the term should follow the description, not replace it."],"refs":["oyebode","fish","icd11"]};
TEACH["5.2.2.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Mood is the weather and affect is what you can see out of the window at this moment. A patient can report feeling nothing while looking distressed, or describe devastation while smiling. Those mismatches are the finding, not a failure of the interview.","teach":["Mood is the sustained, pervasive, subjectively experienced emotional state. Affect is the observed, moment-to-moment expression of emotion.","Describe affect by four qualities: range, from full to restricted or blunted to flat; reactivity, whether it changes appropriately with content; congruity, whether it matches what is being said; and stability, whether it is labile.","Blunted affect is a reduction in the intensity of emotional expression. Flat affect is its virtual absence. Both are negative symptoms of schizophrenia and are distinguished from depressive anhedonia by the absence of subjective misery.","Incongruity of affect is emotional expression that does not match the content, such as laughing while describing a bereavement. It is characteristic of schizophrenia and must be distinguished from embarrassment and from cultural difference.","Lability of affect is rapid fluctuation. Emotional incontinence, or pathological laughing and crying, is uncontrollable emotional expression disproportionate to any felt emotion, seen in pseudobulbar palsy, stroke, multiple sclerosis and motor neurone disease.","Anhedonia is loss of the capacity for pleasure, distinguished into anticipatory and consummatory forms. Apathy is loss of motivation without subjective distress. Alexithymia is difficulty identifying and describing one's own feelings.","Belle indifference is an apparent lack of concern about a serious symptom, historically associated with conversion disorder. It is neither sensitive nor specific and should not be used as diagnostic evidence.","Anxiety, in descriptive terms, is fear without an identified object; phobic anxiety is fear attached to a specific object or situation and is out of proportion, cannot be reasoned away, and leads to avoidance.","Elation must be distinguished from ordinary happiness by its persistence, its independence from circumstances, its infectiousness or irritability, and the accompanying changes in energy, sleep and judgement."],"cards":[["By which four qualities should affect be described?","Range, from full through restricted and blunted to flat; reactivity, whether it changes appropriately with content; congruity, whether it matches what is being described; and stability, whether it is labile."],["Distinguish blunted affect from depressive anhedonia.","Blunted affect is a reduction in the outward intensity of emotional expression, a negative symptom, typically without subjective distress. Anhedonia is a subjectively experienced loss of the capacity for pleasure and is accompanied by distress."],["What is emotional incontinence and where is it seen?","Uncontrollable laughing or crying disproportionate to, or in the absence of, corresponding felt emotion. It occurs in pseudobulbar palsy, stroke, multiple sclerosis and motor neurone disease, and may respond to low-dose SSRIs."]],"note":["Belle indifference is neither sensitive nor specific and should not be used as diagnostic evidence for conversion disorder.","Incongruity of affect must be distinguished from embarrassment, from cultural difference and from the effect of medication.","Apathy is a distinct construct from depression and from anhedonia, and is prominent in frontal syndromes and dementia.","Alexithymia is associated with somatisation and with adverse childhood experience.","Pathological laughing and crying may respond to low-dose SSRIs."],"refs":["oyebode","fish","lishman"]};
TEACH["5.2.2.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"A delusion is not simply a false belief. Plenty of people hold false beliefs perfectly normally, and some delusions turn out to be true. What makes it a delusion is how it is held: with absolute conviction, on inadequate grounds, unshakeable by evidence, and arrived at in a way nobody else can follow.","teach":["Jaspers' criteria for a delusion: held with extraordinary conviction and incorrigible certainty; impervious to counter-argument or experience; and with content that is impossible or false, out of keeping with the person's cultural and educational background.","The husband whose wife really is unfaithful may still have a delusion of infidelity, if the belief is held on delusional grounds. Truth of content does not exclude it.","Primary delusions arise de novo and are un-understandable: delusional mood, a sense that something is going on; delusional perception, a normal perception given delusional meaning in two stages; and sudden delusional idea, or autochthonous delusion.","Secondary delusions arise understandably from another experience, most often mood. Guilt, poverty and nihilistic delusions in depression; grandiose delusions in mania.","Named delusional syndromes: Capgras, that a familiar person has been replaced by an impostor; Fregoli, that a persecutor is disguising themselves as different people; Cotard, nihilistic delusions of being dead or of the organs rotting; de Clerambault, erotomania; Othello, morbid jealousy; and Ekbom, delusional infestation.","Overvalued idea: a comprehensible, acceptable belief pursued beyond reason, which comes to dominate life, but is not held with delusional conviction and is not resisted. Seen in anorexia nervosa, morbid jealousy, hypochondriasis and querulous behaviour.","Obsession: an intrusive recurrent thought, image or impulse, recognised as one's own, resisted, and regarded as senseless. The three distinguishing features are ownership, resistance and insight.","Formal thought disorder is disorder of the form rather than the content: derailment or knight's move thinking; tangentiality; circumstantiality; neologism; word salad or verbigeration; thought block; and perseveration.","Flight of ideas belongs to mania and has understandable connections, including clang associations, punning and distraction. Loosening of associations belongs to schizophrenia and does not.","Poverty of speech is reduced quantity; poverty of content is normal quantity conveying little. Both are negative symptoms."],"cards":[["Give Jaspers' three criteria for a delusion.","Held with extraordinary conviction and incorrigible certainty; impervious to counter-argument and contrary experience; and with impossible or false content that is out of keeping with the person's cultural and educational background."],["Distinguish an overvalued idea, an obsession and a delusion.","An overvalued idea is comprehensible and acceptable but pursued beyond reason, dominating life, without delusional conviction and without resistance. An obsession is intrusive, recognised as one's own, resisted, and regarded as senseless. A delusion is held with unshakeable conviction on inadequate grounds and is not resisted."],["How does flight of ideas differ from loosening of associations?","Flight of ideas retains understandable connections between thoughts, including clang associations, punning and distraction by external stimuli, and occurs in mania. Loosening of associations lacks understandable connection and occurs in schizophrenia."]],"note":["Delusional perception is a two-stage phenomenon: a normal perception followed by an un-understandable delusional interpretation. It is a first-rank symptom.","Truth of the content does not exclude a delusion; what matters is the grounds and the manner in which the belief is held.","Capgras and Fregoli are delusional misidentification syndromes and occur in organic as well as functional disorders.","Cotard syndrome occurs in severe depression, particularly in older people, and responds to ECT.","Culture must be considered before calling a belief delusional; religious and culturally sanctioned beliefs are not delusions."],"refs":["oyebode","fish","icd11"]};
TEACH["5.2.2.4"] = {"free":true,"simple":"A hallucination is a perception with nothing there. Not a vivid image, not a strong impression, but something the person hears or sees in the world outside them, with the full force of a real perception, and which they cannot switch off. Getting that definition exactly right is worth several marks every sitting.","teach":["Hallucination: a percept arising without an external stimulus, experienced in external objective space, with the full force and clarity of a true perception, and not subject to voluntary control.","Pseudohallucination: experienced in inner subjective space, recognised as not real, and lacking the substantiality of a true perception. The distinction is about the space in which it occurs and the insight retained, not the vividness.","Illusion: a misperception of a real external stimulus. Completion, affect and pareidolia are the three types.","Auditory hallucinations: elementary sounds, or verbal. Second-person voices address the patient and occur in mood disorder and schizophrenia. Third-person voices discuss the patient, and running commentary describes their actions; both are first-rank symptoms.","Visual hallucinations should prompt a search for an organic cause: delirium, Lewy body disease, occipital pathology, epilepsy, or substance intoxication or withdrawal. They are less typical of schizophrenia.","Olfactory and gustatory hallucinations suggest temporal lobe epilepsy. Tactile hallucinations, formication, occur in cocaine and alcohol withdrawal and in delusional infestation.","Special forms: functional hallucination, where a real stimulus in the same modality triggers the hallucination and both are perceived together; reflex hallucination, where a stimulus in one modality triggers a hallucination in another; extracampine hallucination, occurring outside the sensory field, such as a voice from another city.","Hypnagogic hallucinations occur while falling asleep and hypnopompic on waking. Both occur in health and are not pathological.","Charles Bonnet syndrome: complex visual hallucinations in a person with visual impairment, with insight retained and no psychiatric illness. Musical hallucinosis is the auditory equivalent with deafness.","Autoscopy is seeing oneself. Peduncular hallucinosis follows midbrain or thalamic lesions and produces vivid visual hallucinations often with insight."],"cards":[["Give the full definition of a hallucination.","A percept arising in the absence of any external stimulus, experienced in external objective space, with the full force and clarity of a true perception, and not subject to voluntary control."],["Distinguish a functional from a reflex hallucination.","In a functional hallucination a real stimulus triggers a hallucination in the same modality and both are perceived simultaneously, such as a voice heard whenever a tap runs. In a reflex hallucination a stimulus in one modality triggers a hallucination in another."],["What is Charles Bonnet syndrome?","Complex, well-formed visual hallucinations occurring in a person with visual impairment, with insight preserved and in the absence of psychiatric illness or cognitive impairment."]],"note":["The pseudohallucination distinction rests on inner subjective space and retained insight, not on vividness.","Visual hallucinations should raise the question of an organic cause before a psychiatric one.","Hypnagogic and hypnopompic hallucinations occur in health and are part of the narcolepsy tetrad.","Extracampine hallucinations occur outside the sensory field and are not in themselves diagnostic of psychosis.","Describe the experience fully in the patient's own words before applying the term."],"refs":["oyebode","fish","lishman"]};
TEACH["5.2.2.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Some of the most striking things in psychiatry are not said, they are done. A patient who lets you place their arm in the air and leaves it there, or who repeats every word you say, is showing you a catatonic sign, and catatonia is treatable and dangerous if missed.","teach":["Catatonia is a syndrome of psychomotor disturbance occurring in mood disorders, schizophrenia, and organic and medical conditions. It is commoner in mood disorder than in schizophrenia, which is not what most people expect.","Signs: stupor, mutism, negativism, waxy flexibility (catalepsy), posturing, ambitendency, automatic obedience, echolalia and echopraxia, mannerisms, stereotypies, and mitgehen."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oyebode","fish","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["5.2.2.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Cognition is tested, not asked about. A patient who says their memory is terrible usually has depression. A patient who says their memory is fine while their family looks stricken usually has dementia. The complaint points one way and the truth often points the other.","teach":["Domains: orientation, attention and concentration, memory (immediate, recent and remote; verbal and visual), language, praxis, visuospatial function, and executive function.","Consciousness and attention come first, because impairment of these invalidates every subsequent test. A patient who cannot attend cannot be tested for memory."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oyebode","lishman","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":10,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["5.2.2.7"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A handful of rare syndromes carry names, and they get asked about constantly because they are memorable. Each one is a single striking idea: the impostor, the pursuer in disguise, the person who believes they are dead, the person convinced somebody famous loves them.","teach":["Capgras syndrome: the delusion that a familiar person has been replaced by an identical impostor. Delusional misidentification, occurring in schizophrenia and in organic disorder, particularly right hemisphere and frontal pathology.","Fregoli syndrome: the delusion that a persecutor is taking on the appearance of different people, so strangers are the same person in disguise. The reverse of Capgras."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oyebode","fish","puriforen"],"n":{"teach":10,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["5.2.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Descriptive psychopathology asks what the symptom is. Dynamic psychopathology asks what it is for. Both are legitimate questions, and confusing them, or dismissing the second, leaves you unable to understand why a patient does something that makes no sense on the surface.","teach":["Dynamic psychopathology explains symptoms in terms of unconscious conflict, defence and meaning, in contrast to descriptive psychopathology, which observes and classifies without inference.","Freud's structural model: id, driven by instinct and the pleasure principle; ego, mediating with reality; superego, conscience and ideals. The topographical model divides mind into conscious, preconscious and unconscious."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gabbard","oyebode","noptp"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["5.2.4"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Every psychiatric presentation deserves the question, could this be physical? Not because it usually is, but because when it is and you missed it, the consequences are severe, and because the people most likely to be missed are the ones already carrying a psychiatric diagnosis.","teach":["Baseline investigations in a new presentation: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, calcium, glucose or HbA1c, B12 and folate. ESR or CRP where inflammation is suspected. Urine drug screen where relevant.","Additional by indication: ECG before QT-prolonging drugs and in eating disorders; lipids and glucose before and during antipsychotics; syphilis and HIV serology where indicated; autoantibodies and CSF where autoimmune encephalitis is suspected; imaging where there are red flags.","Red flags for organic cause: first presentation after 40 or before puberty; focal neurological signs; seizures; rapid or fluctuating course; visual hallucinations; new headache or visual change; personality change out of keeping; cognitive impairment out of proportion to mood; and atypical or treatment-resistant presentation.","Delirium is the commonest organic presentation and is defined by impaired attention with acute onset and fluctuating course. Screen with the 4AT or Confusion Assessment Method.","Specific conditions that present psychiatrically: hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism; Cushing's and Addison's disease; hypercalcaemia; B12 deficiency; neurosyphilis; HIV; systemic lupus erythematosus; Wilson's disease; acute intermittent porphyria; temporal lobe epilepsy; and autoimmune encephalitis.","Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis is the one that must not be missed: a young patient, often female, with rapid-onset psychosis, then seizures, orofacial dyskinesias, autonomic instability and reduced consciousness, sometimes with an ovarian teratoma. It is treatable.","Physical examination is not optional. Weight, blood pressure, pulse, temperature, and a neurological examination where indicated. Physical examination in psychiatric admission is frequently omitted and this is a recognised patient safety failure.","Diagnostic overshadowing is the mechanism by which physical illness is missed in psychiatric patients: symptoms are attributed to the psychiatric diagnosis and not investigated.","People with severe mental illness die 15 to 20 years early, predominantly from physical disease. Investigation is not defensive medicine; it is the main modifiable contributor to that gap."],"cards":[["Name six red flags suggesting an organic cause for a psychiatric presentation.","First presentation after 40 or before puberty; focal neurological signs; seizures; rapid or fluctuating course; visual hallucinations; new headache or visual disturbance; personality change out of keeping; and cognitive impairment out of proportion to mood."],["Which treatable encephalitis must not be missed in first-episode psychosis?","Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis, typically in a young patient, often female, with rapid-onset psychosis followed by seizures, orofacial dyskinesias, autonomic instability and reduced consciousness, sometimes associated with an ovarian teratoma."],["What baseline investigations are appropriate in a new psychiatric presentation?","FBC, U&Es, LFTs, thyroid function, calcium, glucose or HbA1c, B12 and folate, with urine drug screen, ECG, inflammatory markers, syphilis and HIV serology, and imaging added according to indication."]],"note":["Diagnostic overshadowing is the commonest mechanism by which physical illness is missed in psychiatric patients.","Physical examination on psychiatric admission is frequently omitted and is a recognised safety failure.","Delirium is defined by impaired attention with acute onset and fluctuating course; screen with the 4AT.","The 15 to 20 year life expectancy gap in severe mental illness is predominantly physical disease.","Wilson's disease, acute intermittent porphyria and neurosyphilis are the classic treatable rarities to keep in mind."],"refs":["maudphys","lishman","semliaison"]};
TEACH["5.2.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There is no scan that diagnoses a psychiatric illness. Imaging in psychiatry has one job: to find the thing that is not psychiatric. Ordering it without that question in mind produces incidental findings, anxiety and no answers.","teach":["The clinical purpose of imaging in psychiatry is exclusion of structural, vascular, inflammatory, infective or neoplastic pathology, not confirmation of a psychiatric diagnosis.","CT: fast, widely available, superior for acute haemorrhage and bone, uses ionising radiation. Use in the acute setting and where MRI is contraindicated."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lishman","oxneuropsy","nice"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["5.2.6"] = {"free":true,"simple":"A psychiatrist is a doctor first. The patient with severe mental illness who dies fifteen years early does not die of psychiatry, they die of heart disease and lung disease, in a system that saw them constantly and examined them rarely.","teach":["The scale of the problem: people with severe mental illness die 15 to 20 years earlier than the general population, predominantly from cardiovascular and respiratory disease and cancer, not from suicide.","Contributors: smoking at two to three times the general population rate; obesity and metabolic effects of medication; physical inactivity; poor diet; and unequal access to and quality of physical health care.","Diagnostic overshadowing is the mechanism: symptoms are attributed to the psychiatric diagnosis and not investigated. It is the single most important concept in this item.","Core competencies expected: physical examination including cardiovascular, respiratory, abdominal and neurological systems; interpretation of basic investigations including ECG; recognition and initial management of medical emergencies; and knowing when to refer.","Conditions a psychiatrist must be able to recognise and initiate management for: delirium, sepsis, hypoglycaemia, acute coronary syndrome, stroke, seizures and status epilepticus, alcohol withdrawal and Wernicke's encephalopathy, opioid overdose, and refeeding syndrome.","Drug-specific emergencies: lithium toxicity, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, serotonin syndrome, clozapine-induced agranulocytosis, myocarditis and ileus, and hyponatraemia.","ECG interpretation is a core skill: rate, rhythm, axis, intervals with particular attention to QTc, and ischaemic change. A QTc above 500 ms or an increase of more than 60 ms from baseline requires action.","Physical health monitoring: the schedules for antipsychotics, lithium, clozapine and valproate covered in item 4.4.3. These are not administrative tasks; they are the intervention that closes the mortality gap.","The professional point: physical health care of people with severe mental illness is a psychiatric responsibility, not somebody else's. Where the psychiatrist does not take it on, in practice nobody does."],"cards":[["What is the life expectancy gap in severe mental illness and what causes it?","Fifteen to twenty years, driven predominantly by cardiovascular, respiratory and malignant disease rather than suicide, through smoking, obesity, medication effects, physical inactivity and unequal access to physical health care."],["What is diagnostic overshadowing?","Attributing physical symptoms to a person's psychiatric diagnosis or intellectual disability rather than investigating them as one would in any other patient, resulting in delayed or missed diagnosis of physical illness."],["Name five medical emergencies a psychiatrist must recognise and initiate management for.","Delirium, sepsis, hypoglycaemia, alcohol withdrawal with Wernicke's encephalopathy, opioid overdose, refeeding syndrome, status epilepticus, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, serotonin syndrome and lithium toxicity."]],"note":["Smoking rates in severe mental illness are two to three times those of the general population and account for a large share of the mortality gap.","QTc above 500 ms, or an increase of more than 60 ms from baseline, requires action.","Physical health checks in severe mental illness are a national quality indicator and audit performance is consistently poor.","The Maudsley Practice Guidelines for Physical Health Conditions in Psychiatry is the College-listed reference.","Where the psychiatrist does not take responsibility for physical health, in practice no one does."],"refs":["maudphys","semliaison","nice"]};
TEACH["5.2.7"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A scale exists to make change visible. It does not make a diagnosis, and a number without an interview is a number about nothing. But knowing which scale is filled in by the patient and which by the clinician, and roughly how many items each has, is straightforward marks.","teach":["Depression, observer-rated: Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression, HAM-D, 17 or 21 items, weighted towards somatic symptoms; Montgomery-Asberg Depression Rating Scale, MADRS, 10 items, less somatic and therefore better in physical illness.","Depression, self-rated: Beck Depression Inventory, 21 items; PHQ-9, 9 items, mapping onto DSM criteria and widely used in primary care; Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale, HADS, 14 items, deliberately excluding somatic items for use in the medically ill."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxhandbk","maudsley","lawrie"],"n":{"teach":10,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["5.2.8.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"You will be asked whether somebody is safe. Nobody can answer that, and the honest version of the job is different: you are deciding what happens next and reducing what can be reduced. A note that claims certainty is a note that will not survive contact with the inquiry.","teach":["Risk assessment is not prediction. Suicide is a rare event, and even an instrument with excellent sensitivity and specificity produces overwhelmingly more false positives than true positives when applied to a rare outcome. This is arithmetic, not pessimism.","NICE guidance is explicit: risk assessment tools and scales must not be used to predict future suicide or repetition of self-harm, and must not be used to determine who is offered treatment or who is discharged.","What risk assessment is for: structuring the clinical encounter, identifying modifiable factors, and generating a management plan. The plan is the output, not the score.","Static factors, which cannot be changed: previous self-harm or attempt, which is the strongest single predictor; male sex for completed suicide; older age; history of psychiatric illness; childhood adversity; and family history.","Dynamic factors, which can be changed and are therefore the clinically useful ones: current suicidal intent and planning, hopelessness, active symptoms, substance use, access to means, social isolation, recent loss, physical pain and illness, and lack of engagement with services.","High-risk periods: the first weeks after discharge from psychiatric inpatient care; after a change in treatment; following a life event, particularly loss, humiliation or entrapment; and in the early days of antidepressant treatment in young people.","Protective factors are real but must not be over-weighted: children at home, religious belief, future orientation, and a therapeutic relationship. They can disappear quickly.","Means restriction is one of the few interventions with population-level evidence. Ask specifically about medication stockpiles, ligature points and access to firearms, and act on the answer.","The formulation, not the category: high, medium and low risk labels have no predictive value and give false reassurance. Describe the risk, what drives it, what would increase or reduce it, and what the plan is."],"cards":[["Why do risk assessment tools fail to predict suicide at the individual level?","Suicide is a rare outcome, so even an instrument with high sensitivity and specificity generates overwhelmingly more false positives than true positives. Positive predictive value falls as prevalence falls, regardless of the quality of the instrument."],["What does NICE guidance say about risk assessment tools?","That they must not be used to predict future suicide or repetition of self-harm, and must not be used to determine who is offered treatment or who is discharged. They may be used to structure assessment, not to make the decision."],["Distinguish static from dynamic risk factors and say which matter clinically.","Static factors, such as previous attempts, sex, age and history, cannot be changed and identify a population at elevated risk. Dynamic factors, such as intent, hopelessness, active symptoms, substance use, access to means and social isolation, can be modified and are what the management plan acts upon."]],"note":["Previous self-harm is the strongest single predictor of subsequent suicide.","The weeks immediately after discharge from psychiatric inpatient care carry markedly elevated risk.","Asking about suicide does not increase risk; this is well established and worth telling frightened relatives.","Means restriction has population-level evidence, including analgesic pack size limits and barriers at known sites.","High, medium and low risk categories have no predictive validity and should not be used as though they did."],"refs":["nice","semgen","noptp"]};
TEACH["5.2.8.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"You are expected to know one structured tool properly. Not because the tool decides anything, but because using one forces you to consider the things you would otherwise skip when you are tired and the clinic is running late.","teach":["Three approaches: unstructured clinical judgement, which is flexible and unreliable; actuarial instruments, which apply fixed statistical predictors to produce a probability; and structured professional judgement, which uses defined items to guide but not replace clinical formulation.","Structured professional judgement is the approach recommended in UK practice. It combines the consistency of a defined item set with the flexibility of clinical reasoning, and it produces a formulation and management plan rather than a score."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["puriforen","nice","semgen"],"n":{"teach":9,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["6.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"A hospital ward is not one person deciding things. It is a group of people who each see something the others cannot, sitting in a room once a week and pooling it. The psychiatrist is not the cleverest person there. The psychiatrist is the one who has to sign the decision and carry it legally, which is a different job from being right.","teach":["The multidisciplinary team is not a courtesy. Different disciplines see different things: the occupational therapist watches a person cook, the nurse watches them at three in the morning, the social worker sees the housing letter. None of that reaches a ward round otherwise.","Core disciplines: psychiatry, mental health nursing, clinical psychology, occupational therapy, social work, pharmacy, and increasingly peer support workers with lived experience.","The specific role of the psychiatrist: diagnostic formulation, prescribing, assessment and management of physical health in mental illness, statutory duties under mental health legislation, and clinical leadership of the risk formulation.","The Care Programme Approach, introduced in England in 1991, required assessment of need, a written care plan, a named care coordinator and regular review. NHS England replaced it in 2021 with personalised care and support planning, which keeps the four functions and drops the bureaucracy.","Biopsychosocial is not a slogan, it is a checklist. Every formulation should answer: what is happening in the body, what is happening in the mind, what is happening around this person.","Physical treatments: medication, ECT, and the neuromodulation techniques. Psychological treatments: the therapies in section 9. Social treatments: housing, benefits, employment support, carer support, structure and occupation.","Individual Placement and Support is the strongest evidence-based social intervention in severe mental illness. Place the person in real competitive employment first, then support them, rather than training them indefinitely for a job they never get.","Coordination fails at the joins. The commonest failure in serious incident reports is not a wrong decision but an unclear one: nobody knew who was responsible for the next step.","Case management alone, without a clinical team, does not reduce admissions. Assertive community treatment with small caseloads and shared responsibility does, though its advantage narrows where community services are already good."],"cards":[["What are the four functions of the Care Programme Approach, and what replaced it?","Assessment of health and social care need, a written care plan, a named care coordinator, and regular review. NHS England replaced CPA in 2021 with personalised care and support planning, retaining the four functions."],["What is Individual Placement and Support, and why is it distinctive?","A supported employment model in severe mental illness that places the person into competitive paid employment first and provides ongoing support, rather than the traditional train-then-place approach. It has the strongest evidence base of any vocational intervention in psychiatry."],["Which elements of care are specific to the psychiatrist within a multidisciplinary team?","Diagnostic formulation, prescribing, the assessment and management of physical health in mental illness, statutory duties under mental health legislation, and clinical leadership of the risk formulation."]],"note":["Care Programme Approach, England, 1991; formally replaced by personalised care and support planning in 2021.","Assertive community treatment reduces admission and improves engagement; its advantage shrinks where standard community care is already well resourced.","Individual Placement and Support: place first, then train, with time-unlimited support and integration into the clinical team.","Serious incident reviews most often identify unclear responsibility at handover rather than an incorrect clinical decision."],"refs":["semliaison","companion","nice"]};
TEACH["6.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"You can stop an illness before it starts, stop it early once it has, or stop it from wrecking the rest of someone's life. Those are three different jobs and psychiatry is much better at the third than the first.","teach":["Primary prevention stops the disorder arising. Secondary prevention detects it early and shortens it. Tertiary prevention limits the disability that follows.","The other axis, from Gordon: universal interventions offered to everyone, selective interventions offered to a group at raised risk, and indicated interventions offered to people already showing early signs.","Real primary prevention in psychiatry: iodisation of salt, folate fortification, reduction of lead exposure, road traffic legislation reducing head injury, and alcohol pricing policy. Note how few of these are delivered by psychiatrists.","Minimum unit pricing for alcohol in Scotland, introduced 2018, was followed by a fall in alcohol-specific deaths, the clearest recent example of a population-level psychiatric prevention measure.","Suicide prevention by means restriction has the best evidence of any suicide intervention: barriers at jumping sites, catalytic converters, blister packaging of paracetamol, and the removal of ligature points.","The paracetamol pack-size legislation of 1998 was followed by a sustained fall in paracetamol overdose deaths and in liver transplant listings.","Secondary prevention: early intervention in psychosis services reduce duration of untreated psychosis, improve symptomatic and functional outcome, and reduce admission over the first two years.","Duration of untreated psychosis is one of the few modifiable predictors of outcome in schizophrenia, and the relationship holds after adjustment for premorbid function.","Tertiary prevention: relapse prevention with maintenance medication, rehabilitation, supported employment, and physical health screening in severe mental illness, where the mortality gap is largely cardiovascular and largely preventable."],"cards":[["Distinguish primary, secondary and tertiary prevention, with a psychiatric example of each.","Primary prevention stops the disorder arising, for example alcohol minimum unit pricing. Secondary prevention detects it early and shortens it, for example early intervention in psychosis. Tertiary prevention limits the resulting disability, for example rehabilitation and supported employment."],["Describe Gordon's classification of preventive interventions.","Universal interventions offered to a whole population; selective interventions offered to a subgroup at raised risk; and indicated interventions offered to individuals already showing early signs of disorder."],["Which suicide prevention approach has the strongest evidence base?","Means restriction: barriers at jumping sites, catalytic converters, blister and pack-size limits on analgesics, and removal of ligature points. The UK paracetamol pack-size legislation of 1998 was followed by a sustained fall in paracetamol overdose deaths and liver transplant listings."]],"note":["Primary, secondary, tertiary prevention map onto before onset, early in course, and limiting disability.","Gordon's classification: universal, selective, indicated.","Means restriction has the strongest evidence base of any suicide prevention measure.","Scotland introduced minimum unit pricing for alcohol in 2018.","The UK paracetamol pack-size legislation dates from 1998.","Duration of untreated psychosis is a modifiable predictor of outcome in first-episode psychosis."],"refs":["nice","companion","semgen"]};
TEACH["6.3.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"ECT is a deliberately induced seizure under anaesthetic. It is the fastest treatment psychiatry has for a person who has stopped eating and drinking and is about to die of depression. It also has a reputation built on how it was done in the 1950s, without anaesthetic, which is not how it is done now.","teach":["Mechanism: unknown in detail. The candidates are enhanced neuroplasticity and neurotrophic signalling, hippocampal neurogenesis, and modulation of GABAergic and monoaminergic transmission. Anyone who claims certainty here is overstating.","The seizure is the therapeutic element. Subconvulsive stimulation is not effective, which is one of the clearest facts in the field.","NICE indications: severe depression that is life-threatening and a rapid response is required; treatment-resistant severe depression; catatonia; and a prolonged or severe manic episode.","ECT is a first-line, not last-line, treatment where the illness is immediately life-threatening through refusal of food and fluid, or where the person has responded to it before.","Response rate in severe depression is of the order of 70 to 80 per cent, higher than any pharmacological alternative, and highest in psychotic depression.","The relapse rate after successful ECT without continuation treatment is very high, above half within six months, so continuation medication or continuation ECT is essential rather than optional.","There is no absolute contraindication. Raised intracranial pressure is the principal relative contraindication; recent myocardial infarction, cerebral aneurysm and phaeochromocytoma raise the anaesthetic risk.","Mortality is comparable to that of minor surgery under general anaesthesia, in the region of one death per 100,000 treatments, and the risk is anaesthetic rather than convulsive.","Cognitive effects: transient post-ictal confusion in everyone, anterograde memory disturbance that resolves over weeks, and retrograde autobiographical memory loss which is the effect patients most object to and which can persist.","Bilateral electrode placement works faster and slightly better than unilateral; right unilateral at high dose produces less cognitive impairment for similar efficacy over a full course.","The evidence in mania and catatonia is strong. In schizophrenia the effect on core psychotic symptoms is limited, but it is useful in catatonic and treatment-resistant presentations, particularly alongside clozapine."],"cards":[["State the NICE indications for ECT.","Severe depression that is life-threatening where a rapid response is required; depression that has not responded to adequate trials of other treatments; catatonia; and a prolonged or severe manic episode."],["What is the relapse rate after a successful course of ECT without continuation treatment?","Above fifty per cent within six months, which is why continuation pharmacotherapy or continuation ECT is required rather than optional."],["Compare bilateral and right unilateral electrode placement.","Bilateral placement acts faster and is marginally more effective. High-dose right unilateral achieves comparable efficacy across a full course with significantly less cognitive impairment, particularly less retrograde autobiographical memory loss."],["Is there an absolute contraindication to ECT?","No. Raised intracranial pressure is the principal relative contraindication. Recent myocardial infarction, cerebral aneurysm and phaeochromocytoma increase anaesthetic risk and require specialist assessment."]],"note":["Subconvulsive stimulation is ineffective; the seizure itself is therapeutic.","Response rates in severe depression are around 70 to 80 per cent, highest in psychotic depression.","Mortality is approximately one per 100,000 treatments and is anaesthetic in origin.","Retrograde autobiographical memory loss is the cognitive effect most likely to persist.","ECT is first-line where the illness is immediately life-threatening or where there has been a previous good response."],"refs":["ecthandbk","nice","maudsley"]};
TEACH["6.3.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Getting the dose right in ECT is like tuning a radio. Too little and nothing happens at all. Far too much and you get the same result with more damage. The right setting is a little above the point at which the person's brain will seize, and that point is different for every person.","teach":["Seizure threshold is the minimum stimulus charge producing an adequate generalised seizure. It varies up to fortyfold between individuals and rises across a course of treatment.","Threshold is higher in older people, in men, and with bilateral placement. It is lowered by anything proconvulsant."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["ecthandbk","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["6.3.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"If medication and ECT have both failed, three other things exist. One puts a magnet against the scalp. One puts an electrode deep inside the brain. One cuts a small bundle of fibres and cannot be undone. They sit in that order of seriousness, and the last is almost never done.","teach":["Repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation, rTMS, uses a magnetic coil on the scalp to induce focal cortical currents. No anaesthetic, no seizure, and the person is awake.","The standard antidepressant protocol is high-frequency stimulation of the left dorsolateral prefrontal cortex; low-frequency right-sided stimulation is an alternative."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","companion"],"n":{"teach":12,"cards":4,"note":6}};
TEACH["6.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Before you build a service you have to know how many people need it, which is not the same as how many people are asking for it. Most people who need psychiatric care never reach it, and a service designed around the queue will always be the wrong size.","teach":["Bradshaw's taxonomy of need: normative need, defined by an expert; felt need, what people say they want; expressed need, felt need converted into a demand; and comparative need, inferred by comparison with a similar population.","Need is not demand and neither is supply. A service planned on expressed need alone systematically underserves the people least able to ask."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["healthecon","drummond","companion"],"n":{"teach":11,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["6.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Rehabilitation is not the ward where nothing happens. It is the specialty for people whose illness did not resolve, whose job is to build the largest possible life around what remains. Its measure is not symptom reduction but what the person can do on a Tuesday.","teach":["Rehabilitation psychiatry serves people with complex psychosis: treatment-resistant illness, cognitive impairment, negative symptoms, comorbid substance use, and long histories of admission.","This group is a small proportion of people with severe mental illness but consumes a large share of the mental health budget, mostly in out-of-area placements."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","companion","semgen"],"n":{"teach":12,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["6.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A diagnosis does not remove a person's rights. It changes which questions the law asks about a particular decision, at a particular moment. Someone can be too unwell to consent to an operation on Monday and perfectly able to make a will on Friday.","teach":["Capacity is decision-specific and time-specific. There is no such thing as a person who globally lacks capacity, and a diagnosis alone never establishes incapacity.","Testamentary capacity is governed by Banks v Goodfellow, 1870: the person must understand the nature of making a will, the extent of their property, the claims of those who might expect to benefit, and must have no disorder of mind that perverts their sense of right."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["eastman","puriforen","forrester"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":6}};
TEACH["6.7"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Four questions come up again and again in ordinary clinical work and each has a legal answer you are expected to know: is this person being abused, who handles their money, should they be driving, and can they decide this for themselves.","teach":["The Mental Capacity Act 2005, England and Wales, has five principles: presume capacity; take all practicable steps to support decision-making; an unwise decision is not incapacity; act in the person's best interests; and choose the least restrictive option.","The two-stage test: first, is there an impairment of or disturbance in the functioning of the mind or brain; second, does it mean the person cannot make this decision."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["eastman","nice","companion"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":6}};
TEACH["6.8"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The same illness does not look the same everywhere. In one place low mood is described as sadness, in another as heat in the head or a heavy heart. If you only recognise the version you were taught, you will miss the illness and call the person difficult instead.","teach":["Culture shapes how distress is experienced, how it is expressed, what it is attributed to, who is consulted, and what response is expected. It does not usually create new illnesses.","Idioms of distress are culturally shared ways of expressing suffering. Kleinman's distinction is fundamental: disease is the biological process, illness is the person's lived experience of it, and sickness is the social meaning attached.","Explanatory models are the person's own account of what is wrong, why it happened, what will happen, and what should be done. Eliciting it is the single most useful cross-cultural clinical skill.","DSM-5 provides the Cultural Formulation Interview, sixteen questions covering cultural definition of the problem, perceived causes, supports and stressors, cultural factors affecting coping and help-seeking, and factors affecting the clinical relationship.","ICD-11 and DSM-5 both moved from culture-bound syndromes to cultural concepts of distress, recognising that these are not exotic exceptions but the normal cultural shaping of universal processes.","Named presentations: ataque de nervios in Latino populations; dhat, semen-loss anxiety, in South Asia; koro, genital retraction anxiety, in South East Asia; taijin kyofusho, fear of offending others, in Japan; brain fag in West Africa; and susto in Latin America.","Somatic presentation of depression is the global norm rather than the exception. Presenting emotional distress in psychological terms is characteristic of educated Western populations, not of humanity generally.","The AESOP study found markedly raised incidence rates of psychosis in Black Caribbean and Black African populations in England, of the order of several times the White British rate.","The excess is not explained by misdiagnosis or by higher rates in countries of origin. The evidence points to social factors: discrimination, social defeat, migration, urbanicity and adversity.","Black patients in England are substantially more likely to be detained under the Mental Health Act, more likely to be admitted via police contact, and less likely to be referred for psychological therapy.","The Independent Review of the Mental Health Act, 2018, identified racial disparity in detention as one of the four principal problems requiring reform.","Institutional racism, defined in the Macpherson report of 1999, is the collective failure of an organisation to provide an appropriate and professional service to people because of their colour, culture or ethnic origin.","Migration itself is a risk factor, and the risk is higher in second-generation migrants than in first, which points away from selection and towards the experience of living as a minority.","Cultural competence has been criticised for producing checklists about groups. Cultural humility, a stance of enquiry about this particular person, is the more defensible position."],"cards":[["Distinguish disease, illness and sickness in Kleinman's terms.","Disease is the biological process as defined by the clinician; illness is the person's subjective lived experience of it; sickness is the social meaning and role attached to it by the wider community."],["What is the Cultural Formulation Interview?","A sixteen-question DSM-5 instrument covering the cultural definition of the problem, perceived causes, supports and stressors, cultural factors in coping and help-seeking, and factors affecting the clinical relationship."],["What did the AESOP study find, and how is it explained?","Markedly raised incidence of psychosis in Black Caribbean and Black African populations in England, several times the White British rate. It is not explained by misdiagnosis or by rates in countries of origin; the evidence points to social factors including discrimination, social defeat, migration and urbanicity."],["Name four cultural concepts of distress and their regions.","Ataque de nervios in Latino populations; dhat, semen-loss anxiety, in South Asia; koro, genital retraction anxiety, in South East Asia; taijin kyofusho, fear of offending others, in Japan."]],"note":["ICD-11 and DSM-5 both use cultural concepts of distress in place of culture-bound syndromes.","Somatic presentation of depression is the global norm; psychologising is characteristic of educated Western populations.","Psychosis risk is higher in second-generation than first-generation migrants.","The Independent Review of the Mental Health Act, 2018, identified racial disparity in detention as a principal driver of reform.","Macpherson, 1999, defined institutional racism as a collective organisational failure.","Cultural humility is preferred to cultural competence as a clinical stance."],"refs":["bhugra","morgan","icd11","dsm5"]};
TEACH["7.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Having a baby is the single largest psychiatric risk event in a woman's life. The risk of becoming psychotic in the month after childbirth is many times higher than at any other time, and the illness that follows can move from normal to dangerous in a day.","teach":["Postpartum psychosis affects roughly one to two per thousand deliveries. Onset is early, usually within the first two weeks and often within the first few days.","It is a psychiatric emergency. Presentation is characteristically fluctuating, with confusion, perplexity, mood lability, delusions frequently concerning the baby, and rapid change over hours.","The relationship to bipolar disorder is close. A woman with bipolar disorder has a risk of the order of one in four to one in two of a severe postpartum episode, and a personal or family history of postpartum psychosis raises it further.","Treatment is admission, ideally to a mother and baby unit, with antipsychotic and mood stabiliser treatment. ECT is highly effective and works quickly, and is used where the illness is severe or the woman is not eating or drinking.","Prognosis for the individual episode is good, with most women recovering fully. Recurrence risk in subsequent pregnancies is high, of the order of one in two to one in three.","Postnatal depression affects around one in ten women, with onset typically within the first three months. It is not distinguishable in symptom profile from depression at other times.","The Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale is the standard screening instrument, ten items, with a threshold conventionally around 13, though it is a screening rather than a diagnostic tool. Item 10 asks about self-harm and must always be reviewed.","The baby blues affect the majority of women, peak around days three to five, are self-limiting within about ten days, and require reassurance rather than treatment. Distinguishing this from emerging illness is a routine clinical task.","Effects on the child are real. Maternal depression is associated with impaired mother-infant interaction and with later cognitive and emotional difficulty in the child, mediated largely through the interaction rather than directly.","Perinatal anxiety disorders are at least as common as depression and are systematically under-detected. Postpartum OCD with intrusive thoughts of harming the infant is specifically under-recognised and specifically treatable.","Intrusive harm thoughts in OCD are ego-dystonic, resisted and distressing, and are not associated with increased risk to the child. Distinguishing them from psychotic thoughts about the baby is one of the most important discriminations in perinatal psychiatry.","Suicide is a leading cause of maternal death in the year after birth in the UK, and the MBRRACE-UK reports have made this a recurrent national finding.","Prescribing in pregnancy is about relative risk, not zero risk. Untreated severe mental illness carries its own substantial risk to mother and baby, and stopping medication abruptly on discovering pregnancy is a common and harmful error.","Sodium valproate must not be used in any woman of childbearing potential unless the conditions of the pregnancy prevention programme are met. It carries a high risk of major congenital malformation and of neurodevelopmental impairment.","Lithium in the first trimester is associated with a raised risk of cardiac malformation including Ebstein's anomaly, though the absolute risk is lower than once believed. Levels must be monitored closely, and around delivery the risk of toxicity rises with fluid shifts.","Paroxetine has been associated with cardiac malformation and is generally avoided; SSRIs as a class are associated with a small increase in persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn and with neonatal adaptation syndrome.","Breastfeeding: most antidepressants and antipsychotics are compatible. Lithium is relatively contraindicated because of significant infant exposure; clozapine is contraindicated because of agranulocytosis risk in the infant.","Service structure: specialist perinatal community teams, mother and baby units admitting mother and infant together, and a pre-conception counselling role for women with existing severe mental illness."],"cards":[["Describe postpartum psychosis and its relationship to bipolar disorder.","A psychiatric emergency affecting one to two per thousand deliveries, with onset usually within the first two weeks, characterised by fluctuating confusion, perplexity, mood lability and delusions often concerning the infant. A woman with bipolar disorder has a risk of the order of one in four to one in two, and recurrence risk in a subsequent pregnancy is around one in two to one in three."],["What is the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale?","A ten-item screening instrument for perinatal depression with a conventional threshold around 13. It is a screening rather than a diagnostic tool, and item 10, which asks about self-harm, must always be reviewed individually."],["Distinguish postpartum OCD harm thoughts from psychotic thoughts about the infant.","OCD intrusive harm thoughts are ego-dystonic, actively resisted, distressing to the mother, and not associated with increased risk to the child. Psychotic thoughts are ego-syntonic or delusionally held, may be acted upon, and constitute an emergency. This is among the most important discriminations in perinatal psychiatry."],["State the position on sodium valproate in women of childbearing potential.","It must not be used unless the conditions of the pregnancy prevention programme are met, because of a high risk of major congenital malformation and of neurodevelopmental impairment in the child."],["Which psychotropics are problematic in breastfeeding?","Lithium is relatively contraindicated because of significant infant exposure and the need for infant monitoring. Clozapine is contraindicated because of the risk of agranulocytosis in the infant. Most other antidepressants and antipsychotics are compatible."]],"note":["Baby blues peak at days three to five and resolve within about ten days without treatment.","Suicide is a leading cause of maternal death in the year after birth in the UK.","Lithium in the first trimester raises the risk of cardiac malformation including Ebstein's anomaly.","SSRIs are associated with a small increase in persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn.","ECT is highly effective and rapid in severe postpartum psychosis.","Abrupt cessation of medication on discovering pregnancy is a common and harmful error."],"refs":["maudsley","nice","semliaison"]};
TEACH["7.3.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"On a medical ward the question is almost never what psychiatric illness does this person have. It is which of these three things is happening: they are physically ill and confused, they are physically ill and frightened, or they had a psychiatric illness before any of this started.","teach":["Rates of psychiatric disorder in the general hospital are high. Depression, anxiety, delirium, alcohol problems and cognitive impairment together affect a large minority of medical inpatients.","Delirium first. Any new disturbance of consciousness, attention or cognition in a physically ill person is delirium until proven otherwise, and it is missed in a large proportion of cases.","Attention is the discriminating feature. A person who cannot sustain attention long enough to complete a simple sequence has delirium; a person with intact attention and low mood does not.","Diagnosing depression in physical illness is complicated by somatic overlap. Fatigue, poor appetite, sleep disturbance and weight loss all arise from the illness itself.","Two solutions to the overlap: the inclusive approach counts every symptom regardless of cause, which over-diagnoses; the substitutive approach replaces somatic items with cognitive ones such as hopelessness, worthlessness, anhedonia and suicidal thinking, which is more useful clinically.","Anhedonia, hopelessness, guilt and suicidal ideation carry the diagnostic weight in a physically ill patient. Tiredness carries almost none.","Screening instruments designed for this setting: the Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale, which deliberately excludes somatic items, and the PHQ-9 and GAD-7 which do not.","Always take a medication history. Corticosteroids, interferon, some antivirals, dopamine agonists, opioids, antimuscarinics and benzodiazepines all cause psychiatric symptoms directly.","Always take an alcohol and substance history. Undeclared alcohol dependence presenting as postoperative agitation on day three is a classic and dangerous scenario.","Capacity assessment is the commonest specific request in liaison work, usually about refusal of treatment or of discharge destination. Assess the decision in question, not the person in general.","A person may refuse treatment for a reason that seems irrational, and still have capacity. The Mental Capacity Act principle that an unwise decision is not incapacity is the point most often forgotten by referring teams.","The Mental Health Act cannot be used to treat a physical illness, except where the physical treatment is treatment for the mental disorder or its consequences, such as refeeding in anorexia nervosa or treating the consequences of an overdose taken as a symptom of the mental disorder.","Medically unexplained symptoms are common and are not the same as feigning. ICD-11 uses bodily distress disorder; DSM-5 uses somatic symptom disorder, which notably no longer requires the symptoms to be medically unexplained.","Examination and investigation are part of a psychiatric assessment here: vital signs, oxygen saturation, glucose, electrolytes including sodium and calcium, renal and liver function, thyroid function, B12 and folate, infection screen, and imaging where indicated.","Collateral history is essential and frequently omitted. What this person was like a week ago is the single most useful piece of information on a medical ward."],"cards":[["How should depression be diagnosed in a physically ill patient?","Using the substitutive approach: replace somatic criteria that overlap with the physical illness, such as fatigue, appetite and sleep change, with cognitive criteria including anhedonia, hopelessness, worthlessness, guilt and suicidal ideation, which carry the diagnostic weight."],["What is the discriminating clinical feature of delirium?","Inattention. A disturbance of attention and awareness developing acutely and fluctuating over the day, with an additional disturbance in cognition, in the context of a physiological cause. Any new cognitive disturbance in a physically ill patient is delirium until proven otherwise."],["Can the Mental Health Act be used to treat a physical illness?","Only where the physical treatment is treatment for the mental disorder or its consequences, for example refeeding in anorexia nervosa or treating the physical consequences of an overdose taken as a manifestation of the mental disorder. It cannot be used to treat unrelated physical illness."],["Which screening instrument is designed for the general hospital and why?","The Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale, which deliberately excludes somatic items so that the score is not inflated by symptoms arising from the physical illness itself."]],"note":["Corticosteroids, interferon, dopamine agonists, opioids and antimuscarinics all cause psychiatric symptoms directly.","Undeclared alcohol dependence presenting as postoperative agitation is a classic scenario.","An unwise decision does not indicate incapacity.","ICD-11 uses bodily distress disorder; DSM-5 somatic symptom disorder no longer requires symptoms to be medically unexplained.","Collateral history describing the person a week earlier is the most useful single piece of ward information."],"refs":["semliaison","oxhandbk","icd11"]};
TEACH["7.3.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Each medical specialty produces its own psychiatric problems, and a liaison psychiatrist is expected to know which ones. Oncology brings adjustment and delirium. Renal brings depression and capacity. Cardiology brings anxiety that looks like heart disease.","teach":["Oncology: adjustment disorder is the commonest diagnosis, followed by depression and delirium. Distress screening with the Distress Thermometer is standard practice.","Depression in cancer is under-treated because it is regarded as understandable. Understandable and treatable are not alternatives."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semliaison","maudsley","maudphys"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":4,"note":6}};
TEACH["7.3.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Damage to different parts of the brain produces different personalities, and the pattern is consistent enough to locate the lesion from the behaviour. That is the whole content of neuropsychiatry, and it is heavily examined.","teach":["Frontal lobe syndromes divide into three. Dorsolateral prefrontal damage produces the dysexecutive syndrome: poor planning, perseveration, poor set-shifting and reduced verbal fluency.","Orbitofrontal damage produces disinhibition, impulsivity, tactlessness, emotional lability and loss of social judgement, with intelligence often preserved. This is the Phineas Gage picture."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lishman","oxneuropsy","semliaison"],"n":{"teach":18,"cards":6,"note":6}};
TEACH["7.3.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Pain is not a reading from a damage meter. It is what the brain decides the body needs to be told, and it can be turned up or down by attention, mood, meaning and expectation. That is not a claim that pain is imaginary. It is a claim about how it is produced.","teach":["The gate control theory, Melzack and Wall 1965, proposed a gating mechanism in the dorsal horn modulated by descending input from the brain. It ended the idea of pain as simple transmission and is the foundational concept in this field.","Melzack's later neuromatrix theory holds that pain is an output of a distributed brain network, which explains phantom limb pain, in which there is no periphery at all."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semliaison","nice","maudphys"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["7.3.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Grief is not an illness and treating it as one causes harm. But a small proportion of bereaved people get stuck in a way that does not resolve on its own, and telling the difference is the clinical skill.","teach":["Normal grief involves yearning, sadness, preoccupation with the deceased, and often transient hallucinatory experiences of the person's voice or presence, which are normal and should not be pathologised.","Guilt in normal grief concerns the circumstances of the death. Pervasive worthlessness and global guilt point towards depression rather than grief."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semliaison","icd11","dsm5"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.4.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"You cannot predict who will kill themselves. The research on this is unusually clear and unusually ignored. What you can do is understand why this person is in danger now, and change some part of it.","teach":["Suicide is rare at population level, which is why prediction fails. Around 5,000 to 6,000 people die by suicide each year in the UK, roughly three-quarters of them men.","The male to female ratio in completed suicide is around three to one; the ratio is reversed for self-harm and attempted suicide. This is the gender paradox of suicide.","Risk factors: male sex, older age, previous self-harm, mental illness particularly depression, psychosis and substance misuse, physical illness and pain, social isolation, unemployment, bereavement, recent discharge from psychiatric care, and access to means.","Previous self-harm is the strongest single predictor, with risk of subsequent suicide many times the general population rate, and highest in the first year after an episode.","The two weeks after discharge from a psychiatric inpatient bed carry a very high risk, and the first three days are the highest of all. The National Confidential Inquiry has made follow-up within three days a standard.","Risk scales do not work. NICE guidance is explicit that risk assessment tools and scales should not be used to predict suicide or self-harm, or to determine who should receive treatment.","The reason is statistical: with a rare outcome, any scale produces enormous numbers of false positives and still misses many of those who die. Most people who die by suicide are not in the high-risk category.","What replaces scoring is a clinical formulation: why this person, why now, what is driving it, what is protective, what could change it, and what specific plan follows.","NICE NG225, self-harm 2022, requires a psychosocial assessment for everyone presenting with self-harm, regardless of whether they are judged low risk, and prohibits using risk stratification to deny that assessment.","Self-harm is not attempted suicide, but it is not benign either. Motivations include relief of unbearable affect, self-punishment, communication of distress, and dissociation, and the presence of these does not exclude suicidal intent.","Assess intent through the circumstances: planning, precautions against discovery, timing, final acts such as notes or wills, perceived lethality, and the person's reaction to survival.","Perceived lethality matters more than actual lethality. Someone who took eight paracetamol believing it would kill them has high intent.","Protective factors are weak protection when acute risk is high. Children at home, religious belief and future plans reduce risk at population level and cannot be relied on to prevent an act during acute crisis.","Means restriction is the intervention with the best evidence: removing ligature points, restricting paracetamol pack sizes, barriers at jumping sites, and safe storage or removal of medication and firearms.","The safety plan is a collaborative written document naming warning signs, personal coping strategies, people and places that provide distraction, people to contact for help, professionals to contact, and steps taken to make the environment safe.","Interventions with evidence: DBT reduces self-harm in borderline personality disorder; brief contact interventions such as follow-up calls and postcards show promise; and safety planning is associated with reduced subsequent attempts.","Ask directly. Asking about suicide does not increase risk, and this has been examined repeatedly. Vague questioning produces vague answers and misses the plan."],"cards":[["What does NICE say about suicide risk assessment tools?","That risk assessment tools and scales should not be used to predict future suicide or self-harm, or to determine who should be offered treatment. A psychosocial assessment and clinical formulation replace risk stratification."],["Why does statistical prediction of suicide fail?","Because suicide is a rare outcome. With a low base rate, any instrument generates very large numbers of false positives while still missing many of those who go on to die, and most people who die by suicide are not classified as high risk."],["Which single historical factor most strongly predicts suicide, and when is risk highest?","Previous self-harm, which raises the risk of subsequent suicide many times above the general population rate, with the risk highest in the first year after an episode."],["What are the components of a safety plan?","Recognition of personal warning signs; internal coping strategies; people and social settings that provide distraction; individuals who can be asked for help; professionals and services to contact in crisis; and specific steps taken to make the environment safer by restricting means."],["When is risk highest after psychiatric discharge?","In the first two weeks after discharge, with the first three days carrying the highest risk of all. Follow-up within three days of discharge is a national standard."]],"note":["Around 5,000 to 6,000 people die by suicide annually in the UK, roughly three-quarters of them men.","The gender paradox: men die more often, women self-harm and attempt more often.","Perceived lethality is more informative than actual lethality.","NICE NG225 requires a psychosocial assessment for everyone presenting with self-harm.","Asking directly about suicide does not increase risk.","Means restriction has the strongest evidence of any suicide prevention intervention."],"refs":["nice","companion","semgen"]};
TEACH["7.4.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Crisis teams exist on one idea: that most people in a mental health emergency get better faster at home, if someone comes to the house every day, than they would on a ward. The evidence for that is reasonable. Whether services deliver it is another matter.","teach":["Caplan's crisis theory, 1964, describes crisis as a temporary state of disequilibrium when a person's usual coping fails in the face of a problem they can neither solve nor avoid.","Caplan's phases: initial rise in tension, failure of usual coping with further rise, mobilisation of internal and external resources, and finally either resolution or breakdown into disorganisation."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","companion"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.4.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"In an emergency the first job is not to name the psychiatric diagnosis. It is to rule out the physical causes that will kill the person tonight if you decide this is a mental health problem and stop looking.","teach":["The acutely disturbed patient has a differential that runs organic first, always: hypoglycaemia, hypoxia, head injury, intracranial bleed, infection including sepsis and encephalitis, metabolic derangement, thyroid storm, and drug intoxication or withdrawal.","Delirium is the great mimic. It presents as agitation, psychosis, mood change or apparent catatonia, and the discriminating features are acute onset, fluctuation, impaired attention and altered conscious level."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxhandbk","maudsley","semliaison"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["7.4.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"When someone has to be treated against their will there are two laws and they answer different questions. One asks whether the person can decide. The other does not care whether they can decide, only whether they have a mental disorder and need treating for it.","teach":["De-escalation comes first and is not a formality. Space, posture, tone, removing the audience, offering choices, and addressing the actual grievance resolve most incidents without restraint.","Rapid tranquillisation is the use of medication to calm a person quickly where de-escalation has failed and there is risk of harm. NICE NG10 governs it."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","eastman","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["7.6.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Sexual problems are extremely common, rarely asked about, and often entirely treatable. A great many of them are caused by the drugs psychiatrists prescribe, and a great many of those go unreported because nobody asks.","teach":["The classical human sexual response cycle, from Masters and Johnson, has four phases: excitement, plateau, orgasm and resolution. Kaplan added desire as a preceding phase, giving the triphasic model of desire, excitement and orgasm.","Basson proposed a circular model, particularly for women in long-term relationships, in which responsive desire follows arousal rather than preceding it. This changed how low desire is understood and treated."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["icd11","maudsley","companion"],"n":{"teach":18,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["7.6.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The College syllabus names this section using older terms. The classification has changed substantially since, in a direction that matters clinically: distress about the mismatch is no longer treated as a mental disorder in itself.","teach":["Terminology has moved and the exam expects the current position. ICD-10 used gender identity disorders and transsexualism; ICD-11 uses gender incongruence and moved it out of the mental and behavioural disorders chapter entirely.","ICD-11 places gender incongruence in a new chapter on conditions related to sexual health. The stated purpose was to preserve access to care while removing the classification of being transgender as a mental disorder."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["icd11","dsm5","nice"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["7.1.1.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Depression is common enough that in any room of twenty adults, one is likely to be depressed right now, and about one in six will be at some point in their life.","teach":["Point prevalence in the community is of the order of 4 to 5 per cent for major depression; twelve-month prevalence around 6 per cent; lifetime prevalence in the region of 15 to 20 per cent.","Female to male ratio is approximately two to one, and this emerges in adolescence rather than being present in childhood.","The sex difference does not appear before puberty and narrows again in later life, which constrains any explanation based on fixed biology alone.","The Adult Psychiatric Morbidity Survey is the UK source. Common mental disorder overall affects around one in six adults in any given week.","Depression is the leading cause of years lived with disability worldwide in Global Burden of Disease estimates.","Prevalence is markedly higher in the presence of chronic physical illness, roughly two to three times, and in lower socioeconomic groups.","Only a minority of depressed people in the community receive treatment, and of those, most are treated in primary care."],"cards":[["Give the point, twelve-month and lifetime prevalence of major depression.","Approximately 4 to 5 per cent point prevalence, around 6 per cent twelve-month prevalence, and 15 to 20 per cent lifetime prevalence in community samples."],["What is the sex ratio in depression and when does it emerge?","Approximately two to one, female to male. It is absent before puberty, emerges during adolescence, and narrows again in later life."]],"note":["Depression is the leading global cause of years lived with disability.","Prevalence is two to three times higher in chronic physical illness.","The Adult Psychiatric Morbidity Survey is the UK epidemiological source.","Most treated depression is managed in primary care."],"refs":["companion","nice"]};
TEACH["7.1.1.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Depression runs in families, but not in the way eye colour does. What is inherited is a susceptibility that only becomes illness when something happens, and the size of the something needed is smaller the more susceptibility you carry.","teach":["Heritability of major depression is around 35 to 40 per cent, considerably lower than for bipolar disorder or schizophrenia.","Genetics is polygenic: many common variants of individually tiny effect. Large genome-wide association studies have identified over a hundred loci.","The 5-HTTLPR gene-environment interaction reported by Caspi in 2003 was hugely influential and has failed to replicate in large samples and meta-analysis. Teach it as a cautionary tale, not as a finding.","Brown and Harris, in Social Origins of Depression, distinguished provoking agents, being severe life events and difficulties, from vulnerability factors, which included loss of mother before age eleven, three or more children under fourteen at home, lack of a confiding relationship, and lack of employment.","Kendler's work established that severe life events, particularly those involving loss and humiliation, precede a large proportion of depressive episodes.","The kindling hypothesis, from Post: early episodes require substantial precipitants, later episodes require progressively less, and eventually episodes arise autonomously.","The monoamine hypothesis is inadequate as stated. Moncrieff's 2022 umbrella review found no consistent evidence that depression is caused by low serotonin, and this is now the honest teaching position.","Better-supported biology: HPA axis dysregulation with non-suppression on the dexamethasone suppression test; reduced hippocampal volume in recurrent depression; and inflammatory markers including raised IL-6 and CRP in a subgroup.","The neurotrophic hypothesis proposes reduced BDNF signalling and impaired neuroplasticity, restored by antidepressant treatment, and fits the delayed onset of antidepressant action better than the monoamine account.","Psychological: Beck's cognitive triad and negative schemas; Seligman's learned helplessness and its later attributional reformulation; and Nolen-Hoeksema's response styles theory, in which rumination prolongs episodes and is commoner in women.","Adverse childhood experience is one of the most robust risk factors, with a dose-response relationship to adult depression."],"cards":[["What did Brown and Harris identify?","Provoking agents, being severe life events and ongoing difficulties, and vulnerability factors, comprising loss of mother before age eleven, three or more children under fourteen at home, absence of a confiding relationship, and lack of employment outside the home."],["What is the current status of the monoamine hypothesis?","Inadequate as an explanation of causation. Moncrieff's 2022 umbrella review found no consistent evidence that depression is caused by reduced serotonin. The neurotrophic and HPA axis accounts fit the evidence better, though antidepressants remain effective regardless of the causal mechanism."],["State the kindling hypothesis.","Post's proposal that early depressive episodes require substantial environmental precipitants, that successive episodes require progressively smaller ones, and that later episodes may arise autonomously without identifiable triggers."],["What is the heritability of major depression?","Approximately 35 to 40 per cent, substantially lower than the heritability of bipolar disorder or schizophrenia. The genetic architecture is polygenic, with over a hundred loci identified in large genome-wide association studies."]],"note":["The 5-HTTLPR gene-environment interaction failed to replicate and should be taught as a cautionary example.","HPA axis dysregulation shows as dexamethasone non-suppression.","Raised IL-6 and CRP are found in a subgroup of depressed patients.","Nolen-Hoeksema's rumination response style is commoner in women and prolongs episodes.","Adverse childhood experience shows a dose-response relationship with adult depression."],"refs":["companion","stahl","gross"]};
TEACH["7.1.1.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Depression is not just sadness. Some depressed people are not sad at all: they are flat, or irritable, or convinced they are rotting from the inside. What is constant is that nothing gives pleasure and everything costs effort.","teach":["ICD-11 requires depressed mood or markedly diminished interest, nearly every day for at least two weeks, with additional characteristic symptoms across affective, cognitive-behavioural and neurovegetative clusters.","Core symptoms: low mood, anhedonia and reduced energy. Anhedonia is the most discriminating and the most useful to ask about directly.","Biological symptoms: early morning wakening, diurnal variation with mood worst in the morning, appetite and weight loss, psychomotor change and loss of libido.","Atypical features run the other way: hypersomnia, hyperphagia with weight gain, leaden paralysis, mood reactivity and rejection sensitivity.","Cognitive symptoms: poor concentration, indecisiveness, guilt, worthlessness, hopelessness and thoughts of death. Hopelessness is the strongest cognitive correlate of suicidal intent.","Psychotic depression: mood-congruent delusions of guilt, poverty, nihilism or deserved punishment, and second-person derogatory auditory hallucinations. It affects a substantial minority of severe inpatient depression.","Cotard's syndrome is the nihilistic delusion of being dead, of not existing, or of having no internal organs, occurring in severe psychotic depression and in some organic states.","Depressive stupor is the extreme, with immobility and mutism, and overlaps with catatonia.","Depressive pseudodementia in older people: cognitive impairment with prominent don't know answers, an abrupt onset, the person complaining of memory rather than concealing it, and improvement with antidepressant treatment.","Masked depression presents with somatic complaint, and is the commonest presentation worldwide. Somatic presentation is the global norm; psychological presentation is characteristic of educated Western populations.","Rating scales: Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression, clinician-rated with a heavy somatic loading; Montgomery-Åsberg, clinician-rated and more sensitive to change; Beck Depression Inventory, self-rated; PHQ-9, self-rated and used in primary care."],"cards":[["Which symptom is most discriminating in depression?","Anhedonia, the loss of interest or pleasure, which is more specific than low mood, particularly in physically ill and older patients, and should be asked about directly."],["Describe the atypical features of depression.","Hypersomnia, hyperphagia with weight gain, leaden paralysis, preserved mood reactivity and long-standing interpersonal rejection sensitivity. They run in the opposite direction to the classical biological features."],["What is Cotard's syndrome?","A nihilistic delusion of being dead, of not existing, or of having lost internal organs or bodily functions, occurring in severe psychotic depression and in certain organic states."],["How is depressive pseudodementia distinguished from dementia?","Abrupt onset with a clearer date of onset, the person complaining of memory problems rather than concealing them, prominent don't know rather than near-miss answers, variable performance, preserved insight, and improvement with antidepressant treatment."]],"note":["ICD-11 requires two weeks of near-daily symptoms.","Hopelessness is the strongest cognitive correlate of suicidal intent.","Psychotic depression features mood-congruent delusions of guilt, poverty and nihilism.","Somatic presentation of depression is the global norm.","HAM-D is clinician-rated with heavy somatic loading; MADRS is more sensitive to change; PHQ-9 is self-rated."],"refs":["oyebode","icd11","companion"]};
TEACH["7.1.1.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"For mild depression, talking works as well as tablets and has fewer side effects. For severe depression, tablets work and talking alone usually does not. Most of the argument is about the middle.","teach":["NICE NG222, 2022, recommends a menu of options for less severe depression with shared decision-making, including guided self-help, group CBT, group behavioural activation, exercise, and individual CBT, with antidepressants not first-line unless the person prefers them.","For more severe depression the recommended options include individual CBT combined with an antidepressant, individual CBT alone, individual behavioural activation, or an antidepressant alone."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","stahl"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.1.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Most depressive episodes end. The problem is that most people who have one go on to have another, and each episode makes the next more likely.","teach":["The median duration of an untreated episode is around six months; the great majority resolve within a year.","Recurrence is the rule rather than the exception. Around half of those with a first episode have a second; after two episodes the risk of a third is around 70 per cent; after three it is around 90 per cent."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["companion","nice"],"n":{"teach":10,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.2.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Bipolar disorder affects roughly one person in a hundred, and unlike depression it affects men and women equally. It also starts young, usually before twenty-five, which is why it interrupts education and early careers so severely.","teach":["Bipolar I, involving at least one manic episode, has a lifetime prevalence of around 1 per cent. Bipolar II, involving hypomania and depression, adds a further 1 per cent or so.","The bipolar spectrum including subthreshold presentations is estimated at around 2 to 4 per cent, though the boundaries are contested.","The sex ratio is approximately equal for bipolar I. Bipolar II and rapid cycling are commoner in women.","Mean age of onset is in the late teens to early twenties, substantially earlier than unipolar depression.","Diagnostic delay is a major clinical problem. The interval from first episode to correct diagnosis is commonly measured in years, most often because a depressive presentation is treated as unipolar.","Prevalence shows little variation across countries and cultures, in contrast with depression, which supports a stronger biological contribution.","Bipolar disorder is over-represented in creative and professional groups in some studies, but this literature is weak and prone to selection bias, and should be stated cautiously."],"cards":[["Give the prevalence and sex ratio of bipolar disorder.","Bipolar I affects approximately 1 per cent lifetime, with bipolar II adding a further 1 per cent or so. The sex ratio is approximately equal for bipolar I, though bipolar II and rapid cycling are commoner in women."],["Why is diagnosis of bipolar disorder commonly delayed?","Because the first presentation is usually depressive and is treated as unipolar depression. The interval from first episode to correct diagnosis is commonly measured in years."]],"note":["Mean age of onset is late teens to early twenties, earlier than unipolar depression.","Prevalence varies little across countries, unlike depression.","The bipolar spectrum including subthreshold presentations is estimated at 2 to 4 per cent."],"refs":["companion","nice"]};
TEACH["7.1.2.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Of all the common psychiatric disorders, this is the one where genes matter most. If one identical twin has it, the other has roughly a two in three chance, which is a bigger number than almost anything else in psychiatry.","teach":["Heritability is around 70 to 80 per cent, among the highest in psychiatry and higher than for schizophrenia by most estimates.","Monozygotic concordance is around 40 to 70 per cent; dizygotic around 5 to 10 per cent. First-degree relatives carry roughly a tenfold increased risk."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semgen","companion","stahl"],"n":{"teach":12,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.2.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Mania is not happiness. It is a state in which everything is speeded up and nothing is filtered, so a person spends money they do not have, says things they cannot take back, and cannot understand why anyone is worried.","teach":["Mania requires elevated, expansive or irritable mood with increased activity or energy, lasting at least one week or requiring hospitalisation, with marked impairment.","Irritable mania is common and is frequently missed because clinicians look for elation. Irritability rather than euphoria is the predominant mood in a substantial proportion.","Core features: grandiosity, reduced need for sleep, pressure of speech, flight of ideas, distractibility, increased goal-directed activity, and excessive involvement in activities with a high potential for painful consequences.","Hypomania differs from mania in degree and duration: at least four consecutive days, a clear change observable by others, but without marked impairment, without psychosis and without requiring admission.","Psychosis occurs in mania and does not indicate schizophrenia. Delusions are typically mood-congruent and grandiose, though mood-incongruent delusions occur and worsen prognosis.","Schneiderian first-rank symptoms occur in mania and therefore have no diagnostic specificity for schizophrenia, which is why both ICD-11 and DSM-5 removed them from the definition.","Speech in mania: pressure of speech, flight of ideas with retained but loosening connections, clang associations, punning and distractibility. Formal thought disorder in mania preserves more logical linkage than in schizophrenia.","Insight is characteristically absent in mania and characteristically present in hypomania, which is why hypomania is more often reported retrospectively.","Mixed states: depressive and manic features simultaneously. ICD-11 retains a mixed episode category; DSM-5 uses a with mixed features specifier applied to an episode of either polarity.","Mixed states carry the highest suicide risk of any phase of the illness, because depressive cognition coexists with the energy and impulsivity to act.","Bipolar depression differs from unipolar in probabilistic ways: earlier onset, more episodes, more atypical features with hypersomnia and hyperphagia, more psychomotor retardation, more psychosis, and a family history of bipolar disorder.","Rapid cycling is four or more episodes in twelve months. It is commoner in women, associated with hypothyroidism and with antidepressant exposure, and predicts poorer response to lithium."],"cards":[["Distinguish mania from hypomania.","Mania lasts at least one week or requires hospitalisation, causes marked impairment, may include psychosis, and is characterised by absent insight. Hypomania lasts at least four consecutive days, represents an observable change from baseline, but causes no marked impairment, involves no psychosis, and does not require admission."],["Why do first-rank symptoms lack diagnostic specificity?","Because they occur in mania and in organic states as well as in schizophrenia. Both ICD-11 and DSM-5 have removed them from the definition of schizophrenia on this basis."],["Which phase of bipolar illness carries the highest suicide risk, and why?","Mixed states, because depressive cognition and hopelessness coexist with the energy, agitation and impulsivity required to act."],["Which features suggest that a depressive episode is bipolar rather than unipolar?","Earlier age of onset, a greater number of previous episodes, atypical features with hypersomnia and hyperphagia, marked psychomotor retardation, psychotic features, postpartum onset, and a family history of bipolar disorder."]],"note":["Irritability rather than elation is the predominant mood in a substantial proportion of manic episodes.","Rapid cycling is four or more episodes in twelve months, commoner in women, and predicts poorer lithium response.","Flight of ideas retains logical linkage; schizophrenic thought disorder does not.","Mood-incongruent delusions in mania worsen prognosis.","DSM-5 uses a mixed features specifier; ICD-11 retains a mixed episode category."],"refs":["oyebode","icd11","dsm5"]};
TEACH["7.1.2.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Lithium remains the best drug in psychiatry for this illness, seventy years after it was introduced, and it is the only one that reliably reduces suicide. It also needs blood tests forever, which is why it is prescribed less than it should be.","teach":["Acute mania: an antipsychotic is first-line for speed, with haloperidol, olanzapine, quetiapine, risperidone and aripiprazole all effective. Any antidepressant should be stopped.","Lithium and valproate are effective in acute mania but act more slowly than antipsychotics. Combination of an antipsychotic with lithium or valproate is more effective than either alone in severe mania."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","bnf"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.2.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"This is a lifelong illness that people live with rather than recover from, and the striking thing about the long term is how much of it is spent depressed rather than manic. The depression is what disables people.","teach":["Bipolar disorder is recurrent in almost all cases. More than 90 per cent of those with a single manic episode have further episodes.","The proportion of time spent symptomatic is high, and depressive symptoms account for roughly three times as much of that time as manic symptoms. This is the single most important prognostic fact."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["companion","nice","maudphys"],"n":{"teach":12,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.3.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"About one person in a hundred develops schizophrenia. That figure is quoted everywhere and it hides something important: the risk is not the same everywhere or for everyone, and where it varies tells you what causes it.","teach":["Lifetime risk is conventionally given as around 1 per cent, though careful reviews suggest lifetime morbid risk closer to 0.7 per cent.","Incidence is around 15 per 100,000 per year, and shows considerable variation between places and groups, which the old teaching of uniform incidence understated.","The McGrath systematic reviews established that incidence varies fivefold between sites, and that this variation is systematic rather than noise.","Sex: incidence is somewhat higher in men, with a ratio around 1.4 to 1. Onset is earlier in men, in the early twenties, against the late twenties in women, and women show a second smaller peak around the menopause.","The oestrogen hypothesis proposes that oestrogen is protective, explaining both the later female onset and the postmenopausal peak.","Urban birth and urban upbringing raise risk, with a dose-response relationship to the degree of urbanicity and to the duration of urban residence in childhood.","Migration raises risk substantially, and the risk is higher in second-generation migrants than first, and higher where the person is a visible minority in the area they live.","The AESOP study found incidence several times higher in Black Caribbean and Black African populations in England than in the White British population, not explained by misdiagnosis or by rates in countries of origin.","The ethnic density effect: risk for a minority individual falls as the proportion of their own group in the local area rises, which points to social experience rather than to ethnicity itself.","Winter and spring birth carries a small excess risk, of the order of 5 to 10 per cent, replicated in northern hemisphere studies and reversed in the southern hemisphere.","Prevalence is lower than cumulative incidence would predict because of raised mortality and because some people recover.","Socioeconomic status: both social causation, in which adversity causes illness, and social drift, in which illness causes downward mobility, contribute, and the classic Goldberg and Morrison study supported drift by examining fathers' occupations."],"cards":[["What is the lifetime risk and incidence of schizophrenia, and how much does incidence vary?","Lifetime morbid risk is around 0.7 to 1 per cent and incidence around 15 per 100,000 per year. The McGrath reviews established that incidence varies approximately fivefold between sites, systematically rather than randomly."],["Describe the sex differences in schizophrenia.","Incidence is somewhat higher in men, with a ratio of about 1.4 to 1. Onset is earlier in men, in the early twenties, compared with the late twenties in women, and women show a second smaller peak of onset around the menopause, consistent with the oestrogen protection hypothesis."],["What is the ethnic density effect?","The observation that the risk of psychosis for a member of an ethnic minority falls as the proportion of their own ethnic group in the local area rises, which points to social experience rather than to ethnicity itself as the operative factor."],["Distinguish social causation from social drift.","Social causation holds that socioeconomic adversity contributes to causing the illness. Social drift holds that the illness causes downward socioeconomic mobility. Goldberg and Morrison supported drift by showing that patients' fathers' occupational status was not lowered, while the patients' own was."]],"note":["Urban birth and upbringing show a dose-response relationship with risk.","Second-generation migrants carry higher risk than first-generation.","Winter and spring birth carries a small excess risk, reversed in the southern hemisphere.","AESOP found incidence several times higher in Black Caribbean and Black African populations in England.","Prevalence is lower than cumulative incidence because of raised mortality and some recovery."],"refs":["morgan","companion","bhugra"]};
TEACH["7.1.3.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Nobody knows what causes schizophrenia, and anyone who says otherwise is selling something. What is known is a list of things that raise the risk, most of which act very early, long before anyone becomes unwell.","teach":["Heritability is around 80 per cent. Monozygotic concordance is around 40 to 50 per cent, dizygotic around 10 to 15 per cent, and a child of one affected parent has a risk of around 13 per cent, rising to around 40 per cent with two affected parents.","The monozygotic concordance being well under 100 per cent is the crucial fact: genes are necessary for the risk, and insufficient for the illness."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semgen","lynall","stahl","morgan"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["7.1.3.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"There are two kinds of symptom and they behave completely differently. Positive symptoms are things that have been added: voices, beliefs, disorganisation. Negative symptoms are things that have been taken away: motivation, speech, emotion. The drugs work on the first and barely touch the second.","teach":["ICD-11 requires at least two symptoms from a specified list, one of which must be a core positive symptom, persisting for at least one month.","The core symptom groups: persistent delusions; persistent hallucinations; disorganised thinking; experiences of influence, passivity or control; negative symptoms; grossly disorganised behaviour; and psychomotor disturbance.","ICD-11 abolished the subtypes of schizophrenia, including paranoid, hebephrenic and catatonic, because they lacked stability over time and predictive validity. DSM-5 did the same. This is a change frequently examined.","Positive symptoms: delusions, hallucinations, and formal thought disorder. Hallucinations in schizophrenia are typically auditory, in the third person, with running commentary, and occur in clear consciousness.","Delusional perception is the attribution of abnormal significance to a normally perceived object, in two stages, and is the most specific of the first-rank symptoms.","Passivity phenomena: made actions, made feelings, made impulses and somatic passivity, all involving loss of the sense of ownership of one's own mental or bodily activity.","Formal thought disorder: loosening of associations, derailment or knight's move thinking, tangentiality, neologism, word salad in severe cases, and thought block.","Negative symptoms: the five domains are blunted affect, alogia, avolition, anhedonia and asociality. They divide into diminished expression and avolition-apathy factors.","Primary negative symptoms are intrinsic to the illness. Secondary negative symptoms arise from depression, from antipsychotic-induced parkinsonism, from institutionalisation or from persistent positive symptoms, and are treatable by removing the cause.","Distinguishing primary from secondary negative symptoms is a routine and important clinical task, since the second is remediable and the first largely is not.","Cognitive impairment is a core feature, not a complication. It affects processing speed, working memory, verbal learning, attention and executive function, is present before onset, and predicts functional outcome better than positive symptoms do.","Catatonia is now classified separately in both ICD-11 and DSM-5, applicable across diagnoses rather than being a subtype of schizophrenia.","The prodrome typically involves social withdrawal, declining function, odd beliefs and perceptual abnormalities, and is recognised in retrospect far more reliably than prospectively.","Insight is best regarded dimensionally, comprising awareness of illness, attribution of symptoms and acceptance of treatment need, which can vary independently."],"cards":[["What happened to the subtypes of schizophrenia?","ICD-11 and DSM-5 both abolished them, including paranoid, hebephrenic and catatonic types, because they lacked temporal stability, predictive validity and treatment implications. Catatonia is now classified separately and applies across diagnoses."],["Name the five domains of negative symptoms and distinguish primary from secondary.","Blunted affect, alogia, avolition, anhedonia and asociality. Primary negative symptoms are intrinsic to the illness. Secondary negative symptoms arise from depression, antipsychotic-induced parkinsonism, institutionalisation or untreated positive symptoms, and are remediable by treating the cause."],["What is delusional perception?","The attribution of abnormal and highly personal significance to a normally perceived object or event, occurring in two stages: normal perception followed by delusional interpretation. It is the most specific of Schneider's first-rank symptoms."],["What is the status of cognitive impairment in schizophrenia?","A core feature rather than a complication. It affects processing speed, working memory, verbal learning, attention and executive function, is detectable before illness onset, and predicts functional outcome better than positive symptoms."]],"note":["ICD-11 requires at least two symptoms including one core positive symptom, for at least one month.","Auditory hallucinations in schizophrenia are typically third person with running commentary, in clear consciousness.","Passivity phenomena involve loss of ownership of one's own mental or bodily activity.","The prodrome is recognised reliably in retrospect and unreliably in prospect.","Insight is dimensional, comprising awareness, attribution and acceptance of treatment need."],"refs":["oyebode","fish","icd11"]};
TEACH["7.1.3.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Every antipsychotic blocks dopamine D2 receptors and they are all roughly equally effective, except one. Clozapine works when nothing else has, nobody fully understands why, and it is prescribed years later than it should be.","teach":["All effective antipsychotics are D2 receptor antagonists or partial agonists, and clinical potency correlates with D2 affinity. Around 65 to 70 per cent striatal D2 occupancy gives antipsychotic effect; above about 80 per cent extrapyramidal side effects appear.","The first-generation and second-generation distinction is pharmacologically crude. The meaningful differences are in adverse effect profile rather than in efficacy."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","nice","stahl","lawrie"],"n":{"teach":18,"cards":6,"note":6}};
TEACH["7.1.3.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The old teaching was that a third get better, a third stay the same and a third get worse. It is roughly right and it hides the more important fact: people with this illness die about fifteen to twenty years early, and mostly from heart disease rather than from psychiatry.","teach":["The classical rule of thirds is approximately supported: around a fifth to a third achieve good long-term outcome, a similar proportion have a severe deteriorating course, and the remainder fall between.","Outcome is heterogeneous, and pessimistic prediction at first presentation is not justified by the data."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["companion","nice","maudphys"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.4.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Anxiety disorders are the commonest psychiatric disorders there are, more common than depression, and they are the ones least likely to reach any treatment at all.","teach":["Anxiety disorders as a group have the highest twelve-month and lifetime prevalence of any psychiatric category, with lifetime prevalence estimates in the region of a quarter to a third.","Specific phobia is the commonest individual anxiety disorder, with lifetime prevalence around 10 per cent, followed by social anxiety disorder at around 10 to 12 per cent lifetime.","Generalised anxiety disorder has a twelve-month prevalence of around 3 per cent and lifetime around 5 to 6 per cent.","Panic disorder has a lifetime prevalence of around 2 to 4 per cent; agoraphobia around 1 to 2 per cent.","Female to male ratio is approximately two to one across most anxiety disorders, with social anxiety disorder showing a more equal ratio.","Age of onset is early. Specific phobia and separation anxiety begin in childhood, social anxiety in adolescence with a median around age thirteen, and generalised anxiety and panic disorder in the twenties and thirties.","Comorbidity is the rule. Most people with one anxiety disorder have another, and around half have comorbid depression at some point.","The treatment gap is large. Anxiety disorders have long delays to treatment, often more than a decade for social anxiety disorder, and a substantial proportion never present at all.","In primary care, anxiety commonly presents somatically with palpitations, chest pain, dizziness, gastrointestinal symptoms and headache, and is investigated physically for a long time before being recognised."],"cards":[["Which is the commonest psychiatric disorder category, and which the commonest individual anxiety disorder?","Anxiety disorders as a group have the highest lifetime prevalence of any psychiatric category, in the region of a quarter to a third. Specific phobia is the commonest individual anxiety disorder at around 10 per cent lifetime prevalence."],["Give the typical ages of onset across the anxiety disorders.","Specific phobia and separation anxiety in childhood; social anxiety disorder in adolescence with a median around age thirteen; generalised anxiety disorder and panic disorder in the twenties and thirties."]],"note":["Female to male ratio is around two to one, more equal in social anxiety disorder.","Generalised anxiety disorder has around 3 per cent twelve-month prevalence.","Delay to treatment in social anxiety disorder commonly exceeds a decade.","Somatic presentation in primary care leads to prolonged physical investigation."],"refs":["companion","nice"]};
TEACH["7.1.4.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Anxiety is a survival system that has become oversensitive. The alarm works, it is just going off at the wrong things, and the more you avoid whatever set it off, the more convinced the system becomes that it was right.","teach":["Heritability across the anxiety disorders is moderate, of the order of 30 to 50 per cent, with panic disorder at the higher end. Much of what is inherited is a general vulnerability rather than a disorder-specific one.","The shared vulnerability is best described as trait neuroticism or negative affectivity, which loads onto both anxiety and depression and largely explains their genetic correlation."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gross","companion","stahl"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.4.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Every anxiety disorder has the same physical symptoms. What tells them apart is what the person is frightened of, and what they do to avoid it.","teach":["The autonomic symptoms are common to all: palpitations, sweating, tremor, dry mouth, breathlessness, chest tightness, nausea, dizziness, paraesthesiae, hot flushes and chills.","Discrimination is by cognitive content and by behaviour. Ask what the person fears will happen, and what they do to stop it.","Generalised anxiety disorder: excessive worry about multiple everyday domains, difficult to control, present more days than not, with restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension and sleep disturbance. ICD-11 requires several months.","Panic disorder: recurrent unexpected panic attacks with a discrete surge of intense fear peaking within minutes, plus persistent concern about further attacks or a maladaptive change in behaviour. The fear is of the attack itself.","The distinguishing feature of panic disorder is fear of the bodily sensations and their catastrophic meaning: heart attack, suffocation, fainting, madness or loss of control.","Agoraphobia is fear of situations from which escape might be difficult or help unavailable, typically public transport, open spaces, enclosed spaces, queues and crowds, and being outside the home alone.","In ICD-11 agoraphobia is a diagnosis in its own right and no longer defined by its relationship to panic.","Social anxiety disorder: fear of scrutiny and of negative evaluation in social or performance situations, with fear of showing anxiety symptoms visibly. Blushing, tremor, sweating and fear of these being noticed are central.","Clark and Wells' model identifies the maintaining processes: shifting to self-focused attention, constructing a negative image of how one appears, safety behaviours, anticipatory anxiety and post-event processing.","Specific phobia: marked fear of a circumscribed object or situation, with immediate anxiety on exposure and avoidance. Blood-injection-injury phobia is distinctive in producing a biphasic vasovagal response with fainting rather than sustained tachycardia.","Applied tension is the specific treatment for blood-injection-injury phobia, because the mechanism is a drop in blood pressure rather than sympathetic arousal.","Separation anxiety disorder and selective mutism are both classified with the anxiety disorders in ICD-11 and DSM-5, and separation anxiety may be diagnosed in adults.","Health anxiety, hypochondriasis, sits with obsessive-compulsive and related disorders in ICD-11 rather than with the anxiety disorders, and is covered under 7.1.6.","Always exclude physical mimics: thyrotoxicosis, arrhythmia, phaeochromocytoma, hypoglycaemia, asthma, and stimulant or caffeine use. Also consider alcohol and benzodiazepine withdrawal, which present with prominent anxiety.","Rating scales: GAD-7 for generalised anxiety, the Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale, the Beck Anxiety Inventory, and the Liebowitz Social Anxiety Scale."],"cards":[["How are the anxiety disorders distinguished from one another?","By cognitive content and by behaviour rather than by autonomic symptoms, which are common to all. Ask what the person fears will happen and what they do to prevent it: fear of the panic attack itself in panic disorder, fear of negative evaluation in social anxiety, fear of inability to escape in agoraphobia, and diffuse worry across multiple domains in generalised anxiety disorder."],["What is distinctive about blood-injection-injury phobia?","It produces a biphasic vasovagal response with an initial rise then a fall in heart rate and blood pressure, leading to fainting rather than sustained tachycardia. Applied tension, which raises blood pressure, is therefore the specific treatment rather than relaxation."],["Summarise the Clark and Wells maintaining processes in social anxiety.","A shift to self-focused attention, construction of a negative observer-perspective image of how one appears, use of safety behaviours which prevent disconfirmation, anticipatory anxiety before events, and post-event processing which rehearses and consolidates the negative appraisal."],["Which physical conditions mimic anxiety disorders?","Thyrotoxicosis, cardiac arrhythmia, phaeochromocytoma, hypoglycaemia and asthma, together with stimulant and caffeine use and alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal."]],"note":["ICD-11 makes agoraphobia a diagnosis in its own right, independent of panic.","Separation anxiety disorder may be diagnosed in adults in both current systems.","Health anxiety sits with obsessive-compulsive and related disorders in ICD-11.","A panic attack peaks within minutes; the fear is of the attack itself.","GAD-7, HAM-A, BAI and the Liebowitz scale are the standard instruments."],"refs":["icd11","oyebode","nice"]};
TEACH["7.1.4.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"For all of these, the treatment is the same in principle: stop avoiding the thing. Medication makes that easier to bear, and in the disorders where avoidance is central, the medication alone rarely finishes the job.","teach":["CBT is first-line for every anxiety disorder in NICE guidance, with disorder-specific protocols rather than a generic package.","Exposure is the active ingredient wherever avoidance is present. It must be graded, prolonged, repeated and without safety behaviours."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","companion"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.4.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Untreated, these disorders tend to persist for years and shrink a person's life quietly rather than dramatically. Treated properly, most people improve substantially, which makes the low treatment rate the real problem.","teach":["Anxiety disorders tend to run a chronic fluctuating course when untreated, with symptoms waxing and waning around a persistently raised baseline.","Generalised anxiety disorder has a particularly chronic course, with low spontaneous remission rates and frequent relapse after remission."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["companion","nice"],"n":{"teach":12,"cards":2,"note":4}};
TEACH["7.1.5.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"OCD affects about two people in a hundred, which is far more than most clinicians see, because people hide it. The average person waits over ten years before telling anyone, usually because the thoughts feel shameful.","teach":["Lifetime prevalence is around 2 to 3 per cent; twelve-month prevalence around 1 to 1.5 per cent.","The sex ratio is approximately equal in adults. In childhood-onset OCD boys predominate.","Bimodal age of onset: an early peak around age ten to twelve, commoner in boys and more associated with tics, and a later peak in the early twenties.","Onset after age thirty-five is unusual and should prompt consideration of an organic cause or another diagnosis.","Delay to treatment is long, commonly more than a decade, driven by shame about the content of obsessions and by lack of recognition in primary care.","OCD was ranked by the World Health Organization among the leading causes of disability worldwide, which surprises people given how little service resource it receives.","Familial aggregation is clear: first-degree relatives have a substantially raised risk, higher for early-onset cases.","ICD-11 created a grouping of obsessive-compulsive and related disorders, containing OCD, body dysmorphic disorder, olfactory reference disorder, hypochondriasis, hoarding disorder, and body-focused repetitive behaviour disorders such as trichotillomania and excoriation disorder."],"cards":[["Give the prevalence, sex ratio and age of onset pattern in OCD.","Lifetime prevalence around 2 to 3 per cent, with an approximately equal adult sex ratio and male predominance in childhood-onset cases. Onset is bimodal, with an early peak around age ten to twelve associated with tics, and a later peak in the early twenties."],["Which disorders are grouped with OCD in ICD-11?","Body dysmorphic disorder, olfactory reference disorder, hypochondriasis, hoarding disorder, and the body-focused repetitive behaviour disorders including trichotillomania and excoriation disorder."]],"note":["Delay to treatment commonly exceeds a decade, driven by shame about obsessional content.","Onset after age thirty-five should prompt consideration of an organic cause.","OCD ranks among the leading global causes of disability.","First-degree relative risk is raised, particularly for early-onset cases."],"refs":["icd11","companion","nice"]};
TEACH["7.1.5.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Everybody has intrusive horrible thoughts. The difference in OCD is not the thought, it is what the person concludes about themselves for having had it, and what they then do to make sure it does not come true.","teach":["Heritability is around 40 to 50 per cent, higher in early-onset cases, where estimates reach 65 per cent.","OCD shows familial and genetic overlap with tic disorders, particularly Tourette syndrome, and early-onset tic-related OCD may be a distinct subtype."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["companion","stahl","nice"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.5.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"An obsession is a thought you cannot get rid of and do not want. A compulsion is what you do to make the thought go away. The relief lasts minutes, and then it comes back stronger, which is the trap.","teach":["Obsessions are recurrent, persistent, intrusive and unwanted thoughts, images or urges, experienced as the person's own, that cause marked anxiety or distress and are resisted.","The person recognises them as a product of their own mind. This distinguishes obsessions from thought insertion, which is the classic examination discrimination.","Compulsions are repetitive behaviours or mental acts the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or according to rigid rules, aimed at reducing distress or preventing a feared event, and not realistically connected or clearly excessive.","Common obsessional themes: contamination, harm to self or others, symmetry and order, forbidden sexual or religious thoughts, and doubt.","Common compulsions: washing and cleaning, checking, counting, ordering and arranging, repeating, and mental rituals such as praying, counting or reviewing.","Insight is variable, and both ICD-11 and DSM-5 provide an insight specifier including poor or absent insight, which is important because absent insight can be mistaken for delusion.","OCD with absent insight is still OCD. The distinguishing feature from psychosis is the presence of driven ritual and the ego-dystonic history.","Resistance is characteristic but not universal, and diminishes with chronicity, so its absence in longstanding illness does not exclude the diagnosis.","Avoidance is central and often overlooked. A person who never enters the kitchen has no visible compulsion but severe OCD.","Ego-dystonic and ego-syntonic: obsessions are ego-dystonic, felt as alien to the person's values. Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder features are ego-syntonic, felt as reasonable, and the two are distinct diagnoses that co-occur less often than the naming suggests.","Body dysmorphic disorder: preoccupation with a perceived defect in appearance not observable or slight to others, with repetitive checking, comparing, camouflaging or reassurance seeking, and a high rate of cosmetic procedure and of suicide.","Hoarding disorder: persistent difficulty discarding possessions regardless of value, due to a perceived need to save and distress at discarding, resulting in accumulation that compromises living space.","Trichotillomania and excoriation disorder are body-focused repetitive behaviours, characterised by pleasure or relief rather than by anxiety reduction from an obsession.","Assessment instrument: the Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale, Y-BOCS, with a symptom checklist and ten severity items scoring obsessions and compulsions separately on time, interference, distress, resistance and control."],"cards":[["How is an obsession distinguished from thought insertion?","An obsession is recognised by the person as a product of their own mind, however unwanted. Thought insertion involves the belief that the thought has been placed in the mind by an external agency and is not the person's own."],["Describe the Y-BOCS.","The Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale: a symptom checklist plus ten severity items rating obsessions and compulsions separately across time occupied, interference, distress, resistance and degree of control. It is the standard severity and outcome measure in OCD."],["Distinguish ego-dystonic from ego-syntonic, and OCD from OCPD.","Obsessions are ego-dystonic, experienced as alien to the person's own values and resisted. Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder traits are ego-syntonic, experienced as reasonable and consistent with the self. They are distinct diagnoses and co-occur less often than the shared name implies."],["What is body dysmorphic disorder?","Preoccupation with a perceived defect in physical appearance that is unobservable or slight to others, accompanied by repetitive behaviours such as mirror checking, comparing, camouflaging or reassurance seeking. It carries high rates of cosmetic procedure and of suicide."]],"note":["Both ICD-11 and DSM-5 include an insight specifier; absent insight does not exclude OCD.","Resistance diminishes with chronicity and its absence does not exclude the diagnosis.","Avoidance may replace visible compulsions entirely.","Hoarding disorder is defined by difficulty discarding, not by acquisition alone.","Trichotillomania and excoriation involve relief or pleasure rather than obsession-driven anxiety reduction."],"refs":["oyebode","icd11","nice"]};
TEACH["7.1.5.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There is one psychological treatment that works in OCD and it is uncomfortable by design: touch the thing, do not wash, and wait. The waiting is the treatment, because the anxiety comes down on its own and the person finds out that it does.","teach":["Exposure and response prevention is the specific psychological treatment: exposure to the trigger combined with prevention of the compulsive response, allowing habituation without the ritual.","ERP works because it disconfirms the belief that the feared outcome will occur without the ritual, and because anxiety habituates if the ritual is not performed."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","stahl"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["7.1.5.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Left alone, OCD tends to stay. Treated properly, most people improve a great deal, though a fair number keep some symptoms. The single biggest determinant of outcome is whether anyone did proper exposure work.","teach":["The untreated course is typically chronic with fluctuating severity. Complete spontaneous remission is uncommon, occurring in a minority.","Course patterns: chronic with fluctuation is commonest, episodic with clear remissions occurs in a minority, and a small proportion follow a deteriorating course."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["companion","nice"],"n":{"teach":11,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["7.1.6.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Around one person in fifty is convinced they have a serious illness that doctors keep failing to find. They are not pretending, and they are not reassured by a normal scan, which is the defining feature.","teach":["Prevalence estimates vary with definition. In the general population, clinically significant health anxiety affects around 1 to 5 per cent; in medical outpatient settings the figure is far higher, around 10 to 20 per cent.","The sex ratio is approximately equal, which distinguishes it from most anxiety disorders.","Onset is typically in early to middle adulthood, and often follows a personal illness, a bereavement, or a serious illness in someone close.","ICD-11 classifies hypochondriasis within obsessive-compulsive and related disorders, on the basis of the shared intrusive preoccupation, checking and reassurance seeking. This is a change from ICD-10, where it sat with somatoform disorders.","DSM-5 split the old hypochondriasis into two: somatic symptom disorder, where somatic symptoms are prominent, and illness anxiety disorder, where they are minimal or absent.","The DSM-5 split is examinable: around three-quarters of people previously diagnosed with hypochondriasis meet criteria for somatic symptom disorder, and around a quarter for illness anxiety disorder.","Health anxiety is dimensional, and severe hypochondriasis sits at one end of a continuum that includes ordinary and adaptive health concern.","Cyberchondria describes escalation of health anxiety through internet symptom searching, which functions as a checking behaviour and worsens the disorder.","Costs are substantial and largely borne by acute medical services through repeated investigation, and the group is high-utilising rather than treatment-avoidant."],"cards":[["Where does ICD-11 classify hypochondriasis, and why?","Within obsessive-compulsive and related disorders, on the basis of shared intrusive preoccupation, repetitive checking and reassurance seeking. This is a change from ICD-10, where it was classified with the somatoform disorders."],["How did DSM-5 divide the old category of hypochondriasis?","Into somatic symptom disorder, where distressing somatic symptoms are prominent, and illness anxiety disorder, where somatic symptoms are minimal or absent. Approximately three-quarters of previously diagnosed cases meet criteria for the former."]],"note":["Prevalence is around 1 to 5 per cent in the community and 10 to 20 per cent in medical outpatients.","The sex ratio is approximately equal, unlike most anxiety disorders.","Onset commonly follows personal illness, bereavement or serious illness in someone close.","Cyberchondria describes escalation through internet symptom searching."],"refs":["icd11","dsm5","semliaison"]};
TEACH["7.1.6.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Everybody's body produces odd sensations all day. Most people ignore them. In health anxiety the sensation gets noticed, interpreted as dangerous, and then checked, and the checking makes the sensation more noticeable.","teach":["The cognitive-behavioural model, from Warwick and Salkovskis, is the dominant account and maps closely onto the OCD model.","Dysfunctional assumptions about health, such as bodily changes always signify disease or a good doctor would find a cause, are acquired through early experience of illness in self or family."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semliaison","companion"],"n":{"teach":11,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.6.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The person is not worried about the symptom. They are worried about what the symptom means. Ask them what they think it is, and they will name a specific disease, usually cancer or a brain tumour.","teach":["ICD-11 hypochondriasis requires persistent preoccupation with, or fear of, having one or more serious progressive or life-threatening illnesses, with excessive health-related behaviours or maladaptive avoidance, persisting at least several months.","The preoccupation persists despite appropriate medical evaluation and reassurance. Non-response to reassurance is the diagnostic hallmark."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["icd11","oyebode","semliaison"],"n":{"teach":12,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.6.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The treatment is not more tests and it is not less contact. It is a single named doctor, appointments at fixed intervals whether or not there are symptoms, and psychological work on what the sensations are taken to mean.","teach":["CBT is the treatment of choice and has the best evidence, with a substantial effect size maintained at follow-up.","The CBT model is shared with the patient explicitly, reframing the problem from is there a disease to why is this worry so persistent and so hard to settle."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","semliaison","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.6.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Untreated it tends to persist for years and swallow enormous amounts of medical time. Treated, most people improve substantially, which makes the failure to offer treatment the expensive part.","teach":["Untreated, the course is typically chronic and fluctuating, with periods of intensification following illness in others, medical news, or new bodily sensations.","Around a third to a half remit substantially over several years without specific treatment, and the remainder run a persistent course."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semliaison","companion"],"n":{"teach":10,"cards":2,"note":4}};
TEACH["7.1.7.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"A large fraction of everyone attending a hospital clinic has symptoms that no disease explains. This is not rare and it is not a diagnosis of exclusion made once a year. It is a substantial part of medicine.","teach":["Medically unexplained symptoms account for a very large proportion of secondary care attendances, with figures around a third across medical outpatient clinics.","Rates by specialty are high everywhere: gynaecology, neurology, gastroenterology, cardiology and rheumatology all report figures in the region of a third or more.","Full somatisation disorder as defined in ICD-10, requiring multiple symptoms across systems over years, is uncommon, of the order of 0.1 to 1 per cent.","Subthreshold and abridged somatisation is far commoner, and is where the clinical burden lies.","Female to male ratio is around two to one for the full syndrome, and the disorder typically begins before age thirty.","ICD-11 replaced the somatoform disorders with bodily distress disorder, defined by distressing bodily symptoms with excessive attention directed to them, persisting for several months.","DSM-5 uses somatic symptom disorder, and made a crucial change: the symptoms no longer need to be medically unexplained. The diagnosis rests on disproportionate thoughts, feelings and behaviours about the symptoms.","That change was deliberate. Requiring symptoms to be unexplained made the diagnosis dependent on the limits of current medical knowledge and encouraged endless investigation.","Functional syndromes with high overlap: irritable bowel syndrome, fibromyalgia, chronic fatigue syndrome, non-cardiac chest pain, chronic pelvic pain and temporomandibular disorder. The same patients frequently meet criteria for several.","Health service costs are very high, driven by investigation, repeated referral and inpatient stay, and are several times those of matched controls."],"cards":[["How did DSM-5 change the approach to somatic symptoms, and why?","Somatic symptom disorder no longer requires symptoms to be medically unexplained. The diagnosis rests on disproportionate and persistent thoughts, feelings and behaviours relating to the symptoms. The change removed the dependence of the diagnosis on the current limits of medical knowledge and reduced the incentive for endless investigation."],["What is bodily distress disorder?","The ICD-11 replacement for the somatoform disorders: distressing bodily symptoms accompanied by excessive attention directed to them, persisting for several months and associated with significant impairment, graded mild, moderate or severe."]],"note":["Around a third of medical outpatient attendances involve medically unexplained symptoms.","Full ICD-10 somatisation disorder is uncommon at 0.1 to 1 per cent; subthreshold presentations are far commoner.","Female to male ratio is around two to one, with onset before age thirty.","Functional syndromes overlap heavily and the same patients often meet several sets of criteria."],"refs":["icd11","dsm5","semliaison"]};
TEACH["7.1.7.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Distress has to come out somewhere. In cultures and families where saying I am unhappy is not possible or not useful, it comes out as a body that hurts, and the body is not lying.","teach":["Predisposing factors: childhood illness experience, childhood adversity including abuse and neglect, parental illness behaviour, and family communication patterns in which physical complaint receives care and emotional complaint does not.","Childhood sexual and physical abuse is over-represented in severe somatisation, particularly in chronic pelvic pain and in functional gastrointestinal disorders."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semliaison","bhugra","companion"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.7.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The person has a long history, a thick file, several specialties involved, and no diagnosis that has stuck. That pattern is itself the diagnostic information, and it is visible before the first question is asked.","teach":["The classical picture: multiple symptoms across multiple organ systems, over years, beginning before age thirty, with a long history of consultation and investigation and no adequate physical explanation.","Symptom clusters classically span gastrointestinal, pain, sexual and pseudoneurological domains, and the multiplicity across systems is more informative than any single symptom."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["icd11","dsm5","semliaison"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.7.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The aim is not to cure the symptoms. The aim is to stop the damage the system is doing, keep the person functioning, and treat the depression underneath. Damage limitation is a legitimate and skilled clinical goal.","teach":["The primary intervention is management of the health system's behaviour: one named coordinating clinician, usually the general practitioner, with a clear plan shared across all involved services.","Scheduled appointments at fixed intervals rather than symptom-triggered contact, so that contact is not contingent on new symptoms."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semliaison","nice","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.7.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Some people recover completely, particularly if the problem is caught early. The ones who have been through years of investigation rarely do, which is an argument for getting this right the first time rather than the fifth.","teach":["Prognosis correlates strongly with duration. Recent-onset functional symptoms have good outcomes; symptoms established over years rarely resolve completely.","Around half of patients with functional symptoms in secondary care are improved at one year, with substantial variation by syndrome and by setting."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semliaison","companion"],"n":{"teach":11,"cards":2,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.8.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Dissociation is the mind disconnecting parts of experience that normally hang together. Almost everybody does it mildly. A small number of people do it severely, and this is the most contested territory in psychiatry.","teach":["Transient dissociative experience is common in the general population: absorption, highway hypnosis, and depersonalisation during stress or fatigue.","Depersonalisation-derealisation disorder has a lifetime prevalence of around 1 to 2 per cent, and is substantially under-recognised.","Dissociative amnesia is uncommon in the general population but is over-represented in the aftermath of trauma and in forensic settings.","Functional neurological symptom disorder, previously conversion disorder, is common: it accounts for a substantial proportion of new neurology outpatient attendances, with figures around 15 per cent, second only to headache in some series.","Non-epileptic attack disorder accounts for a large proportion of referrals to specialist epilepsy services, in the region of 20 to 30 per cent of admissions for refractory seizures.","Dissociative identity disorder prevalence is disputed. Estimates range from around 1 per cent in some community samples to figures that many clinicians regard as implausible, and the diagnosis is applied very unevenly between countries.","The North American and European diagnostic rates for dissociative identity disorder differ substantially, which is itself the strongest argument that diagnostic practice rather than epidemiology is driving the numbers.","Female predominance is marked in dissociative disorders overall, of the order of two to three to one, and is greatest in functional neurological presentations.","Onset is typically in adolescence or early adulthood, and childhood trauma is over-represented across the group.","ICD-11 groups dissociative disorders together, including dissociative neurological symptom disorder, dissociative amnesia, trance disorder, possession trance disorder, dissociative identity disorder, partial dissociative identity disorder and depersonalisation-derealisation disorder."],"cards":[["How common is functional neurological symptom disorder in neurology clinics?","It accounts for a substantial proportion of new neurology outpatient attendances, around 15 per cent, second only to headache in some series. Non-epileptic attack disorder accounts for roughly 20 to 30 per cent of admissions for apparently refractory seizures."],["Why is the prevalence of dissociative identity disorder contested?","Because diagnostic rates differ substantially between countries, notably between North America and Europe, which suggests that diagnostic practice rather than underlying epidemiology drives the numbers."]],"note":["Depersonalisation-derealisation disorder has a lifetime prevalence of around 1 to 2 per cent and is under-recognised.","Transient dissociation is common in the general population.","Female predominance is around two to three to one across the group.","ICD-11 includes trance and possession trance disorders within the group.","Childhood trauma is over-represented across the dissociative disorders."],"refs":["icd11","oyebode","semliaison"]};
TEACH["7.1.8.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There are two rival explanations and the argument between them is genuinely unresolved. One says these conditions are caused by trauma. The other says they are caused by suggestion, including by clinicians. The evidence does not settle it cleanly.","teach":["Janet introduced dissociation, proposing that overwhelming experience produces a splitting of consciousness into separate streams unavailable to each other.","Freud's account of conversion proposed that unacceptable impulses are repressed and converted into physical symptoms, with primary gain being reduction of intrapsychic conflict and secondary gain the external advantages."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oyebode","semliaison","companion"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.8.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The diagnosis is made on positive signs, not on the absence of disease. A functional weakness behaves differently from a neurological one, and the difference can be demonstrated at the bedside in a minute.","teach":["Depersonalisation is the experience of detachment from oneself, as if observing from outside, with feelings of unreality about the self. Derealisation is the same detachment applied to surroundings.","Both occur with intact reality testing. The person knows it feels as if, which distinguishes them from psychosis and is the key discrimination.","Dissociative amnesia is inability to recall important autobiographical information, usually of a traumatic or stressful nature, inconsistent with ordinary forgetting.","Dissociative fugue involves purposeful travel or wandering with amnesia for identity or for personal history. ICD-11 treats it as a specifier of dissociative amnesia rather than a separate disorder.","Dissociative identity disorder involves disruption of identity with two or more distinct personality states, with recurrent gaps in recall of everyday events, and marked discontinuity in sense of self and agency.","ICD-11 adds partial dissociative identity disorder, where non-dominant personality states do not recurrently take executive control.","Functional neurological symptom disorder presents with motor or sensory symptoms incompatible with recognised neurological disease, and requires positive clinical signs.","Hoover's sign: weakness of voluntary hip extension with normal involuntary hip extension when the opposite hip is flexed against resistance. It is the classic positive sign for functional leg weakness.","Other positive signs: the give-way or collapsing weakness pattern; drift without pronation in functional arm weakness; the tremor entrainment test, in which a functional tremor entrains to or stops during an externally paced rhythmic movement; and inconsistent findings between formal testing and spontaneous movement.","Non-epileptic attacks: gradual onset, prolonged duration often exceeding two minutes, closed eyes with active resistance to opening, side-to-side head or body movement, pelvic thrusting, waxing and waning course, ictal crying, and preserved awareness during bilateral motor activity.","Absence of postictal confusion, absence of tongue biting on the lateral tongue, and a normal serum prolactin at the appropriate interval support a non-epileptic attack, though none is definitive alone.","La belle indifférence, the apparent lack of concern about a dramatic deficit, is unreliable and should not be used diagnostically. It occurs in organic disease and is absent in most functional presentations.","Trance and possession trance disorders involve narrowing of awareness with stereotyped behaviours experienced as controlled by an external agent, and must be distinguished from culturally sanctioned religious practice, which is not a disorder.","The diagnosis should be given clearly and positively. Telling the person the scan is normal without giving a diagnosis is the commonest and most damaging clinical failure in this area.","Explain the mechanism: the hardware is intact and the software is not running the command correctly, which is a real and treatable problem."],"cards":[["Describe Hoover's sign.","Weakness of voluntary hip extension on the affected side, with normal involuntary hip extension detected when the opposite hip is flexed against resistance. It is the classic positive sign of functional leg weakness."],["Which features distinguish non-epileptic attacks from epileptic seizures?","Gradual onset, prolonged duration often exceeding two minutes, closed eyes with active resistance to opening, side-to-side head or body movement, pelvic thrusting, waxing and waning course, ictal crying, and preserved awareness during bilateral motor activity. Absence of postictal confusion and of lateral tongue biting supports the diagnosis, though no single feature is definitive."],["What distinguishes depersonalisation from psychosis?","Intact reality testing. The person experiences the detachment as if it were unreal while knowing that it is not, using as if language. In psychosis the conviction is held as fact."],["What is the status of la belle indifférence?","Unreliable and not to be used diagnostically. It occurs in organic neurological disease and is absent in the majority of functional presentations."],["What is the tremor entrainment test?","A functional tremor will entrain to the frequency of, or stop during, an externally paced rhythmic movement performed with the contralateral limb, whereas an organic tremor will not."]],"note":["ICD-11 treats fugue as a specifier of dissociative amnesia.","Partial dissociative identity disorder is new in ICD-11.","Diagnosis rests on positive signs, not on the absence of disease.","Culturally sanctioned trance practice is not a disorder.","Telling a person only that the scan is normal, without giving a diagnosis, is the commonest clinical failure here."],"refs":["oyebode","icd11","oxneuropsy"]};
TEACH["7.1.8.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"For functional neurological symptoms the most powerful treatment is the explanation. A person who understands what is wrong, believes it is real, and believes it can change, is already improving before therapy starts.","teach":["The explanation is the intervention, not the preamble to it. Diagnosis delivered clearly, positively and with a mechanism predicts outcome in functional neurological disorder.","Demonstrate the positive sign to the person. Showing them Hoover's sign, and explaining that it proves the pathway works, is more convincing than any amount of assertion."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","oxneuropsy","semliaison"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.8.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Outcome is much worse than the textbooks used to say. The old teaching that conversion symptoms clear up on their own came from short follow-up and wishful thinking.","teach":["The classical teaching that acute conversion symptoms remit spontaneously applies mainly to symptoms of very short duration with an obvious precipitant.","Long-term follow-up of functional neurological disorder shows that the majority remain symptomatic. Studies with follow-up of a decade or more find persistent symptoms in a substantial majority."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxneuropsy","semliaison","companion"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.9.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Around one adult in ten meets criteria for a personality disorder, and in psychiatric services the figure is far higher. In prisons and in secure hospitals it is the majority.","teach":["Community prevalence of any personality disorder is around 10 per cent, though estimates vary considerably with instrument and threshold.","Prevalence in primary care attenders is around 20 to 30 per cent; in psychiatric outpatients around 40 to 50 per cent; in psychiatric inpatients higher still.","In prison populations, structured assessment finds personality disorder in the majority, with antisocial personality disorder predominating in men.","Borderline personality disorder has a community prevalence of around 1 to 2 per cent, and accounts for a large share of clinical contact, particularly in emergency and crisis settings.","Antisocial personality disorder has a community prevalence of around 1 to 3 per cent, with a marked male predominance of roughly three to one.","The apparent female predominance in borderline personality disorder in clinical samples, roughly three to one, is not found to the same degree in community samples, which suggests a referral and diagnostic bias.","That discrepancy matters clinically. Women presenting with emotional dysregulation are more likely to be given the diagnosis; men with the same presentation are more likely to be labelled antisocial or to be managed in the criminal justice system.","Personality disorder is associated with substantially reduced life expectancy, of the order of one to two decades, driven by suicide, accidents, substance misuse and physical illness.","Comorbidity with axis I disorders is very high and is the usual reason for presentation. Personality disorder worsens the outcome of every comorbid disorder studied.","Diagnosis is under-recorded in UK services, partly because of the stigma attached and partly because clinicians are reluctant to give a diagnosis they believe carries no treatment."],"cards":[["Give the prevalence of personality disorder across settings.","Around 10 per cent in the community, 20 to 30 per cent in primary care attenders, 40 to 50 per cent in psychiatric outpatients, higher in inpatients, and the majority in prison populations on structured assessment."],["Why is the sex ratio in borderline personality disorder disputed?","Because the marked female predominance of around three to one seen in clinical samples is not found to the same degree in community samples, indicating referral and diagnostic bias. Women presenting with emotional dysregulation are more likely to receive the diagnosis, while men with similar presentations are more often labelled antisocial or managed within the criminal justice system."]],"note":["Borderline personality disorder has a community prevalence of around 1 to 2 per cent.","Antisocial personality disorder is around 1 to 3 per cent with a male predominance of about three to one.","Life expectancy is reduced by one to two decades.","Personality disorder worsens the outcome of every comorbid disorder studied.","The diagnosis is under-recorded in UK services because of stigma and therapeutic pessimism."],"refs":["tyrer11","companion","nice"]};
TEACH["7.1.9.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Personality is roughly half inherited and half made, and the made half is mostly about what happened when the person was small and frightened. Nothing about that means it cannot change.","teach":["Heritability of personality traits is around 40 to 50 per cent, and of personality disorder categories similar, with the balance attributable to non-shared rather than shared environment.","The non-shared environment finding is important: what makes siblings different from each other matters more than what they have in common."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["tyrer11","gabbard","gross"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.9.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Personality disorder is not about having difficult traits. Everyone has those. It is about a persistent pattern, present since young adulthood, across situations, that damages the person's relationships and their sense of who they are.","teach":["The general criteria matter more than the categories: an enduring pattern of inner experience and behaviour deviating markedly from cultural expectation, pervasive and inflexible across situations, stable and of long duration with onset in adolescence or early adulthood, and leading to distress or impairment.","ICD-11 abolished the categorical subtypes entirely. This is the single most examinable change in personality disorder classification.","The ICD-11 system has three steps: first determine whether personality disorder is present at all; then grade severity as mild, moderate or severe; then optionally specify trait domains.","Severity is graded on the degree of disturbance in self functioning, being identity, self-worth, accuracy of self-view and self-direction, and in interpersonal functioning, being the ability to develop and maintain close and mutually satisfying relationships.","The five ICD-11 trait domain qualifiers: negative affectivity, detachment, dissociality, disinhibition and anankastia. A borderline pattern qualifier was retained separately, largely for pragmatic reasons of continuity of care and research.","The retention of the borderline pattern qualifier was a compromise, criticised by those who wanted full dimensionality and welcomed by those who wanted continuity with the treatment evidence base.","DSM-5 kept the ten categorical diagnoses in the main text and placed an alternative dimensional model in Section III, so both systems have moved dimensionally but at different speeds.","The DSM-5 clusters remain examinable: Cluster A odd or eccentric, comprising paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal; Cluster B dramatic, emotional or erratic, comprising antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic; Cluster C anxious or fearful, comprising avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive.","Borderline personality disorder features: frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, unstable intense relationships alternating between idealisation and devaluation, identity disturbance, impulsivity, recurrent self-harm or suicidal behaviour, affective instability, chronic emptiness, inappropriate intense anger, and transient stress-related paranoid ideation or dissociation.","Transient psychotic and dissociative phenomena under stress are part of the disorder and do not indicate a psychotic illness.","Antisocial personality disorder features: disregard for and violation of the rights of others, deceitfulness, impulsivity, irritability and aggression, reckless disregard for safety, consistent irresponsibility, and lack of remorse, with evidence of conduct disorder before age fifteen.","Psychopathy is not the same as antisocial personality disorder. The PCL-R construct includes the affective and interpersonal features of callousness, superficial charm, grandiosity and lack of empathy, which the DSM antisocial criteria largely omit.","Schizotypal personality disorder is classified with the schizophrenia spectrum in ICD-11 rather than with personality disorders, reflecting its genetic relationship to schizophrenia.","Assessment requires longitudinal information and collateral history. A single crisis presentation is not a basis for the diagnosis, and diagnosing during an acute episode of another disorder is a recognised error.","Assessment instruments: SCID-5-PD, IPDE, and for screening the Standardised Assessment of Personality Abbreviated Scale, SAPAS, which is eight items."],"cards":[["Describe the ICD-11 approach to personality disorder.","Three steps. First, determine whether personality disorder is present, using general criteria of disturbance in self and interpersonal functioning persisting over at least two years. Second, grade severity as mild, moderate or severe based on the degree of that disturbance. Third, optionally specify trait domains. The categorical subtypes were abolished, with a borderline pattern qualifier retained as a pragmatic compromise."],["Name the five ICD-11 trait domain qualifiers.","Negative affectivity, detachment, dissociality, disinhibition and anankastia."],["Give the DSM-5 clusters and their members.","Cluster A, odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal. Cluster B, dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic. Cluster C, anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive."],["How does psychopathy differ from antisocial personality disorder?","Psychopathy, as measured by the PCL-R, includes affective and interpersonal features of callousness, superficial charm, grandiosity, shallow affect and lack of empathy, which the DSM antisocial criteria largely omit in favour of behavioural indicators. Most people with psychopathy meet antisocial criteria; most people meeting antisocial criteria are not psychopathic."],["Where is schizotypal personality disorder classified in ICD-11?","Within the schizophrenia and other primary psychotic disorders grouping rather than with the personality disorders, reflecting its genetic and phenomenological relationship to schizophrenia."]],"note":["Severity in ICD-11 is graded on disturbance of self functioning and of interpersonal functioning.","DSM-5 retained the ten categories in the main text with a dimensional alternative in Section III.","Transient stress-related paranoia and dissociation are part of borderline personality disorder.","Diagnosis requires longitudinal information; diagnosing during an acute episode of another disorder is an error.","SAPAS is an eight-item screening instrument."],"refs":["icd11","dsm5","tyrer11"]};
TEACH["7.1.9.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"For a long time the answer was that nothing worked and these patients were untreatable. That was wrong. Structured psychological therapy works, and the drugs, which were the main thing offered, largely do not.","teach":["The single most important principle: personality disorder is treatable, and therapeutic nihilism causes measurable harm through exclusion from services.","Structured psychological therapy is the treatment. Several models have randomised evidence, and the common factors across them may matter as much as the specific techniques."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","gabbard","tyrer11"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.9.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The most surprising finding in this field is that borderline personality disorder gets better. Most people no longer meet criteria after ten years. What does not recover as reliably is the ability to work and to hold relationships.","teach":["The old assumption of lifelong stability was wrong. Longitudinal studies transformed the picture.","The McLean Study of Adult Development, Zanarini, followed patients for up to two decades. Around 85 per cent achieved symptomatic remission of two years or more, and remission was largely sustained, with low rates of subsequent relapse."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["tyrer11","companion","nice"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.10.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Psychosis caused by physical disease is far commoner than psychiatrists assume, because most of it is seen and treated on medical wards and never reaches a psychiatric service.","teach":["Delirium is the commonest organic psychosis by a wide margin. It affects around 20 per cent of general medical inpatients, over half of hip fracture patients, and the majority of ventilated intensive care patients.","Delirium prevalence rises sharply with age and with pre-existing cognitive impairment, and dementia is the strongest single risk factor.","Delirium is missed in a large proportion of cases, particularly the hypoactive form, which is the commonest presentation in the elderly and carries the worse prognosis.","Psychosis in dementia: psychotic symptoms occur in a large minority across dementias, in around a third of Alzheimer's disease, and in the great majority of dementia with Lewy bodies.","Substance-induced psychosis is common, particularly with stimulants, cannabis, synthetic cannabinoids and alcohol, and accounts for a substantial share of first psychotic presentations in young people.","Psychosis in epilepsy: postictal psychosis affects a small percentage of people with epilepsy, and interictal psychosis a similar proportion, with temporal lobe epilepsy over-represented.","Autoimmune encephalitis, particularly anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis, is rare but is increasingly recognised and is a treatable cause of new psychosis in young people.","Steroid-induced psychiatric disturbance is dose-related and common, occurring in a substantial minority at high doses.","Endocrine causes: thyroid disease, Cushing's syndrome, hyperparathyroidism and hypoglycaemia.","The single most useful epidemiological fact is that first-onset psychosis over the age of forty should raise the index of suspicion for an organic cause substantially."],"cards":[["How common is delirium in the general hospital?","Around 20 per cent of general medical inpatients, over half of patients admitted with hip fracture, and the majority of ventilated intensive care patients. It is missed in a large proportion of cases, particularly the hypoactive form."],["Which age at onset should raise suspicion of an organic psychosis?","First onset over the age of forty, which substantially raises the probability of an organic cause and warrants thorough physical assessment and investigation."]],"note":["Dementia is the strongest single risk factor for delirium.","Psychotic symptoms occur in around a third of Alzheimer's disease and the great majority of dementia with Lewy bodies.","Substance-induced psychosis accounts for a substantial share of first presentations in young people.","Temporal lobe epilepsy is over-represented in epilepsy-related psychosis.","Steroid-induced psychiatric disturbance is dose-related."],"refs":["lishman","semliaison","oxneuropsy"]};
TEACH["7.1.10.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There is a long list of physical causes of psychosis and remembering it as a list is useless in an emergency. Remember it as a sequence: what is in the blood, what is in the brain, what is on the drug chart.","teach":["A workable structure: metabolic and endocrine; infective and inflammatory; structural and neurological; drug-related and toxic; and nutritional.","Metabolic: hyponatraemia, hypercalcaemia, hypoglycaemia, hepatic and renal failure, hypoxia and hypercapnia."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lishman","oxneuropsy","semliaison"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.10.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Organic psychosis looks different from schizophrenia in ways you can see at the bedside. Consciousness fluctuates, the hallucinations are visual, the person is disorientated, and it started this week rather than over months.","teach":["The cardinal discriminator is disturbance of consciousness. Clouded, fluctuating or impaired consciousness indicates an organic cause; primary psychosis occurs in clear consciousness.","Delirium: acute onset over hours to days, fluctuating course typically worse at night, inattention, disorganised thinking, and altered level of consciousness ranging from hyperalert to drowsy.","Inattention is the core feature of delirium and is what should be tested. Serial sevens, months of the year backwards, or digit span are adequate bedside tests.","Three motor subtypes of delirium: hyperactive, hypoactive and mixed. Hypoactive delirium is the commonest in the elderly, is the most often missed, and carries the worst prognosis.","Hallucinations in delirium are typically visual, vivid and often frightening, frequently of animals or people, and may be Lilliputian, meaning miniature figures.","Delusions in organic states are typically poorly systematised, fleeting, persecutory and changeable, in contrast with the elaborated stable delusions of schizophrenia.","Other organic pointers: disorientation in time and place, fluctuating cognition, focal neurological signs, abnormal vital signs, first onset over forty, atypical or mixed symptoms, and absence of family history.","Peduncular hallucinosis is vivid, often pleasant visual hallucination with retained insight, associated with midbrain and thalamic lesions.","Charles Bonnet syndrome is complex visual hallucination in people with visual impairment, occurring with intact cognition and full insight, and is not a psychosis.","Alcoholic hallucinosis occurs in clear consciousness, is predominantly auditory, and follows heavy drinking, distinguishing it from delirium tremens.","Dementia with Lewy bodies: recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations, fluctuating cognition and attention, spontaneous parkinsonism, and REM sleep behaviour disorder, with severe neuroleptic sensitivity.","Postictal psychosis characteristically follows a cluster of seizures after a lucid interval of hours to days, with prominent affective and religious content, and is self-limiting.","Investigation of new psychosis: full blood count, urea and electrolytes, calcium, liver function, thyroid function, glucose, B12 and folate, inflammatory markers, urine drug screen, syphilis and HIV serology where indicated, and neuroimaging in first episode with atypical features.","EEG is useful where delirium, encephalitis or non-convulsive status is suspected. Diffuse slowing supports an organic cause, and a normal EEG in a floridly disturbed patient argues against delirium.","Lumbar puncture and autoantibody testing where encephalitis is suspected. Do not delay treatment of suspected herpes encephalitis while awaiting results."],"cards":[["What is the cardinal discriminator between organic and primary psychosis?","Disturbance of consciousness. Clouded, fluctuating or impaired consciousness indicates an organic cause, whereas primary psychosis occurs in clear consciousness."],["Name the three motor subtypes of delirium and state which matters most.","Hyperactive, hypoactive and mixed. Hypoactive delirium is the commonest in older people, is the most frequently missed because the patient is quiet, and carries the worst prognosis."],["How do delusions in organic states differ from those in schizophrenia?","Organic delusions are typically poorly systematised, fleeting, changeable and predominantly persecutory, in contrast with the more elaborated and stable delusional systems of schizophrenia."],["Distinguish Charles Bonnet syndrome, peduncular hallucinosis and alcoholic hallucinosis.","Charles Bonnet syndrome is complex visual hallucination in visual impairment, with intact cognition and full insight. Peduncular hallucinosis is vivid, often pleasant visual hallucination with retained insight, associated with midbrain and thalamic lesions. Alcoholic hallucinosis is predominantly auditory, occurs in clear consciousness after heavy drinking, and is distinct from delirium tremens."],["What investigations are indicated in new-onset psychosis?","Full blood count, urea and electrolytes, calcium, liver and thyroid function, glucose, B12 and folate, inflammatory markers, urine drug screen, and syphilis and HIV serology where indicated, with neuroimaging in first episode with atypical features and EEG where delirium, encephalitis or non-convulsive status is suspected."]],"note":["Inattention is the core feature of delirium and must be tested directly.","Lilliputian hallucinations of miniature figures occur in delirium.","Dementia with Lewy bodies features severe neuroleptic sensitivity.","Postictal psychosis follows a seizure cluster after a lucid interval and is self-limiting.","Do not delay treatment of suspected herpes encephalitis while awaiting investigation results."],"refs":["lishman","oyebode","oxneuropsy"]};
TEACH["7.1.10.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Treat the cause. That sounds obvious and it is constantly not done, because it is quicker to prescribe an antipsychotic for the agitation than to find out why an eighty-year-old suddenly cannot pay attention.","teach":["The primary treatment of any organic psychosis is treatment of the underlying cause. Symptomatic management is supportive and buys time; it is not the treatment.","Delirium: identify and treat the precipitants, which are usually multiple. Infection, dehydration, constipation, urinary retention, pain, hypoxia, electrolyte disturbance, and medication."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.10.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Delirium was long regarded as a temporary nuisance that resolves when the infection clears. It does not. People who have had delirium are more likely to die, more likely to end up in a care home, and more likely to develop dementia.","teach":["Delirium is an independent predictor of mortality. In-hospital mortality is substantially raised, and the excess persists after discharge and after adjustment for illness severity and comorbidity.","Delirium predicts institutionalisation. A large proportion of older people who develop delirium in hospital do not return to their previous accommodation."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","semliaison","lishman"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.11.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"This line is the College's catch-all. It covers the disorders that do not fit the other headings: reactions to stress, sleep disorders, impulse control problems and the psychiatric disorders of the elderly that general adult services still see.","teach":["Post-traumatic stress disorder: lifetime prevalence around 4 to 8 per cent in Western populations, with twelve-month prevalence around 3 per cent. Rates are far higher in populations exposed to conflict, torture and displacement.","Most people exposed to trauma do not develop PTSD. Conditional risk varies markedly by trauma type: highest after rape and torture, lowest after accidents and natural disasters."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["icd11","nice","companion"],"n":{"teach":11,"cards":2,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.11.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Some disorders are defined by what happened to the person rather than by what is wrong with them. Trauma-related disorders are the clearest case, and they are the only diagnoses in psychiatry that name a cause in the definition.","teach":["PTSD is unusual in requiring a specified aetiological event, which makes it the only major diagnosis with causation written into the criteria.","Risk factors divide into pretrauma, peritrauma and posttrauma. Peritraumatic and posttraumatic factors are the stronger predictors, which is clinically useful."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["companion","stahl","icd11"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.11.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The trauma disorders are identified by one thing: the past is not experienced as the past. The memory arrives with the sensory quality and the fear of the original event, as though it were happening now.","teach":["ICD-11 PTSD requires three clusters: re-experiencing the event in the here and now, in the form of vivid intrusive memories, flashbacks or nightmares accompanied by fear or horror; deliberate avoidance of reminders; and a persistent sense of current threat with hypervigilance or exaggerated startle.","The ICD-11 formulation is deliberately narrower than DSM-5, which retains four clusters including negative alterations in cognitions and mood."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["icd11","dsm5","oyebode"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.11.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"For trauma the treatment is to go back over what happened, in a structured way, with someone who knows what they are doing. For insomnia the treatment is to spend less time in bed, which sounds wrong and works better than any sleeping tablet.","teach":["PTSD, NICE NG116: trauma-focused CBT or EMDR are the first-line psychological treatments. Both have substantial evidence and neither is clearly superior.","Trauma-focused CBT includes imaginal reliving, in vivo exposure to avoided situations, cognitive restructuring of appraisals, and updating of the trauma memory with information the person did not have at the time."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","companion"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.1.11.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Most people recover from trauma without treatment. The ones who do not, and are not treated, are still symptomatic decades later, which makes the failure to offer treatment expensive in every sense.","teach":["PTSD: the majority of people who develop symptoms after trauma recover within the first year without specific treatment. Symptoms persisting beyond a year are much less likely to remit spontaneously.","Around a third of people with PTSD develop a chronic course lasting many years, and cases lasting decades after the original event are well documented."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","companion"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.5.1.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Anorexia nervosa is not the commonest eating disorder, but it is the one that kills. It has the highest mortality of any psychiatric disorder, and that single fact should govern how urgently it is treated.","teach":["Lifetime prevalence is around 1 to 4 per cent in women and around 0.3 per cent in men, with higher figures in more recent studies using broader DSM-5 criteria.","Point prevalence in young women is around 0.3 to 1 per cent.","Female to male ratio is around ten to one in clinical samples, though community studies suggest the true ratio is closer to three or four to one and that men are under-diagnosed.","Peak age of onset is in adolescence, between about fifteen and nineteen, and onset after thirty is unusual and should prompt consideration of another cause.","Incidence rose through the twentieth century and has been broadly stable in recent decades in adults, with evidence of rising incidence in younger adolescents.","There was a marked rise in eating disorder presentations in adolescents during and after the COVID-19 pandemic, reported across multiple countries.","Higher-risk groups: ballet dancers, models, athletes in weight-dependent and aesthetic sports, and people with type 1 diabetes.","Diabulimia, the deliberate omission of insulin for weight control in type 1 diabetes, is common, dangerous and specifically under-recognised.","Historically anorexia nervosa was described as a disorder of affluent Western societies, and that picture has not held: it occurs across social classes and in non-Western countries, though the fat phobia presentation varies culturally.","Anorexia nervosa has the highest standardised mortality ratio of any psychiatric disorder, with figures commonly cited around five to six times expected, and around a fifth of deaths are by suicide."],"cards":[["Give the prevalence, sex ratio and age of onset of anorexia nervosa.","Lifetime prevalence around 1 to 4 per cent in women and 0.3 per cent in men. The clinical sex ratio is around ten to one female to male, though community data suggest a true ratio nearer three or four to one with men under-diagnosed. Peak onset is in adolescence between fifteen and nineteen."],["What is diabulimia and why does it matter?","The deliberate omission or reduction of insulin for weight control in type 1 diabetes. It is common, specifically under-recognised, and dangerous, producing rapid deterioration in glycaemic control, ketoacidosis and accelerated microvascular complications."]],"note":["Anorexia nervosa has the highest standardised mortality ratio of any psychiatric disorder.","Around a fifth of deaths in anorexia nervosa are by suicide.","Higher-risk groups include dancers, models, weight-dependent athletes and people with type 1 diabetes.","Presentations rose markedly in adolescents during and after the COVID-19 pandemic.","Onset after age thirty is unusual and should prompt consideration of another cause."],"refs":["nice","companion"]};
TEACH["7.5.1.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Nobody develops anorexia because of a magazine. Thinness ideals are everywhere and almost nobody develops the illness, which means the interesting question is what makes a small number of people vulnerable to something everyone is exposed to.","teach":["Heritability is substantial, in the region of 50 to 60 per cent, considerably higher than was assumed when this was regarded as a purely sociocultural disorder.","Genome-wide association work has reframed the illness. The 2019 study by Watson and colleagues identified eight loci and found genetic correlations both with psychiatric traits, particularly OCD, anxiety and depression, and with metabolic and anthropometric traits including insulin-glucose metabolism and body mass index."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","companion","semgen"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.5.1.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The person is starving and does not experience themselves as starving. That gap between what the body is doing and what the person perceives is the illness, and it is why simply pointing out the weight achieves nothing.","teach":["ICD-11 requires significantly low body weight for the person's height, age and developmental stage, not due to another condition or unavailability of food; a persistent pattern of behaviour to prevent weight restoration; and either excessive preoccupation with weight or shape, or, in some presentations, no such preoccupation.","ICD-11 uses a BMI threshold of under 18.5 in adults, or under the fifth percentile in children and adolescents, with a separate category of anorexia nervosa with dangerously low body weight below BMI 14.","ICD-11 and DSM-5 both allow diagnosis without fat phobia, recognising the non-fat-phobic presentation described particularly in Chinese and other non-Western populations.","DSM-5 subtypes: restricting type and binge-eating or purging type. DSM-5 also removed the amenorrhoea criterion, since it excluded men, prepubertal girls and women on hormonal contraception.","Physical signs of starvation: bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, lanugo hair, dry skin, hair loss, acrocyanosis, peripheral oedema and delayed capillary refill.","Signs of purging: Russell's sign, being calluses on the dorsum of the hand from induced vomiting; dental enamel erosion particularly of the lingual surfaces; parotid enlargement; and subconjunctival haemorrhage.","Biochemical: hypokalaemia particularly with purging, hyponatraemia, hypophosphataemia, hypoglycaemia, hypomagnesaemia, raised urea if dehydrated, deranged liver function, and low white cell count.","Endocrine: low luteinising and follicle stimulating hormone with low oestradiol producing amenorrhoea; low T3 with sick euthyroid pattern; raised cortisol; and raised growth hormone with low IGF-1.","Cardiac: bradycardia, prolonged QT, arrhythmia, reduced cardiac muscle mass and mitral valve prolapse. Sudden cardiac death is a principal mechanism of mortality.","Bone: osteopenia and osteoporosis develop early and are not fully reversible, making duration of amenorrhoea a key clinical variable.","Cognition: concentration and flexibility are impaired by starvation, which limits psychological therapy at very low weight.","Assessment tools: SCOFF is the five-item screen; the Eating Disorder Examination is the reference standard interview; and the MEED guidance, Medical Emergencies in Eating Disorders, replaced MARSIPAN in 2022 as the UK risk assessment framework.","The SUSS test, sit up squat stand, assesses proximal myopathy and is part of physical risk assessment.","Weight and BMI alone are inadequate for risk assessment. Rate of weight loss, cardiovascular parameters, biochemistry, muscle power and engagement all contribute, and a person can be at high risk at a BMI that appears acceptable."],"cards":[["What are the ICD-11 requirements for anorexia nervosa?","Significantly low body weight for height, age and developmental stage, not due to another condition or to food unavailability, with BMI under 18.5 in adults or under the fifth percentile in children; a persistent pattern of behaviours to prevent weight restoration; and excessive preoccupation with weight or shape, though a non-fat-phobic presentation is recognised. Anorexia nervosa with dangerously low body weight is specified below BMI 14."],["Name the physical signs of starvation and of purging.","Starvation: bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, lanugo hair, dry skin, hair loss, acrocyanosis and peripheral oedema. Purging: Russell's sign of calluses on the dorsum of the hand, dental enamel erosion particularly lingual, parotid enlargement and subconjunctival haemorrhage."],["What is MEED and what did it replace?","Medical Emergencies in Eating Disorders, the 2022 Royal College of Psychiatrists guidance which replaced MARSIPAN as the UK framework for assessing and managing physical risk in eating disorders."],["Why is BMI alone inadequate for risk assessment?","Because risk depends on the rate of weight loss, cardiovascular parameters including heart rate, blood pressure and postural drop, biochemistry particularly potassium and phosphate, muscle power measured by the sit up squat stand test, and engagement. A person can be at high physical risk at a BMI that appears acceptable."],["Which endocrine changes occur in anorexia nervosa?","Low LH and FSH with low oestradiol producing amenorrhoea; low T3 in a sick euthyroid pattern; raised cortisol; and raised growth hormone with low IGF-1, reflecting peripheral growth hormone resistance."]],"note":["DSM-5 removed the amenorrhoea criterion and retains restricting and binge-purge subtypes.","Both systems allow diagnosis without fat phobia.","Osteopenia develops early and is not fully reversible; duration of amenorrhoea is the key variable.","SCOFF is the five-item screen; the Eating Disorder Examination is the reference standard.","Sudden cardiac death is a principal mechanism of mortality."],"refs":["icd11","nice","maudphys"]};
TEACH["7.5.1.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"You cannot do therapy with a starving brain. Weight has to come up before anything psychological can take hold, and bringing it up too fast can kill the person, which is the central tension in treating this illness.","teach":["NICE NG69 first-line psychological treatments in adults: individual eating-disorder-focused CBT known as CBT-ED, the Maudsley Anorexia Nervosa Treatment for Adults known as MANTRA, and Specialist Supportive Clinical Management.","In children and young people, anorexia-nervosa-focused family therapy, FT-AN, is first-line and has the strongest evidence in this age group."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudphys","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.5.1.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Roughly half recover fully, a quarter improve, and a quarter stay ill for life. The mortality is the highest in psychiatry, and it comes from two directions: the body giving out, and suicide.","teach":["The broad picture at long-term follow-up: around 45 to 50 per cent achieve full recovery, around 30 per cent improve partially, and around 20 per cent run a chronic course.","Recovery is slow. Median time to recovery is measured in years, and studies with follow-up under five years systematically understate eventual recovery rates."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","companion","maudphys"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.5.2.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Bulimia is commoner than anorexia and far less visible, because most people with it are a normal weight. That is precisely why it goes undetected for years.","teach":["Lifetime prevalence is around 1 to 2 per cent in women and around 0.5 per cent in men.","Bulimia nervosa is more common than anorexia nervosa, and binge eating disorder is more common than both, with lifetime prevalence around 2 to 3 per cent.","Female to male ratio in clinical samples is around ten to one, with community data again suggesting substantial under-diagnosis in men.","Peak age of onset is later than anorexia nervosa, typically in the late teens to early twenties.","Most people with bulimia nervosa are of normal weight, which is the principal reason for delayed detection, and average duration before presentation is several years.","Incidence rose sharply from the 1970s, when the disorder was first described by Russell in 1979, peaked in the 1990s, and has declined somewhat since in several countries.","Russell's original 1979 paper described bulimia nervosa as an ominous variant of anorexia nervosa, and the eponym Russell's sign comes from the same work.","Comorbidity is very high: depression, anxiety disorders, substance misuse and personality disorder, with substance misuse notably more common than in anorexia nervosa.","Childhood sexual abuse and other adversity are over-represented, more so than in anorexia nervosa, though they remain non-specific risk factors.","Mortality is raised but substantially less than in anorexia nervosa, with standardised mortality ratios around twice expected."],"cards":[["Compare the prevalence of the eating disorders.","Binge eating disorder is the most common at around 2 to 3 per cent lifetime, followed by bulimia nervosa at around 1 to 2 per cent in women, with anorexia nervosa the least common. Bulimia nervosa has a later peak age of onset than anorexia nervosa, in the late teens to early twenties."],["Why is bulimia nervosa detected late?","Because most people with the disorder are of normal weight, so there is no visible sign, and the behaviours are concealed through shame. Average duration before presentation is several years."]],"note":["Russell first described bulimia nervosa in 1979 as an ominous variant of anorexia nervosa.","Incidence rose from the 1970s, peaked in the 1990s, and has declined somewhat since.","Substance misuse comorbidity is notably higher than in anorexia nervosa.","Standardised mortality is around twice expected, substantially lower than in anorexia nervosa.","Community data suggest substantial under-diagnosis in men."],"refs":["nice","companion"]};
TEACH["7.5.2.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The engine of bulimia is dieting. Restriction creates the physiological and psychological conditions for a binge, the binge produces shame, the shame produces more restriction, and the cycle powers itself.","teach":["Heritability is around 50 to 60 per cent, similar to anorexia nervosa, with shared genetic vulnerability across the eating disorders.","Dietary restraint is the central maintaining mechanism. Restriction, both caloric and the imposition of rigid rules, creates physiological hunger and cognitive preoccupation that make binge eating far more likely."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","companion","stahl"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.5.2.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"A binge is not simply eating too much. It is eating an unmistakably large amount in a short time, alone, with a sense of being unable to stop, followed by shame and by something done to undo it.","teach":["ICD-11 bulimia nervosa requires frequent recurrent episodes of binge eating, recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviours to prevent weight gain, excessive preoccupation with body weight or shape, and marked distress or impairment, over at least one month.","A binge is defined by two elements: eating an amount definitely larger than most people would eat in a similar period and circumstance, and a sense of loss of control over the eating.","Subjective binges, where the person feels out of control but the amount is not objectively large, are common and are clinically important but do not meet the strict definition.","Compensatory behaviours: self-induced vomiting, laxative misuse, diuretic misuse, fasting, excessive exercise, and omission or reduction of insulin in type 1 diabetes.","DSM-5 requires the binges and compensatory behaviours to occur at least once weekly for three months, having reduced the threshold from twice weekly in DSM-IV.","The distinguishing feature from anorexia nervosa binge-purge subtype is body weight. If the person is significantly underweight, the diagnosis is anorexia nervosa regardless of the binge-purge behaviour.","Binge eating disorder involves binge eating without regular compensatory behaviour, with marked distress, and is associated with obesity though it is not defined by it.","Physical signs: Russell's sign on the dorsum of the hand, dental enamel erosion of the lingual surfaces, parotid and submandibular enlargement, subconjunctival haemorrhage, and oesophagitis.","Electrolyte disturbance is the principal danger. Vomiting produces hypokalaemia and hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis; laxative misuse produces hypokalaemia with metabolic acidosis.","Hypokalaemia causes cardiac arrhythmia and is the mechanism of sudden death in bulimia nervosa. A potassium below 3.0 requires urgent attention and below 2.5 is an emergency.","Other complications: Mallory-Weiss tears, oesophageal rupture which is rare and often fatal, gastric dilatation and rupture during a binge, cardiomyopathy from ipecac misuse, and menstrual irregularity.","Laxative cessation causes rebound fluid retention and oedema, which the person experiences as weight gain and which commonly precipitates relapse. Warn about it explicitly.","Comorbid self-harm and impulsive behaviour are common, and the multi-impulsive subtype, with substance misuse, self-harm and sexual disinhibition, carries a poorer prognosis.","Screening: SCOFF, five items, with two or more positive answers indicating likely eating disorder. Ask directly about vomiting and laxatives; they are not volunteered."],"cards":[["Define a binge.","Eating, in a discrete period, an amount of food definitely larger than most people would eat under similar circumstances, accompanied by a sense of loss of control over the eating. Both elements are required; a subjective binge without the objectively large amount does not meet the strict definition."],["What are the electrolyte consequences of vomiting and of laxative misuse?","Vomiting produces hypokalaemia with hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis. Laxative misuse produces hypokalaemia with metabolic acidosis. Hypokalaemia causes cardiac arrhythmia and is the mechanism of sudden death; a potassium below 3.0 requires urgent attention and below 2.5 is an emergency."],["How is bulimia nervosa distinguished from the binge-purge subtype of anorexia nervosa?","By body weight. If the person is significantly underweight, the diagnosis is anorexia nervosa binge-purge subtype regardless of the presence of binge eating and purging."],["Why must patients be warned before stopping laxatives?","Because laxative cessation causes rebound fluid retention and oedema, which the person experiences as rapid weight gain and which commonly precipitates relapse. Explaining that the change is fluid, not fat, prevents this."]],"note":["DSM-5 reduced the frequency threshold to once weekly for three months.","Binge eating disorder involves bingeing without regular compensatory behaviour.","Oesophageal rupture is rare and often fatal; gastric rupture can occur during a binge.","The multi-impulsive subtype carries a poorer prognosis.","SCOFF has five items, with two or more positive answers indicating likely eating disorder."],"refs":["icd11","nice","maudphys"]};
TEACH["7.5.2.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Bulimia responds better to treatment than almost anything else in psychiatry. There is a specific therapy that works, a self-help version that works nearly as well, and a drug that works, and most people never receive any of them.","teach":["NICE NG69 first-line for adults: bulimia-nervosa-focused guided self-help, based on a cognitive behavioural self-help programme with brief supportive sessions.","If guided self-help is unacceptable, contraindicated or ineffective after four weeks, individual eating-disorder-focused CBT, CBT-ED, is offered."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","companion"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["7.5.2.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Of all the eating disorders this is the one with the best outlook. Around half recover fully with proper treatment, and the ones who respond usually respond early, which tells you quickly whether the plan is working.","teach":["Around 45 to 50 per cent achieve full recovery, around 25 to 30 per cent improve partially, and around 20 per cent run a chronic course.","Outcome is considerably better than in anorexia nervosa, and treatment response is faster."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","companion","maudphys"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"There have never been this many old people. Not in Britain, not anywhere, not at any point in human history. Every problem in this specialty follows from that one fact.","teach":["Around a fifth of the UK population is aged 65 or over, and the proportion continues to rise. The over-85 group is the fastest growing segment in both absolute and proportional terms.","The oldest old, those over 85, matter disproportionately because dementia prevalence roughly doubles every five years after 65, so the fastest-growing group carries the highest risk.","Dementia prevalence rises from around 1 to 2 per cent at 65 to around 20 to 25 per cent or more above 85.","Population ageing has two causes: falling fertility and rising life expectancy. Falling fertility contributes more than longevity to the changing age structure.","The old age dependency ratio, the number of people of pensionable age per hundred of working age, is the standard measure of the economic consequence, and it is rising across all high-income countries.","Compression of morbidity, Fries' hypothesis, proposes that the onset of disability is postponed faster than death, so people live longer with fewer years of disability. The evidence for this is mixed rather than settled.","The competing expansion of morbidity account holds that medical advance keeps people alive with disease, extending years lived with disability. The truth appears to differ by condition.","Globally the shift is faster in low- and middle-income countries than it was in Europe, giving those countries far less time to build services. Around two-thirds of people with dementia worldwide live in low- and middle-income countries.","Age-specific dementia incidence appears to have fallen in several high-income countries over recent decades, probably through better cardiovascular health and education, while absolute numbers continue to rise because there are more old people.","That distinction between falling age-specific incidence and rising absolute numbers is examinable and frequently confused.","Life expectancy gains in the UK stalled from around 2011, and healthy life expectancy has not risen with total life expectancy, so the added years are frequently years of ill health.","Socioeconomic inequality in healthy life expectancy is very large, with differences of around two decades between the most and least deprived areas in England."],"cards":[["How does dementia prevalence change with age?","It roughly doubles every five years after 65, rising from around 1 to 2 per cent at 65 to 20 to 25 per cent or more above 85. The over-85 group is the fastest growing segment of the population, which is why absolute numbers rise steeply."],["Distinguish falling incidence from rising prevalence of dementia.","Age-specific dementia incidence appears to have fallen in several high-income countries, probably through improved cardiovascular health and education, while the absolute number of people with dementia continues to rise because the population contains far more older people."],["Contrast compression and expansion of morbidity.","Fries' compression of morbidity hypothesis holds that the onset of disability is postponed faster than death is, so people live longer with proportionally fewer disabled years. The expansion of morbidity account holds that medical advance keeps people alive with disease, increasing years lived with disability. The evidence differs by condition."]],"note":["Around a fifth of the UK population is aged 65 or over.","Falling fertility contributes more than rising longevity to population ageing.","Around two-thirds of people with dementia worldwide live in low- and middle-income countries.","UK life expectancy gains stalled from around 2011, and healthy life expectancy has not kept pace.","Healthy life expectancy differs by around two decades between the most and least deprived areas of England."],"refs":["oxoldage","companion"]};
TEACH["8.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Old age psychiatry exists because an eighty-year-old with depression is a different clinical problem from a thirty-year-old with depression: different drugs, different physical illnesses, different social situation, and a family who needs help as much as the patient does.","teach":["The specialty was recognised in the UK in 1989, and the model developed by Tom Arie at Goodmayes and later Nottingham is the origin of the community-based multidisciplinary approach.","The founding principles: home assessment as the default, a multidisciplinary team, close working with geriatric medicine and social services, and support for carers as part of the work rather than an addition to it."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","nice","companion"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Assessing an older person takes longer and involves more people. You need someone who knew them before, you need the drug chart, and you need to see them where they live, because the house tells you things the interview will not.","teach":["Home assessment is the default in this specialty, not a special measure. The home reveals self-care, nutrition, safety, hoarding, alcohol, medication adherence and the reality of the support available.","Collateral history is essential rather than desirable. What the person was like six months ago is the single most informative piece of data, and only someone who knew them can supply it.","Establish the timeline explicitly: gradual decline over years suggests neurodegeneration, stepwise decline suggests vascular disease, and onset over days suggests delirium.","Sensory impairment must be corrected before assessment. Hearing aids in, glasses on, adequate lighting. Undetected deafness is repeatedly mistaken for cognitive impairment and for paranoia.","Hearing loss is an independent modifiable risk factor for dementia, and hearing aid use is associated with reduced risk in observational studies.","Medication review is part of the psychiatric assessment. Anticholinergic burden is a specific and measurable risk, and anticholinergic drugs impair cognition and increase dementia risk.","Common culprits with anticholinergic effects: oxybutynin, amitriptyline, older antihistamines, some antipsychotics, and procyclidine. The Anticholinergic Cognitive Burden scale quantifies the load.","Physical examination is not optional: cardiovascular status, neurological examination including gait, signs of parkinsonism, and evidence of falls, dehydration, infection or pain.","Gait is highly informative. A shuffling gait suggests parkinsonism, a magnetic gait suggests normal pressure hydrocephalus, and gait slowing accompanies vascular cognitive impairment.","Cognitive testing: MMSE is widely known but copyright-restricted; MoCA is more sensitive to mild impairment and to executive dysfunction; ACE-III gives domain scores; and the 6-CIT and GPCOG are brief primary care tools.","Test scores must be interpreted against education, first language, sensory impairment, and premorbid ability. A highly educated person with a normal MMSE may have substantial decline from their own baseline.","Informant-rated instruments such as the IQCODE and, in Down syndrome, the DLD and DSQIID, are essential where formal testing is unusable.","Depression screening: the Geriatric Depression Scale, which excludes somatic items, and the Cornell Scale for Depression in Dementia, which is informant-based and is the correct instrument where dementia is present.","Investigations in cognitive impairment: full blood count, urea and electrolytes, calcium, glucose or HbA1c, liver and thyroid function, B12 and folate, with syphilis and HIV serology where indicated, and structural imaging.","Functional assessment matters more than test scores for decisions. Activities of daily living, instrumental activities such as finances, cooking, medication and driving, and observed rather than reported performance.","Occupational therapy home assessment provides functional information that no clinic assessment can, and often changes the plan more than any investigation.","Capacity assessment is routine here: capacity for treatment, for accommodation decisions, for finances, and for driving, each assessed separately and at the relevant time."],"cards":[["Why is home assessment the default in old age psychiatry?","Because the home reveals self-care, nutrition, hygiene, safety, hoarding, alcohol use, medication adherence and the reality of available support, none of which is reliably obtained in a clinic. It also provides the functional information that determines decisions."],["Which cognitive instruments are used, and how do they differ?","MMSE is widely known but copyright-restricted; MoCA is more sensitive to mild impairment and executive dysfunction; ACE-III gives domain-level scores; and 6-CIT and GPCOG are brief primary care screens. All must be interpreted against education, first language, sensory impairment and premorbid ability."],["What is anticholinergic burden and why does it matter?","The cumulative anticholinergic effect of a person's total medication, quantified by scales such as the Anticholinergic Cognitive Burden scale. Higher burden impairs cognition, increases falls and delirium, and is associated with increased dementia risk. Common contributors include oxybutynin, amitriptyline, older antihistamines and procyclidine."],["Which depression instruments are appropriate in older people?","The Geriatric Depression Scale, which excludes somatic items confounded by physical illness, and the Cornell Scale for Depression in Dementia, which is informant-based and is the correct instrument where dementia is present."],["What does gait examination contribute?","A shuffling festinant gait suggests parkinsonism; a magnetic or apraxic gait suggests normal pressure hydrocephalus; and gait slowing accompanies vascular cognitive impairment. Gait is among the most informative single elements of the physical examination."]],"note":["Correct sensory impairment before assessing cognition; deafness is mistaken for impairment and for paranoia.","Hearing loss is an independent modifiable risk factor for dementia.","IQCODE is the informant-rated instrument where formal testing is unusable.","A stepwise timeline suggests vascular disease; days suggests delirium; years suggests neurodegeneration.","Occupational therapy home assessment often changes the plan more than any investigation."],"refs":["oxoldage","nice","lishman"]};
TEACH["8.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"In old age the body and the mind stop being separable problems. A stroke changes personality, Parkinson's causes hallucinations, deafness causes paranoia, and a thyroid problem looks exactly like dementia.","teach":["Parkinson's disease: depression affects around 40 per cent and frequently precedes motor symptoms by years, being part of the disease rather than a reaction to it.","Apathy in Parkinson's disease is distinct from depression and is often mistaken for it. Apathy is loss of motivation without sadness or negative cognition, and it responds poorly to antidepressants."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","lishman","oxneuropsy"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.6"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Older men have the highest suicide rate of any group in most countries. They give fewer warnings, use more lethal methods, and are far less likely to have been asked.","teach":["Suicide rates rise with age in men in most countries, and men over 75 have among the highest rates of any demographic group.","In the UK the highest male rate has shifted towards middle age in recent decades, but the older male rate remains high and the international pattern of rising rate with age persists.","The ratio of attempts to completed suicide falls sharply with age. In young people it is of the order of 200 to 1; in older people it may be closer to 4 to 1.","That single figure changes clinical practice: an act of self-harm in an older person must be treated as a serious suicide attempt until demonstrated otherwise.","Older people use more lethal methods, are more likely to be alone, take more precautions against discovery, and are physically less able to survive an attempt.","Warning behaviours are less prominent. Older people communicate intent less often, and when they do it is more often to a general practitioner than to a mental health service.","The great majority of older people who die by suicide have seen their general practitioner in the preceding months, and a substantial proportion within the preceding month. This is the principal missed opportunity.","Depression is present in the great majority of older people who die by suicide, and is frequently untreated or undertreated rather than unrecognised.","Risk factors: male sex, depression, physical illness particularly with pain or functional impairment, bereavement especially in the first year, social isolation, alcohol misuse, and access to means.","Painful and disabling physical illness is a much stronger contributor than in younger groups, and malignancy, neurological disease and chronic pain are specifically associated.","Hopelessness is a stronger predictor than depression severity, and it is what should be asked about directly.","The distinction between wanting to die and wanting the situation to end is clinically useful and is frequently the opening for a real conversation.","Assessment is the same in principle but the threshold for concern must be lower, and physical illness must never be allowed to render suicidal thinking understandable and therefore unaddressed.","Treating depression in older people reduces suicidal ideation, and depression in this group responds to treatment as well as it does in younger people, contrary to a persistent clinical assumption.","Means restriction applies here too: review of stockpiled medication, particularly hoarded analgesics and hypnotics, is a specific and effective intervention.","Assisted dying legislation is a live policy question in the UK and elsewhere. The relationship between requests for hastened death, treatable depression, and inadequate palliative care is central to the debate and should be described neutrally."],"cards":[["How does the ratio of attempts to completed suicide change with age?","It falls sharply. In young people the ratio is of the order of 200 to 1, whereas in older people it may be closer to 4 to 1. Any act of self-harm in an older person must therefore be treated as a serious suicide attempt until demonstrated otherwise."],["What is the principal missed opportunity in older adult suicide prevention?","Primary care contact. The great majority of older people who die by suicide have consulted their general practitioner in the preceding months, and a substantial proportion within the preceding month, most commonly with depression that is untreated or undertreated."],["Which risk factors are specific to older adults?","Painful and disabling physical illness, particularly malignancy, neurological disease and chronic pain; bereavement especially within the first year; loss of independence; social isolation; and access to stockpiled medication. Male sex and depression carry weight as at any age."],["Which cognitive feature predicts suicide best in older people?","Hopelessness, which predicts more strongly than depression severity and should be asked about directly. The distinction between wanting to die and wanting the current situation to end is clinically useful."]],"note":["Men over 75 have among the highest suicide rates of any demographic group internationally.","Older people give fewer warnings, use more lethal methods and take more precautions against discovery.","Depression in older people responds to treatment as well as in younger people.","Review of stockpiled medication is a specific and effective means restriction intervention.","Physical illness must never render suicidal thinking understandable and therefore unaddressed."],"refs":["oxoldage","nice","companion"]};
TEACH["8.7"] = {"free":false,"simple":"People do not become different people when they get old. Traits soften a little and personality disorder becomes less dramatic, but the person who was difficult at forty is usually the person who is difficult at eighty, in a smaller arena.","teach":["Personality traits are broadly stable across the lifespan, with modest normative change: neuroticism and extraversion decline slightly, agreeableness and conscientiousness rise slightly into later life.","This pattern, the maturity principle, is replicated across cultures and suggests some genuine normative development rather than pure stability."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","tyrer11"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.8"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Older people were long thought to be unsuitable for therapy, on the grounds that they were too set in their ways and had too little future left. Both claims were wrong, and the outcomes are as good as in younger adults.","teach":["Freud held that people over about fifty lacked the elasticity of mental process required for analysis. This claim was made without evidence and has been thoroughly refuted.","Outcomes of psychological therapy in older adults are equivalent to those in younger adults across CBT, IPT and problem-solving therapy, in depression and in anxiety."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","gabbard","nice"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.9"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Old age brings a series of losses close together: a partner, friends, health, the house, the driving licence, the role. Grief is the normal response and it is not depression, but the two are hard to separate when the losses do not stop.","teach":["Bereavement is the commonest major life event in later life, and multiple bereavements accumulate, so grief for one death occurs against the background of several.","Normal grief in older people follows the same course as at any age: yearning, sadness, preoccupation, and often transient hallucinatory experiences of the deceased, which are normal."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","icd11","semliaison"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.10"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Sleep changes with age in normal ways that get treated as illness, and it also breaks in serious ways that get treated as normal ageing. Telling the two apart is most of the work.","teach":["Normal age-related changes: reduced total sleep time, reduced slow wave sleep which declines markedly from midlife, increased sleep fragmentation with more awakenings, reduced sleep efficiency, and phase advance with earlier bedtime and earlier waking.","Slow wave sleep declines most, and by late life may be almost absent in some people, which is a normal finding rather than pathology."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","maudsley","nice"],"n":{"teach":19,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["8.11"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Older people continue to have sexual lives, and services behave as though they do not. That silence causes real harm, from untreated dysfunction to unrecognised abuse in care homes.","teach":["Sexual activity continues into late life for a substantial proportion of people, and the decline with age is far less steep than clinicians and the public assume.","The English Longitudinal Study of Ageing and comparable surveys show that a majority of people in their sixties and seventies remain sexually active, and that interest persists even where activity declines."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","eastman","companion"],"n":{"teach":18,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.1.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Alzheimer's is the commonest dementia by a wide margin, and the single biggest risk factor for it is simply being old. After 65 the chance roughly doubles every five years.","teach":["Alzheimer's disease accounts for around 60 to 70 per cent of all dementia, and mixed Alzheimer and vascular pathology is commoner still at post mortem than either alone.","Prevalence doubles approximately every five years after 65: around 1 to 2 per cent at 65, around 5 per cent at 75, and 20 to 25 per cent or more above 85.","Around 900,000 people in the UK live with dementia, and projections take this above a million within the next decade on current trends.","Women are affected more than men, largely but not entirely explained by greater female longevity.","Age-specific incidence has fallen in several high-income countries over recent decades, attributed to better cardiovascular health and rising educational attainment, while absolute numbers continue to rise.","Down syndrome carries a very high risk: virtually all develop Alzheimer neuropathology by age forty, with clinical dementia typically emerging in the fifties.","Early-onset Alzheimer's disease, before 65, accounts for a small minority, and autosomal dominant familial cases are a small minority of those."],"cards":[["What proportion of dementia is Alzheimer's disease, and how does prevalence change with age?","Around 60 to 70 per cent of dementia, with mixed Alzheimer and vascular pathology commoner still at post mortem. Prevalence doubles roughly every five years after 65, from 1 to 2 per cent at 65 to over 20 per cent above 85."],["Why does Down syndrome carry such high Alzheimer risk?","Because the amyloid precursor protein gene lies on chromosome 21 and is present in triple dose. Virtually all people with Down syndrome have Alzheimer neuropathology by age forty, with clinical dementia typically emerging in the fifties."]],"note":["Around 900,000 people in the UK live with dementia.","Women are affected more than men, largely through greater longevity.","Age-specific incidence has fallen while absolute numbers rise.","Early-onset disease before 65 is a small minority, and familial autosomal dominant cases are a minority of those."],"refs":["oxoldage","nice"]};
TEACH["8.5.1.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Two proteins go wrong. One clumps outside the cells and one tangles inside them. The argument for thirty years has been about which one matters, and the drugs built on the first answer have mostly failed.","teach":["Pathology: extracellular amyloid beta plaques and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles of hyperphosphorylated tau, with synaptic loss and neuronal death.","Amyloid beta is cleaved from amyloid precursor protein by beta and gamma secretase in the amyloidogenic pathway; alpha secretase cleavage is non-amyloidogenic."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lishman","semgen","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.1.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"It starts with recent memory. Old memories are intact, sometimes vividly, while what happened this morning is gone. Then language goes, then navigation, then recognition, and the personality is preserved longest.","teach":["Onset is insidious and progression gradual over years. A clear date of onset argues against Alzheimer's disease.","The earliest and most prominent deficit is episodic memory, particularly anterograde: difficulty learning and retaining new information, with relative preservation of remote memory early on.","The memory deficit is one of encoding and storage rather than retrieval. Cueing and recognition testing do not rescue performance, which distinguishes it from the retrieval deficit of subcortical and depressive presentations.","That distinction is clinically usable: in Alzheimer's disease the information was never stored, so a prompt does not help.","Progression through the four As: amnesia, aphasia, apraxia and agnosia, appearing broadly in that order.","Language: word-finding difficulty and anomia first, then reduced fluency and comprehension, then eventually echolalia and mutism.","Visuospatial: topographical disorientation, getting lost in familiar places, and constructional apraxia. Getting lost while driving a familiar route is a common presenting complaint.","Insight is usually reduced early, so the person minimises while the family is alarmed. The head-turning sign, in which the person turns to the relative to answer questions, is a useful bedside observation.","Neuropsychiatric symptoms occur in the great majority over the course: apathy is commonest, then depression, anxiety, agitation, irritability, and psychosis in around a third.","Delusions in Alzheimer's disease are typically simple and persecutory, most often of theft, and reflect the memory deficit as much as psychosis.","Misidentification syndromes occur: Capgras, believing a familiar person is an impostor; the phantom boarder, believing strangers live in the house; and failure to recognise one's own reflection.","Apathy is the commonest neuropsychiatric symptom throughout and is the most distressing for families, who frequently interpret it as not trying.","Late stage: incontinence, immobility, dysphagia, primitive reflexes and weight loss. Death is typically from pneumonia.","Atypical presentations exist and are frequently misdiagnosed: posterior cortical atrophy with predominant visuospatial failure, logopenic aphasia with predominant language failure, and a frontal variant.","Posterior cortical atrophy typically presents younger, to ophthalmology, with a person unable to read or judge distance despite normal eyes.","Neuroimaging: medial temporal lobe and hippocampal atrophy on MRI, with temporoparietal hypometabolism on FDG-PET. Imaging supports rather than establishes the diagnosis.","Biomarkers: CSF showing reduced amyloid beta 42 with raised total and phosphorylated tau; amyloid PET; and increasingly plasma phosphorylated tau 217, which performs well and is likely to change practice."],"cards":[["What is the nature of the memory deficit in Alzheimer's disease?","A deficit of encoding and storage rather than retrieval. Cueing and recognition testing do not improve performance, because the information was never stored, which distinguishes it from the retrieval deficits of subcortical dementia and depression."],["Name the four As of Alzheimer's disease.","Amnesia, aphasia, apraxia and agnosia, appearing broadly in that order as the disease progresses."],["Which neuropsychiatric symptom is commonest, and which delusions are characteristic?","Apathy is the commonest throughout the illness and the most distressing for families, who often misinterpret it as lack of effort. Delusions are typically simple and persecutory, most often of theft, and misidentification syndromes including Capgras and the phantom boarder occur."],["Describe posterior cortical atrophy.","An atypical Alzheimer presentation with predominant visuospatial and visuoperceptual failure, typically presenting at a younger age and often first to ophthalmology, with a person unable to read or judge distance despite normal eyes."],["Which biomarkers support the diagnosis?","CSF showing reduced amyloid beta 42 with raised total and phosphorylated tau; amyloid PET imaging; medial temporal atrophy on MRI with temporoparietal hypometabolism on FDG-PET; and increasingly plasma phosphorylated tau 217, which performs well and is likely to change practice."]],"note":["A clear date of onset argues against Alzheimer's disease.","The head-turning sign, turning to the relative to answer, is a useful bedside observation.","Psychosis occurs in around a third over the course.","Death is typically from pneumonia in the late stage.","Logopenic aphasia and a frontal variant are further atypical presentations."],"refs":["lishman","oxoldage","nice"]};
TEACH["8.5.1.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There are two old drugs that help a little for a while, and two new ones that clear the plaques and were judged not worth the money or the risk. The most effective interventions in dementia are still not drugs.","teach":["Cholinesterase inhibitors: donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine, recommended by NICE for mild to moderate Alzheimer's disease. They are symptomatic, produce modest improvement in cognition and function, and do not alter progression.","Adverse effects are predominantly cholinergic: nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, anorexia, bradycardia, syncope and vivid dreams. Bradycardia and syncope matter most, and an ECG is prudent before starting."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":19,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.1.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Alzheimer's disease shortens life by several years and the end is usually pneumonia rather than the dementia itself. Recognising it as a terminal illness changes what good care looks like.","teach":["Median survival from diagnosis is around seven to ten years, with wide variation. Survival is shorter with later age at diagnosis, male sex, greater severity at diagnosis, and comorbidity.","Rate of decline is variable and cannot be predicted reliably at diagnosis, which is worth saying to families who ask."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","nice"],"n":{"teach":12,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.2.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"This is the dementia you can do something about before it happens, because the things that damage the heart damage the brain in exactly the same way.","teach":["Vascular dementia is the second commonest dementia, accounting for around 15 to 20 per cent of cases as a pure diagnosis.","Mixed Alzheimer and vascular pathology is more common at post mortem than either pure form, and pure vascular dementia is less common than clinical diagnosis suggests.","Prevalence rises with age, though the relative contribution of vascular pathology to dementia is greater in the young-old and in men.","Male predominance is present, in contrast with Alzheimer's disease, reflecting the distribution of cardiovascular risk factors.","Incidence is higher in populations with a high burden of cardiovascular disease, including South Asian and Black populations in the UK, and in areas of higher deprivation.","Around a quarter to a third of stroke survivors have significant cognitive impairment, and stroke roughly doubles the risk of subsequent dementia.","Vascular dementia incidence has fallen in high-income countries alongside improvements in blood pressure control, statin use and smoking reduction, which is the clearest natural experiment in dementia prevention."],"cards":[["What proportion of dementia is vascular, and what is more common than either pure form?","Around 15 to 20 per cent as a pure diagnosis. Mixed Alzheimer and vascular pathology is more common at post mortem than either pure form alone."],["What evidence supports vascular dementia as preventable?","Its incidence has fallen in high-income countries alongside improvements in blood pressure control, statin use and smoking reduction, which is the clearest natural experiment in dementia prevention."]],"note":["Male predominance contrasts with Alzheimer's disease.","Stroke roughly doubles the risk of subsequent dementia.","A quarter to a third of stroke survivors have significant cognitive impairment.","Incidence is higher in populations with greater cardiovascular disease burden and in deprived areas."],"refs":["oxoldage","lishman"]};
TEACH["8.5.2.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There are several different diseases hiding under one label. A single stroke in the wrong place, dozens of tiny strokes, or a slow strangling of the small vessels deep in the brain all end up called vascular dementia.","teach":["Subtypes: multi-infarct dementia from multiple cortical infarcts; strategic single infarct dementia; small vessel disease with subcortical ischaemic changes; hypoperfusion dementia; and haemorrhagic causes.","Subcortical small vessel disease is the commonest form and the one most often missed, producing gradual rather than stepwise decline and therefore mimicking Alzheimer's disease."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lishman","oxneuropsy","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":12,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.2.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The pattern is different from Alzheimer's. Memory is relatively preserved but everything is slow, planning is impossible, the person is apathetic, and the gait went wrong before the memory did.","teach":["Classical description: abrupt onset, stepwise deterioration, fluctuating course, focal neurological signs, and preservation of personality until late. This applies to multi-infarct disease and not to the commoner small vessel form.","Small vessel subcortical disease produces gradual decline, which is why the classical description misleads and why vascular dementia is under-diagnosed.","Cognitive profile: prominent executive dysfunction, reduced processing speed, impaired attention and set-shifting, with memory relatively preserved and responsive to cueing.","The cueing response distinguishes it from Alzheimer's disease: in vascular cognitive impairment the material was encoded but is hard to retrieve, so a prompt helps.","Physical signs: gait disturbance, often early and often preceding cognitive complaint; urinary symptoms; pseudobulbar palsy; focal weakness; extrapyramidal signs; and brisk reflexes with extensor plantars.","Early gait disturbance is one of the most useful discriminating features, and gait change preceding cognitive change points away from Alzheimer's disease.","Neuropsychiatric features: depression is very common and more so than in Alzheimer's disease, apathy is prominent, and emotionalism with pathological crying occurs.","Insight is often better preserved than in Alzheimer's disease, which contributes to the higher rate of depression.","Imaging: infarcts, extensive white matter hyperintensities, lacunes and microbleeds. Fazekas grading describes white matter change severity.","White matter hyperintensities are common in normal ageing, so their presence alone does not establish the diagnosis; the burden and the clinical correlation matter.","Diagnostic criteria: NINDS-AIREN requires dementia, cerebrovascular disease on examination and imaging, and a relationship between the two demonstrated by onset within three months of a stroke or by abrupt or stepwise course.","The Hachinski Ischaemic Score, an older bedside instrument, scores features favouring vascular over degenerative dementia including abrupt onset, stepwise deterioration, fluctuation, nocturnal confusion, preserved personality, depression, somatic complaints, emotional incontinence, hypertension, stroke history, atherosclerosis and focal signs.","Vascular cognitive impairment is the preferred current umbrella term, covering the whole spectrum from mild impairment to dementia, and reflecting that the vascular contribution is usually part of a mixed picture."],"cards":[["Contrast the cognitive profile of vascular and Alzheimer dementia.","Vascular cognitive impairment produces prominent executive dysfunction, reduced processing speed and impaired attention, with relatively preserved memory that improves with cueing because the material was encoded but is hard to retrieve. Alzheimer's disease produces an encoding and storage deficit in which cueing does not help."],["Which physical sign most usefully discriminates vascular dementia?","Early gait disturbance, often preceding cognitive complaint. Gait change before memory change points away from Alzheimer's disease. Urinary symptoms, pseudobulbar palsy and focal neurological signs also support the diagnosis."],["What are the NINDS-AIREN criteria?","Dementia, plus cerebrovascular disease demonstrated on examination and imaging, plus a demonstrated relationship between the two, shown by onset within three months of a recognised stroke or by an abrupt or stepwise deteriorating course."],["What is the Hachinski Ischaemic Score?","An older bedside instrument scoring clinical features favouring vascular over degenerative dementia, including abrupt onset, stepwise deterioration, fluctuating course, nocturnal confusion, relative preservation of personality, depression, somatic complaints, emotional incontinence, hypertension, history of stroke and focal neurological signs."]],"note":["The classical abrupt stepwise picture applies to multi-infarct disease, not to the commoner small vessel form.","Depression is commoner than in Alzheimer's disease, partly because insight is better preserved.","Fazekas grading describes white matter hyperintensity severity.","White matter change is common in normal ageing and does not alone establish the diagnosis.","Vascular cognitive impairment is the preferred umbrella term."],"refs":["lishman","oxoldage","nice"]};
TEACH["8.5.2.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There is no drug for vascular dementia. What there is instead is the whole of cardiovascular medicine, applied properly, which is more useful than any dementia drug currently available.","teach":["Cholinesterase inhibitors and memantine are not recommended by NICE for pure vascular dementia. They are used only where there is suspected comorbid Alzheimer's disease, dementia with Lewy bodies or Parkinson's disease dementia.","That restriction is frequently ignored, and prescribing a cholinesterase inhibitor for pure vascular dementia is not supported by guidance."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","oxoldage","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.2.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Survival is shorter than in Alzheimer's disease, because the same vessels that damaged the brain also supply the heart. People die of the underlying disease as much as of the dementia.","teach":["Median survival is shorter than in Alzheimer's disease, commonly cited as around three to five years from diagnosis, reflecting cardiovascular comorbidity.","Death is frequently from myocardial infarction or further stroke rather than from the complications of dementia itself."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","lishman"],"n":{"teach":10,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.3.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"This is the dementia that is most often missed and where getting it wrong is most dangerous, because giving the wrong drug can kill the person.","teach":["Dementia with Lewy bodies accounts for around 5 to 15 per cent of dementia in clinical series and around 15 to 20 per cent at post mortem, so it is substantially under-diagnosed in life.","It is probably the second or third commonest neurodegenerative dementia, and the discrepancy between clinical and pathological rates is one of the largest in the field.","Parkinson's disease dementia develops in the majority of people with Parkinson's disease of long duration, with cumulative incidence exceeding 75 per cent at twenty years.","Together, the Lewy body dementias account for a substantial share of dementia and a disproportionate share of the clinical difficulty.","Male predominance is present in dementia with Lewy bodies, in contrast with Alzheimer's disease.","Mean age of onset is broadly similar to Alzheimer's disease, in the mid-seventies, though presentation is often later relative to symptom onset because of diagnostic delay.","REM sleep behaviour disorder in an older person carries a very high risk of subsequent Lewy body disease, with conversion in the majority over sufficient follow-up.","Under-diagnosis matters clinically because of neuroleptic sensitivity, which makes the missed diagnosis actively dangerous rather than merely inaccurate."],"cards":[["How common is dementia with Lewy bodies and why does under-diagnosis matter?","Around 5 to 15 per cent of dementia in clinical series but 15 to 20 per cent at post mortem, indicating substantial under-diagnosis in life. It matters because of severe neuroleptic sensitivity, which makes the missed diagnosis dangerous rather than merely inaccurate."],["What proportion of people with Parkinson's disease develop dementia?","The majority with long disease duration, with cumulative incidence exceeding 75 per cent at twenty years."]],"note":["Male predominance contrasts with Alzheimer's disease.","REM sleep behaviour disorder in an older person carries very high risk of subsequent Lewy body disease.","The gap between clinical and pathological diagnosis rates is among the largest in dementia.","Mean age of onset is in the mid-seventies."],"refs":["oxoldage","lishman"]};
TEACH["8.5.3.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The same abnormal protein clumps in the same way in both conditions. The only thing that separates them is which symptom came first, which is a genuinely arbitrary distinction that everyone acknowledges and nobody has replaced.","teach":["Both are alpha-synucleinopathies. Lewy bodies are intracytoplasmic inclusions composed principally of aggregated alpha-synuclein, with ubiquitin.","Distribution determines the syndrome: brainstem-predominant Lewy bodies produce Parkinson's disease; cortical and limbic distribution produces dementia."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lishman","oxneuropsy","semgen"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.3.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Look for four things: attention that comes and goes over hours, detailed visual hallucinations of people or animals, stiffness and slowness, and acting out dreams. Three of the four and the diagnosis is close to certain.","teach":["The 2017 consensus criteria specify four core clinical features: fluctuating cognition with pronounced variation in attention and alertness; recurrent well-formed and detailed visual hallucinations; REM sleep behaviour disorder, which may precede cognitive decline; and one or more spontaneous cardinal features of parkinsonism.","Two core features, or one core feature plus one indicative biomarker, gives probable dementia with Lewy bodies.","Indicative biomarkers: reduced dopamine transporter uptake on SPECT or PET, which is the DaTscan; abnormal MIBG myocardial scintigraphy; and polysomnographic confirmation of REM sleep without atonia.","Fluctuation is the most difficult feature to elicit and the most useful. It occurs over minutes to hours, not over days, with episodes of staring, unresponsiveness and disorganised speech resembling delirium.","Ask the carer specifically: are there times when he seems switched off, or stares into space, and are those times different from other times on the same day.","Visual hallucinations are typically well formed, detailed and recurrent, most often of people, children or animals, and are often not frightening. Insight into them is variable.","Parkinsonism in dementia with Lewy bodies is typically symmetrical, with less tremor and more axial rigidity and postural instability than idiopathic Parkinson's disease.","Cognitive profile: attention, executive function and visuospatial impairment dominate, with memory relatively preserved early. This differs sharply from Alzheimer's disease.","Visuospatial impairment is prominent and early. The pentagon copy and clock drawing are affected disproportionately.","Supportive features: severe neuroleptic sensitivity; repeated falls and syncope; transient unexplained loss of consciousness; autonomic dysfunction with orthostatic hypotension, constipation and urinary incontinence; hypersomnia; hyposmia; depression; and systematised delusions.","Autonomic dysfunction is often prominent and under-recognised, and orthostatic hypotension contributes substantially to falls.","Parkinson's disease dementia presents with the same cognitive and neuropsychiatric profile, differing only in the order of onset.","Delirium is frequently the presenting event, and delirium that fails to resolve in an older person should raise suspicion of underlying Lewy body disease.","Imaging: relative preservation of medial temporal lobe structures on MRI, in contrast with Alzheimer's disease, and occipital hypometabolism on FDG-PET with the cingulate island sign."],"cards":[["Name the four core clinical features in the 2017 consensus criteria.","Fluctuating cognition with pronounced variation in attention and alertness; recurrent well-formed and detailed visual hallucinations; REM sleep behaviour disorder, which may precede cognitive decline; and one or more spontaneous cardinal features of parkinsonism."],["What are the indicative biomarkers?","Reduced dopamine transporter uptake in the basal ganglia on SPECT or PET, the DaTscan; abnormal MIBG myocardial scintigraphy; and polysomnographic confirmation of REM sleep without atonia."],["How should fluctuation be elicited?","By asking the carer directly whether there are periods when the person seems switched off or stares into space, and whether those periods differ from other times on the same day. Fluctuation occurs over minutes to hours rather than days and resembles delirium."],["Contrast the cognitive profile with Alzheimer's disease.","Dementia with Lewy bodies produces early and prominent impairment of attention, executive function and visuospatial ability, with memory relatively preserved. Alzheimer's disease produces early and prominent episodic memory impairment. Pentagon copying and clock drawing are disproportionately affected in Lewy body disease."],["Which imaging features support the diagnosis?","Relative preservation of medial temporal lobe structures on MRI, in contrast with Alzheimer's disease, and occipital hypometabolism on FDG-PET with the cingulate island sign of preserved posterior cingulate metabolism."]],"note":["Two core features, or one core feature with an indicative biomarker, gives probable DLB.","Parkinsonism is typically symmetrical with less tremor and more axial rigidity.","Visual hallucinations are typically of people, children or animals and are often not frightening.","Autonomic dysfunction with orthostatic hypotension contributes substantially to falls.","Delirium that fails to resolve should raise suspicion of underlying Lewy body disease."],"refs":["lishman","oxoldage","nice"]};
TEACH["8.5.3.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The single most important instruction in this whole section is: do not give an antipsychotic. If you must, use quetiapine or clozapine at a tiny dose, and expect trouble anyway.","teach":["Severe neuroleptic sensitivity occurs in a substantial proportion, up to half, and may be irreversible or fatal. It comprises marked rigidity, autonomic instability, sudden cognitive deterioration and reduced consciousness.","Avoid all antipsychotics where possible. Where absolutely necessary, quetiapine or clozapine at very low dose are least likely to worsen the motor state, and even these carry risk."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.3.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Decline is faster than in Alzheimer's disease and the burden on families is heavier, because on top of the memory problems there are falls, hallucinations, and a person who is present one hour and absent the next.","teach":["Median survival from diagnosis is shorter than in Alzheimer's disease, commonly cited around five to seven years, with some series reporting shorter.","Cognitive decline is faster than in Alzheimer's disease, and functional decline faster still because of the motor and autonomic components."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","lishman"],"n":{"teach":11,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.4.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"This is the dementia of people who are too young to have dementia. It starts in the fifties, memory is often fine, and the family has usually been told for years that it is a midlife crisis or depression.","teach":["Frontotemporal dementia is the second commonest cause of dementia in people under 65, after Alzheimer's disease, and accounts for a substantial proportion of young-onset dementia.","Overall prevalence is low, of the order of 15 to 20 per 100,000 in the 45 to 65 age group, but it is over-represented in specialist young-onset services.","Mean age of onset is in the late fifties, though the range extends from the twenties to the eighties.","The sex ratio is approximately equal, with some evidence of male predominance in the behavioural variant.","Around 40 per cent have a family history, and around 10 to 20 per cent have an autosomal dominant pattern, which is far higher than in Alzheimer's disease.","Diagnostic delay is long, commonly several years, because the presentation is behavioural and is attributed to psychiatric illness, relationship problems or personality.","Frontotemporal dementia is a leading cause of misdiagnosis in psychiatry, with people receiving diagnoses of depression, bipolar disorder, personality disorder or midlife crisis before the correct diagnosis is made."],"cards":[["How common is frontotemporal dementia and in whom?","It is the second commonest cause of dementia under the age of 65 after Alzheimer's disease, with prevalence of the order of 15 to 20 per 100,000 in the 45 to 65 age group. Mean age of onset is in the late fifties."],["What proportion is familial?","Around 40 per cent have a family history and 10 to 20 per cent show an autosomal dominant pattern, far higher than in Alzheimer's disease."]],"note":["Diagnostic delay commonly runs to several years.","It is a leading cause of psychiatric misdiagnosis, being labelled depression, bipolar disorder or personality disorder.","The sex ratio is approximately equal with possible male predominance in the behavioural variant.","The age range extends from the twenties to the eighties."],"refs":["lishman","oxneuropsy"]};
TEACH["8.5.4.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Three proteins account for nearly all of it, and one gene mutation is shared with motor neurone disease, which is why the two diseases turn up in the same families.","teach":["Frontotemporal lobar degeneration is the pathological term; frontotemporal dementia is the clinical syndrome. The relationship between the two is not one to one.","Three principal molecular pathologies: FTLD-tau in around 40 per cent, FTLD-TDP with TDP-43 inclusions in around 50 per cent, and FTLD-FUS in a small minority."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semgen","lishman","oxneuropsy"],"n":{"teach":12,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.4.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The person changes rather than forgets. They become rude, or indifferent, or eat sweets constantly, or do the same thing at the same time every day, and they cannot see that anything has changed.","teach":["Three main clinical syndromes: behavioural variant frontotemporal dementia, semantic variant primary progressive aphasia, and non-fluent agrammatic variant primary progressive aphasia.","Behavioural variant accounts for around half of cases and is the presentation most likely to reach psychiatry.","The Rascovsky criteria require three of six features: early behavioural disinhibition; early apathy or inertia; early loss of sympathy or empathy; early perseverative, stereotyped or compulsive behaviour; hyperorality and dietary change; and a neuropsychological profile of executive deficits with relative sparing of memory and visuospatial function.","Loss of empathy is often the earliest and most distressing change for families, described as the person becoming cold or no longer caring.","Disinhibition takes the form of tactless remarks, socially inappropriate behaviour, impulsive spending, sexual disinhibition, shoplifting and criminal behaviour. New offending in middle age warrants consideration of this diagnosis.","Hyperorality and dietary change: a marked preference for sweet foods is characteristic, with overeating, altered food preference, and sometimes consumption of inedible objects.","Stereotyped and ritualistic behaviour: fixed daily routines, repetitive phrases, hoarding, and compulsions that resemble OCD but lack the anxiety and the ego-dystonic quality.","Insight is absent from early on, which is a discriminating feature and explains why the person is brought by others and denies any problem.","Memory and visuospatial function are relatively preserved early, so a normal MMSE is common and is a frequent cause of false reassurance.","Standard bedside cognitive tests are insensitive. Frontal assessment requires verbal fluency, Luria three-step, go no-go, cognitive estimates and tests of social cognition.","The Frontal Assessment Battery and the Cambridge Behavioural Inventory are useful, and informant-based behavioural measures are more sensitive than patient-performed cognitive tests.","Semantic variant: fluent speech with loss of word meaning, anomia, impaired single word comprehension, surface dyslexia, and loss of object knowledge, with predominantly left anterior temporal atrophy.","Non-fluent agrammatic variant: effortful halting speech, agrammatism, apraxia of speech, with preserved single word comprehension, and left posterior frontoinsular atrophy.","Right temporal variant presents with prosopagnosia, loss of person knowledge, and prominent behavioural change including rigidity and loss of empathy.","Imaging: frontal and anterior temporal atrophy on MRI, often markedly asymmetrical, with corresponding hypometabolism on FDG-PET. Imaging is more informative here than in most dementias.","The phenocopy syndrome describes people, typically men, with behavioural features but no progression and no imaging change over years. Its nature is disputed and it is a genuine diagnostic trap."],"cards":[["State the Rascovsky criteria for behavioural variant frontotemporal dementia.","Three of six features: early behavioural disinhibition; early apathy or inertia; early loss of sympathy or empathy; early perseverative, stereotyped or compulsive behaviour; hyperorality and dietary change; and a neuropsychological profile of executive deficits with relative sparing of memory and visuospatial function."],["Why is a normal MMSE common in frontotemporal dementia?","Because memory and visuospatial function are relatively preserved early while the deficits are behavioural and executive. Standard bedside tests are insensitive, and frontal assessment with verbal fluency, Luria three-step, go no-go, cognitive estimates and social cognition tasks is required, alongside informant-based behavioural measures."],["Distinguish the two aphasic variants.","Semantic variant produces fluent speech with loss of word meaning, anomia, impaired single word comprehension, surface dyslexia and loss of object knowledge, with left anterior temporal atrophy. Non-fluent agrammatic variant produces effortful halting speech, agrammatism and apraxia of speech with preserved single word comprehension, and left posterior frontoinsular atrophy."],["What is the phenocopy syndrome?","A presentation, typically in men, with the behavioural features of frontotemporal dementia but no progression and no imaging change over years of follow-up. Its nature is disputed and it constitutes a genuine diagnostic trap."],["Which behavioural change is often earliest and most distressing to families?","Loss of empathy and sympathy, described as the person becoming cold, indifferent or no longer caring about others, frequently preceding any cognitive complaint."]],"note":["Insight is absent from early on, which is a discriminating feature.","A marked preference for sweet foods is characteristic of hyperorality.","New offending in middle age warrants consideration of this diagnosis.","Right temporal variant presents with prosopagnosia and loss of person knowledge.","Imaging is more informative in this dementia than in most others."],"refs":["lishman","oxneuropsy","oyebode"]};
TEACH["8.5.4.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"There is no treatment for the disease. What there is instead is management of behaviour that is genuinely dangerous, and support for a family watching someone become a stranger while still physically well.","teach":["No disease-modifying treatment exists. Cholinesterase inhibitors are not recommended and may worsen behaviour, since there is no cholinergic deficit in this disease.","Prescribing a cholinesterase inhibitor in frontotemporal dementia is a common error arising from the assumption that dementia drugs treat all dementia."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","oxneuropsy"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.4.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"It is faster than Alzheimer's disease and the people affected are younger, so more years of life are lost. The family carries the burden for longer while still working and raising children.","teach":["Median survival is around six to eight years from symptom onset, and around three to four years from diagnosis given the typical diagnostic delay.","Survival is shorter than in Alzheimer's disease, and the FTD-MND overlap has the worst prognosis of all, often under three years."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxneuropsy","lishman","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":12,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.5.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Delirium is the commonest complication of being old and in hospital, and it is missed more often than it is found. Two out of three cases go unrecognised, mostly the quiet ones.","teach":["Prevalence on admission is around 15 to 20 per cent in older medical inpatients, and incidence during admission adds a similar proportion again.","Prevalence is around 50 per cent after hip fracture, up to 50 per cent after cardiac surgery, and over 70 per cent in ventilated intensive care patients.","In nursing homes prevalence is around 15 per cent, and in the palliative care setting delirium affects the great majority in the last days of life.","Around two-thirds of cases are not recognised, and the hypoactive form accounts for most of the missed cases.","Hypoactive delirium is the commonest subtype in older people, is quiet and therefore unnoticed, and carries the worst prognosis.","Risk factors divide into predisposing, being characteristics of the person, and precipitating, being the acute insults. The relationship is inverse: a very vulnerable person needs only a small insult.","Predisposing: age over 65, existing cognitive impairment or dementia, frailty, sensory impairment, multiple comorbidities, polypharmacy, previous delirium, and depression.","Dementia is the single strongest predisposing factor, raising risk around fivefold, and delirium superimposed on dementia is the commonest presentation in older inpatients.","Precipitating: infection, surgery, dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, hypoxia, pain, constipation, urinary retention, medication and drug withdrawal, and change of environment.","Delirium is a marker of vulnerability as much as of the acute insult, which is why it predicts poor outcome so strongly."],"cards":[["Give the prevalence of delirium across settings.","Around 15 to 20 per cent on admission in older medical inpatients with a similar incidence during admission; around 50 per cent after hip fracture and after cardiac surgery; over 70 per cent in ventilated intensive care patients; around 15 per cent in nursing homes; and the great majority of patients in the last days of life."],["What proportion of delirium is missed, and which subtype accounts for it?","Around two-thirds of cases go unrecognised, with the hypoactive subtype accounting for most of the missed cases. Hypoactive delirium is the commonest form in older people and carries the worst prognosis."],["Describe the relationship between predisposing and precipitating factors.","It is inverse. A person with high predisposing vulnerability, such as advanced dementia and frailty, requires only a minor precipitant such as constipation, whereas a robust younger person requires a major insult. Delirium is therefore a marker of vulnerability as much as of the acute illness."]],"note":["Dementia is the single strongest predisposing factor, raising risk around fivefold.","Delirium superimposed on dementia is the commonest presentation in older inpatients.","Precipitants include infection, surgery, dehydration, hypoxia, pain, constipation, retention and medication.","Sensory impairment, polypharmacy and previous delirium are important predisposing factors."],"refs":["nice","oxoldage","semliaison"]};
TEACH["8.5.5.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Nobody fully knows what delirium is at the level of the brain. The best current account is that inflammation from somewhere else in the body reaches an already vulnerable brain and the networks that sustain attention fall apart.","teach":["No single mechanism accounts for delirium. Several converging pathways are proposed and probably all contribute.","The neuroinflammatory hypothesis is the leading account: systemic inflammation from infection, surgery or injury produces cytokine signalling to the brain, microglial activation and neuronal dysfunction."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semliaison","lishman","nice"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.5.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"The person cannot hold their attention on anything, it started this week, and it is worse in the evening than it was this morning. Those three things are the diagnosis.","teach":["The four defining elements: acute onset over hours to days; fluctuating course; disturbance of attention and awareness; and additional disturbance in cognition, all in the context of a physiological cause.","Inattention is the core feature and must be tested directly. Months of the year backwards, serial sevens, digit span, or simply whether the person can sustain a conversation.","Fluctuation over the course of a day is the most characteristic feature and is best established from nursing observation and from family, since a single assessment may catch a lucid period.","A person who appears entirely normal at eleven in the morning may be floridly delirious at eight in the evening, which is why single assessments mislead.","Three motor subtypes: hyperactive with agitation, restlessness and hypervigilance; hypoactive with lethargy, reduced activity and withdrawal; and mixed.","Hypoactive delirium is commonest in older people, is most often missed, is frequently mistaken for depression or for dementia progression, and has the worst prognosis.","Additional features: disorganised thinking with rambling or incoherent speech; perceptual disturbance, typically visual illusions and hallucinations; fleeting poorly systematised persecutory delusions; disorientation; emotional lability; and disturbed sleep-wake cycle with nocturnal worsening.","Sleep-wake cycle reversal is characteristic and precedes the frank delirium in many cases.","Assessment tools: the 4AT is brief, requires no training and is recommended for routine use, scoring alertness, AMT4 orientation, attention by months backwards, and acute change or fluctuation.","The Confusion Assessment Method, CAM, requires acute onset with fluctuation and inattention, plus either disorganised thinking or altered level of consciousness. CAM-ICU is the intensive care version.","Differentiating delirium from dementia: onset over hours to days rather than months to years; fluctuation within a day; impaired attention and consciousness; and a reversible physiological cause.","Differentiating delirium from depression: hypoactive delirium mimics depression closely, and attention testing plus the acute time course separates them.","Delirium superimposed on dementia is the hardest and commonest problem. The key is change from the person's own baseline, which requires collateral history.","Investigation: full examination and observations, blood glucose, full blood count, urea and electrolytes, calcium, liver function, C-reactive protein, urinalysis and culture, chest radiograph, ECG, and imaging or lumbar puncture where indicated.","Urine dipstick alone must not be used to diagnose urinary tract infection in older people, since asymptomatic bacteriuria is very common and treating it causes harm without benefit."],"cards":[["State the four defining elements of delirium.","Acute onset over hours to days; a fluctuating course; disturbance of attention and awareness; and an additional disturbance in cognition, occurring in the context of an underlying physiological cause."],["What is the 4AT?","A brief delirium assessment requiring no special training, recommended for routine use, scoring alertness, AMT4 orientation questions, attention tested by reciting the months backwards, and evidence of acute change or fluctuating course."],["How is delirium distinguished from dementia?","By onset over hours to days rather than months to years, fluctuation within a single day, impaired attention and altered level of consciousness, and the presence of a reversible physiological cause. Where delirium is superimposed on dementia, the key is change from the person's own recent baseline, which requires collateral history."],["Why must urine dipstick not be used alone to diagnose urinary tract infection in older people?","Because asymptomatic bacteriuria is very common in this population, so a positive dipstick does not indicate infection. Treating it exposes the person to antibiotic harm without benefit and diverts attention from the true precipitant of the delirium."],["Which subtype is commonest in older people and why does it matter?","Hypoactive delirium, characterised by lethargy, reduced activity and withdrawal. It is the most frequently missed, is commonly mistaken for depression or dementia progression, and carries the worst prognosis."]],"note":["Sleep-wake cycle reversal is characteristic and often precedes frank delirium.","A single assessment may catch a lucid interval, so nursing and family observation are essential.","CAM requires acute onset with fluctuation and inattention plus disorganised thinking or altered consciousness.","Perceptual disturbance is typically visual; delusions are fleeting and poorly systematised.","Investigation includes glucose, electrolytes, calcium, inflammatory markers, culture, imaging and ECG."],"refs":["nice","oyebode","oxoldage"]};
TEACH["8.5.5.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Almost everything that works in delirium is not a drug. Get the glasses on, get the lights right, get the person up and walking, find the infection, stop the anticholinergic, and let them sleep at night.","teach":["Treat the cause, and expect there to be more than one. Delirium in an older person is usually multifactorial, so finding one precipitant does not end the search.","Medication review is mandatory. Stop or reduce anticholinergics, opioids where possible, benzodiazepines, and any recently started drug."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.5.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Delirium was treated for decades as a temporary nuisance. It is not. People who have had it die sooner, lose their independence, and are more likely to develop dementia, and the longer it lasts the worse all of that gets.","teach":["In-hospital mortality is substantially raised, and the excess persists for months after discharge, after adjustment for age, illness severity, comorbidity and pre-existing cognitive impairment.","Delirium is an independent predictor of mortality rather than simply a marker of severe illness, which is the crucial epidemiological point."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","oxoldage","semliaison"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.6.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Depression is not commoner in old age than in middle age, which surprises people. What is commoner is depression that nobody recognises, in a person who has plenty of reason to be sad.","teach":["Major depression is no more common in older people than in younger adults, with community prevalence of the order of 2 to 5 per cent, and by some measures slightly lower.","Clinically significant depressive symptoms falling short of major depression are far commoner, affecting around 10 to 15 per cent of older people in the community.","This subthreshold or subsyndromal depression carries real disability, real mortality and real treatment response, and treating only major depression misses most of the burden.","Prevalence rises sharply with setting: around 10 per cent in primary care attenders, 20 to 30 per cent in medical inpatients, and 30 to 40 per cent in care home residents.","Care home prevalence is the highest of any setting, and depression there is very poorly detected and very poorly treated.","The female predominance narrows with age, so the sex ratio in later life is closer to equal than in younger adults.","Depression in older people is under-recognised in primary care, partly through somatic presentation, partly through the assumption that low mood is an understandable response to age, illness or loss.","That assumption is the central clinical error in this area. Understandable and treatable are not alternatives.","Late-onset depression, first episode after around 60, differs from early-onset recurrent depression, with less family history and more vascular and structural brain change."],"cards":[["Is depression commoner in old age?","Major depression is not, with community prevalence of around 2 to 5 per cent, comparable to or slightly lower than in younger adults. However, clinically significant subthreshold depressive symptoms affect around 10 to 15 per cent, and these carry real disability, mortality and treatment response."],["How does depression prevalence vary by setting in older people?","Around 10 per cent in primary care attenders, 20 to 30 per cent in medical inpatients, and 30 to 40 per cent in care home residents, which is the highest of any setting and the most poorly detected."]],"note":["The female predominance narrows with age towards an equal ratio.","Subthreshold depression carries the greater share of population burden.","Understandability is not a reason to leave depression untreated.","Late-onset depression has less family history and more vascular and structural change."],"refs":["oxoldage","nice"]};
TEACH["8.5.6.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"In older people, depression is more often something happening in the blood vessels of the brain than something happening in the mind. That is a real difference from depression at thirty, and it changes what you expect from treatment.","teach":["Genetic loading is lower in late-onset depression than in early-onset recurrent depression, and family history is less often present.","The vascular depression hypothesis, Alexopoulos 1997, proposes that cerebrovascular disease predisposes to, precipitates or perpetuates depression in later life."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","lishman","companion"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.6.3"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Older people with depression often do not say they are sad. They say they cannot sleep, they have no appetite, their memory has gone, and they hurt. The mood is the last thing they mention, if they mention it at all.","teach":["Reported low mood is less prominent than in younger adults. Anhedonia, loss of interest and loss of pleasure are more reliable and should be asked about directly.","Somatic presentation is the norm: fatigue, poor sleep, poor appetite, weight loss, constipation, and pain. Distinguishing these from physical illness is the central diagnostic problem.","Use the substitutive approach: give the diagnostic weight to hopelessness, worthlessness, guilt, anhedonia and suicidal ideation rather than to fatigue, sleep and appetite.","Hypochondriacal preoccupation and health anxiety are commoner presentations of depression in older people than in younger.","Cognitive complaint is common and depressive pseudodementia is the extreme form: subjective memory complaint, don't know answers, abrupt onset, preserved insight, and improvement with treatment.","The person with pseudodementia complains of memory; the person with dementia conceals it. This distinction is imperfect but useful.","A substantial proportion of people with depressive pseudodementia develop dementia within several years, so the resolution of cognitive symptoms does not end the story and follow-up is needed.","Agitation is common and prominent in older people, more so than in younger adults, with pacing, hand-wringing and importuning.","Psychotic depression is proportionately commoner in older people, with mood-congruent delusions of guilt, poverty, nihilism, illness and deserved punishment.","Cotard's syndrome, the nihilistic delusion of being dead or having no organs, is classically described in this group.","Delusions of infestation and of bodily decay occur, and delusional parasitosis in an older person should prompt assessment for depression as well as for organic causes.","Depressive stupor with mutism and immobility occurs and overlaps with catatonia, which should be screened for since it is treatable.","Irritability and behavioural change may be the presenting feature, particularly where dementia coexists.","Rating scales: the Geriatric Depression Scale, in 30, 15 or 4-item forms, which excludes somatic items and is validated in this population; and the Cornell Scale for Depression in Dementia, which is informant-based and is the correct instrument where dementia is present.","The PHQ-9 and HADS are usable but the GDS is better matched to this population.","Always ask about suicidal ideation directly. Older people rarely volunteer it and expect not to be asked.","Always exclude physical causes: thyroid function, B12 and folate, calcium, full blood count, renal and liver function, and a medication review."],"cards":[["How does depression present differently in older people?","With less reported low mood and more somatic complaint: fatigue, poor sleep, poor appetite, weight loss and pain. Anhedonia, hopelessness, worthlessness, guilt and suicidal ideation carry the diagnostic weight. Agitation, hypochondriacal preoccupation, cognitive complaint and psychotic features are all proportionately commoner than in younger adults."],["Distinguish depressive pseudodementia from dementia.","Pseudodementia shows abrupt onset with a clearer date, complaint of memory difficulty rather than concealment, prominent don't know responses rather than near-miss errors, variable effort and performance, preserved insight, and improvement with antidepressant treatment. A substantial proportion nonetheless develop dementia within several years, so follow-up is required."],["Which rating scales are appropriate in this population?","The Geriatric Depression Scale, available in 30, 15 and 4-item forms, which excludes somatic items and is validated in older people; and the Cornell Scale for Depression in Dementia, which is informant-based and is the correct instrument where dementia is present."],["Which psychotic features are characteristic of depression in old age?","Mood-congruent delusions of guilt, poverty, nihilism, illness and deserved punishment. Cotard's syndrome, the nihilistic delusion of being dead or lacking internal organs, is classically described in this group, and delusions of infestation and bodily decay occur."]],"note":["Anhedonia is more reliable than reported low mood in this population.","The substitutive approach gives weight to cognitive rather than somatic criteria.","Depressive stupor overlaps with catatonia, which should be screened for as it is treatable.","Older people rarely volunteer suicidal ideation and expect not to be asked.","Exclude thyroid disease, B12 and folate deficiency, hypercalcaemia and anaemia in every case."],"refs":["oxoldage","oyebode","nice"]};
TEACH["8.5.6.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Depression in older people responds to treatment just as well as it does at thirty. It just takes longer to work, needs a smaller starting dose, and requires someone to check that the person is not becoming hyponatraemic.","teach":["Older people respond to antidepressants as well as younger people. The belief that they do not is a persistent and harmful clinical myth.","Start low and go slow, but reach a therapeutic dose. Under-dosing is the commonest reason for apparent non-response in this group, and starting low is not a reason for staying low."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":20,"cards":5,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.6.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Older people with depression get better as often as younger ones, but they relapse more and they die sooner. The dying sooner is not mainly from suicide; it is from the heart.","teach":["Acute treatment response rates are comparable to those in younger adults, at around 50 to 60 per cent, though remission takes longer.","Relapse and recurrence rates are higher than in younger adults, which is the basis for longer maintenance treatment."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","nice","companion"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.7.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Bipolar disorder does not disappear with age, but it changes shape. It becomes more depressive, more frequent, and a substantial number of people have their first manic episode after seventy, which is almost always caused by something in the brain.","teach":["Prevalence of bipolar disorder in people over 65 in the community is around 0.5 to 1 per cent, lower than in younger adults.","The apparent decline in prevalence with age is partly real, through excess mortality, and partly artefactual through diagnostic under-recognition."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","companion"],"n":{"teach":8,"cards":2,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.7.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"If someone becomes manic for the first time at seventy-five, the first assumption should not be bipolar disorder. It should be a stroke, a tumour, a drug or a dementia, until you have shown otherwise.","teach":["Late-onset mania has weaker genetic loading and less family history than early-onset bipolar disorder, and a higher rate of identifiable neurological cause.","Secondary mania causes: stroke particularly right-sided lesions, tumour, traumatic brain injury, epilepsy, multiple sclerosis, HIV, neurosyphilis, and frontotemporal dementia."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lishman","oxoldage","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":12,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.7.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Mania in an old person often looks like confusion rather than elation. They are irritable and disorganised rather than euphoric, and the first thought on the ward is usually delirium or dementia.","teach":["Elation is less prominent than in younger adults. Irritability, hostility and querulousness are the more usual presentation.","Confusion and disorientation are common, so mania in an older person is frequently mistaken for delirium, and the two may coexist."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","oyebode","lishman"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.7.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The same drugs are used but at lower doses and with far more monitoring, because the kidneys are older, the heart is older, and the person is probably taking six other things.","teach":["The evidence base is thin. Most bipolar trials exclude older people, so treatment is extrapolated from younger populations, and this should be acknowledged rather than glossed over.","The GERI-BD trial is the principal randomised evidence in older adults, comparing lithium with valproate in late-life mania and finding lithium modestly more effective and reasonably tolerated."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","nice","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":18,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.7.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Bipolar disorder in old age is more relentless than it was earlier: episodes come closer together, more of them are depressive, and cognition does not fully recover between them.","teach":["Episode frequency tends to increase with age and with the number of previous episodes, consistent with the kindling model.","The proportion of time spent depressed increases with age, and depression rather than mania dominates the later course."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","companion","maudphys"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.8.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Some people develop schizophrenia-like illness for the first time in their sixties, seventies or eighties. It is commoner in women, commoner in the deaf, and it was argued about for fifty years before anyone agreed what to call it.","teach":["Terminology has a history. Manfred Bleuler coined late-onset schizophrenia in 1943 for onset after 40; Roth described late paraphrenia in 1955 for onset after 60.","An international consensus in 2000 settled on two terms: late-onset schizophrenia for onset between 40 and 60, and very-late-onset schizophrenia-like psychosis for onset after 60.","Around 15 to 20 per cent of people with schizophrenia have onset after 40, and a smaller proportion after 60.","Prevalence of very-late-onset schizophrenia-like psychosis in the community is around 0.5 to 1 per cent of people over 65, rising with age.","Female predominance is marked and increases with age at onset, reaching ratios of the order of five or more to one in the oldest onset group.","That female predominance is the reverse of early-onset schizophrenia, where men predominate, and is one of the strongest arguments that late-onset presentations are a distinct group.","Psychotic symptoms are far commoner overall in old age than schizophrenia-like psychosis alone, since psychosis in dementia and in delirium accounts for a much larger number of cases.","Sensory impairment, particularly deafness, is markedly over-represented, and social isolation is nearly universal in this group.","Never-married status, living alone and childlessness are consistently over-represented, though whether these are cause or consequence is not resolved."],"cards":[["Give the current terminology for late-onset psychosis.","An international consensus in 2000 defined late-onset schizophrenia for onset between 40 and 60, and very-late-onset schizophrenia-like psychosis for onset after 60. The historical terms are Manfred Bleuler's late-onset schizophrenia from 1943 and Roth's late paraphrenia from 1955."],["What is the sex ratio in very-late-onset schizophrenia-like psychosis?","Markedly female-predominant, with ratios of the order of five or more to one in the oldest onset group, which is the reverse of early-onset schizophrenia and one of the strongest arguments that this is a distinct group."]],"note":["Around 15 to 20 per cent of people with schizophrenia have onset after 40.","Community prevalence after 65 is around 0.5 to 1 per cent, rising with age.","Deafness and social isolation are markedly over-represented.","Psychosis in dementia and delirium accounts for far more cases overall than late-onset schizophrenia-like psychosis."],"refs":["oxoldage","companion"]};
TEACH["8.5.8.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Deafness and isolation are the two things that keep appearing. If you cannot hear what people are saying and there is nobody to check your interpretation against, an ordinary misunderstanding has nothing to correct it.","teach":["Genetic loading is lower than in early-onset schizophrenia, and family history of schizophrenia is less often present, though it is higher than in the general population.","Sensory impairment, particularly acquired conservative deafness of long standing, is strongly associated. It is one of the most consistent findings in this literature."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","lishman","companion"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.8.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The delusions are usually about the immediate physical world: people coming through the walls, things being moved, neighbours watching. Everything else about the person, the personality, the warmth, the way they talk, is intact.","teach":["Persecutory delusions dominate and are typically well systematised, highly specific, and concerned with the immediate physical environment and with neighbours.","Partition delusions are characteristic and near-diagnostic: the belief that people, objects, gases or radiation pass through what should be solid barriers, walls, floors, ceilings and doors."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oyebode","oxoldage","lishman"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.8.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Antipsychotics work, at a fraction of the usual dose, and the hardest part is not the prescribing. It is getting someone who believes the neighbours are gassing them to accept a tablet from a stranger.","teach":["Antipsychotics are effective, with response rates broadly comparable to early-onset schizophrenia, though the evidence base is limited by exclusion of older people from trials.","Doses required are substantially lower, commonly a quarter to a half of those used in younger adults, and starting doses lower still."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["maudsley","nice","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.8.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The symptoms respond well to treatment and the person usually stays themselves. What does not resolve is the isolation, and a proportion turn out to have been in the early stages of a dementia.","teach":["Symptomatic response to antipsychotic treatment is good, with the majority showing substantial improvement in positive symptoms.","Functional outcome is better than in early-onset schizophrenia, because negative symptoms and cognitive deterioration are absent or mild and premorbid functioning was intact."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","companion"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.9.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Anxiety in older people is common, disabling, and almost invisible in services, partly because it is assumed to be a normal reaction to being old and frail.","teach":["Anxiety disorders remain common in later life, with community prevalence estimates of around 5 to 10 per cent for any anxiety disorder, though estimates vary widely with method.","Anxiety symptoms below diagnostic threshold are much commoner, affecting perhaps 15 to 20 per cent, and carry real disability."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","nice"],"n":{"teach":11,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.9.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Older people have more genuinely frightening things to worry about, and less capacity to escape them. Distinguishing reasonable concern from disorder is a matter of proportion and of what the worry is doing to the person's life.","teach":["Aetiology differs from younger adults chiefly in the prominence of physical and situational contributors rather than in different mechanisms.","Physical illness is a major contributor: cardiac and respiratory disease directly produce symptoms indistinguishable from anxiety, and chronic illness produces realistic apprehension."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","companion","semliaison"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.9.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Older people rarely say they are anxious. They say their heart is racing, they cannot go out, they need someone with them, and they have been to the doctor about their chest four times this year.","teach":["Somatic presentation predominates. Palpitations, breathlessness, dizziness, gastrointestinal symptoms, tremor and chest discomfort lead the presentation, and psychological symptoms are volunteered last if at all.","The overlap with genuine cardiac and respiratory disease makes discrimination difficult, and both may be present simultaneously rather than as alternatives."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","oyebode","nice"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.9.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The treatment is the same as at any age with one large exception: do not reach for a benzodiazepine. In an older person they cause the falls, the confusion and the fractures that everyone was trying to prevent.","teach":["Psychological treatment is first-line and works as well in older people as in younger, though delivery requires the adaptations described under psychotherapy in later life.","CBT has the best evidence, with effect sizes comparable to those in younger adults, and relaxation training has evidence in this age group specifically."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","maudsley","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.9.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Untreated, anxiety in later life persists and shrinks the person's world one activity at a time. Treated, it responds as well as at any age, but almost nobody is offered treatment.","teach":["The untreated course is chronic and fluctuating, with generalised anxiety disorder in particular showing low rates of spontaneous remission.","Anxiety in older people is more persistent than in younger adults in longitudinal studies, partly because the situational contributors do not resolve."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","nice"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.10.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Substance misuse in old age is mostly two things: alcohol, and the tablets a doctor prescribed thirty years ago and nobody ever stopped.","teach":["Substance misuse in older people is substantially under-detected. Screening tools were developed in younger populations and the classical social consequences, such as job loss and offending, do not apply to a retired person.","Alcohol is the dominant substance. Around 10 to 20 per cent of older people drink above recommended limits, with higher rates in men."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","oxoldage","addmed"],"n":{"teach":12,"cards":3,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.10.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Older bodies handle drugs differently, so the same amount does more. Add loneliness, pain, bereavement and a doctor who never reviewed the prescription, and the picture makes itself.","teach":["Pharmacokinetic changes: reduced total body water and lean mass raise the concentration of water-soluble drugs including alcohol; increased body fat prolongs the half-life of lipid-soluble drugs including benzodiazepines.","Reduced hepatic mass and blood flow slow phase I oxidative metabolism, while phase II glucuronidation is relatively preserved, which is why lorazepam and oxazepam are preferred."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","maudsley","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["8.5.10.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"It presents as falls, confusion, poor sleep and self-neglect. Nobody presents saying they drink too much, and nobody asks, so it is found when the person is admitted and starts withdrawing on the second night.","teach":["Presentation is atypical and non-specific. Falls, confusion, cognitive impairment, self-neglect, malnutrition, incontinence, insomnia and depression are the usual routes in.","Falls are the commonest presentation of alcohol misuse in older people, and alcohol should be considered in every unexplained fall."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","lishman","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.10.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Older people do well in treatment, often better than younger ones. The obstacles are that nobody asks, nobody refers, and services are designed for people forty years younger.","teach":["Older people respond to treatment for alcohol problems at least as well as younger people, with better retention and better outcomes in several studies.","Age-specific treatment produces better engagement and outcomes than mixed-age services, where older people frequently feel out of place and drop out."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","addmed","maudsley"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.10.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"The outlook is better than most clinicians expect, especially for the person who started drinking late after a bereavement. The damage that has already been done to the brain is what limits recovery.","teach":["Treatment outcomes in older people are good, and better than in younger populations in several studies, with higher completion and higher abstinence rates.","Late-onset drinkers have substantially better outcomes than early-onset drinkers, with less physical damage, better social support and better premorbid functioning."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["semaddict","oxoldage","lishman"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.11.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"This heading covers what is left: the conditions that turn up in old age psychiatry without a chapter of their own, and two of them are far commoner than anyone records.","teach":["Self-neglect and squalor: Diogenes syndrome, or severe domestic squalor, affects around 0.5 per 1,000 older people per year, and presentation is usually through neighbours, housing or environmental health rather than through health services.","Hoarding disorder affects around 2 to 6 per cent of the population, with prevalence rising with age and severity increasing over the life course."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","icd11","oxid"],"n":{"teach":11,"cards":3,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.11.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Self-neglect on this scale is not laziness and it is not usually dementia. It is most often a lifelong personality pattern of extreme self-sufficiency meeting the point at which self-sufficiency stops working.","teach":["Diogenes syndrome, named by Clark in 1975, comprises severe self-neglect, domestic squalor, social withdrawal, refusal of help, and frequently hoarding or syllogomania.","The name is a misnomer. Diogenes the Cynic chose poverty deliberately as a philosophical position and was gregarious, which is the opposite of the syndrome named after him."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","icd11","lishman"],"n":{"teach":13,"cards":4,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.11.3"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Elder abuse is common, hidden, and usually committed by someone the person depends on. It is asked about far less often than child abuse and is detected far less often too.","teach":["Elder abuse affects a substantial minority of older people, with prevalence estimates around 10 per cent in community samples and considerably higher in institutional settings.","The World Health Organization defines it as a single or repeated act, or lack of appropriate action, occurring within any relationship where there is an expectation of trust, which causes harm or distress to an older person."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["eastman","oxoldage","nice"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":5,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.11.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"When you suspect abuse, the safeguarding process is not something you hand over and forget. It is a clinical intervention, and the person's own wishes have to be at the centre of it unless they cannot be.","teach":["The Care Act 2014 places a statutory duty on local authorities in England to make enquiries where an adult with care and support needs is experiencing, or is at risk of, abuse or neglect and is unable to protect themselves.","The three conditions must all be met for the statutory duty: care and support needs, experiencing or at risk of abuse or neglect, and unable to protect themselves as a result of those needs."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["eastman","nice","oxoldage"],"n":{"teach":17,"cards":5,"note":4}};
TEACH["8.5.11.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Squalor, hoarding and abuse all have poor outcomes, and all of them have better outcomes if someone keeps turning up. The single most effective intervention across this whole group is persistence.","teach":["Severe domestic squalor has a poor prognosis. Mortality is high, with figures around 45 per cent within five years reported in some series, driven by physical neglect, malnutrition, hypothermia and untreated illness.","Recurrence after clearance is very common where the underlying problem is untreated. Cleaning the house without addressing the person achieves nothing and can be experienced as a violation."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["oxoldage","nice","eastman"],"n":{"teach":15,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["9.1.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Freud's central claim was that most of what drives us is hidden from us, and that it leaks out sideways: in slips of the tongue, in dreams, and above all in how we treat the person sitting opposite. Psychodynamic therapy is the deliberate use of that last leak.","teach":["Freud's topographical model: conscious, preconscious and unconscious. His later structural model: id, driven by instinct and the pleasure principle; ego, negotiating with reality; superego, carrying conscience and the ego ideal.","Defence mechanisms are the ego's ways of keeping unbearable material out of awareness. Anna Freud catalogued them; her father discovered them.","Mature defences: sublimation, humour, altruism, anticipation, suppression. Neurotic: repression, displacement, reaction formation, intellectualisation, isolation of affect, undoing. Immature: projection, denial, splitting, acting out, projective identification, regression, somatisation.","Vaillant's hierarchy from the Grant Study showed that mature defences predict better long-term adjustment, one of the few empirical findings in this territory.","Melanie Klein: object relations from the first months of life. The paranoid-schizoid position, where the world is split into good and bad, gives way to the depressive position, where the child recognises that the loved and hated object are one person, bringing guilt and the wish to repair.","Klein's projective identification is not simply projecting: unwanted feeling is put into the other person, who then actually begins to feel it. This is the mechanism behind much of what happens to teams around personality disorder.","Jung split from Freud over the sexual theory of the libido. His terms: the collective unconscious, archetypes, the persona, the shadow, anima and animus, and individuation as the goal of the second half of life.","Winnicott: the good-enough mother, who fails the infant gradually and tolerably; the holding environment; transitional objects and transitional phenomena; the true and false self.","Bowlby broke with classical theory by grounding attachment in ethology rather than drive. The child's tie to the mother is a primary biological system, not a by-product of feeding.","Transference is the displacement onto the therapist of feelings originally belonging to a significant figure in the patient's past. Countertransference is the therapist's emotional response to the patient.","Countertransference was originally regarded as a contaminant to be eliminated. It is now treated as data: what the patient makes you feel is information about what they do to people.","Therapeutic alliance is the collaborative bond and shared purpose. It is the single most robust predictor of outcome across every therapeutic modality, and it predicts outcome more strongly than the choice of therapy.","Resistance is anything in the patient that opposes the work. Negative therapeutic reaction is the paradoxical worsening that follows a good interpretation, classically explained by unconscious guilt.","Acting out is the enactment of unconscious material in behaviour rather than its expression in words. Working through is the repeated re-encounter with the same conflict in different forms until it loses its force.","Interpretation makes the unconscious conscious; insight is the patient's own grasp of it. Insight without emotional experience changes little, which is Alexander's corrective emotional experience.","Brief dynamic therapy uses a strict time limit and a single agreed focus. Malan's triangles are the tools: the triangle of conflict, being defence, anxiety and hidden feeling; and the triangle of person, linking current relationships, the transference and past figures.","Supportive psychotherapy does not interpret. It strengthens existing defences, restores function and holds the person, and is the right choice where insight-oriented work would be destabilising: acute crisis, psychosis, severe ego fragility.","Indications for exploratory work: psychological mindedness, a capacity to tolerate distress without acting on it, a circumscribed focus, and a genuine wish to understand rather than only to be relieved."],"cards":[["Distinguish transference from countertransference, and state the modern view of the latter.","Transference is the displacement onto the therapist of feelings belonging to significant figures in the patient's past. Countertransference is the therapist's emotional reaction to the patient. Originally regarded as a contaminant to be eliminated, it is now used as clinical data about the patient's impact on others."],["Describe Klein's two positions.","The paranoid-schizoid position, in which the object is split into wholly good and wholly bad and the dominant anxiety is persecutory; and the depressive position, in which the loved and hated object are recognised as one person, bringing guilt, concern and the wish to make reparation."],["What are Malan's two triangles?","The triangle of conflict: defence, anxiety and hidden feeling. The triangle of person: current relationships, the transference relationship and past figures, usually parental. Interpretation moves around both."],["Which factor most robustly predicts outcome across therapies?","The therapeutic alliance, the collaborative bond and shared sense of purpose between patient and therapist. It predicts outcome more strongly than the choice of therapeutic modality."],["Name Winnicott's principal contributions.","The good-enough mother who fails the infant gradually and tolerably; the holding environment; transitional objects and transitional phenomena; and the distinction between the true self and the false self."]],"note":["Structural model: id, ego, superego. Topographical model: conscious, preconscious, unconscious.","Anna Freud catalogued the defence mechanisms; Vaillant ranked them and showed mature defences predict better long-term adjustment.","Projective identification places unwanted feeling in the other person, who then experiences it.","Jung: collective unconscious, archetypes, persona, shadow, anima and animus, individuation.","Negative therapeutic reaction is worsening after an accurate interpretation, classically attributed to unconscious guilt.","Supportive therapy strengthens defences rather than interpreting them, and is indicated where insight would destabilise."],"refs":["gabbard","companion","tyrer11"]};
TEACH["9.2"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Family therapy starts from a different assumption: the problem is not inside the child who was referred. The problem is in the pattern between people, and the child is simply the one showing it. Change the pattern and the symptom often has nothing left to do.","teach":["General Systems Theory, from von Bertalanffy, supplies the frame. A family is a system: it has boundaries, subsystems, feedback loops, and it seeks homeostasis.","Circular rather than linear causality. A does not cause B; A and B maintain each other. Asking who started it is the wrong question."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","gabbard","rutter"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":5}};
TEACH["9.3.1"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Behaviour therapy does not ask why you are frightened. It arranges for you to meet the thing you fear, in a controlled way, for long enough that your body gives up being frightened on its own. That giving-up is habituation and it is the engine of every exposure treatment.","teach":["The theoretical basis is learning theory: classical conditioning from Pavlov, operant conditioning from Skinner, and Mowrer's two-factor theory of how phobias are acquired and maintained.","Two-factor theory: the fear is acquired by classical conditioning and maintained by operant conditioning, because avoidance is negatively reinforced by the relief it brings.","Habituation is the decline in response to a repeated stimulus. It requires the stimulus to be maintained; escape aborts habituation and strengthens the avoidance.","Systematic desensitisation, Wolpe: construct a hierarchy, train in relaxation, then pair graded imaginal or in vivo exposure with relaxation. Based on reciprocal inhibition, the idea that relaxation and anxiety cannot coexist.","Modern exposure therapy has largely abandoned relaxation pairing. Graded exposure without relaxation works, and prolonged exposure works better than brief.","Exposure must be graded, prolonged, repeated and without safety behaviours. Safety behaviours prevent disconfirmation of the feared belief and are the commonest reason exposure fails.","Flooding is exposure at maximum intensity from the outset rather than by gradation. Effective but poorly tolerated.","Cue exposure is exposure to the conditioned stimuli associated with substance use, allowing craving to extinguish; used in addictions.","Exposure and response prevention is the specific behavioural treatment of OCD: exposure to the trigger with prevention of the compulsion, so the anxiety habituates without the ritual.","Operant techniques: positive and negative reinforcement, positive and negative punishment, shaping by successive approximation, chaining, token economy, time out, and response cost.","Extinction is withdrawal of the reinforcer. Expect an extinction burst, a temporary worsening, before the behaviour declines, and warn families about it or they abandon the plan.","Intermittent reinforcement produces behaviour most resistant to extinction, which is why inconsistent responding to a behaviour entrenches it.","Functional analysis is the ABC of behaviour: Antecedents, Behaviour, Consequences. It asks what the behaviour achieves for this person rather than what it means.","Common functions of behaviour: escape or avoidance, attention, tangible gain, and sensory or automatic reinforcement. The function, not the topography, determines the intervention.","Social skills training uses instruction, modelling, rehearsal, feedback and homework, with attention to generalisation outside the session, which is where it usually fails.","Measurement is not optional in behavioural work. Baseline recording, an agreed target, repeated measurement using scales or frequency counts, and single-case designs such as the ABAB reversal."],"cards":[["State Mowrer's two-factor theory.","A phobia is acquired by classical conditioning, in which a neutral stimulus becomes associated with fear, and maintained by operant conditioning, because avoidance is negatively reinforced by the relief that follows it."],["What are the requirements for effective exposure?","Exposure must be graded, prolonged enough for habituation, repeated, and free of safety behaviours. Escape before habituation aborts the process and strengthens avoidance."],["Describe functional analysis.","Analysis of Antecedents, Behaviour and Consequences to determine what the behaviour achieves for the person. Common functions are escape or avoidance, attention, tangible gain, and sensory or automatic reinforcement. The function, not the appearance of the behaviour, determines the intervention."],["What is an extinction burst?","A temporary increase in the frequency or intensity of a behaviour immediately after its reinforcer is withdrawn, before the behaviour declines. Families must be warned of it or they conclude the plan has failed."],["Distinguish systematic desensitisation from modern graded exposure.","Systematic desensitisation, from Wolpe, pairs graded exposure with trained relaxation on the principle of reciprocal inhibition. Modern graded exposure omits the relaxation pairing, which adds little, and emphasises prolonged exposure without safety behaviours."]],"note":["Intermittent reinforcement produces the greatest resistance to extinction.","Flooding is maximum-intensity exposure from the outset; effective but poorly tolerated.","Exposure and response prevention is the behavioural treatment of OCD.","Cue exposure allows conditioned craving to extinguish in addictions.","Social skills training most often fails at generalisation outside the session.","Single-case designs such as ABAB reversal are used to demonstrate behavioural change."],"refs":["companion","gross","tyrer11"]};
TEACH["9.3.2"] = {"free":true,"simple":"Cognitive therapy says the event is not what upsets you. Your reading of the event is what upsets you, and that reading arrives so fast it feels like the truth rather than an opinion. The work is to slow it down enough to be looked at.","teach":["Beck's cognitive model has three levels. Core beliefs, or schemas, are absolute statements about self, world and future. Dysfunctional assumptions are the conditional rules built on them. Negative automatic thoughts are the moment-to-moment output.","Beck's cognitive triad in depression: negative view of the self, of the world, and of the future.","Negative automatic thoughts are involuntary, plausible to the person, and consistent with the underlying schema. They are the accessible surface of the system and therefore where therapy starts.","Cognitive distortions: arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation, dichotomous thinking, catastrophising, mind reading, emotional reasoning, and should statements.","Schemas are laid down early, remain latent, and are activated by events matching their content. This is the diathesis-stress element of the model.","Structure is intrinsic, not incidental: agenda setting, mood check, review of homework, agreed focus, summarising, feedback, and new homework. Cognitive therapy without structure is not cognitive therapy.","Collaborative empiricism is the stance. Therapist and patient treat the thought as a hypothesis and design a test.","Socratic questioning does not tell the patient they are wrong. It asks for the evidence, the alternative explanation, and the consequence of holding the belief.","Core techniques: thought records, behavioural experiments, activity scheduling, graded task assignment, mastery and pleasure ratings, and the downward arrow to reach the core belief.","Behavioural activation is the behavioural component of cognitive therapy for depression, and trials suggest it is effective on its own, without the cognitive component, in moderate to severe depression.","Model-specific formulations: Clark's model of panic, in which catastrophic misinterpretation of benign bodily sensations creates a vicious circle; Clark and Wells on social anxiety, with self-focused attention and post-event processing; Salkovskis on OCD, in which inflated responsibility drives neutralising.","Ehlers and Clark on PTSD: an excessively negative appraisal of the trauma and its sequelae, combined with a fragmented, poorly elaborated trauma memory.","Culture matters at the level of the assumption. What counts as a dysfunctional belief about family obligation, or about the appropriate expression of distress, is not culturally neutral, and imposing the therapist's norms is a real failure mode.","The evidence base is strongest in depression and the anxiety disorders. In psychosis, CBTp has a smaller effect on positive symptoms than was once claimed, and the trial literature is contested."],"cards":[["Describe the three levels of Beck's cognitive model.","Core beliefs or schemas, absolute statements about self, world and future; dysfunctional assumptions, the conditional rules built on them; and negative automatic thoughts, the involuntary moment-to-moment output that is accessible in session."],["State Beck's cognitive triad.","A negative view of the self, a negative view of the world and current experience, and a negative view of the future."],["Summarise Clark's cognitive model of panic.","Benign bodily sensations are catastrophically misinterpreted as evidence of imminent physical or mental disaster, which increases anxiety, which intensifies the sensations, producing a vicious circle. Safety behaviours prevent disconfirmation."],["What is collaborative empiricism?","The stance in which therapist and patient jointly treat a belief as a hypothesis rather than a fact, and design behavioural experiments and evidence reviews to test it."],["Name six cognitive distortions.","Arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation, and dichotomous thinking. Catastrophising, mind reading, emotional reasoning and should statements are also standard."]],"note":["Session structure is intrinsic: agenda, mood check, homework review, focus, summary, feedback, new homework.","The downward arrow technique moves from an automatic thought to the underlying core belief.","Behavioural activation is effective as a standalone treatment in moderate to severe depression.","Clark and Wells on social anxiety: self-focused attention, safety behaviours and post-event processing.","Salkovskis on OCD: inflated responsibility drives neutralising behaviour.","Ehlers and Clark on PTSD: negative appraisal plus a fragmented trauma memory."],"refs":["companion","nice","gabbard"]};
TEACH["9.4"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Beyond the two big families there is a shelf of named therapies, each built around one idea. Knowing which idea belongs to which name is most of what is asked.","teach":["Interpersonal Therapy, Klerman and Weissman. Time-limited, typically sixteen sessions, for depression. It does not interpret the past; it works on the present interpersonal context.","IPT has four problem areas and you choose one or two: grief, interpersonal role disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits. NICE recommends it for depression and it has good evidence in bulimia nervosa."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gabbard","nice","companion"],"n":{"teach":16,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["9.5"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Does therapy work? Yes, and by roughly the same amount whichever one you pick, which is either the most interesting finding in the field or an artefact of how badly it is measured. Both positions have serious people behind them.","teach":["Eysenck, 1952, claimed that psychotherapy produced no better outcome than spontaneous remission. The claim was methodologically weak but it forced the field into outcome research.","Smith and Glass, 1977, produced the first large meta-analysis of psychotherapy and reported an overall effect size of about 0.85, meaning the average treated person did better than about eighty per cent of untreated controls."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["lawrie","nice","gabbard"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":6}};
TEACH["9.6"] = {"free":false,"simple":"A group does something no individual therapy can. It shows a person, in real time and in front of witnesses, exactly what they do to other people. That is uncomfortable, which is why it works and why people leave.","teach":["Historical roots: Joseph Pratt's tuberculosis classes in Boston, 1905, are usually credited as the first therapeutic groups. The Second World War forced the pace.","The Northfield Experiments at Northfield Military Hospital, involving Bion, Foulkes, Main and Rickman, produced both group analysis and the therapeutic community."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["gabbard","companion"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":5,"note":6}};
TEACH["9.6.1"] = {"free":false,"simple":"Not every group is a psychodynamic group. Most groups in an NHS service are doing something narrower and more practical, and the College expects you to know which is which.","teach":["Cognitive behavioural groups deliver a structured protocol to several people at once. Agenda, psychoeducation, homework and a manual, with the group used for efficiency, normalisation and vicarious learning rather than for its own dynamics.","CBT groups have good evidence in depression, social anxiety, panic and health anxiety, with effect sizes generally somewhat smaller than individual CBT but far better cost-effectiveness."],"cards":[],"note":[],"refs":["nice","gabbard","semaddict"],"n":{"teach":14,"cards":4,"note":6}};
